window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
(function () {
var K = "MADE EASY unofficial key";
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2026-1", year: 2026, no: 1, subject: "bm", topic: "Water absorption of heavy-duty bricks", keySource: K,
q: `Water absorption for burnt clay heavy duty bricks should not be more than :`,
opts: ["10 percent", "20 percent", "5 percent", "15 percent"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 10 percent</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 2180 (heavy-duty burnt clay bricks) limits the average water absorption after 24 h immersion in cold water to <b>10 %</b> by weight. Heavy-duty bricks are used for bridges, heavy foundations and industrial floors, so they must be dense, with a compressive strength of at least 40 N/mm².</p><div class="formula">Water absorption ≤ 10 % (heavy-duty)</div>
<p>For comparison, IS 1077 (common burnt clay bricks) allows up to 20 % for the lower classes, and first-class bricks are usually limited to about 15 % in textbooks.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>IS 2180 limit after 24 h immersion: <b>10 %</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 20 % is the limit for ordinary common bricks (IS 1077), not heavy-duty bricks.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 5 % is stricter than the code asks; even engineering bricks are not limited to this.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 15 % is the usual figure quoted for first-class bricks, still higher than allowed for heavy-duty bricks.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Low absorption = dense brick = high strength and good frost and weather resistance.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-2", year: 2026, no: 2, subject: "bm", topic: "Weathering of felspar to kaolinite", keySource: K,
q: `The decomposition of felspar is represented as K<sub>2</sub>Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.6H<sub>2</sub>O + CO<sub>2</sub> + nH<sub>2</sub>O =`,
opts: ["K<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.2SiO<sub>2</sub>.2H<sub>2</sub>O + 4SiO<sub>2</sub>.nH<sub>2</sub>O", "K<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.SiO<sub>2</sub>.2H<sub>2</sub>O + 4SiO<sub>2</sub>.nH<sub>2</sub>O", "K<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.2SiO<sub>2</sub>.H<sub>2</sub>O + 4SiO<sub>2</sub>.nH<sub>2</sub>O", "K<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> + Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>.SiO<sub>2</sub>.H<sub>2</sub>O + 4SiO<sub>2</sub>.nH<sub>2</sub>O"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) K₂CO₃ + Al₂O₃·2SiO₂·2H₂O + 4SiO₂·nH₂O</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Orthoclase felspar is K₂O·Al₂O₃·6SiO₂ (the question prints it loosely). Carbonated water attacks it (kaolinisation):</p>
<div class="formula">K₂O·Al₂O₃·6SiO₂ + CO₂ + nH₂O → K₂CO₃ + Al₂O₃·2SiO₂·2H₂O + 4SiO₂·nH₂O</div>
<p>The products are soluble potassium carbonate, <b>kaolinite</b> (china clay, Al₂O₃·2SiO₂·2H₂O, the raw material of bricks and tiles) and hydrated silica. Silicon balance: 6 SiO₂ = 2 (in kaolinite) + 4 (as silica), so the equation balances only with 2SiO₂ in the clay.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Al₂O₃·SiO₂·2H₂O leaves one SiO₂ unaccounted for, and is not the kaolinite formula.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Kaolinite carries two molecules of water, not one.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Wrong in both silica and water.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Kaolinite = Al₂O₃·2SiO₂·2H₂O: "one alumina, two silica, two water".</p>` },

{ id: "2026-3", year: 2026, no: 3, subject: "bm", topic: "Colouring oxides in glass", keySource: K,
q: `The metal oxide used to make emerald green glass is :`,
opts: ["MnO<sub>2</sub>", "Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>", "Cobalt oxide", "Iron oxide"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) chromium oxide (printed Cr₂O₇)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Chromium</b> compounds (chromic oxide Cr₂O₃, or a dichromate added to the batch) give glass its bright <b>emerald green</b> colour. The option prints "Cr₂O₇", which is the dichromate ion; either way the colouring element is chromium, and it is the only chromium option.</p>
<p>Common glass colourants: cobalt — blue; chromium — emerald green; ferrous iron — bottle green; manganese — violet/purple (also used in small amounts to "decolourise" iron tints); copper — blue-green or ruby red; gold — ruby red; selenium/cadmium — red/yellow.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> MnO₂ gives violet/amethyst; it is also the glassmaker's decoloriser.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Cobalt oxide gives a deep blue.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Iron oxide gives the dull bottle-green or brown of ordinary bottles, not emerald green.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Chromium is the element behind the colour of the emerald gem too.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-4", year: 2026, no: 4, subject: "bm", topic: "Defects in timber", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List-I</th><th>List-II</th></tr><tr><td>P. Cupping</td><td>1. Caused by wood limbs encased by wood of the free trunk</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Bowing</td><td>2. Caused by grain irregularities in the board</td></tr><tr><td>R. Checks</td><td>3. Small cracks appearing at the ends of boards due to rapid drying</td></tr><tr><td>S. Knots</td><td>4. Unequal shrinking in the radial and tangential direction</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3", "P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1", "P-1, Q-2, R-4, S-3", "P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Defects in timber.</p><ul><li><b>Cupping (P-4)</b>: the board curls across its width because tangential shrinkage is about twice the radial shrinkage.</li><li><b>Bowing (Q-2)</b>: curvature along the length of the board, caused by grain irregularities (cross grain, reaction wood) that make one face shrink more.</li><li><b>Checks (R-3)</b>: small separations of fibres along the grain, typically at board ends, from rapid surface drying.</li><li><b>Knots (S-1)</b>: bases of branches (limbs) enclosed by the wood of the growing trunk.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Puts cupping against grain irregularities and knots against end cracks: both wrong.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Calls cupping a knot and checks a shrinkage warp.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Cupping is not a drying crack and knots are not shrinkage defects.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Warp family: cup (across width), bow (along face), crook (along edge), twist (spiral).</p>` },

{ id: "2026-5", year: 2026, no: 5, subject: "bm", topic: "Effects of cement fineness", keySource: K, selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following statements is <b><i>not</i></b> correct regarding the fineness of cement ?`,
opts: ["Fine cement is more liable to suffer from shrinkage cracking than a coarse cement.", "Fine cement shows faster rate of heat evolution and total quantity of heat evolved is much larger than coarse cement.", "Fine cement will show faster rate of hardening than coarse cement.", "Fine cement shows the same setting time as coarse cement."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Fine cement shows the same setting time as coarse cement</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Finer grinding gives more surface area per gram, so hydration starts and proceeds faster: <b>finer cement sets and hardens faster</b>. Statement (d) says the setting time is the same, which is wrong (MADE EASY's key and explanation).</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> True: fine cement needs more water, shrinks more and is more prone to shrinkage cracking.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Half-true: the <i>rate</i> of heat evolution rises with fineness, but the <i>total</i> heat at full hydration depends on composition and is about the same. This statement is also loosely worded.</li><li><b>(c)</b> True: faster early strength gain (as in rapid-hardening cement).</li></ul>
<p>Our first pick was (b), on the "total heat" wording. On review, the effect on setting is the more basic error the question targets, and the key's (d) is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Fineness changes the <i>speed</i> of hydration (setting, hardening, heat rate), not the final amount of heat.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-6", year: 2026, no: 6, subject: "bm", topic: "Types of cement and their uses", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List-I (Types of cement)</th><th>List-II (Uses)</th></tr><tr><td>P. Rapid hardening</td><td>1. Repair of bridges</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Quick-setting</td><td>2. Dams</td></tr><tr><td>R. High alumina</td><td>3. Concrete under water</td></tr><tr><td>S. Low-heat</td><td>4. Refractory concrete in industries</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3", "P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1", "P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4", "P-1, Q-3, R-4, S-2"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) P-1, Q-3, R-4, S-2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Types of cement and their uses.</p><ul><li><b>Rapid hardening (P-1)</b>: high early strength (3-day strength ≈ 7-day of OPC), so repaired roads and bridges can be reopened quickly.</li><li><b>Quick-setting (Q-3)</b>: sets within minutes, used for concreting under (running) water.</li><li><b>High alumina (R-4)</b>: resists high temperatures and chemical attack, so it is used for refractory concrete in furnaces and industries.</li><li><b>Low-heat (S-2)</b>: low C₃A and C₃S give little heat of hydration, so it suits mass concrete in dams.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Rapid hardening is not a dam cement; low-heat is not for under-water work.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Rapid hardening is not refractory, and quick-setting would crack in a dam.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Quick-setting is not a repair-of-bridges cement and low-heat is not refractory.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Dams want low heat; furnaces want high alumina; repair wants rapid hardening.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-7", year: 2026, no: 7, subject: "bm", topic: "Oxide composition of OPC", keySource: K,
q: `The approximate composition of CaO in Portland cement is in the range of :`,
opts: ["60% – 65%", "50% – 55%", "65% – 70%", "55% – 60%"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 60 % – 65 %</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Typical oxide composition of ordinary Portland cement:</p><div class="formula">OPC oxides: CaO 60–67 %, SiO₂ 17–25 %, Al₂O₃ 3–8 %</div>
<table><tr><th>Oxide</th><th>Per cent</th></tr><tr><td>CaO (lime)</td><td>60–65 (up to 67)</td></tr><tr><td>SiO₂ (silica)</td><td>17–25</td></tr><tr><td>Al₂O₃ (alumina)</td><td>3–8</td></tr><tr><td>Fe₂O₃</td><td>0.5–6</td></tr><tr><td>MgO</td><td>0.5–4</td></tr><tr><td>SO₃</td><td>1–2</td></tr></table>
<p>Lime is the largest constituent; it combines with silica to form C₃S and C₂S. Excess free lime makes the cement unsound; too little lowers strength.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Read the oxide asked from the table.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 50–55 % is too little lime for normal Portland cement (C₃S would be low).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 55–60 % is below the usual 60–65 % range.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 65–70 % is above the usual range; excess lime makes the cement unsound.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Remember "60-20-5-3": lime, silica, alumina, iron oxide, roughly.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-8", year: 2026, no: 8, subject: "bm", topic: "Determining MgO in cement", keySource: K,
q: `The percentage of MgO in cement is calculated using :`,
opts: ["Weight of residue × 32·4", "Weight of residue × 27·4", "Weight of residue × 42·7", "Weight of residue × 72·4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Weight of residue × 72·4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the chemical analysis of cement (IS 4032), magnesium is precipitated as magnesium ammonium phosphate and ignited to <b>magnesium pyrophosphate</b>, Mg₂P₂O₇.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The MgO equivalent of Mg₂P₂O₇ is 2 × 40·3 / 222·6 = 0·362.</li></ol>
<div class="formula">MgO % = W × 0·362 × 100 / 0·5 = 72·4 × W</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>W = weight of ignited residue (Mg₂P₂O₇), g</li><li>0·5 g = weight of cement sample taken</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 32.4 is not the MgO factor from Mg₂P₂O₇ (0.362 × 100/sample).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 27.4 is not the gravimetric factor for MgO.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 42.7 is not the gravimetric factor for MgO.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> MgO is limited to 6 % in OPC because it hydrates late and expands (unsoundness).</p>` },

{ id: "2026-9", year: 2026, no: 9, subject: "bm", topic: "Modulus of rupture of building lime", keySource: K,
q: `The minimum value of Modulus of Rupture for Class A lime should be :`,
opts: ["1·15 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "1·05 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "1·50 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "1·25 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·05 N/mm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 712 (building limes) specifies the transverse strength (modulus of rupture) of lime-sand mortar at 28 days. Class A lime (eminently hydraulic, used for structural work) has the highest requirement, about <b>1·05 N/mm²</b> (often rounded to 1·0); Class B (semi-hydraulic) and Class E (kankar) need about 0·7 N/mm², and fat limes (C) have no strength requirement.</p>
<p>Class A lime also has compressive strength requirements of about 1·75 N/mm² at 14 days and 2·8 N/mm² at 28 days.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1·15 is higher than the code minimum for Class A lime.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·50 is higher than the code minimum for Class A lime.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1·25 N/mm² is higher than the code minimum for Class A lime.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Hydraulic limes (A, B) set under water; fat lime (C) only hardens by carbonation.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-10", year: 2026, no: 10, subject: "bm", topic: "Mortar proportions for different works", keySource: K,
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List-I (Cement mortar for different works)</th><th>List-II (Proportion of cement : Sand in mortar)</th></tr><tr><td>P. Normal brickwork</td><td>1. 1 : 4</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Plastering work</td><td>2. 1 : 3</td></tr><tr><td>R. Grouting cavernous rocks</td><td>3. 1 : 6</td></tr><tr><td>S. Guniting</td><td>4. 1 : 1·5</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3", "P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2", "P-1, Q-2, R-4, S-3", "P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Mortar proportions for different works.</p><ul><li><b>Normal brickwork (P-3)</b>: 1 : 6 cement–sand is the usual masonry mortar.</li><li><b>Plastering (Q-1)</b>: 1 : 4 (1 : 3 to 1 : 6 depending on the coat), richer than masonry mortar.</li><li><b>Grouting cavernous rocks (R-4)</b>: a rich, fluid mix about 1 : 1·5 (or 1 : 1) to fill voids.</li><li><b>Guniting (S-2)</b>: 1 : 3 cement–sand sprayed under pressure.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Brickwork at 1 : 3 and guniting at 1 : 6 are reversed in richness.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Brickwork at 1 : 4 and guniting at 1 : 6 are wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Grouting at 1 : 4 is too lean to fill rock caverns; guniting is not 1 : 1·5.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Richest mix for grout, then gunite (1 : 3), plaster (1 : 4), masonry (1 : 6).</p>` },

{ id: "2026-11", year: 2026, no: 11, subject: "bm", topic: "Size effect on concrete strength", keySource: K,
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the factors influencing strength of concrete ?`,
opts: ["If the size of cube is decreased, the compressive strength tends to increase and modulus of elasticity decreases.", "If the size of cube is decreased, the compressive strength tends to decrease and modulus of elasticity increases.", "If the size of cube is decreased, the compressive strength tends to increase and modulus of elasticity also increases.", "If the size of cube is decreased, the compressive strength tends to decrease and modulus of elasticity also decreases."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) strength increases and modulus of elasticity also increases</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Size effect</b>: a smaller specimen has a lower chance of containing a critical flaw, and platen restraint confines a larger share of its volume, so the measured compressive strength rises as the cube gets smaller (100 mm cubes test a few per cent higher than 150 mm cubes).</p>
<p>The modulus of elasticity of concrete is linked to its strength (IS 456: E<sub>c</sub> = 5000√f<sub>ck</sub>), so a specimen that shows higher strength also shows a higher apparent modulus. Both quantities therefore move the same way.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Strength and modulus do not move in opposite directions here.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Smaller cubes give <i>higher</i>, not lower, strength.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Smaller cubes give <i>higher</i>, not lower, strength.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Smaller specimen → fewer weak spots → higher measured strength.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-12", year: 2026, no: 12, subject: "bm", topic: "Mix design: fine aggregate content", keySource: K,
q: `The cement content in a mix design is 378 kg/m<sup>3</sup>, water content 170 kg, sand is 30% of total aggregate, entrapped air is 1%, specific gravity of cement, coarse aggregate and fine aggregate are, respectively 3·15, 2·70 and 2·60. The fine aggregate is approximately :`,
opts: ["510 kg", "600 kg", "550 kg", "450 kg"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 550 kg</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Absolute volume method for 1 m³ of concrete (IS 10262).</p>
<div class="formula">V<sub>agg</sub> = 1 − V<sub>air</sub> − C/(1000 G<sub>c</sub>) − W/1000</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>V<sub>air</sub> = 0·01 m³; C = 378 kg; G<sub>c</sub> = 3·15; W = 170 kg</li><li>sand = 30 % of the total aggregate volume; G<sub>fa</sub> = 2·60</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>Cement volume = 378/3150 = 0·120 m³; water = 0·170 m³; air = 0·010 m³.</li><li>Aggregate volume = 1 − 0·12 − 0·17 − 0·01 = 0·70 m³.</li><li>Fine aggregate volume = 0·30 × 0·70 = 0·21 m³.</li><li>Mass = 0·21 × 2·60 × 1000 = <b>546 kg ≈ 550 kg</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 510 kg comes from dropping one of the volumes or using a wrong fraction.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 450 kg comes from dropping one of the volumes or using a wrong fraction.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 600 kg would need about 33 % sand.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Coarse aggregate here = 0·49 × 2·70 × 1000 ≈ 1323 kg.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-13", year: 2026, no: 13, subject: "solid", topic: "Stress components and free-surface conditions", keySource: K,
q: `The component of stress acting on infinitesimal element is shown in the figure. Which one of the following statements is <b><i>not</i></b> correct ?<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2026-13.png" alt="A cube with x, y, z axes showing normal stresses σxx, σyy, σzz and shear stresses σxy, σyx, σyz, σzy, σzx, σxz on its faces">`,
opts: ["The normal stress components are σ<sub>xx</sub>, σ<sub>yy</sub>, and σ<sub>zz</sub>. The shear stress components are σ<sub>yz</sub>, σ<sub>zx</sub>, σ<sub>xy</sub>, σ<sub>zy</sub>, σ<sub>xz</sub>, and σ<sub>yx</sub>.", "On a free surface, the two shear stress components in the surface vanish, that is, if z is the normal to a free surface, σ<sub>yz</sub> = σ<sub>zx</sub> = 0.", "If the surfaces are assumed to have friction, then the shear stresses acting on the surface vanish, that is, σ<sub>yz</sub> = σ<sub>zx</sub> = 0.", "Unless there is a pressure acting on a free surface, the stress normal to it also vanishes, that is, σ<sub>zz</sub> = 0."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Shear stresses on a surface vanish only when that surface is <b>frictionless</b> (or free). If the contact surface has <b>friction</b>, tangential tractions are transmitted and σ<sub>zx</sub>, σ<sub>zy</sub> are generally <i>not</i> zero. So (c) is the wrong statement.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct: three normal components (equal subscripts) and six shear components (unequal subscripts), of which only three are independent (σ<sub>xy</sub> = σ<sub>yx</sub>, etc.).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Correct: a traction-free surface with normal z carries no shear, so σ<sub>zx</sub> = σ<sub>zy</sub> = 0.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Correct: with no pressure applied, the normal stress on the free surface is also zero.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> A free surface is a principal plane with zero principal stress — the basis of plane-stress analysis of thin plates.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-14", year: 2026, no: 14, subject: "solid", topic: "Engineering strain in extension and compression", keySource: K,
q: `An element 2 cm long is extended to twice of its initial length and then compressed to its initial length. The engineering strains for the extension and compression are, respectively :`,
opts: ["1 and – 0·5", "2 and – 1", "0·5 and – 1", "0·693 and – 0·693"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and −0·5</h3><div class="formula">ε<sub>eng</sub> = (L<sub>f</sub> − L<sub>0</sub>)/L<sub>0</sub></div>
<ul class="sym"><li>L<sub>0</sub> = original length of that stage; L<sub>f</sub> = final length of that stage</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>Extension: 2 cm → 4 cm: ε = (4 − 2)/2 = <b>1</b>.</li><li>Compression: 4 cm → 2 cm, measured on the length at the start of this stage: ε = (2 − 4)/4 = <b>−0·5</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Engineering strains of the two stages do not cancel because each is referred to a different starting length. True (logarithmic) strains do: ln 2 = +0·693 and ln(1/2) = −0·693.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 2 and −1 divide by the wrong lengths.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·5 and −1 swap the reference lengths.</li><li><b>(d)</b> ±0·693 are the <i>true</i> strains, not engineering strains.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> True strains add up; engineering strains do not.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-15", year: 2026, no: 15, subject: "solid", topic: "Stresses on 45° plane under equal tension and compression", keySource: K,
q: `A rectangular block is subjected to two perpendicular stresses of 10 MPa tension and 10 MPa compression. What are the stresses on planes inclined at 45° with the horizontal plane ?`,
opts: ["Normal Stress = 10 Mpa and Shear Stress = 20 Mpa", "Normal Stress = 0 Mpa and Shear Stress = – 10 Mpa (counter clockwise direction)", "Normal Stress = 5 Mpa and Shear Stress = – 20 Mpa (counter clockwise direction)", "Normal Stress = 10 Mpa and Shear Stress = 5 Mpa"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) σ<sub>n</sub> = 0, τ = 10 MPa</h3><div class="formula">σ<sub>n</sub> = (σ<sub>1</sub>+σ<sub>2</sub>)/2 + (σ<sub>1</sub>−σ<sub>2</sub>)/2 · cos 2θ ;  τ = (σ<sub>1</sub>−σ<sub>2</sub>)/2 · sin 2θ</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>σ<sub>1</sub> = +10 MPa, σ<sub>2</sub> = −10 MPa, θ = 45°</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>(σ<sub>1</sub>+σ<sub>2</sub>)/2 = 0; cos 90° = 0 → σ<sub>n</sub> = <b>0</b>.</li><li>(σ<sub>1</sub>−σ<sub>2</sub>)/2 = 10; sin 90° = 1 → |τ| = <b>10 MPa</b> (sign depends on the convention; the option calls it −10, counter-clockwise).</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Equal and opposite principal stresses are the state of <b>pure shear</b> seen on the 45° planes; Mohr's circle is centred at the origin with radius 10.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> shears exceeds the maximum possible (radius 10 MPa).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 20 MPa shears exceeds the maximum possible (radius 10 MPa).</li><li><b>(d)</b> The normal stress at 45° is zero, not 10 MPa.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> τ<sub>max</sub> = (σ<sub>1</sub> − σ<sub>2</sub>)/2, always on 45° planes.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-16", year: 2026, no: 16, subject: "solid", topic: "Normal stress on a rotated element", keySource: K,
q: `An element in plane stress is subjected to normal stresses p<sub>1</sub> = 120 N/mm<sup>2</sup> and p<sub>2</sub> = 45 N/mm<sup>2</sup> (both are tensile stresses) and shearing stress of 30 N/mm<sup>2</sup> (simple shear). What is the normal stress (p<sub>n</sub>) acting as an element rotated through an angle 45° ?`,
opts: ["P<sub>n</sub> = – 37·5 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "P<sub>n</sub> = 52·5 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "P<sub>n</sub> = – 73·5 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "P<sub>n</sub> = 112·5 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 112·5 N/mm²</h3><div class="formula">p<sub>n</sub> = (p<sub>1</sub>+p<sub>2</sub>)/2 + (p<sub>1</sub>−p<sub>2</sub>)/2 · cos 2θ + q sin 2θ</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>p<sub>1</sub> = 120, p<sub>2</sub> = 45, q = 30 N/mm², θ = 45°</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>(120 + 45)/2 = 82·5.</li><li>(120 − 45)/2 × cos 90° = 0.</li><li>30 × sin 90° = 30.</li><li>p<sub>n</sub> = 82·5 + 0 + 30 = <b>112·5 N/mm²</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> On the complementary plane (θ = 135°) the normal stress is 82·5 − 30 = 52·5 N/mm²; the two always add to p<sub>1</sub> + p<sub>2</sub> = 165.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 52·5 is the stress on the other (perpendicular) plane, or with the shear sense reversed.</li><li><b>(a)</b> −37·5 with both stresses tensile and τ = 30, no plane is compressive (σ<sub>min</sub> = 82·5 − 48 = 34·5).</li><li><b>(c)</b> −73·5 with both stresses tensile and τ = 30, no plane is compressive (σ<sub>min</sub> = 82·5 − 48 = 34·5).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Check: σ<sub>n</sub>(θ) + σ<sub>n</sub>(θ+90°) = σ<sub>x</sub> + σ<sub>y</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-17", year: 2026, no: 17, subject: "solid", topic: "Limiting shear for a given principal stress", keySource: K,
q: `At a point in an elastic material, a direct tensile stress of 70 N/mm<sup>2</sup> and a direct compressive stress of 50 N/mm<sup>2</sup> are applied on planes at right angles to each other. If the maximum principal stress in the material is limited to 75 N/mm<sup>2</sup>, what is the maximum shear stress ?`,
opts: ["65 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "55 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "75 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "25 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 65 N/mm²</h3><div class="formula">σ<sub>1</sub> = (σ<sub>x</sub>+σ<sub>y</sub>)/2 + √[((σ<sub>x</sub>−σ<sub>y</sub>)/2)² + τ²] ;  τ<sub>max</sub> = √[((σ<sub>x</sub>−σ<sub>y</sub>)/2)² + τ²]</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>σ<sub>x</sub> = +70, σ<sub>y</sub> = −50 N/mm²; τ = shear stress that can be added; σ<sub>1</sub> ≤ 75</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>Centre = (70 − 50)/2 = 10.</li><li>75 = 10 + R → Mohr radius R = 65.</li><li>τ<sub>max</sub> = R = <b>65 N/mm²</b> (the applied shear on the planes would be √(65² − 60²) = 25 N/mm²).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> 25 N/mm² is the shear stress on the given planes, not the maximum shear stress.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 55 does not equal the Mohr radius for σ<sub>1</sub> = 75.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 75 does not equal the Mohr radius for σ<sub>1</sub> = 75.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Check: σ<sub>2</sub> = 10 − 65 = −55 N/mm², and σ<sub>1</sub> − σ<sub>2</sub> = 130 = 2τ<sub>max</sub>. ✓</p><p><b>Remember:</b> τ<sub>max</sub> = σ<sub>1</sub> − centre = 75 − 10.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-18", year: 2026, no: 18, subject: "solid", topic: "Failure mode in a compression test", keySource: K,
q: `A material has strength in tension, compression and shear as 30 N/mm<sup>2</sup>, 90 N/mm<sup>2</sup> and 25 N/mm<sup>2</sup>, respectively. If a specimen of diameter 25 mm is tested in compression, then identify the failure load and failure plane.`,
opts: ["Failure load is 14726 N and failure plane is 30° to the plane of axial compression", "Failure load is 24543 N and failure plane is 45° to the plane axial compression", "Failure load is 36574 N and failure plane is 60° to the plane of axial compression", "Failure load is 18745 N and failure plane is 90° to the plane of axial compression"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 24543 N, at 45°</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Under axial compression σ, the largest shear stress is σ/2 on planes at 45°. The specimen fails by whichever limit is reached first.</p>
<ol class="work"><li>Shear limit: σ/2 = 25 → σ = 50 N/mm².</li><li>Compression limit: σ = 90 N/mm².</li><li>50 &lt; 90, so it fails in <b>shear on the 45° plane</b> at σ = 50 N/mm².</li><li>A = π/4 × 25² = 490·87 mm² → P = 50 × 490·87 = <b>24 543 N</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 14 726 N = 30 × A uses the tensile strength, which does not govern in a compression test.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 36 574 N and a 60° plane do not match either limit.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A 90° plane carries no shear; failure by crushing would need 90 × A = 44 179 N.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Cast iron in compression fails on an inclined (shear) plane for exactly this reason.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-19", year: 2026, no: 19, subject: "solid", topic: "Safe UDL on a simply supported beam", keySource: K,
q: `A simply supported beam of span 3·0 m has a cross-section 120 mm × 180 mm. If the permissible stress in the material of the beam is 10 N/mm<sup>2</sup>, what is the maximum uniformly distributed load (UDL) that it can carry ? (Ignore the moment due to self-weight)`,
opts: ["9·72 kN/m", "4·52 kN/m", "5·76 kN/m", "8·80 kN/m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 5·76 kN/m</h3><div class="formula">M = σZ ;  Z = bd²/6 ;  M<sub>max</sub> = wL²/8</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>b = 120 mm, d = 180 mm, σ = 10 N/mm², L = 3 m</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>Z = 120 × 180²/6 = 648 000 mm³.</li><li>M = 10 × 648 000 = 6·48 × 10⁶ N·mm = 6·48 kN·m.</li><li>w = 8M/L² = 8 × 6·48 / 9 = <b>5·76 kN/m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 9·72 kN/m would follow from M = wL²/12 with an extra error; not a simply supported beam.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 4·52 kN/m does not satisfy wL²/8 = σbd²/6.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8·80 kN/m does not satisfy wL²/8 = σbd²/6.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Check: at w = 5·76 kN/m, M = 5·76 × 9/8 = 6·48 kN·m and σ = 6·48 × 10⁶/648 000 = 10 N/mm². ✓</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Depth enters squared: placing the 180 mm side vertical gives 1·5 times the strength of the flat position.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-20", year: 2026, no: 20, subject: "survey", topic: "Pull correction to a steel tape", keySource: K,
q: `A surveyor's steel tape 30 m long has a cross-section of 15 mm × 0·75 mm. With this, line AB is measured as 150 m. If the force applied during measurement is 120 N more than the force applied at the time of calibration, what is the actual length of the line ? (Take the modulus of elasticity for steel as 200 kN/mm<sup>2</sup>)`,
opts: ["149·992 m", "150·008 m", "151·016 m", "148·008 m"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 150·008 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Pull correction to a steel tape.</p><div class="formula">C<sub>p</sub> = (P − P<sub>0</sub>) L / (A E)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>P − P<sub>0</sub> = 120 N; L = 150 000 mm (measured length); A = 15 × 0·75 = 11·25 mm²; E = 2 × 10⁵ N/mm²</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>C<sub>p</sub> = 120 × 150 000 / (11·25 × 2 × 10⁵) = 18 × 10⁶ / 2·25 × 10⁶ = 8 mm.</li><li>Extra pull stretches the tape, so each reading is too small: the correction is <b>additive</b>.</li><li>True length = 150 + 0·008 = <b>150·008 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 149·992 m applies the correction with the wrong sign.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 151·016 m is out by a factor of over 100 (units slip).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 148·008 m is out by a factor of over 100 (units slip).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Pull above standard → tape longer → add; sag → measured too long → subtract.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-21", year: 2026, no: 21, subject: "solid", topic: "Extension of a stepped composite bar", keySource: K,
q: `The stepped bar shown in the figure is made up of two different materials. Material 1 has Young's modulus = 2 × 10<sup>5</sup> N/mm<sup>2</sup>, while that of Material 2 is 1 × 10<sup>5</sup> N/mm<sup>2</sup>. What is the extension of the bar under a pull of 30 kN if both the portions are 20 mm in thickness ? (The dimensions shown below are in mm)<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2026-21.png" alt="Stepped bar under 30 kN pull: Material 1, width 40 mm, length 600 mm; Material 2, width 30 mm, length 800 mm">`,
opts: ["0·5125 mm", "0·4000 mm", "0·1125 mm", "0·2875 mm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·5125 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Extension of a stepped composite bar.</p><div class="formula">δ = Σ P L<sub>i</sub> / (A<sub>i</sub> E<sub>i</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>P = 30 000 N; portion 1: L = 600 mm, A = 40 × 20 = 800 mm², E = 2 × 10⁵; portion 2: L = 800 mm, A = 30 × 20 = 600 mm², E = 1 × 10⁵</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>δ<sub>1</sub> = 30 000 × 600 / (800 × 2 × 10⁵) = 0·1125 mm.</li><li>δ<sub>2</sub> = 30 000 × 800 / (600 × 1 × 10⁵) = 0·4000 mm.</li><li>δ = 0·1125 + 0·4000 = <b>0·5125 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0·4000 mm is only the extension of Material 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·1125 mm is only the extension of Material 1.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·2875 mm is their difference, not their sum.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Segments in series carry the same force; their extensions add.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-22", year: 2026, no: 22, subject: "solid", topic: "Thermal pull in a tie rod with yielding ends", keySource: K,
q: `Two parallel walls, 8 m apart, are stayed together by a steel rod of 20 mm diameter passing through metal plates and nuts at each end. The nuts are screwed up to the plates while the bar is at a temperature of 400 K. What is the pull exerted by the bar after it has cooled to 300 K, if the total yielding at the two ends is 5 mm ? (Take coefficient of thermal expansion for steel as 12 × 10<sup>–6</sup> per K and Young's modulus of steel as 2 × 10<sup>5</sup> N/mm<sup>2</sup>)`,
opts: ["75·398 kN", "240 kN", "115 kN", "36·128 kN"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 36·128 kN</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Thermal pull in a tie rod with yielding ends.</p><div class="formula">σ = E (αΔT L − δ<sub>yield</sub>) / L ;  P = σA</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>α = 12 × 10⁻⁶ /K; ΔT = 100 K; L = 8000 mm; δ<sub>yield</sub> = 5 mm; E = 2 × 10⁵ N/mm²; d = 20 mm</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>Free contraction = 12 × 10⁻⁶ × 100 × 8000 = 9·6 mm.</li><li>Prevented contraction = 9·6 − 5 = 4·6 mm.</li><li>σ = 2 × 10⁵ × 4·6 / 8000 = 115 N/mm².</li><li>A = π/4 × 20² = 314·16 mm² → P = 115 × 314·16 = <b>36 128 N = 36·128 kN</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 75·398 kN = 240 × 314·16 ignores the 5 mm yield.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 240 kN confuses the full stress (240 N/mm²) with a force.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 115 kN is the stress value 115 N/mm² read as a force.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Any end slip simply subtracts from the free thermal movement.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-23", year: 2026, no: 23, subject: "solid", topic: "Principal planes and planes of maximum shear", keySource: K,
q: `Which one of the following statements is <b><i>not</i></b> correct regarding principal stresses and planes ?`,
opts: ["The planes on which shearing stresses are zero are called principal planes and the stresses normal to principal planes are known as principal stresses.", "The principal planes are the planes of maximum or minimum normal stresses.", "The planes of extreme shearing stresses are at 90° to the principal planes.", "The sum of normal stresses in any two mutually perpendicular directions is constant in case of a general two-dimensional stress."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The planes of maximum (extreme) shear stress lie at <b>45°</b> to the principal planes, not 90°. On Mohr's circle the top and bottom points (τ<sub>max</sub>) are 90° of circle angle from the principal points, which is 45° in the body. A plane at 90° to a principal plane is simply the other principal plane.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct: definition of principal planes and principal stresses.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Correct: σ<sub>1</sub> and σ<sub>2</sub> are the maximum and minimum normal stresses.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Correct: σ<sub>x</sub> + σ<sub>y</sub> = σ<sub>x'</sub> + σ<sub>y'</sub> is an invariant.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Body angle = half of Mohr-circle angle.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-24", year: 2026, no: 24, subject: "solid", topic: "Elastic torque capacity of a solid shaft", keySource: K,
q: `What is the maximum torque T<sub>E</sub> that can be applied to a solid steel cylindrical shaft 8 cm in diameter, if the shaft is to remain elastic ? (Take the elastic limit in shear and the shear modulus as τ<sub>0</sub> = 145 MPa and G = 76 GPa, respectively)`,
opts: ["14,580 N-m", "7,290 N-m", "3,645 N-m", "29,160 N-m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 14 580 N·m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Elastic torque capacity of a solid shaft.</p><div class="formula">T<sub>E</sub> = τ<sub>0</sub> · π d³ / 16</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>τ<sub>0</sub> = 145 N/mm²; d = 80 mm (G is not needed for the torque)</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>π d³/16 = π × 512 000 / 16 = 100 531 mm³.</li><li>T<sub>E</sub> = 145 × 100 531 = 14·58 × 10⁶ N·mm = <b>14 580 N·m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 7290 N·m is half the value (π d³/32 used as the section modulus).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3645 N·m is a quarter (radius used in place of diameter wrongly).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 29 160 N·m is double; it exceeds even the fully plastic torque (4/3 × 14 580 = 19 440 N·m).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Polar section modulus Z<sub>p</sub> = πd³/16; the plastic torque is 4/3 of the elastic limit torque.</p>` },

{ id: "2026-25", year: 2026, no: 25, subject: "solid", topic: "Mohr's circle for equal biaxial compression", keySource: K,
q: `Given the state of stress σ<sub>x</sub> = σ<sub>y</sub> = – σ<sub>0</sub> (where σ<sub>0</sub> &gt; 0) and τ<sub>xy</sub> = 0 as shown in the figure, which one of the following statements is <b><i>not</i></b> correct ?<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2026-25.png" alt="Mohr's circle axes σn and τnt; the circle shrinks to the point (−σ0, 0) with R = 0">`,
opts: ["The radius of the Mohr circle R = 0.", "No shear stress exists on any plane passing through this point.", "The state of stress shown here is called a state of 'Pure Shear'.", "The normal stress is same for all planes passing through this point."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Equal normal stresses with no shear is a <b>hydrostatic</b> (isotropic) state of stress. Mohr's circle collapses to the single point (−σ<sub>0</sub>, 0). "Pure shear" is the opposite case: σ<sub>1</sub> = −σ<sub>2</sub>, a circle centred at the origin. So (c) is the wrong statement.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct: R = √[((σ<sub>x</sub> − σ<sub>y</sub>)/2)² + τ²] = 0.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Correct: with R = 0, τ = 0 on every plane.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Correct: every plane carries σ<sub>n</sub> = −σ<sub>0</sub>; every plane is a principal plane.</li></ul>
<p>In 3-D the same state (σ<sub>x</sub> = σ<sub>y</sub> = σ<sub>z</sub>) is pressure, as in a body deep under water.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Hydrostatic stress changes volume only; pure shear changes shape only.</p>` }
);
})();

