// ESE 2024 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (PNGE-D-CVL), Q26–50
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; Q50 dropped).
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2024-26", year: 2024, no: 26, subject: "solid", topic: "True strain versus engineering strain",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the difference between true strains and engineering strains :<ol><li>True strains for equivalent amounts of deformation in tension and compression are equal including for sign.</li><li>True strains are additive. For a deformation consisting of several steps the overall strain is the sum of the strains in each step.</li><li>The volume change is related to the sum of the three normal strains. For constant volume, ε<sub>x</sub> + ε<sub>y</sub> + ε<sub>z</sub> = 0.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3><div class="formula">ε<sub>true</sub> = ln(L/L<sub>0</sub>)</div>
<ul><li><b>1</b> False: true strains for equivalent deformations in tension and compression are equal in <b>magnitude but opposite in sign</b> (doubling: ln 2 = +0·693; halving: ln ½ = −0·693). "Including for sign" is wrong.</li><li><b>2</b> True: ln(L<sub>1</sub>/L<sub>0</sub>) + ln(L<sub>2</sub>/L<sub>1</sub>) = ln(L<sub>2</sub>/L<sub>0</sub>), so true strains add.</li><li><b>3</b> True: ln(V/V<sub>0</sub>) = ε<sub>x</sub> + ε<sub>y</sub> + ε<sub>z</sub>, so for plastic (constant-volume) flow the sum is zero.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Engineering strains lack these properties: 1 → 2 → 1 gives +1 and −0·5, which neither cancel nor add.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> True strain: additive, symmetric, volume-consistent.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-27", year: 2024, no: 27, subject: "solid", topic: "Plasticity, ductility, malleability",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the properties of materials :<ol><li>A material in plastic state is temporarily deformed by the application of load and it has tendency to recover.</li><li>Ductility is the characteristic of a material to be drawn out longitudinally to a reduced section under the action of tensile force.</li><li>Malleability is a property of a material which permits the materials to be partially extended in all directions without rupture.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2", "2 only", "3 only", "1 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> False: in the plastic state a material deforms <b>permanently</b> and does not recover on unloading; temporary, recoverable deformation is elasticity.</li><li><b>2</b> True: ductility is the ability to be drawn into wires (reduced section) under tension, measured by percentage elongation and reduction of area.</li><li><b>3</b> False (as worded): malleability is the ability to be <b>permanently</b> extended in all directions (hammered or rolled into sheets) under compression without rupture; "partially extended" misstates it.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Examples: gold is both very ductile and very malleable; lead is malleable but not very ductile; mild steel is ductile.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ductile → wires (tension); malleable → sheets (compression).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-28", year: 2024, no: 28, subject: "solid", topic: "Conventions of Mohr's circle",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the Mohr's circle of a plane Tensor?`,
opts: ["σ is positive in tension and is plotted to the right of the origin. Compression is negative to the left", "τ is plotted negatively if it rotates the stress block clockwise", "Angles θ from one axis to another around the origin are in different direction", "Any orthogonal set of axes are 90° to one another on Mohr's circle"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In Mohr's circle, normal stress σ is plotted on the horizontal axis with <b>tension positive to the right</b> and compression to the left.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Wrong: in the usual (engineering) convention a shear that tends to rotate the element <b>clockwise</b> is plotted <b>positive</b> (above the axis), so that rotations on the circle and in the body are in the same sense.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Wrong: with that convention, the angle 2θ on the circle is measured in the <b>same</b> direction as θ in the element.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Wrong: perpendicular planes in the element are <b>180°</b> apart on Mohr's circle (diametrically opposite), since circle angles are double the physical angles.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Physical 90° = 180° on Mohr's circle.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-29", year: 2024, no: 29, subject: "solid", topic: "Linear elasticity: dependence of stress field", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following regarding the general situations in linear elasticity :<ol><li>The stress field is a function of only the boundary conditions, geometric shape and loading which are constant.</li><li>The stress field is a function of material properties like volume and deviatoric stiffness as well.</li><li>The loads are time independent.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2 only (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a general linear elastic problem the stress field depends on the boundary conditions, geometry and loads (statement <b>1</b>) and, in general, also on the elastic constants — the volumetric (bulk) and deviatoric (shear) stiffnesses (statement <b>2</b>) — because compatibility couples the stresses through E and ν. Only in special cases (e.g. statically determinate or 2-D simply connected bodies with traction boundary conditions) is the stress field independent of the material.</p>
<ul><li><b>3</b> Not part of the key's answer: linear elasticity can also be applied to time-varying (dynamic) loads; time independence of the load is not a defining feature of the general situation.</li></ul>
<p>Our first pick was (b), treating the stress field as independent of material; on review the general case — where it does depend on K and G — is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Determinate or 2-D traction problems: stresses independent of E; general 3-D: not.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-30", year: 2024, no: 30, subject: "solid", topic: "Plane stress versus plane strain",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding two dimensional stress formulations :<ol><li>In a plane stresses are identical for any given geometry, loading.</li><li>The in-plane stresses are different for plane strain or plane stress.</li><li>Linear viscoelastic stress fields are different for constant load and change with time.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "1 and 2", "3 only", "2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Plane stress versus plane strain.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: for a given 2-D geometry and in-plane traction loading, the in-plane stresses σ<sub>x</sub>, σ<sub>y</sub>, τ<sub>xy</sub> are the <b>same</b> whether the body is in plane stress or plane strain (the governing equations do not involve the elastic constants for simply connected bodies — the Michell–Lévy result).</li><li><b>2</b> False: hence the in-plane stresses are <b>not</b> different; only the out-of-plane stress σ<sub>z</sub> (zero vs ν(σ<sub>x</sub> + σ<sub>y</sub>)) and the strains differ.</li><li><b>3</b> False: by the correspondence principle, under constant loads the stress field of a linear viscoelastic body equals the elastic one and does <b>not change with time</b> (only strains creep).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Plane stress vs strain: same in-plane stresses, different strains and σ<sub>z</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-31", year: 2024, no: 31, subject: "solid", topic: "Extension of a tapered flat bar",
q: `Which one of the following is the equation for extension of whole length of a uniformly tapering rectangular bars where b<sub>1</sub> and b<sub>2</sub> are the limits of widths (b<sub>2</sub> &gt; b<sub>1</sub>), length L, thickness t and the bar is subjected to an axial force P and elastic module E?`,
opts: ["Δ = PL log<sub>e</sub>(b<sub>2</sub>/b<sub>1</sub>) / ((b<sub>2</sub> − b<sub>1</sub>)tE)", "Δ = PL log<sub>10</sub>(b<sub>1</sub>/b<sub>2</sub>) / ((b<sub>2</sub> − b<sub>1</sub>)tE)", "Δ = PL log<sub>10</sub>(b<sub>2</sub>/b<sub>1</sub>) / ((b<sub>1</sub> − b<sub>2</sub>)tE)", "Δ = PLt log<sub>e</sub>(b<sub>2</sub>/b<sub>1</sub>) / ((b<sub>2</sub> − b<sub>1</sub>)E)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Δ = PL ln(b<sub>2</sub>/b<sub>1</sub>) / [(b<sub>2</sub> − b<sub>1</sub>) t E]</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Width varies linearly: b(x) = b<sub>1</sub> + kx, k = (b<sub>2</sub> − b<sub>1</sub>)/L; area A(x) = t b(x).</p>
<div class="formula">Δ = ∫₀ᴸ P dx / (E t (b<sub>1</sub> + kx)) = (P/(Etk)) ln(b<sub>2</sub>/b<sub>1</sub>) = PL ln(b<sub>2</sub>/b<sub>1</sub>) / (Et(b<sub>2</sub> − b<sub>1</sub>))</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> ln(b<sub>1</sub>/b<sub>2</sub>) is negative and the base-10 log needs a factor 2·303.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Uses log<sub>10</sub> and (b<sub>1</sub> − b<sub>2</sub>) &lt; 0.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Puts t in the numerator; extension must decrease with thickness.</li></ul>
<p>Check: as b<sub>2</sub> → b<sub>1</sub>, ln(b<sub>2</sub>/b<sub>1</sub>)/(b<sub>2</sub> − b<sub>1</sub>) → 1/b, giving PL/(btE). ✓</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Flat taper: logarithm; conical taper: 4PL/(πEd<sub>1</sub>d<sub>2</sub>).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-32", year: 2024, no: 32, subject: "solid", topic: "Elongation of a conical bar",
q: `A conical bar tapers uniformly from a diameter of 15 mm to a diameter of 40 mm in a length of 400 mm. What is the elongation of the bar under an axial tensile force of 100 kN? (Take E = 2×10<sup>5</sup> N/mm<sup>2</sup>)`,
opts: ["0·242 mm", "0·121 mm", "0·424 mm", "0·212 mm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·424 mm</h3><div class="formula">δ = 4PL / (π E d<sub>1</sub> d<sub>2</sub>)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>P = 100 000 N; L = 400 mm; E = 2 × 10⁵ N/mm²; d<sub>1</sub> = 15 mm; d<sub>2</sub> = 40 mm</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>4PL = 1·6 × 10⁸.</li><li>πEd<sub>1</sub>d<sub>2</sub> = π × 2 × 10⁵ × 600 = 3·77 × 10⁸.</li><li>δ = 1·6/3·77 = <b>0·424 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·242 mm halves or rearrange the result.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·212 mm halves or rearrange the result.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·121 mm is a quarter.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Stress at the small end = 100 000/(π × 15²/4) = 566 N/mm², which would actually exceed the yield of mild steel; the question is a pure elastic exercise.</p>
<p>Derivation: with d(x) varying linearly, ∫dx/d(x)² from d<sub>1</sub> to d<sub>2</sub> gives L/(d<sub>1</sub>d<sub>2</sub>), hence the product of the end diameters in the formula.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Equivalent uniform diameter = √(d<sub>1</sub>d<sub>2</sub>) = 24·5 mm.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-33", year: 2024, no: 33, subject: "solid", topic: "Thermal stress in a tapered bar fixed at both ends",
q: `A circular section tapering bar is rigidly fixed at both the ends. The diameter changes from 75 mm at one end to 150 mm at the other end, in a length of 1·2 m. What is the maximum stress in the bar if the temperature is raised by 32°C? (Take E = 2×10<sup>5</sup> N/mm<sup>2</sup> and α = 12×10<sup>−6</sup> per 1°C)`,
opts: ["123·6 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "143·6 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "163·6 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "153·6 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 153·6 N/mm²</h3><ol class="work"><li>Free expansion = αTL; it is fully prevented, so the force P compresses the bar by the same amount: 4PL/(πEd<sub>1</sub>d<sub>2</sub>) = αTL.</li><li>P = αTE π d<sub>1</sub>d<sub>2</sub>/4.</li><li>Maximum stress at the small end: σ = P/(πd<sub>1</sub>²/4) = αTE (d<sub>2</sub>/d<sub>1</sub>).</li><li>σ = 12 × 10⁻⁶ × 32 × 2 × 10⁵ × (150/75) = 76·8 × 2 = <b>153·6 N/mm²</b> (compressive).</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The stress at the large end is αTE(d<sub>1</sub>/d<sub>2</sub>) = 38·4 N/mm². For a uniform bar the stress would be αTE = 76·8 N/mm² everywhere.</p><div class="formula">4PL/(πEd₁d₂) = αTL</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 123·6 does not equal αTE·d<sub>2</sub>/d<sub>1</sub>.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 143·6 does not equal αTE·d<sub>2</sub>/d<sub>1</sub>.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 163·6 N/mm² does not equal αTE·d<sub>2</sub>/d<sub>1</sub>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Tapered bar, fixed ends: σ<sub>max</sub> = αTE × (d<sub>large</sub>/d<sub>small</sub>).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-34", year: 2024, no: 34, subject: "solid", topic: "Shrink-fitting a steel tyre",
q: `A thin type of steel is to be shrunk on to a rigid wheel of 900 mm diameter. What is the internal diameter of the tyre if the hoop stress is limited to 120 N/mm<sup>2</sup>? (For the tyre take α = 12×10<sup>−6</sup> per °C and E = 2×10<sup>5</sup> N/mm<sup>2</sup>)`,
opts: ["899·46 mm", "819·46 mm", "900·54 mm", "800·54 mm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 899·46 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Shrink-fitting a steel tyre.</p><div class="formula">σ = E (D − d)/d</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>D = 900 mm (wheel, rigid); d = internal diameter of the tyre before fitting; σ = 120 N/mm²</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>Hoop strain = σ/E = 120/2 × 10⁵ = 6 × 10⁻⁴.</li><li>d = D/(1 + 6 × 10⁻⁴) = 900/1·0006 = <b>899·46 mm</b>.</li><li>Temperature rise needed to slip it on: ΔT = strain/α = 6 × 10⁻⁴/12 × 10⁻⁶ = 50 °C.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(c)</b> 900·54 mm is larger than the wheel; it could not grip.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 819·46 imply strains of about 10 %, far beyond elastic limits.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 800·54 mm imply strains of about 10 %, far beyond elastic limits.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Shrink fit: tyre bore smaller by D·σ/E; heat it by σ/(Eα) to fit.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-35", year: 2024, no: 35, subject: "solid", topic: "Effective stress and effective strain (plasticity)",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the effective stress and effective strain :<ol><li>σ̄ and ε̄ will reduce to σ<sub>x</sub> and ε<sub>x</sub> in an x-direction tension test.</li><li>The incremental work per volume done in deforming a material plastically is dω = ε̄ dσ̄.</li><li>It is usually assumed that the σ̄ vs ε̄ curve describes the strain hardening for loading under a constant stress ratio α, regardless of α*.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 3", "3 only", "1 and 3", "2 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Effective stress and effective strain (plasticity).</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: effective (von Mises) stress and strain are defined so that in a uniaxial tension test they equal σ<sub>x</sub> and ε<sub>x</sub>.</li><li><b>2</b> False: the plastic work increment is dw = σ̄ dε̄ (stress times strain increment), not ε̄ dσ̄.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the usual assumption (isotropic hardening) is that a single σ̄–ε̄ curve describes hardening for any proportional loading path (any constant stress ratio α).</li></ul>
<div class="formula">σ̄ = √(½[(σ₁−σ₂)² + (σ₂−σ₃)² + (σ₃−σ₁)²]) ;  dw = σ̄ dε̄</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Effective quantities collapse multiaxial plasticity onto the tension test curve.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-36", year: 2024, no: 36, subject: "solid", topic: "Temperature rise to transfer load to copper tube",
q: `A solid cylinder of steel is placed inside a copper tube. The assembly is compressed between rigid plates by forces P. What is the value of increase in temperature so that all the load is carried by the copper tube? (Let the parameters with suffix c represent copper and suffix s represent steel)`,
opts: ["t = 4P / (A<sub>s</sub>E<sub>s</sub>(α<sub>c</sub> − α<sub>s</sub>))", "t = 2P / (A<sub>c</sub>E<sub>c</sub>(α<sub>s</sub> − α<sub>c</sub>))", "t = P(α<sub>c</sub> − α<sub>s</sub>) / (A<sub>c</sub>E<sub>c</sub>)", "t = P / (A<sub>c</sub>E<sub>c</sub>(α<sub>c</sub> − α<sub>s</sub>))"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) t = P / [A<sub>c</sub>E<sub>c</sub>(α<sub>c</sub> − α<sub>s</sub>)]</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Initially both parts are compressed by P (sharing it). Heating makes copper (α<sub>c</sub> &gt; α<sub>s</sub>) expand more, so it pushes the plates apart and takes over the load. Steel carries nothing when its free thermal expansion just equals the copper's net length change:</p>
<div class="formula">α<sub>s</sub> t L = α<sub>c</sub> t L − P L/(A<sub>c</sub>E<sub>c</sub>) ⇒ t = P/[A<sub>c</sub>E<sub>c</sub>(α<sub>c</sub> − α<sub>s</sub>)]</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Uses steel properties and a factor 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Has a factor 2 and the wrong sign of (α<sub>s</sub> − α<sub>c</sub>).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Puts (α<sub>c</sub> − α<sub>s</sub>) in the numerator, dimensionally wrong for a temperature.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Load transfers to the member that expands more.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-37", year: 2024, no: 37, subject: "solid", topic: "Moduli of elasticity of metals",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I (Materials)</th><th>List II (Modulus of elasticity)</th></tr><tr><td>P. Aluminium alloys</td><td>1. 190</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Cast iron</td><td>2. 190 – 210</td></tr><tr><td>R. Copper</td><td>3. 70 – 79</td></tr><tr><td>S. Steel</td><td>4. 110 – 120</td></tr><tr><td>T. Wrought iron</td><td>5. 83 – 170</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-3, R-4, S-5, T-1", "P-3, Q-4, R-5, S-1, T-2", "P-4, Q-5, R-1, S-2, T-3", "P-3, Q-5, R-4, S-2, T-1"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) P-3, Q-5, R-4, S-2, T-1 (GPa)</h3><ul><li><b>Aluminium alloys (P-3)</b>: 70–79 GPa.</li><li><b>Cast iron (Q-5)</b>: 83–170 GPa — wide range depending on graphite form (grey to ductile).</li><li><b>Copper (R-4)</b>: 110–120 GPa.</li><li><b>Steel (S-2)</b>: 190–210 GPa.</li><li><b>Wrought iron (T-1)</b>: about 190 GPa.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Gives aluminium the steel range.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Gives cast iron copper's value and steel 190 only.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Gives aluminium copper's value and copper 190.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The modulus governs stiffness (deflection), not strength: an aluminium member deflects about three times as much as an identical steel one under the same load.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> E ratios: steel ≈ 3 × aluminium ≈ 1·8 × copper.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-38", year: 2024, no: 38, subject: "hydro", topic: "Orographic precipitation",
q: `Which one of the following precipitations results from ocean air streams passing over land and being deflected upward by coastal mountains, thus cooling below saturation temperature and spilling moisture?`,
opts: ["Convective precipitation", "Frontal precipitation", "Orographic precipitation", "Cyclonic precipitation"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) orographic precipitation</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Moist air forced up a mountain barrier cools adiabatically; when it falls below its dew point, clouds form and rain falls on the <b>windward</b> slopes. The leeward side lies in a rain shadow. The Western Ghats (Mumbai, Mahabaleshwar) and Cherrapunji receive heavy orographic monsoon rain.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Convective precipitation comes from surface heating causing air to rise (afternoon thunderstorms in the tropics).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Frontal precipitation occurs where warm air rises over a cold air mass along a front.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Cyclonic precipitation comes from converging, rising air in low-pressure systems (frontal or non-frontal).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Lifting mechanism names the type: heat (convective), mountains (orographic), fronts/cyclones.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-39", year: 2024, no: 39, subject: "hydro", topic: "Time of concentration", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following terms refers to the time between the end of the net rainfall and the end of the direct runoff hydrograph?`,
opts: ["Recession time", "Time-to-peak", "Lag time", "Concentration time"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) concentration time (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The <b>time of concentration</b> is the time for water from the hydraulically most remote point to reach the outlet. Once excess rainfall stops, runoff continues until the last of this water arrives, so the time from the end of net rainfall to the end of direct runoff (in the time–area concept, or to the inflection point of the recession) equals t<sub>c</sub>.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Recession time is the duration of the falling limb from the peak to the end of direct runoff — measured from the peak, not from the end of rain. Our first pick was (a); on review the key's definition is accepted.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Time-to-peak runs from the start of runoff (or rain) to the peak.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Lag time is from the centroid of excess rainfall to the peak (or centroid) of runoff.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> t<sub>c</sub>: farthest drop's travel time; rational method uses rain of duration t<sub>c</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-40", year: 2024, no: 40, subject: "hydro", topic: "Infiltration rates by soil and cover",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I (Soil and vegetation)</th><th>List II (Infiltration rate (mm/hr))</th></tr><tr><td>P. Forested loam</td><td>1. 10 – 70</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Loam pasture</td><td>2. 0 – 4</td></tr><tr><td>R. Sand</td><td>3. 3 – 15</td></tr><tr><td>S. Bare clay</td><td>4. 100 – 200</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-3, R-1, S-4", "P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4", "P-4, Q-1, R-3, S-2", "P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) P-4, Q-1, R-3, S-2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Infiltration rates by soil and cover.</p><ul><li><b>Forested loam (P-4)</b>: 100–200 mm/h — leaf litter, roots and burrows keep the surface open.</li><li><b>Loam pasture (Q-1)</b>: 10–70 mm/h.</li><li><b>Sand (R-3)</b>: 3–15 mm/h in this table (fine/compacted sandy surfaces); clean coarse sand can be higher.</li><li><b>Bare clay (S-2)</b>: 0–4 mm/h — fine pores, and the surface seals under raindrop impact.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Gives forest the lowest rate and bare clay the highest.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Gives bare clay 100–200 mm/h.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Swaps sand and bare clay.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Vegetation and organic matter raise infiltration; bare fine soils seal.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-41", year: 2024, no: 41, subject: "hydro", topic: "Runoff coefficients",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I (Type of surface)</th><th>List II (Value of coefficient of runoff)</th></tr><tr><td>P. Wooded areas</td><td>1. 0·70 – 0·95</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Gravel roads and walks</td><td>2. 0·15 – 0·30</td></tr><tr><td>R. Macadamized Roads</td><td>3. 0·01 – 0·20</td></tr><tr><td>S. Watertight roof surfaces</td><td>4. 0·25 – 0·60</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-3, R-1, S-4", "P-3, Q-2, R-4, S-1", "P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4", "P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) P-3, Q-2, R-4, S-1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Runoff coefficients.</p><ul><li><b>Wooded areas (P-3)</b>: 0·01–0·20 — most rain infiltrates or is intercepted.</li><li><b>Gravel roads and walks (Q-2)</b>: 0·15–0·30.</li><li><b>Macadamised roads (R-4)</b>: 0·25–0·60.</li><li><b>Watertight roofs (S-1)</b>: 0·70–0·95 — almost all rain runs off.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Rational formula: Q = C i A / 360  (Q m³/s, i mm/h, A ha)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Gives macadam 0·70–0·95 and roofs 0·25–0·60.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Gives gravel roads the roof range.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Gives woods 0·25–0·60 and roofs 0·01–0·20.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> C rises with imperviousness: forest &lt; gravel &lt; macadam &lt; roofs.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-42", year: 2024, no: 42, subject: "hydro", topic: "Characteristics of groundwater",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding ground water :<ol><li>Ground water is exhaustible and is evenly available.</li><li>Natural replenishment of the ground water resource is a very fast process.</li><li>Ground water is generally better than surface water in respect of biological characteristics.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Characteristics of groundwater.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: groundwater is exhaustible, but it is <b>unevenly</b> distributed (depending on geology and recharge), not evenly available.</li><li><b>2</b> False: natural recharge is generally <b>slow</b> (years to centuries for deep aquifers); overdraft lowers water tables, as seen in Punjab and Haryana.</li><li><b>3</b> True: filtration through soil removes most bacteria, so groundwater is usually much better bacteriologically than surface water (though it has higher dissolved solids and hardness).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Groundwater: clean biologically, hard chemically, slow to recharge.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-43", year: 2024, no: 43, subject: "hydro", topic: "Specific yield of materials",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I (Material)</th><th>List II (Specific yield %)</th></tr><tr><td>P. Clay</td><td>1. 5 – 15</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Sand and gravel</td><td>2. 15 – 25</td></tr><tr><td>R. Sandstone</td><td>3. 0·5 – 5</td></tr><tr><td>S. Limestone</td><td>4. 1 – 10</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-3, R-1, S-4", "P-1, Q-2, R-4, S-3", "P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4", "P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3</h3><ul><li><b>Clay (P-4)</b>: 1–10 % — high porosity but most water is retained by fine pores.</li><li><b>Sand and gravel (Q-2)</b>: 15–25 % — the best aquifers.</li><li><b>Sandstone (R-1)</b>: 5–15 %.</li><li><b>Limestone (S-3)</b>: 0·5–5 % (dense limestone; karstic limestone can yield much more).</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Porosity n = specific yield S<sub>y</sub> + specific retention S<sub>r</sub></div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Gives clay 15–25 %.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Gives clay 5–15 % and sandstone 1–10 %.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Gives sand and gravel only 5–15 %.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Example: clay may have 45 % porosity but only about 3 % specific yield, while gravel with 25 % porosity yields about 22 %.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Clay: high porosity, low yield.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-44", year: 2024, no: 44, subject: "hydro", topic: "Theis drawdown",
q: `A fully penetrating artesian well is pumped at a rate Q = 1500 m<sup>3</sup>/day from an aquifer whose storage coefficient and transmissivity are 4×10<sup>−4</sup> and 0·145 m<sup>2</sup>/min, respectively. Considering ω(u) = 8·62, what is the drawdowns at a distance 3 m from the production well after one hour of pumping?`,
opts: ["8·62 m", "14·53 m", "4·93 m", "2·38 m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 4·93 m</h3><div class="formula">s = Q W(u) / (4π T) ,  u = r² S / (4 T t)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>Q = 1500 m³/day = 1·0417 m³/min; T = 0·145 m²/min; W(u) = 8·62 (given); r = 3 m; t = 60 min; S = 4 × 10⁻⁴</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>Check u = 9 × 4 × 10⁻⁴/(4 × 0·145 × 60) = 1·03 × 10⁻⁴ → W(u) ≈ −0·5772 − ln u = 8·6. ✓</li><li>s = 1·0417 × 8·62/(4π × 0·145) = 8·979/1·822 = <b>4·93 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 8·62 m is just W(u).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 14·53 m uses wrong unit conversions of Q or T.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2·38 m uses wrong unit conversions of Q or T.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Cooper–Jacob check: for u &lt; 0·01, s = (2·303Q/4πT) log(2·25Tt/r²S) gives the same 4·93 m here.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Keep Q and T in the same time unit (here per minute).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-45", year: 2024, no: 45, subject: "hydro", topic: "Hygroscopic water",
q: `Water attached to soil particles through loose chemical bonds is termed as`,
opts: ["Capillary water", "Gravity water", "Hygroscopic water", "Field capacity"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) hygroscopic water</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Hygroscopic water</b> is the thin film adsorbed on soil particles from the atmosphere, held by strong molecular (loose chemical) forces at tensions above about 31 bar. It cannot be used by plants and is removed only by oven drying at 105 °C.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Capillary water is held by surface tension in small pores; it is the water available to plants.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Gravity water drains freely through large pores after rain or irrigation.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Field capacity is a moisture <i>level</i> (after gravity drainage), not a type of bonded water.</li></ul>
<p>Hygroscopic coefficient: moisture content of air-dry soil in equilibrium with saturated air — higher for clays.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Hygroscopic (unavailable) &lt; capillary (available) &lt; gravitational (drains).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-46", year: 2024, no: 46, subject: "hydro", topic: "Field capacity",
q: `The water remaining in the soil after the removal of gravitational water is called`,
opts: ["Capillary water", "Gravity water", "Hygroscopic water", "Field capacity"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) field capacity</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> After a soil is saturated and allowed to drain freely for 1–3 days, the moisture held against gravity (capillary + hygroscopic water) is its <b>field capacity</b>. Plants can extract water down to the <b>permanent wilting point</b>; the difference is the available water.</p>
<div class="formula">Available moisture depth = (FC − PWP) × γ<sub>d</sub>/γ<sub>w</sub> × root depth</div>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Capillary water is part of what remains, but the question asks for the total retained after drainage, which is field capacity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Gravity water is what has been removed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Hygroscopic water is only the tightly held film.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> FC − PWP = available water.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-47", year: 2024, no: 47, subject: "hydro", topic: "Water wasted in irrigation",
q: `The field capacity and permanent wilting point for a given 0·8 m root-zone soil are 35 and 10 per cent, respectively. At a given time, the soil moisture in the given soil is 20 per cent when a farmer irrigates the soil with 250 mm depth of water. Assuming bulk specific gravity of the soil as 1·6, what is the amount of water wasted from the consideration of irrigation?`,
opts: ["23·2%", "58%", "5·8%", "19·2%"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 23·2 %</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Water wasted in irrigation.</p><div class="formula">Depth needed to reach FC = (FC − m) × G<sub>b</sub> × d</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>FC = 0·35; present moisture m = 0·20; G<sub>b</sub> = 1·6; root depth d = 800 mm; applied 250 mm</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>Water that can be stored = 0·15 × 1·6 × 800 = 192 mm.</li><li>Water wasted = 250 − 192 = 58 mm.</li><li>As a percentage of the water applied = 58/250 = <b>23·2 %</b> (application efficiency 76·8 %).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 58 % confuses the 58 mm with a percentage.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 5·8 % is out by ten.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 19·2 % is the stored depth expressed wrongly.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Any water above field capacity drains below the root zone — wasted.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-48", year: 2024, no: 48, subject: "hydro", topic: "Field capacity by soil texture",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I (Soil texture)</th><th>List II (Field capacity (%))</th></tr><tr><td>P. Sand</td><td>1. 5 – 10</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Loam</td><td>2. 27 – 35</td></tr><tr><td>R. Silty clay</td><td>3. 18 – 25</td></tr><tr><td>S. Clay</td><td>4. 32 – 40</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1", "P-1, Q-3, R-2, S-4", "P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4", "P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) P-1, Q-3, R-2, S-4</h3><ul><li><b>Sand (P-1)</b>: 5–10 % — large pores drain quickly.</li><li><b>Loam (Q-3)</b>: 18–25 %.</li><li><b>Silty clay (R-2)</b>: 27–35 %.</li><li><b>Clay (S-4)</b>: 32–40 % — fine pores hold the most water.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Field capacity increases with fineness of texture and organic content. Available water is highest in loams and silt loams (wide FC − PWP), because clays also have a high wilting point.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> gives sand the clay range.</li><li><b>(d)</b> gives sand the clay range.</li><li><b>(c)</b> gives loam 5–10 %.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p>Permanent wilting points rise in the same order: about 2–4 % for sand to 20–25 % for clay.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Finer texture → higher field capacity.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-49", year: 2024, no: 49, subject: "hydro", topic: "Wave height in reservoirs (Molitor)",
q: `If V is the wind velocity and F is the fetch, for F &lt; 32 Km, what is the formula for wave height (h<sub>w</sub>) in gravity dams?`,
opts: ["h<sub>w</sub> = 1·032√(VF) + 0·76 + 0·27F<sup>1/4</sup>", "h<sub>w</sub> = 0·032√(VF) − 0·76 − 0·27F<sup>1/4</sup>", "h<sub>w</sub> = 1·032√(VF) − 0·76 + 0·27F<sup>1/4</sup>", "h<sub>w</sub> = 0·032√(VF) + 0·76 − 0·27F<sup>1/4</sup>"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) h<sub>w</sub> = 0·032√(VF) + 0·763 − 0·271F<sup>1/4</sup></h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Molitor's (Stevenson's) empirical formulae, V in km/h, F in km, h<sub>w</sub> in m:</p>
<div class="formula">F &lt; 32 km: h<sub>w</sub> = 0·032√(VF) + 0·763 − 0·271 F<sup>1/4</sup><br>F &gt; 32 km: h<sub>w</sub> = 0·032√(VF)</div>
<p>Wave pressure on the dam: p<sub>w</sub> = 2·0 γ<sub>w</sub> h<sub>w</sub> (kN/m²), acting at 3h<sub>w</sub>/8 above still water level; total force ≈ 20 h<sub>w</sub>² kN/m. Freeboard is usually taken as 1·5 h<sub>w</sub>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> uses 1·032 instead of 0·032, giving waves many metres high.</li><li><b>(c)</b> uses 1·032 instead of 0·032, giving waves many metres high.</li><li><b>(b)</b> subtracts 0·763, giving negative heights for small fetches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Example: V = 120 km/h, F = 10 km → h<sub>w</sub> ≈ 1·11 + 0·76 − 0·48 = 1·39 m.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-50", year: 2024, no: 50, subject: "hydro", topic: "Hydrodynamic earthquake pressure on dams", dropped: true,
q: `If c<sub>1</sub> = a dimensionless pressure coefficient, α<sub>h</sub> = horizontal acceleration factor, ρ = mass density of water, g = acceleration due to gravity, h = depth of the reservoir; what is the formula for the variation of horizontal hydrodynamic earthquake pressure with depth (P<sub>e</sub>) in gravity dams?`,
opts: ["P<sub>e</sub> = C<sub>1</sub>α<sub>h</sub> g/h", "P<sub>e</sub> = C<sub>1</sub>α<sub>h</sub>/(gh)", "P<sub>e</sub> = C<sub>1</sub>α<sub>h</sub>gh", "P<sub>e</sub> = C<sub>1</sub>g(h + α<sub>h</sub>)"], ans: null,
analysis: `<h3>Dropped by UPSC (not scored)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> UPSC dropped this item. The standard Zangar expression for hydrodynamic pressure on a dam face is</p>
<div class="formula">p<sub>e</sub> = C<sub>s</sub> α<sub>h</sub> γ<sub>w</sub> h = C<sub>s</sub> α<sub>h</sub> ρ g h</div>
<p>where C<sub>s</sub> depends on the depth ratio and the slope of the upstream face (C<sub>s</sub> = (C<sub>m</sub>/2)[y/h(2 − y/h) + √(y/h(2 − y/h))]). The nearest option, (c), omits the density ρ, so none of the options states the formula correctly; this is the likely reason the item was dropped.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> g/h and <b>(b)</b> 1/(gh) have the wrong dimensions.</li><li><b>(c)</b> C α<sub>h</sub> g h lacks ρ (it gives m²/s², not a pressure).</li><li><b>(d)</b> adds a length to a dimensionless factor.</li></ul>
<p>Our own answer before seeing the key was (c), the closest form.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Hydrodynamic force (Westergaard, vertical face): P = 0·726 p<sub>max</sub> h at 0·412h.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2024c.js ----
// ESE 2024 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (PNGE-D-CVL), Q51–75
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; Q53 dropped).
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2024-51", year: 2024, no: 51, subject: "solid", topic: "Assumptions of the shear stress formula",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the distribution shear stress assumptions :<ol><li>For all values of y, q is uniform across the width of the cross-section, irrespective of its shape.</li><li>'F = (dM/dx)' is derived from the assumption that bending stress varies non-linearly across the section and is not zero at the centroid.</li><li>The material is homogenous and isotropic and the value of E is the same for tension as well as compression.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><div class="formula">q = F A ȳ / (I b)</div>
<ul><li><b>1</b> True: the formula assumes the shear stress is uniform across the width b at any level y.</li><li><b>2</b> False: the derivation uses the bending stress σ = My/I, which varies <b>linearly</b> and is <b>zero</b> at the neutral axis (centroid); F = dM/dx then follows from equilibrium of a slice.</li><li><b>3</b> True: homogeneous, isotropic, linearly elastic material with the same E in tension and compression (so the neutral axis passes through the centroid).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Assumption 1 is accurate for narrow rectangles but approximate for wide flanges or circles, where the stress varies across the width.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Shear formula rests on the flexure formula plus uniform q across the width.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-52", year: 2024, no: 52, subject: "solid", topic: "Stress trajectories",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT a property of the curves?`,
opts: ["Both the systems of the curves cross each other at 45°.", "Each system crosses the N.A at 45°.", "At any point of a curve the tangent and normal give the directions of the two principal stresses.", "The intensity of stress along each curve is the greatest when it is parallel to the length of the beam and diminishes along the curve to zero, where it cuts a face of beam at right angles."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Principal stress trajectories</b> in a beam are two families of curves whose tangents give the directions of the principal tensile and compressive stresses.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Not a property: the two systems cross each other at <b>right angles (90°)</b>, since principal planes are mutually perpendicular.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Property: at the neutral axis the state is pure shear, so each family crosses the N.A. at 45°.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Property: tangent and normal at any point give the two principal stress directions.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Property: at the top and bottom fibres the trajectories are parallel to the beam axis (bending stress greatest); they meet a free face at right angles where the stress along them is zero.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Trajectories ⟂ each other, 45° at the N.A. — the pattern of diagonal tension cracks.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-53", year: 2024, no: 53, subject: "solid", topic: "Shear vs bending stress in a circular beam", dropped: true,
q: `A rod of circular section is subjected to a shearing force on a plane perpendicular to its axis. What is the maximum shearing stress in terms of shearing force and rod diameter, if the rod is used as a beam with free ends and a central concentrated load, express the free length in terms of diameter for which the maximum shearing stress, due to shearing force is half the maximum direct stress?`,
opts: ["L = (3/2) D", "L = (1/2) D", "L = (5/2) D", "L = (2/3) D"], ans: null,
analysis: `<h3>Dropped by UPSC (not scored)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> UPSC dropped this item. Working it as intended:</p>
<ol class="work"><li>Central load W on a simply supported span L: shear F = W/2; M<sub>max</sub> = WL/4.</li><li>τ<sub>max</sub> = (4/3)F/A = (4/3)(W/2)/(πD²/4) = 8W/(3πD²).</li><li>σ<sub>max</sub> = M/Z = (WL/4)/(πD³/32) = 8WL/(πD³).</li><li>τ<sub>max</sub> = σ<sub>max</sub>/2 → 8W/(3πD²) = 4WL/(πD³) → L = 2D/3.</li></ol>
<p>This gives (d) L = (2/3)D, our own answer. The question is confusingly worded (it asks two things and calls a simply supported beam one "with free ends"), which is the likely reason it was dropped.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3D/2, <b>(b)</b> D/2 and <b>(c)</b> 5D/2 do not satisfy the condition.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Shear governs only very short, deep beams (span comparable to depth).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-54", year: 2024, no: 54, subject: "solid", topic: "Deflection from end slope",
q: `A beam 3 m long, simply supported at its ends is carrying a point load W at its mid-span. If the slope at the ends of the beam does not exceed 1°, what is the deflection at the mid-span?`,
opts: ["17·45 mm", "17·45 cm", "19·45 mm", "19·45 cm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 17·45 mm</h3><div class="formula">θ<sub>end</sub> = WL²/16EI ;  δ<sub>max</sub> = WL³/48EI ⇒ δ = θ L/3</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>θ = 1° = π/180 = 0·01745 rad; L = 3000 mm</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>δ = 0·01745 × 3000/3 = <b>17·45 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The ratio δ/θ = L/3 holds for a central point load; for a UDL, θ = wL³/24EI and δ = 5wL⁴/384EI give δ = 5θL/16.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 17·45 cm is ten times too large.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 19·45 mm does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 19·45 cm does not match the working above.</li></ul>
<p>The load W and section are not needed: the slope limit alone fixes the deflection, because both depend on the same W/EI.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Central point load: δ = θL/3; UDL: δ = 5θL/16.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-55", year: 2024, no: 55, subject: "sa", topic: "Conjugate beam stability",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the end conditions and internal conditions of a conjugate beam :<ol><li>A stable and statically determinate real beam will have a conjugate beam which is also stable and statically determinate.</li><li>An unstable real beam will have statically indeterminate conjugate beam. Hence if a conjugate beam is found to be statically indeterminate, it is concluded that the real beam is unstable and further analysis is not appropriate.</li><li>A statically indeterminate real beam will have stable conjugate beam. Hence its conjugate load must be such that it maintains equilibrium.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3", "2 and 3", "1 and 2", "1 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Conjugate-beam supports swap roles: fixed ↔ free, pin at end stays a pin, internal pin ↔ internal roller/support. As a result:</p>
<ul><li><b>1</b> True: a determinate, stable real beam has a determinate, stable conjugate beam.</li><li><b>2</b> True: an unstable real beam (mechanism) gives an indeterminate conjugate beam, which signals that the real beam cannot be analysed.</li><li><b>3</b> False: a statically indeterminate real beam gives an <b>unstable</b> conjugate beam; it stays in equilibrium only because the M/EI loading happens to satisfy the extra conditions (compatibility). Calling it "stable" is wrong.</li></ul>
<p>Example: a fixed–fixed real beam gives a free–free conjugate beam, stable only under the special M/EI loading.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Real determinate ↔ conjugate determinate; real indeterminate ↔ conjugate unstable.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-56", year: 2024, no: 56, subject: "solid", topic: "Assumptions in impact loading",
q: `Which one of the following is NOT an assumption of impact loading on a beam?`,
opts: ["The falling weight sticks to the beam and moves/vibrates with it", "No energy loss takes place", "The beam is linearly non elastic", "The deflected shape of the beam is the same under a dynamic load as under the static load"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) the beam is linearly non-elastic</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The energy method for impact (W falling from height h) assumes the beam is <b>linearly elastic</b>, so the energy stored is ½Pδ:</p>
<div class="formula">W(h + δ) = ½ k δ² ⇒ δ = δ<sub>st</sub>[1 + √(1 + 2h/δ<sub>st</sub>)]</div>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Assumed: the weight stays in contact with the beam after impact (no rebound).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Assumed: no energy is lost to heat, sound or local deformation at impact.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Assumed: the dynamic deflected shape is the same as the static one (so the static stiffness k applies).</li></ul>
<p>With h = 0 (suddenly applied load) the impact factor is 2.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Impact factor = 1 + √(1 + 2h/δ<sub>st</sub>).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-57", year: 2024, no: 57, subject: "steel", topic: "Factors in wind effect on structures", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following is NOT an effect of wind on a structure?`,
opts: ["The effect of wind on a structure depends upon the density and velocity of the air", "It depends upon the angle of incidence of the wind", "It depends upon the shape and stiffness of the structure", "It depends upon the smoothness of the structure surface"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) smoothness of the surface (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The wind force on a building, p = ½ρV² C<sub>p</sub> A, depends mainly on:</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> air density and wind velocity (dynamic pressure ½ρV²);</li><li><b>(b)</b> the angle of incidence, which changes the pressure coefficients on each face;</li><li><b>(c)</b> the shape (pressure/force coefficients) and the stiffness (dynamic response, gust factor for flexible structures).</li></ul>
<p>Surface smoothness is treated by the key as not a design factor for structures: for sharp-edged buildings separation is fixed at the corners and the force coefficients do not depend on surface finish. (It matters only for rounded shapes such as chimneys, where IS 875 gives different C<sub>f</sub> for rough and smooth surfaces.) Our first pick was (c); on review the key's answer is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> p<sub>z</sub> = 0·6V<sub>z</sub>²; force = C<sub>f</sub> A<sub>e</sub> p<sub>z</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-58", year: 2024, no: 58, subject: "sa", topic: "Basic structural elements",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I (Basic structural elements)</th><th>List II (Applications)</th></tr><tr><td>P. Tin rods</td><td>1. Carry tensile and compressive loads</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Beams</td><td>2. Members that resist axial compressive force</td></tr><tr><td>R. Columns</td><td>3. Bracing</td></tr><tr><td>S. Trusses</td><td>4. Reinforced concreter</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-3, R-4, S-1", "P-3, Q-4, R-2, S-1", "P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3", "P-1, Q-2, R-3, S-4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) P-3, Q-4, R-2, S-1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Basic structural elements.</p><ul><li><b>Tie rods (P-3)</b> ("tin rods" is a misprint): slender tension members used as bracing.</li><li><b>Beams (Q-4)</b>: flexural members, commonly of reinforced concrete (or steel).</li><li><b>Columns (R-2)</b>: members resisting axial compression.</li><li><b>Trusses (S-1)</b>: assemblies of bars that carry tensile and compressive axial forces.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Calls tie rods compression members.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Makes trusses the bracing and beams axial members.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Gives columns the bracing role.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Ties pull, struts/columns push, beams bend, trusses do both axially.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-59", year: 2024, no: 59, subject: "sa", topic: "Principle of superposition", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the principle of super position :<ol><li>The total displacement of the stress at a point in a structure subjected to several external loadings can be determined by adding together the displacements caused by each of the external loads acting together.</li><li>The material must behave in a non-linear elastic manner.</li><li>The geometry of the structure must not undergo significant change when the loads are applied.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2", "2 only", "3 only", "1 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 only (official key)</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> False as worded: superposition adds the displacements (or stresses) caused by each load acting <b>separately</b>; the statement says "acting together" and mixes "displacement of the stress", so the key treats it as incorrect.</li><li><b>2</b> False: the material must be <b>linearly</b> elastic (Hooke's law), not non-linear.</li><li><b>3</b> True: deformations must be small, so the geometry (and hence equilibrium equations) does not change appreciably — no P-Δ effects.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first pick was (d), reading statement 1 as the intended definition; on review its wording ("acting together") is wrong and the key is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Superposition needs linear material + small displacements.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-60", year: 2024, no: 60, subject: "sa", topic: "Stability condition of a truss", doubt: true,
q: `What is the condition for stability of truss with b number of bars, r number of external support reactions with j number of joints?`,
opts: ["b + r = 2j", "b + r &gt; 2j", "b + r &lt; 2j", "b + r &lt; j"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) b + r &lt; 2j (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a plane truss, each joint gives two equilibrium equations (2j), and the unknowns are the bar forces and reactions (b + r):</p><div class="formula">b + r &lt; 2j unstable; = 2j determinate; &gt; 2j indeterminate</div>
<ul><li>b + r &lt; 2j → <b>unstable</b> (mechanism): fewer unknowns than equations.</li><li>b + r = 2j → statically determinate (if properly arranged).</li><li>b + r &gt; 2j → statically indeterminate.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). The key gives (c), which is the condition for <b>instability</b>. A stable truss requires b + r ≥ 2j (with proper arrangement); the "stable and determinate" condition is b + r = 2j, our own answer (a). The key may have read the question as "condition for instability".</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Compare b + r with 2j.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Stable and determinate.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Stable and indeterminate.</li><li><b>(d)</b> b + r &lt; j is not a standard criterion.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Plane truss: compare b + r with 2j; space truss: with 3j.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-61", year: 2024, no: 61, subject: "sa", topic: "How structural forms carry load",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I (components)</th><th>List II (working)</th></tr><tr><td>P. Trusses</td><td>1. Carry compressive force</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Cables</td><td>2. Located at the crown and supports elevations</td></tr><tr><td>R. Arches</td><td>3. Support their loads in tension</td></tr><tr><td>S. Three-hinged arches</td><td>4. Prevent collapse</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-4, Q-3, R-1, S-2", "P-3, Q-2, R-4, S-1", "P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3", "P-2, Q-3, R-4, S-1"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) P-4, Q-3, R-1, S-2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> How structural forms carry load.</p><ul><li><b>Trusses (P-4)</b>: triangulated so that the framework does not collapse (geometric stability), members carrying axial forces.</li><li><b>Cables (Q-3)</b>: flexible, carry loads in pure tension, taking the funicular shape.</li><li><b>Arches (R-1)</b>: carry loads mainly by compression (the inverted funicular shape).</li><li><b>Three-hinged arches (S-2)</b>: hinges located at the crown and at the supports (springings), making the arch statically determinate.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Calls trusses tension-only and cables hinged.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Makes cables compressive.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Puts the crown/support hinges against trusses.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Cable = tension funicular; arch = compression funicular.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-62", year: 2024, no: 62, subject: "sa", topic: "Maximum shear in a cantilever (rolling loads)",
q: `Which one of the following is the condition for maximum shear in a cantilever?`,
opts: ["It occurs when a series of concentrated loads are not placed at the farthest point away from the fixed support", "It occurs when a series of distributed loads are placed at the farthest point away from the fixed support", "It occurs when a series of concentrated loads are placed at the nearest point away from the fixed support", "It occurs when a series of concentrated loads are placed at the farthest point away from the fixed support"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a cantilever with moving loads, the shear at any section equals the sum of loads beyond that section (towards the free end). The influence line for shear at a section is a rectangle of height 1 from the section to the free end; any load placed beyond the section contributes fully. Hence the maximum shear (at the fixed end) occurs when the whole series of concentrated loads is on the span, and for a section the loads should be placed on the free-end side — "at the farthest point away from the fixed support", as the key states.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Not placing the loads there reduces the shear at intermediate sections.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Refers to distributed loads, whereas the criterion asked is for concentrated load series.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Loads near the fixed support do not contribute to shear at sections further out.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Cantilever ILD for shear: rectangle from the section to the free end.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-63", year: 2024, no: 63, subject: "env", topic: "Water demand of institutions",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I (Type of commercial building/institution)</th><th>List II (Water demand in Litres per day)</th></tr><tr><td>P. Restaurants</td><td>1. 45 per capita</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Nurses homes and medical quarters</td><td>2. 70 per seat</td></tr><tr><td>R. Cinemas, concert halls and theatre</td><td>3. 135 per capita</td></tr><tr><td>S. Factories</td><td>4. 15 per seat</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3", "P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3", "P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1", "P-2, Q-3, R-4, S-1"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) P-2, Q-3, R-4, S-1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 1172 / CPHEEO water requirements for buildings other than residences:</p>
<ul><li><b>Restaurants (P-2)</b>: 70 L per seat.</li><li><b>Nurses' homes and medical quarters (Q-3)</b>: 135 L per head.</li><li><b>Cinemas, concert halls, theatres (R-4)</b>: 15 L per seat.</li><li><b>Factories (S-1)</b>: 45 L per head (where bathrooms are provided; 30 L without).</li></ul>
<p>Other values: hospitals 340–450 L per bed, hotels 180 L per bed, offices 45 L per head, schools 45 L (day) / 135 L (boarding).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> gives restaurants 15 L per seat.</li><li><b>(c)</b> gives restaurants 15 L per seat.</li><li><b>(b)</b> gives nurses' homes 45 L and factories 135 L.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Seats: restaurant 70, cinema 15; persons: factory/office 45, quarters 135.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-64", year: 2024, no: 64, subject: "env", topic: "Groundwater versus surface water quality",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding quality difference of ground water and surface water :<ol><li>The Turbidity of ground water is high, whereas surface water is little.</li><li>Total dissolved solids of ground water is lower than surface water.</li><li>Concentration of inorganic compounds or ions of ground water is higher than surface water.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2", "2 only", "3 only", "1 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> False: groundwater is filtered through soil and has <b>low</b> turbidity; surface water is often turbid, especially in floods.</li><li><b>2</b> False: groundwater has <b>higher</b> TDS because it dissolves minerals during long contact with rocks.</li><li><b>3</b> True: concentrations of inorganic ions (Ca, Mg, HCO₃, Fe, F, NO₃) are generally higher in groundwater — hence hardness, iron and fluoride problems.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Groundwater is usually better bacteriologically and more uniform in temperature and quality; surface water has more turbidity, colour, organic matter and pathogens.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Groundwater: clear but mineralised; surface water: turbid but soft.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-65", year: 2024, no: 65, subject: "env", topic: "Maximum daily demand",
q: `If P is population of town/city, Q is total quantity of water required during one year for a town/city; what is the formula for maximum daily per capita demand for a town/city?`,
opts: ["1·8 × Q/(P × 365)", "1·8 × Q/(P × 24)", "1·5 × Q/(P × 365)", "1·5 × Q/(P × 24)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1·8 × Q/(365P)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Average daily per capita demand = Q/(365P). The maximum daily demand is taken as <b>1·8 times</b> the average (Goodrich/CPHEEO); maximum hourly demand is 1·5 × the maximum-day hourly average, i.e. 2·7 × the annual average hourly rate.</p>
<div class="formula">q<sub>max day</sub> = 1·8 q<sub>avg</sub> = 1·8 Q/(365 P)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> divides an annual quantity by 24 (hours), giving nonsense units.</li><li><b>(d)</b> divides an annual quantity by 24 (hours), giving nonsense units.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·5 is the hourly peaking factor applied to the maximum day, not the daily factor.</li></ul>
<p>Distribution mains are designed for the maximum hourly demand (or maximum day + fire demand, whichever is greater); treatment plants for the maximum daily demand.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Max day 1·8×, max hour 2·7× the average.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-66", year: 2024, no: 66, subject: "env", topic: "Incremental increase method",
q: `If P<sub>n</sub> = Population at the end of n decade, P<sub>o</sub> = Present population, P<sub>av</sub> = Average arithmetic increase in the population (decadal), P<sub>I</sub> = Average incremental increase in population, n = Number of decades; what is the mathematical representation of Incremental Increase Method?`,
opts: ["P<sub>n</sub> = P<sub>I</sub> × n + (P<sub>av</sub> + P<sub>o</sub>)", "P<sub>n</sub> = P<sub>I</sub> + (P<sub>av</sub> + P<sub>o</sub>) × n", "P<sub>n</sub> = P<sub>o</sub> + (P<sub>av</sub> + P<sub>I</sub>) × n", "P<sub>n</sub> = P<sub>o</sub> × n + (P<sub>av</sub> + P<sub>I</sub>)"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) P<sub>n</sub> = P<sub>o</sub> + (P<sub>av</sub> + P<sub>I</sub>) n (as given)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The standard incremental increase formula is</p>
<div class="formula">P<sub>n</sub> = P<sub>o</sub> + n x̄ + n(n + 1)/2 · ȳ</div>
<p>where x̄ is the average decadal increase and ȳ the average incremental increase. Among the options, (c) is the only one that starts from the present population P<sub>o</sub> and adds n times the increases (it is the simplified form for the first decade, n(n + 1)/2 = n when n = 1).</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> does not start from the present population.</li><li><b>(b)</b> does not start from the present population.</li><li><b>(d)</b> multiplies P<sub>o</sub> by n, which would make the population grow n-fold.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Arithmetic: P<sub>o</sub> + n x̄; incremental: + n(n+1)ȳ/2; geometric: P<sub>o</sub>(1 + r)<sup>n</sup>.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-67", year: 2024, no: 67, subject: "env", topic: "Colloidal impurities",
q: `The impurities in water which are extremely small size particles and cannot be removed by settling and filtration are known as`,
opts: ["Suspended impurities", "Colloidal impurities", "Dissolved impurities", "Picked up impurities"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) colloidal impurities</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Colloids</b> (about 1 nm to 1 μm) are too small to settle in a reasonable time and pass through ordinary filters. They carry like electric charges (usually negative) that keep them dispersed; they cause turbidity and colour and are removed only after <b>coagulation</b> (alum, ferric salts) destabilises them into flocs.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Suspended impurities (&gt; 1 μm) settle or are filtered out.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Dissolved impurities (&lt; 1 nm, ions) need chemical treatment, ion exchange or membranes.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Picked up impurities" is not a size class.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Suspended → settle; colloidal → coagulate; dissolved → chemical/membrane.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-68", year: 2024, no: 68, subject: "env", topic: "Function of a flocculator",
q: `What is the function of flocculator in the typical water treatment scheme for surface water?`,
opts: ["To rapid dispersion of chemical coagulant(s) to encourage destabilization of colloids", "To permit the settlement of chemical 'flocs' along with colloidal particles", "To provide for gentle mixing of the destabilized colloids to promote agglomeration of colloids into large easily settleable flocs", "To remove the flocs and colloids which escape from the settlement in the secondary sedimentation tank"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) gentle mixing to build settleable flocs</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> After rapid mixing destabilises the colloids, the <b>flocculator</b> stirs gently (velocity gradient G ≈ 20–75 s⁻¹, detention 20–40 min) so that the micro-flocs collide and grow into large, settleable flocs without being broken.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Rapid dispersion of coagulant is the job of the <b>flash (rapid) mixer</b> (G ≈ 300–1000 s⁻¹, 30–60 s).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Settling of the flocs takes place in the <b>clarifier (sedimentation tank)</b>.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Removing flocs that escape settling is the job of the <b>rapid sand filter</b> (and "secondary sedimentation" belongs to wastewater treatment).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Flash mix → flocculate → settle → filter → disinfect.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-69", year: 2024, no: 69, subject: "env", topic: "Rapid mixing in lime-soda softening",
q: `What is the function of rapid mixing in the typical water treatment flow scheme for ground water with high hardness?`,
opts: ["To drive out the objectionable dissolved gas such as H<sub>2</sub>S and CO<sub>2</sub>", "To permit the settlement of chemical precipitates under gravity", "To reform the calcium and magnesium bicarbonates to prevent settlement of CaCO<sub>3</sub> under Mg(OH)<sub>2</sub> precipitates in the distribution lines", "To disperse the lime and soda ash to form the chemical precipitates"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) to disperse lime and soda ash</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In lime–soda softening, lime and soda ash are added and must be <b>rapidly and uniformly dispersed</b> so that they react with the hardness ions to precipitate CaCO₃ and Mg(OH)₂, which are then flocculated and settled.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Driving out H₂S and CO₂ is the job of <b>aeration</b>.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Settlement of the precipitates takes place in the <b>sedimentation tank (clarifier)</b>.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Converting carbonates back to bicarbonates to stop after-precipitation in pipes is <b>recarbonation</b> (CO₂ dosing) after softening.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Softening train: aerate → rapid mix (lime/soda) → flocculate → settle → recarbonate → filter.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-70", year: 2024, no: 70, subject: "env", topic: "Buston's fire demand formula",
q: `If Q is the water requirement in L/min and P is population in thousands, what is Buston's formula to determine fire demand?`,
opts: ["Q = 5663√P", "Q = 3182√P", "Q = 4637(1 − 0·01√P)", "Q = 100√P"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Buston: Q = 5663√P</h3><table><tr><th>Formula</th><th>Q (L/min), P in thousands</th></tr><tr><td><b>Buston</b></td><td>5663 √P</td></tr><tr><td>Kuichling</td><td>3182 √P</td></tr><tr><td>National Board of Fire Underwriters</td><td>4637 √P (1 − 0·01√P)</td></tr><tr><td>Freeman</td><td>1136 (P/5 + 10)</td></tr><tr><td>CPHEEO (Indian)</td><td>100 √P kL/day</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Identify the coefficient 5663 for Buston.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 3182√P is Kuichling's formula.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4637√P(1 − 0·01√P) is the NBFU formula (the option omits the leading √P).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 100√P (in kL/day) is the CPHEEO lump formula.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> For a city of 1 lakh (P = 100): Buston 56 630 L/min, NBFU 41 733, Kuichling 31 820 L/min.</p><div class="formula">Buston: Q = 5663√P (L/min, P in thousands)</div><p><b>Remember:</b> Buston gives the largest fire demand of these formulae.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-71", year: 2024, no: 71, subject: "env", topic: "Soffit of a sewer",
q: `What is meant by soffit?`,
opts: ["The system of pipes which conveys discharges in separate pipes to the drainage system", "A pipe or conduit which is owned by a local authority for conveyance of the sewage", "The highest point of the interior of a sewer pipe at any cross section", "The horizontal pipe lay below the floor level or below basement to receive the discharge of soil or waste water"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) highest point of the inside of a sewer</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The <b>soffit</b> (crown) is the top of the internal surface of a sewer at any section; the <b>invert</b> is the lowest internal point. Sewers of different sizes are often joined soffit-to-soffit (crowns at the same level) so that the smaller sewer does not back up.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> A system with separate pipes for soil and waste is a <b>two-pipe system</b>.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A sewer vested in a local authority is a <b>public sewer</b>.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A horizontal pipe below the floor collecting soil and waste discharge is the <b>building drain</b> (house drain).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Invert = bottom inside; soffit/crown = top inside.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-72", year: 2024, no: 72, subject: "env", topic: "Composting toilet as extension of pit privy",
q: `What is the logical extension of the pit privy?`,
opts: ["Septic tank and tile field", "Composting toilet", "Seepage pits", "Plastic pipe with holes"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) composting toilet</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A pit privy collects excreta in a pit where it decomposes slowly; its improved, logical extension is the <b>composting toilet</b> (e.g. twin-pit or ventilated improved pit with alternating chambers), in which faeces are kept aerobic with added carbon material, composted, and safely emptied as manure after one to two years.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> A septic tank with tile field is a water-carried on-site system, a different technology step.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Seepage pits dispose of septic-tank effluent; they are not an extension of a dry privy.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Perforated plastic pipes are used in soak-away/drain fields, not as a privy improvement.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Dry sanitation ladder: pit privy → VIP latrine → composting (twin-pit) toilet.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-73", year: 2024, no: 73, subject: "env", topic: "Good house drainage",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding a good building/house drainage system :<ol><li>The pipes should be of non-absorbent materials.</li><li>The system should have traps at all necessary points.</li><li>The branch drains should be as long as possible.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: pipes should be smooth, durable and non-absorbent (cast iron, PVC, glazed stoneware) so they do not soak sewage or harbour deposits.</li><li><b>2</b> True: traps (water seals) are needed at every fixture and at the junction with the sewer to stop foul gases entering the house.</li><li><b>3</b> False: branch drains should be <b>as short as possible</b>, with straight runs and adequate slopes, to minimise blockages and cost.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other requirements: self-cleansing velocity (0·75 m/s), ventilation and anti-siphonage pipes, inspection chambers at changes of direction, and the drain laid away from foundations.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> House drainage: short, straight, trapped, ventilated, accessible.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-74", year: 2024, no: 74, subject: "env", topic: "Drawback of aerobic digestion",
q: `What is a drawback of aerobically digested sludges?`,
opts: ["Odor reduction", "Enhanced putrescible", "Degraded of organic acids", "Difficulty in dewatering"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) difficulty in dewatering</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Aerobically digested sludges are biologically stable and inoffensive, but the fine, dispersed flocs have <b>poor dewatering</b> characteristics (low cake solids on vacuum filters or centrifuges), and the process consumes much aeration energy and yields no methane.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Odour reduction is an advantage, not a drawback.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Aerobic digestion <b>reduces</b> putrescibility (it stabilises the sludge).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Degradation of organic acids is part of the stabilisation; not a drawback.</li></ul>
<p>Anaerobic digestion, by contrast, produces biogas and a sludge that dewaters better, but needs careful pH and temperature control.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Aerobic digestion: stable, odourless, but power-hungry and hard to dewater.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-75", year: 2024, no: 75, subject: "env", topic: "Odour control in tannery effluent",
q: `What needs to be maintained to avoid the odour problem in tannery effluent treatment?`,
opts: ["Low pH", "pH between 7 – 8", "pH between 9·5 – 10", "High pH"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) pH 9·5–10</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Tannery effluents (especially from liming/unhairing) contain sulphides. At low or neutral pH sulphide is released as H₂S gas, causing a rotten-egg odour and corrosion. Keeping the pH at about <b>9·5–10</b> holds sulphide in the ionic HS⁻/S²⁻ form, so it stays in solution until it is oxidised (catalytic aeration with MnSO₄) or precipitated.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Low pH releases maximum H₂S — the worst condition.</li><li><b>(b)</b> pH 7–8 still releases a large fraction of H₂S (pK<sub>a1</sub> ≈ 7).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Very high pH is unnecessary and creates handling and neutralisation problems; the defined optimum range is 9·5–10.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> H₂S ⇌ HS⁻ at pH 7; above pH 9 almost no H₂S escapes.</p>` }
);

