// ESE 2024 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (PNGE-D-CVL), Q101–125
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A).
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2024-101", year: 2024, no: 101, subject: "rcc", topic: "Design shear and service load of a slab",
q: `A one way slab has effective span 3·6 m and is 150 mm thick. The live load expected on it is 3 kN/m<sup>2</sup>. What are the design shear and loads for checking serviceability respectively?`,
opts: ["18·225 kN, 6·75 kN", "16·225 kN, 5·75 kN", "18·225 kN, 7·75 kN", "15·225 kN, 8·75 kN"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 18·225 kN and 6·75 kN (per m width)</h3><ol class="work"><li>Self-weight = 0·15 × 25 = 3·75 kN/m²; live load 3·0 kN/m².</li><li>Service load (serviceability, γ<sub>f</sub> = 1·0) = 3·75 + 3 = <b>6·75 kN/m²</b>.</li><li>Factored load w<sub>u</sub> = 1·5 × 6·75 = 10·125 kN/m².</li><li>Design shear V<sub>u</sub> = w<sub>u</sub>L/2 = 10·125 × 3·6/2 = <b>18·225 kN</b> per metre width.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Design moment for completeness: M<sub>u</sub> = w<sub>u</sub>L²/8 = 16·4 kN·m per m.</p><div class="formula">Service = DL + LL</div><div class="formula">Factored = 1.5(DL + LL)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 16·225 / 5·75 / 8·75 use wrong loads.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 15·225 / 8·75 use wrong loads.</li><li><b>(c)</b> has the right shear but adds 1 kN/m² of finishes to the service load, which are not given.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Strength: 1·5(DL + LL); serviceability: 1·0(DL + LL).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-102", year: 2024, no: 102, subject: "rcc", topic: "Flanged sections: position of neutral axis",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the strength of flanged sections in flexure where the moment of resisting capacity of the flanged sections depends upon the depth of neutral axis x<sub>u</sub> :<ol><li>If x<sub>u</sub> ≤ D<sub>f</sub>, compressive force is in the flange only.</li><li>If (3/7)x<sub>u</sub> &gt; D<sub>f</sub>, compressive stress in the flange is uniform.</li><li>If x<sub>u</sub> &gt; D<sub>f</sub> and (3/7)x<sub>u</sub> &gt; D<sub>f</sub>, compressive stress in flange is non uniform.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3", "2 and 3", "1 and 2", "1 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the IS 456 stress block, stress is constant (0·446 f<sub>ck</sub>) from the top down to 3x<sub>u</sub>/7 and parabolic below.</p>
<ul><li><b>1</b> True: if x<sub>u</sub> ≤ D<sub>f</sub>, the whole compression zone lies in the flange; the section is designed as rectangular of width b<sub>f</sub>.</li><li><b>2</b> True: if 3x<sub>u</sub>/7 &gt; D<sub>f</sub> (i.e. D<sub>f</sub>/d small), the whole flange lies in the constant-stress zone, so flange stress is uniform (0·446 f<sub>ck</sub>) — IS 456 Annex G-2.2.</li><li><b>3</b> False: the stated conditions are the same uniform case as 2; the flange stress is <b>non-uniform</b> only when D<sub>f</sub> &gt; 3x<sub>u</sub>/7 (thick flange), for which IS 456 uses an equivalent y<sub>f</sub> = 0·15x<sub>u</sub> + 0·65D<sub>f</sub>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> D<sub>f</sub> ≤ 3x<sub>u</sub>/7 (≈ 0·2d) → flange fully at 0·446 f<sub>ck</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-103", year: 2024, no: 103, subject: "rcc", topic: "Cantilever with UDL deflection",
q: `What is the maximum deflection for cantilever subjected to udl throughout? (Where E is the modulus of elasticity of concrete, I is the moment of inertia, L is the length of the span, P is the point load)`,
opts: ["5ωL<sup>3</sup>/384EI", "PL<sup>3</sup>/3EI", "ωL<sup>4</sup>/8EI", "PL<sup>3</sup>/84EI"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) ωL⁴/8EI</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a cantilever with UDL ω per unit length, the moment-area method gives the free-end deflection as the first moment of the M/EI diagram about the tip: area ωL³/6EI at 3L/4 from the tip → <b>ωL⁴/8EI</b>. The slope at the tip is ωL³/6EI.</p><div class="formula">δ = wL⁴/(8EI) (moment-area)</div>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5ωL⁴/384EI is the simply supported beam with UDL (the option also has L³).</li><li><b>(b)</b> PL³/3EI is a cantilever with a point load at the free end, not a UDL.</li><li><b>(d)</b> PL³/84EI is not a standard result (a fixed beam with central load gives PL³/192EI).</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>First moment of M/EI about the tip.</li><li>δ = <b>ωL⁴/8EI</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Cantilever: UDL wL⁴/8EI; point PL³/3EI; moment ML²/2EI.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-104", year: 2024, no: 104, subject: "rcc", topic: "Inclined length of a stair step",
q: `Which one of the following is the equation for width of the step in a staircase, consider R being the rise, T being tread, D/2 being the depth and b is the width?`,
opts: ["b = √(R<sup>2</sup>/T<sup>2</sup>)", "b = √(R<sup>2</sup> + T<sup>2</sup>)", "b = √(R<sup>2</sup>·T<sup>2</sup>)", "b = √(R<sup>2</sup> − T<sup>2</sup>)"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) b = √(R² + T²)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the design of a stair spanning along its slope, each step is a triangle with legs R (rise) and T (tread). The inclined dimension along the soffit of one step — used to convert horizontal loads to inclined slab dimensions and to compute self-weight — is the hypotenuse:</p>
<div class="formula">b = √(R² + T²)</div>
<p>The average thickness of the waist + steps for self-weight is then D + R/2 (vertically), and the load on plan = γ(D√(R² + T²)/T + R/2).</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> R/T is the slope ratio, not a length.</li><li><b>(c)</b> √(R²T²) = RT has dimensions of area.</li><li><b>(d)</b> √(R² − T²) is imaginary for normal stairs (T &gt; R).</li></ul>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Each step is a right triangle with legs R and T.</li><li>Inclined length = √(R² + T²).</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Comfort rule: 2R + T ≈ 600–640 mm.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-105", year: 2024, no: 105, subject: "rcc", topic: "ACI safety provision",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct under safety provisions of the ACI code?`,
opts: ["Design strength is greater than required strength and design moment is greater than required moment", "Design strength is greater than required strength and design moment is less than required moment", "Design strength is less than required strength and design moment is greater than required moment", "Design strength is less than required strength and design moment is less than required moment"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> ACI 318 strength design requires, for every action,</p>
<div class="formula">Design strength φS<sub>n</sub> ≥ Required strength U ;  e.g. φM<sub>n</sub> ≥ M<sub>u</sub></div>
<p>The nominal strength is reduced by the strength-reduction factor φ (0·9 for tension-controlled flexure, 0·75 for shear, 0·65 for tied columns), and the loads are increased by load factors (U = 1·2D + 1·6L). The design moment capacity must therefore be at least the required (factored) moment.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Design moment less than required moment would be unsafe.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Design strength less than required strength violates the basic safety inequality.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Design strength less than required strength violates the basic safety inequality.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Capacity ≥ demand, always.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-106", year: 2024, no: 106, subject: "cpm", topic: "Segregation in conveying concrete",
q: `In the conveying of most building concrete from the mixer or truck to the form is done in bottom-dump buckets, then the chief danger during conveying is that of`,
opts: ["Transferring", "Moisture and temperature", "Segregation", "Vibrations"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) segregation</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> During handling — dumping, chuting, dropping from height, or jolting in buckets — the coarse aggregate tends to separate from the mortar. <b>Segregation</b> gives honeycombing, weak zones and poor surface finish, so the main precaution in conveying is to keep the mix cohesive: limit free fall to about 1–1·5 m, use bottom-dump buckets that discharge vertically, and avoid long chutes.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> "Transferring" is the operation itself, not the danger.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Moisture loss and temperature matter in hot weather or long hauls, but segregation is the chief danger in conveying.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Vibration during haul can cause segregation, but it is the consequence (segregation) that is the danger.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Convey without segregation; place without free fall; compact without bleeding.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-107", year: 2024, no: 107, subject: "bm", topic: "Properties of admixtures",
q: `Which one of the following is NOT a property of an admixture?`,
opts: ["Improve workability", "Increase strength", "Improve durability", "Increase permeability"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) increase permeability</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Admixtures are added to improve concrete: water reducers and superplasticisers improve <b>workability</b> (a) or allow a lower w/c and higher <b>strength</b> (b); air entrainers, pozzolanas and waterproofers improve <b>durability</b> (c). They are used to <b>reduce</b> permeability, not increase it — higher permeability would let water, chlorides and CO₂ in and shorten the life of the structure.</p>
<p>Mineral admixtures (fly ash, silica fume, GGBS) refine pores and greatly reduce permeability; integral waterproofing compounds block capillaries.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Improving workability is a genuine admixture function (plasticisers and superplasticisers).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Increasing strength is genuine: water reducers lower the w/c ratio, and pozzolanas add C-S-H.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Improving durability is genuine (air entrainers, pozzolanas, corrosion inhibitors).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Good concrete = low permeability = durability.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-108", year: 2024, no: 108, subject: "rcc", topic: "ACI creep-time function",
q: `Which one of the following is the creep co-efficient at any time? (Where C<sub>ct</sub> is the creep co-efficient at any time and C<sub>cu</sub> is the ultimate creep co-efficient and t is the time in days after loading)`,
opts: ["C<sub>ct</sub> = t<sup>0·60</sup>/(10 + t<sup>0·60</sup>) C<sub>cu</sub>", "C<sub>ct</sub> = t<sup>0·50</sup>/(10 + t<sup>0·50</sup>) C<sub>cu</sub>", "C<sub>ct</sub> = t<sup>0·40</sup>/(10 + t<sup>0·40</sup>) C<sub>cu</sub>", "C<sub>ct</sub> = t<sup>0·70</sup>/(10 + t<sup>0·70</sup>) C<sub>cu</sub>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) C<sub>ct</sub> = t<sup>0·6</sup>/(10 + t<sup>0·6</sup>) · C<sub>cu</sub></h3><p><b>Concept:</b> ACI 209 (Branson's equation) gives the development of creep with time after loading:</p>
<div class="formula">C<sub>t</sub> = t<sup>0·60</sup>/(10 + t<sup>0·60</sup>) · C<sub>u</sub> ,  C<sub>u</sub> ≈ 2·35 (standard conditions)</div>
<p>Check: at t = 28 days, t<sup>0·6</sup> = 7·4 → 0·43 C<sub>u</sub>; at 1 year, 34·5 → 0·78 C<sub>u</sub>; at 10 years ≈ 0·94 C<sub>u</sub>. The companion shrinkage function is ε<sub>sh,t</sub> = t/(35 + t) · ε<sub>sh,u</sub> (moist cured).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Exponent 0.50 is not the ACI 209 value; ACI uses t^0.60/(10 + t^0.60).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Exponent 0.40 is not the ACI 209 value; it would make creep develop too slowly.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Exponent 0.70 is not the ACI 209 value; it would make creep develop too fast.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> ACI 209: creep t<sup>0·6</sup>/(10 + t<sup>0·6</sup>); shrinkage t/(35 + t).</p>` },

{ id: "2024-109", year: 2024, no: 109, subject: "rcc", topic: "Design steps for T-beams", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding the design of T-beams :<ol><li>To establish flange thickness h<sub>f</sub> based on flexural requirements of the slab, which normally spans transversely between perpendicular T beams.</li><li>Determine the effective flange width b<sub>f</sub> according to ACI limits.</li><li>Choose web dimensions b<sub>w</sub> and d based on either negative bending requirements at the supports or shear requirements by setting a reasonable upper limit on the nominal unit shear stress V<sub>u</sub> in the beam web.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Nilson's design sequence for T-beams in a floor system:</p>
<ul><li><b>1</b> Treated as false by the key: the flange thickness is normally already fixed by the <b>slab design</b> (the slab spans between the beams) — it is an input, not a step of the beam design "based on flexural requirements".</li><li><b>2</b> True: determine b<sub>f</sub> from the code limits (ACI: span/4, 16h<sub>f</sub> + b<sub>w</sub>, centre-to-centre spacing; IS 456: l<sub>0</sub>/6 + b<sub>w</sub> + 6D<sub>f</sub>).</li><li><b>3</b> True: choose b<sub>w</sub> and d from negative moment at supports (where the web acts as a rectangular beam) or from a limit on shear stress.</li></ul>
<p>Our first pick was (d); on review the key's view — that h<sub>f</sub> comes from the slab design already done — is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> T-beam: slab gives h<sub>f</sub>; code gives b<sub>f</sub>; support moment/shear gives b<sub>w</sub>, d.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-110", year: 2024, no: 110, subject: "rcc", topic: "ACI minimum thickness of one-way slabs",
q: `What is the minimum thickness 'h' of nonprestressed one-way slabs of length 'L' for simply supported?`,
opts: ["L/24", "L/10", "L/20", "L/28"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) L/20</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> ACI 318 Table 7.3.1.1 — minimum thickness of non-prestressed one-way slabs (normal-weight concrete, Grade 60 steel), unless deflections are computed:</p><div class="formula">ACI one-way slab: l/20 (SS), l/24, l/28, l/10</div>
<table><tr><th>Support condition</th><th>h<sub>min</sub></th></tr><tr><td>Simply supported</td><td><b>L/20</b></td></tr><tr><td>One end continuous</td><td>L/24</td></tr><tr><td>Both ends continuous</td><td>L/28</td></tr><tr><td>Cantilever</td><td>L/10</td></tr></table>
<p>(For beams the corresponding values are L/16, L/18·5, L/21 and L/8.) IS 456 uses span/effective-depth ratios instead: 20 (simply supported), 26 (continuous), 7 (cantilever), with modification factors.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Read the support condition and apply the ratio.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> L/24 is the ACI 318 limit for one end continuous.</li><li><b>(b)</b> L/10 is the ACI 318 limit for a cantilever.</li><li><b>(d)</b> L/28 is the ACI 318 limit for both ends continuous.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> ACI slabs: 20 – 24 – 28 – 10.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-111", year: 2024, no: 111, subject: "rcc", topic: "Bending moment in a square slab",
q: `What is the maximum bending moment of elastic plates in a square slab where q is the load shared on length l?`,
opts: ["0·625 ql<sup>2</sup>", "0·00625 ql<sup>2</sup>", "0·0625 ql<sup>2</sup>", "0·000625 ql<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·0625 ql²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Each strip is treated as a simply supported beam carrying half the load:</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>In a square slab simply supported on four sides the load is shared equally by the two directions (Rankine–Grashoff: q<sub>x</sub> = q<sub>y</sub> = q/2 for l<sub>x</sub> = l<sub>y</sub>).</li></ol>
<div class="formula">M = (q/2) l²/8 = ql²/16 = 0·0625 ql²</div>
<p>Plate theory (Timoshenko, ν = 0·3) gives a slightly smaller centre moment of about 0·048 ql², because of torsional restraint at the corners; the Grashoff strip method gives the simple value 0·0625 ql² intended here.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.625ql² is ten times too large; it exceeds even a simply supported beam's ql²/8.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.00625ql² is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.000625ql² is a hundred times too small.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Square slab, equal sharing: ql²/16 each way.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-112", year: 2024, no: 112, subject: "rcc", topic: "Load factor for retaining wall stem",
q: `What is the load factor for stem in the design procedure for cantilever retaining walls with safety?`,
opts: ["1·2", "1·6", "2", "2·5"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·6</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the ACI-based design procedure for cantilever retaining walls (Nilson), the stem is designed as a vertical cantilever for the lateral earth pressure multiplied by the load factor for lateral earth pressure, <b>1·6</b> (ACI load combinations treat soil pressure H with factor 1·6). The footing is proportioned with service loads for stability (FS ≥ 1·5 against sliding and 2 against overturning), and then designed for factored loads.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1·2 is the factor for dead load in ACI combinations.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2 is the usual factor of safety against overturning, not a load factor.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2·5 is not a code value here.</li></ul>
<p>IS 456 would use γ<sub>f</sub> = 1·5 on earth pressure for limit state of collapse.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> ACI: 1·2D + 1·6L + 1·6H.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-113", year: 2024, no: 113, subject: "survey", topic: "Aerial camera in topographic surveys",
q: `Which one of the following types of equipment is used in topographic surveys and also for recording the shapes of buildings?`,
opts: ["Electromagnetic distance measurement devices", "GPS", "Satellite camera", "Aerial camera"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) aerial camera</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Metric <b>aerial cameras</b> take overlapping vertical photographs from aircraft or drones; photogrammetric plotting of stereo pairs gives contour (topographic) maps and also records the plan and height of buildings (used for city mapping and 3-D models).</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> EDM measures distances between points; it does not record shapes.</li><li><b>(b)</b> GPS gives coordinates of occupied points only.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Satellite cameras cover large areas but at coarser scale; building shapes need high-resolution imagery, and the standard answer for topographic and building mapping is the aerial camera.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Photogrammetry: 60 % forward and 30 % side overlap for stereo coverage.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-114", year: 2024, no: 114, subject: "survey", topic: "Taping correction formulae",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I (Correction)</th><th>List II (Formula)</th></tr><tr><td>P. Absolute length (c<sub>a</sub>)</td><td>1. (1/24)(W/P)<sup>2</sup>L</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Sag (c<sub>g</sub>)</td><td>2. h<sup>2</sup>/2L</td></tr><tr><td>R. Alignment (c<sub>m</sub>)</td><td>3. (c/l)L</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-1, R-3", "P-3, Q-2, R-1", "P-3, Q-1, R-2", "P-2, Q-3, R-1"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) P-3, Q-1, R-2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Taping correction formulae.</p><ul><li><b>Absolute length (P-3)</b>: C<sub>a</sub> = (c/l) L, where c is the correction per tape length l and L the measured length.</li><li><b>Sag (Q-1)</b>: C<sub>s</sub> = (1/24)(W/P)² L, with W the weight of the suspended span and P the pull — always negative.</li><li><b>Alignment / slope (R-2)</b>: C = h²/2L, where h is the offset (or height difference) over length L — always negative.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> gives the absolute correction the h²/2L form.</li><li><b>(d)</b> gives the absolute correction the h²/2L form.</li><li><b>(b)</b> gives sag the slope formula.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Always negative: sag, slope, misalignment.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-115", year: 2024, no: 115, subject: "survey", topic: "Error from bubble off centre",
q: `If sensitivity of a bubble tube is 30″ per 2 mm division what would be the error in staff reading on a vertically held staff at a distance of 200 m, when the bubble is out of centre by 2·5 divisions?`,
opts: ["0·073 m", "0·73 m", "0·0073 m", "7·3 m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·073 m</h3><div class="formula">e = n α D (α in radians)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>α = 30″ per division; n = 2·5 divisions; D = 200 m</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>Tilt of the line of sight = 2·5 × 30″ = 75″ = 75/206 265 = 3·636 × 10⁻⁴ rad.</li><li>Error = 3·636 × 10⁻⁴ × 200 = <b>0·0727 m ≈ 0·073 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Radius of curvature of the tube for information: R = 2 mm/(30/206 265) = 13·75 m.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0·73 m is out by powers of ten.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·0073 m is out by powers of ten.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 7·3 m is out by powers of ten.</li></ul>
<p>Such errors cancel when back- and foresights are equal and the bubble is centred for each reading.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 1″ ≈ 4·85 × 10⁻⁶ rad: 1″ over 100 m ≈ 0·5 mm.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-116", year: 2024, no: 116, subject: "survey", topic: "Culmination",
q: `When a celestial body crosses the observer's meridian, it is said to be`,
opts: ["culminate", "vernal equinox", "obliquity", "celestial pole"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) culminate</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A celestial body <b>culminates</b> (transits) when it crosses the observer's meridian. At <b>upper culmination</b> it reaches its maximum altitude; circumpolar stars also have a <b>lower culmination</b> below the pole. Meridian observations of the sun or stars give latitude (φ = δ ± z) and local time.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> The vernal equinox (First Point of Aries) is where the sun crosses the celestial equator northward — the origin of right ascension.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Obliquity (about 23·44°) is the angle between the ecliptic and the celestial equator.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The celestial pole is the point where the earth's axis meets the celestial sphere.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Transit = culmination = crossing the meridian.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-117", year: 2024, no: 117, subject: "survey", topic: "Why declination and RA are not observed",
q: `Why the observations of field astronomy do not involve the measurement of declination and right ascension?`,
opts: ["Because the altitude and azimuth are constantly changed to the motion of the celestial body", "Because the stars do not occupy fixed positions on the celestial sphere", "Because the distance of the sun from the earth is variable", "Because of the obliquity of the ecliptic"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A surveyor's instrument measures <b>altitude and azimuth</b> (horizon system) with the time of observation; declination δ and right ascension (equatorial system) are not measured in the field but taken from the star almanac. The key's reason: the positions of stars on the celestial sphere are not strictly fixed — precession, nutation, aberration and proper motion change δ and RA slowly — so their apparent places must be computed and published for each date (Nautical Almanac / star catalogues) rather than measured by the observer.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Altitude and azimuth do change continuously, but that is why they are observed with the time, not why δ and RA are excluded.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The variable sun–earth distance affects the sun's semi-diameter and parallax corrections only.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The obliquity of the ecliptic relates ecliptic and equatorial coordinates; it does not prevent measuring δ and RA.</li></ul>
<p>In the field, the astronomical triangle PZS is solved using the observed altitude/azimuth, the latitude and the tabulated δ.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Observe altitude, azimuth and time; take δ and RA from the almanac.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-118", year: 2024, no: 118, subject: "survey", topic: "Trigonometric levelling in geodetic work", selfCorrected: true,
q: `If the equality of back sight distance and foresight cannot be maintained, under such condition, which one of the following levelling types is most important part of geodetic surveying?`,
opts: ["Spirit levelling", "Reciprocal levelling", "Trigonometric levelling", "Ordinary levelling"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) trigonometric levelling (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In geodetic surveying over long lines — mountainous country, triangulation stations kilometres apart — equal back- and foresight distances cannot be kept, and spirit levelling is impractical. Heights are then found by <b>trigonometric levelling</b>: measuring vertical angles and distances and applying curvature and refraction corrections (often by reciprocal vertical angles). It is a principal method of geodetic height determination.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Spirit levelling relies on equal BS and FS distances to cancel collimation and curvature errors.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Ordinary levelling also needs balanced sights to cancel the errors.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Reciprocal levelling handles one long unequal sight (e.g. across a river) but still uses a level, not this general case.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Combined correction = 0·0673 D² m (D in km)</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Long geodetic lines: trigonometric heights with reciprocal observations.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-119", year: 2024, no: 119, subject: "survey", topic: "Features of GPS surveying",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding global positioning system (GPS) :<ol><li>GPS cannot be used in all weather conditions.</li><li>In GPS surveying inter visibility between stations or points surveyed is not necessary.</li><li>High cost of GPS surveying has restricted the realization of the full potential of GPS.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> False: GPS works day and night in <b>all weather</b>; radio signals pass through clouds and rain (only heavy canopy or buildings block them).</li><li><b>2</b> True: each receiver needs a view of the sky (satellites), not of the other stations — intervisibility between survey points is not required, unlike triangulation and traversing.</li><li><b>3</b> True (as keyed): the high cost of geodetic receivers and software has restricted full use of GPS, especially historically.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other limitations: need for open sky, multipath near reflecting surfaces, and heights referred to the ellipsoid (geoid model needed for MSL heights).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> GPS: all weather, no intervisibility, but needs sky view.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-120", year: 2024, no: 120, subject: "survey", topic: "Remote sensing surveys", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding remote sensing surveys :<ol><li>Different types of land use are distinguishable on images.</li><li>Most images lack the horizontal perspective.</li><li>For surveys of small areas, the cost of mobilizing a remote sensing mission may be uneconomical</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2", "2 and 3", "2 only", "3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The statements are read as limitations/characteristics of remote sensing in surveying:</p>
<ul><li><b>1</b> Not counted by the key: although land-use classes can often be separated, many types are <b>not</b> distinguishable on images without ground truth (similar spectral signatures), so the general claim is treated as false.</li><li><b>2</b> True: images are taken from above (nadir view) and lack the horizontal (side) perspective, making features such as building facades and height hard to read.</li><li><b>3</b> True: mobilising an aerial or satellite mission for a small area is often uneconomical compared with ground survey.</li></ul>
<p>Our first pick was (d), 3 only; on review the lack of horizontal perspective is a standard listed limitation and the key is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Remote sensing: big areas cheaply, but needs ground truthing.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-121", year: 2024, no: 121, subject: "survey", topic: "Aerial photogrammetry", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding aerial photogrammetry :<ol><li>Aerial photogrammetry has the ease with which topography of inaccessible areas can be detailed.</li><li>In aerial photogrammetry, there is possibility of omitting of few field data.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only (official key)</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: photogrammetry maps inaccessible terrain (mountains, swamps, forests) easily, since no one needs to walk the ground.</li><li><b>2</b> Treated as false by the key: a photograph records <b>everything</b> visible at the instant of exposure, so there is no chance of omitting field data (an advantage over ground survey, where the surveyor may miss details). Hidden features (under trees) are a separate limitation.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first pick was (c); on review the key's reading — that photographs give a complete record and do not omit data — is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Photogrammetry: complete record, fast, good for inaccessible areas; needs ground control.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-122", year: 2024, no: 122, subject: "survey", topic: "Electromagnetic spectrum regions",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I (Region)</th><th>List II (Wavelength)</th></tr><tr><td>P. Reflected IR band</td><td>1. Less than 0·03 nm</td></tr><tr><td>Q. X-ray</td><td>2. 0·1 – 30 cm</td></tr><tr><td>R. Gamma ray</td><td>3. 0·7 – 3·0 μm</td></tr><tr><td>S. Microwave</td><td>4. 0·03 – 3·0 nm</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-3, R-1, S-4", "P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2", "P-4, Q-1, R-3, S-2", "P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2</h3><ul><li><b>Reflected IR (P-3)</b>: 0·7–3·0 μm (near and short-wave IR), used for vegetation mapping.</li><li><b>X-ray (Q-4)</b>: 0·03–3·0 nm.</li><li><b>Gamma ray (R-1)</b>: less than 0·03 nm.</li><li><b>Microwave (S-2)</b>: 0·1–30 cm, used by radar (all-weather imaging).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Visible light lies between 0·4 and 0·7 μm; thermal IR 3–14 μm.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> gives IR the microwave range.</li><li><b>(c)</b> gives IR the X-ray range and X-rays the gamma range.</li><li><b>(d)</b> gives IR the X-ray range and X-rays the gamma range.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p>Remote sensing uses mainly the visible, reflected IR, thermal IR and microwave windows where the atmosphere is transparent.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Gamma &lt; X-ray &lt; UV &lt; visible &lt; IR &lt; microwave &lt; radio.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-123", year: 2024, no: 123, subject: "survey", topic: "Transferring alignment down a shaft",
q: `Accurate transfer of surface alignment down a vertical shaft using two plumbwires can be achieved by`,
opts: ["Rise and fall method", "Collimation method", "Bowditch's method", "Weisbach triangle method"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) Weisbach triangle method</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Two plumb wires hung down the shaft define the tunnel line. Underground, a theodolite is set slightly off the line of the wires, forming a very thin triangle (the <b>Weisbach triangle</b>). The small angle at the instrument and the measured distances give the offset needed to bring the line of sight exactly onto the line of the wires, so the alignment is carried underground.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Rise and fall is a method of reducing levels, not of transferring alignment.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The collimation (height of instrument) method is a method of reducing levels.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Bowditch's method adjusts the closing error of a traverse.</li></ul>
<p>An alternative is the Weiss quadrilateral; modern practice uses gyro-theodolites.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Shaft plumbing: two wires + Weisbach triangle.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-124", year: 2024, no: 124, subject: "survey", topic: "External distance of a circular curve",
q: `If R is the radius of the circle and Δ is deflection angle, what is the formula for external distance (E) in circular curve for use in design and setting out?`,
opts: ["E = R(sec Δ/2 − 1)", "E = R(1 − sec Δ/2)", "E = R(cos Δ/2 − 1)", "E = R(1 − cos Δ/2)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) E = R(sec Δ/2 − 1)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The external (apex) distance is from the point of intersection of the tangents to the midpoint of the curve. The distance from the centre to the PI is R sec(Δ/2), so</p>
<div class="formula">E = R sec(Δ/2) − R = R(sec Δ/2 − 1)</div>
<p>Related quantities: tangent length T = R tan(Δ/2); mid-ordinate M = R(1 − cos Δ/2); long chord = 2R sin(Δ/2); curve length = πRΔ/180.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Distance centre → PI = R sec(Δ/2).</li><li>External distance E = R(sec(Δ/2) − 1).</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> is negative for 0 &lt; Δ &lt; 180°.</li><li><b>(c)</b> is negative for 0 &lt; Δ &lt; 180°.</li><li><b>(d)</b> is the mid-ordinate (versine), not the external distance.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> External: sec − 1; mid-ordinate: 1 − cos.</p>` },

{ id: "2024-125", year: 2024, no: 125, subject: "survey", topic: "Geology and tunnel alignment", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following situations is NOT at all suitable for tunneling?`,
opts: ["If the beds are parallel to horizontal or have approximately zero degree inclination", "If the beds are vertically dipping and the axis of the tunnel is perpendicular to the strike", "If the strike of involved beds is parallel to the axis of the tunnel", "If the dip of involved beds is parallel to the axis of the tunnel"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) strike parallel to the tunnel axis (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> When the tunnel runs <b>along the strike</b> of dipping beds, its two sides lie in different rock layers and the beds dip across the opening: the down-dip side is under unequal pressure and slabs tend to slide into the tunnel along bedding planes. Water also follows the beds into the tunnel. This is the least favourable orientation.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Horizontal beds in massive rock give a stable roof if the beds are thick.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Vertical beds crossed at right angles (tunnel perpendicular to strike) — the best case, with each bed supporting itself like a series of arches.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Tunnel parallel to the dip direction (i.e. across the strike) is also favourable; our own answer was (d), but this is the across-strike case and is suitable. On review the key is accepted.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Tunnel across the strike: good; along the strike in dipping beds: bad.</p>` }
);

