// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; Q25 dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2023-1", year: 2023, no: 1, subject: "geo", topic: "Terzaghi's definition of a shallow foundation",
q: `According to Terzaghi, a foundation is shallow if its`,
opts: ["depth is equal to or less than its width", "depth is twice the width", "width is thrice the depth", "width is one fourth of depth"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) depth ≤ width</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Terzaghi classed a foundation as <b>shallow</b> when its depth D<sub>f</sub> is equal to or less than its width B (D<sub>f</sub>/B ≤ 1). For such footings the shear strength of the soil above the base level is neglected and the overburden is treated only as a surcharge q = γD<sub>f</sub> in his bearing capacity equation q<sub>u</sub> = cN<sub>c</sub> + γD<sub>f</sub>N<sub>q</sub> + 0·5γBN<sub>γ</sub>.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> D<sub>f</sub> = 2B is already a deep foundation in Terzaghi's sense.</li><li><b>(c)</b> B = 3D<sub>f</sub> is shallow, but it is a particular case, not the definition.</li><li><b>(d)</b> B = D<sub>f</sub>/4 means D<sub>f</sub> = 4B — a deep foundation.</li></ul>
<p>Later authors (Meyerhof, Skempton) extended shallow-foundation theory up to D<sub>f</sub>/B of about 2–4.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Shallow: D<sub>f</sub> ≤ B (Terzaghi).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-2", year: 2023, no: 2, subject: "geo", topic: "Definition of ultimate bearing capacity",
q: `The ultimate bearing capacity is`,
opts: ["the minimum net pressure intensity causing shear failure of soil", "the minimum gross pressure intensity at the base of the foundation at which the soil fails in shear", "the difference in intensities of the gross pressure after the construction of the structure", "the total pressure at the base of the footing due to the weight of the superstructure"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) minimum gross pressure at the base at which the soil fails in shear</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 6403 defines the <b>ultimate bearing capacity</b> q<sub>u</sub> as the minimum gross pressure intensity at the base of the foundation at which the soil fails in shear.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> The minimum <b>net</b> pressure causing shear failure is the <b>net ultimate bearing capacity</b>, q<sub>nu</sub> = q<sub>u</sub> − γD<sub>f</sub>.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The difference between gross pressure after construction and the overburden is the <b>net pressure</b>.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The total pressure at the base due to the superstructure is the <b>gross pressure intensity</b>, an applied load, not a capacity.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">q<sub>s</sub> = q<sub>nu</sub>/F + γD<sub>f</sub> (safe bearing capacity)</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Gross ↔ total at base; net ↔ excess over overburden.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-3", year: 2023, no: 3, subject: "geo", topic: "Types of geosynthetics",
q: `Which one of the following is NOT the common nomenclature of geosynthetics?`,
opts: ["Geotextiles", "Geogrids", "Geogrouts", "Geonets"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) geogrouts</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Geosynthetics are polymer products used with soil. The common families are: <b>geotextiles</b> (woven/non-woven fabrics — separation, filtration, drainage), <b>geogrids</b> (open grids — reinforcement), <b>geonets</b> (net-like — in-plane drainage), <b>geomembranes</b> (impermeable sheets — liners), <b>geocells</b>, <b>geocomposites</b> and geosynthetic clay liners.</p>
<p>"Geogrout" is not a geosynthetic; grouting is a separate ground-improvement method (injecting cement or chemicals).</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Geotextiles (woven or non-woven fabrics) are a standard geosynthetic type.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Geogrids (open grids for reinforcement) are a standard geosynthetic type.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Geonets (net cores for in-plane drainage) are a standard geosynthetic type.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Functions: separation, filtration, drainage, reinforcement, containment.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-4", year: 2023, no: 4, subject: "geo", topic: "Geotextiles in road works",
q: `Which one of the following is an advantage when geotextiles is used in road works?`,
opts: ["Prior stripping of the natural soil", "Economy of aggregate", "Increase of different settlings", "Slower consolidation of fills"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) economy of aggregate</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A geotextile laid on a soft subgrade separates it from the granular layers, so the aggregate is not lost by punching into the soft soil and fines do not migrate up. The base can be thinner, so <b>less aggregate</b> is needed.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> A geotextile often <b>avoids</b> stripping or replacing poor natural soil — stripping is not an advantage.</li><li><b>(c)</b> It <b>reduces</b> differential settlement by spreading load.</li><li><b>(d)</b> As a drainage layer it <b>speeds up</b> consolidation of fills, not slows it.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Geotextile under roads: separate, filter, drain, reinforce.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-5", year: 2023, no: 5, subject: "geo", topic: "SPT corrections and bearing capacity",
q: `Consider the following statements related to ultimate bearing capacity of footing based on the SPT and CPT values :<ol><li>The empirical correlations established in the USA between N and soil properties indicate the value of N conforms to certain standard energy ratios.</li><li>The value of N to be used for getting ϕ is the corrected value for standard energy.</li><li>The angle ϕ obtained by this method can't be used for obtaining the bearing capacity factors.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> SPT corrections and bearing capacity.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: US correlations (Terzaghi–Peck, Peck–Hanson–Thornburn) are based on hammers delivering about 60 % of the theoretical energy (N<sub>60</sub>).</li><li><b>2</b> True: the N value used to estimate ϕ must first be corrected to that standard energy (and for overburden), N<sub>60</sub> or (N<sub>1</sub>)<sub>60</sub>.</li><li><b>3</b> False: the ϕ so obtained <b>is</b> used to read the bearing capacity factors N<sub>c</sub>, N<sub>q</sub>, N<sub>γ</sub> (e.g. IS 6403 charts linking N, ϕ and the factors).</li></ul>
<div class="formula">N<sub>60</sub> = N × (E<sub>r</sub>/60) × corrections</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Correct N first (energy, overburden, dilatancy), then use it.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-6", year: 2023, no: 6, subject: "geo", topic: "Basis of the Unified Soil Classification",
q: `The unified classification system is based on the recognition of the type and predominance of the constituents considering`,
opts: ["grain length", "gradation", "elasticity", "non-compressibility"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) gradation</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Unified Soil Classification System (Casagrande; IS 1498 follows it) identifies soils by the type and predominance of constituents considering <b>grain size, gradation</b>, plasticity and compressibility. Coarse soils are named from grain size and grading (GW, GP, SW, SP, with C<sub>u</sub> and C<sub>c</sub>); fine soils from the plasticity chart (CL, CH, ML, MH).</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> "Grain length" is not a classification parameter.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Elasticity is not used.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Compressibility (low/high, L/H) is used for fine soils — not "non-compressibility".</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Well graded: C<sub>u</sub> ≥ 4 (gravel) / 6 (sand), 1 ≤ C<sub>c</sub> ≤ 3.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-7", year: 2023, no: 7, subject: "geo", topic: "Cofferdams for small heights",
q: `Which one of the following coffer dams is suitable for small heights?`,
opts: ["Cantilever sheet pile coffer dam", "Braced coffer dam", "Embankment protected coffer dam", "Double wall coffer dam"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) cantilever sheet pile cofferdam</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A single row of sheet piles driven deep enough to act as a cantilever is economical only for <b>small retained heights</b> (about 3–4 m), since cantilever bending moments grow rapidly with height.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Braced cofferdams (sheet piles with walings and struts) are used for deeper excavations in water.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Embankment-protected cofferdams use earth banks on the sheet piles; they suit larger heads where space is available.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Double-wall cofferdams (two rows of sheets tied and filled) are used for greater depths and large areas.</li></ul>
<p>For still greater heights, cellular cofferdams (circular or diaphragm cells) are used.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Height ↑: cantilever → braced → double wall → cellular.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-8", year: 2023, no: 8, subject: "geo", topic: "Immediate settlement of a flexible footing",
q: `A footing 3 m × 1·5 m in plan transmits a pressure of 160 kN/m<sup>2</sup> on a cohesive soil having E = 8 × 10<sup>4</sup> kN/m<sup>2</sup> and μ = 0·48. What is the immediate settlement at the centre, assuming the footing to be flexible? (Take I<sub>w</sub> = 1·52)`,
opts: ["2·11 mm", "3·21 mm", "3·51 mm", "4·62 mm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3·51 mm</h3><div class="formula">S<sub>i</sub> = q B (1 − μ²) I<sub>w</sub> / E</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>q = 160 kN/m²; B = 1·5 m; μ = 0·48 → 1 − μ² = 0·7696; I<sub>w</sub> = 1·52 (centre, L/B = 2); E = 80 000 kN/m²</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>qB/E = 160 × 1·5/80 000 = 0·003 m.</li><li>S<sub>i</sub> = 0·003 × 0·7696 × 1·52 = 0·00351 m = <b>3·51 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2·11 mm uses the corner or average factor.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3·21 mm uses wrong μ or width.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4·62 mm uses wrong μ or width.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A rigid footing would settle more uniformly, at about 0·8 times the flexible-centre value (≈ 2·8 mm here).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Flexible footing: centre settlement ≈ 2 × corner settlement.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-9", year: 2023, no: 9, subject: "geo", topic: "Boussinesq pressure diagrams",
q: `By means of Boussinesq's stress distribution theory, which one of the following pressure distribution diagrams can be prepared?`,
opts: ["Strain isobar", "Vertical pressure distribution on a horizontal plane", "Vertical pressure distribution on an inclined plane", "Pressure distribution diagram on an oblique plane"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) vertical pressure distribution on a horizontal plane</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> From σ<sub>z</sub> = (3Q/2πz²)[1/(1 + (r/z)²)]<sup>5/2</sup>, three standard diagrams are drawn:</p>
<ul><li>the <b>stress isobar</b> (pressure bulb) — locus of equal σ<sub>z</sub>;</li><li><b>vertical pressure distribution on a horizontal plane</b> at a given depth (bell-shaped curve);</li><li>vertical pressure distribution along a vertical line.</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> "Strain isobar" is not a Boussinesq diagram; isobars are of stress.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Inclined or oblique planes are not among the standard diagrams.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Inclined or oblique planes are not among the standard diagrams.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Pressure bulb at 0·1q extends to about 1·5B (square) or 3B (strip).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-10", year: 2023, no: 10, subject: "geo", topic: "Assumptions of Coulomb's wedge theory",
q: `Which one of the following is an assumption made by Coulomb in the development of his earth pressure theory for sand and for active state?`,
opts: ["The rupture surface is non-uniform surface", "There is a wall friction on the pressure surface", "Failure is three dimensional", "The soil is cohesive"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) there is wall friction</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Coulomb's wedge theory (1776) assumes: dry, homogeneous, isotropic <b>cohesionless</b> soil; a <b>plane</b> rupture surface through the heel; a rigid sliding wedge; failure in two dimensions (plane strain); and <b>friction between the wall and the soil</b> (angle δ), so the thrust is inclined to the wall normal.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> The rupture surface is assumed <b>plane</b>, not curved/non-uniform.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Failure is two-dimensional.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The basic theory is for cohesionless sand (extensions for c exist).</li></ul>
<p>Rankine, by contrast, assumes a smooth wall (δ = 0).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Coulomb: wedge, plane slip, wall friction. Rankine: smooth wall, plastic state.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-11", year: 2023, no: 11, subject: "geo", topic: "External failure of MSE walls",
q: `Which one of the following is a correct general consideration for a mechanically stabilized earth wall failure?`,
opts: ["Compression in reinforcement", "Bearing capacity failure", "Shearing in the wall", "Partly sliding of wall soil"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) bearing capacity failure</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An MSE wall is checked for <b>external stability</b> as a coherent gravity block — sliding on the base, overturning, <b>bearing capacity</b> of the foundation soil and overall (global) stability — and for <b>internal stability</b> — tensile rupture and pull-out of the reinforcement, and facing connections.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Reinforcement works in <b>tension</b>; compression is not a failure mode.</li><li><b>(c)</b> "Shearing in the wall" is not a standard mode for the reinforced block.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Sliding is of the whole reinforced mass on its base, not "partly sliding of wall soil".</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> MSE: external (slide, overturn, bearing, global) + internal (rupture, pull-out).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-12", year: 2023, no: 12, subject: "geo", topic: "Assumptions of Terzaghi's consolidation theory",
q: `Which one of the following is the part of assumptions made by Terzaghi while developing the mathematical statement of the consolidation process?`,
opts: ["The soil is non-homogeneous", "The soil particles and water are incompressible", "The partial deformation of soil is due to partial change in volume", "Co-efficient of permeability is variable during consolidation"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) soil particles and water are incompressible</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Terzaghi's one-dimensional consolidation theory assumes: homogeneous, fully saturated soil; <b>incompressible soil grains and water</b>; Darcy's law holds; flow and compression are one-dimensional; k and m<sub>v</sub> remain <b>constant</b>; small strains; and the volume change is entirely due to expulsion of pore water.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> The soil is assumed <b>homogeneous</b>.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The <b>whole</b> deformation is due to change in volume (expulsion of water), not "partial".</li><li><b>(d)</b> k is taken as <b>constant</b>.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">∂u/∂t = c<sub>v</sub> ∂²u/∂z² ,  c<sub>v</sub> = k/(m<sub>v</sub>γ<sub>w</sub>)</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Consolidation = squeezing water out of an incompressible skeleton.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-13", year: 2023, no: 13, subject: "survey", topic: "Porosity of rocks",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?`,
opts: ["The porosity of a rock depends upon size and shape of the rock grains", "Specific gravity is useful for calculating the rock over burden stress", "The porosity does not depend on rock mechanical processes", "The porosity of spherical rock grains is high in case of cubic packing"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Rock porosity <b>does</b> depend on mechanical (and chemical) processes: compaction under overburden reduces it; fracturing, weathering and solution increase it; cementation reduces it. Saying it does not depend on such processes is incorrect.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct: grain size, shape, sorting and packing control porosity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Correct: specific gravity (density) gives the overburden stress σ<sub>v</sub> = Σγz.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Correct: equal spheres in cubic packing have the maximum porosity, 47·6 % (rhombohedral packing 26 %).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Primary porosity (deposition) + secondary porosity (fractures, solution).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-14", year: 2023, no: 14, subject: "geo", topic: "Objectives of sub-surface investigation",
q: `Consider the following objectives related to the sub-surface investigation :<ol><li>To establish the geological profile.</li><li>To establish Hydrogeological conditions.</li><li>To monitor future changes in ground conditions through instrumentation.</li></ol>Which of the above objectives are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: to establish the stratification (geological profile) and the depth and extent of each layer.</li><li><b>2</b> True: to find the groundwater level, its variation and the permeability of strata (hydrogeological conditions).</li><li><b>3</b> True: to install instruments (piezometers, inclinometers, settlement plates) to monitor future changes during and after construction.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other objectives: to obtain samples for laboratory tests, determine engineering properties, and identify hazards (cavities, contaminants).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Investigation = profile + water + properties + monitoring.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-15", year: 2023, no: 15, subject: "survey", topic: "Dip fault",
q: `Dip fault is a fault plane`,
opts: ["parallel to the dip of beds", "parallel to the strike of a bedding plane", "diagonal to the dip of a bedding plane", "with no relationship to the bedding plane"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) parallel to the dip of the beds</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Faults are classed by the orientation of their strike relative to the beds:</p>
<ul><li><b>Dip fault</b>: strikes parallel to the <b>dip direction</b> of the beds (i.e. at right angles to their strike).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Strike fault: parallel to the strike of the beds.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Oblique (diagonal) fault: at an angle to both.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A fault always has some orientation relative to the beds; "no relationship" is not a class.</li></ul>
<p>Faults are also classed by movement: normal, reverse/thrust, strike-slip.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Name by what the fault parallels: dip → dip fault; strike → strike fault.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-16", year: 2023, no: 16, subject: "survey", topic: "Topographical survey",
q: `Which one of the following consists of horizontal and vertical location of certain points by linear and angular measurements and is made to determine the natural features of a country?`,
opts: ["Cadastral survey", "Topographical survey", "Astronomical survey", "Military survey"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) topographical survey</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A <b>topographical survey</b> fixes both horizontal and vertical positions of points by linear and angular measurements, to show natural features (hills, rivers, forests) and artificial features with contours on a map.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Cadastral surveys fix property boundaries for land records.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Astronomical surveys find absolute positions (latitude, longitude, azimuth) from celestial observations.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Military surveys prepare maps of strategic importance.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Topographic map = plan + contours.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-17", year: 2023, no: 17, subject: "survey", topic: "Trigonometric levelling and curvature", doubt: true,
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?`,
opts: ["Trigonometric levelling has never been used in geodetic surveys", "The differences in elevation are determined indirectly by trigonometric levelling", "The electronic distance measurement devices can be used for measuring the vertical distances", "The combined effects of curvature and refraction produce vertical readings that are slightly too short"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The line of sight is horizontal while the level surface curves away below it, so staff readings are <b>too large</b>; refraction reduces this by about one-seventh, but the combined effect still makes readings <b>too large</b> (correction −0·0673 d² m). Statement (d), "slightly too short", is therefore wrong.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Correct: trigonometric levelling finds height differences indirectly from vertical angles and distances.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Correct (in practice): EDM/total stations give slope distances from which vertical components are computed.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Statement (a) is also plainly wrong — trigonometric levelling is a standard method in geodetic surveys (heights of triangulation stations) — and was our own answer. The item appears to have two incorrect statements; the key's (d) is shown.</div>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Also not correct as worded — see the note.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Curvature and refraction: subtract 0·0673 d² (m, d in km) from staff readings.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-18", year: 2023, no: 18, subject: "survey", topic: "Correction for a short tape",
q: `A line AB between the stations A and B was measured as 348·28 m using a 20 m tape, too short by 0·05 m. What is the correct length of line AB?`,
opts: ["349·15 m", "348·41 m", "347·41 m", "346·15 m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 347·41 m</h3><div class="formula">True length = (L′/L) × measured length</div>
<ol class="work"><li>L′ = 19·95 m; L = 20 m.</li><li>True = 19·95/20 × 348·28 = 0·9975 × 348·28 = <b>347·41 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A short tape is laid out more times than it should be, so it over-reads; the true length is less than the measured one (correction −0·87 m).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 349·15 m adds the correction (rule for a long tape).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 348·41 m uses wrong correction amounts.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 346·15 m uses wrong correction amounts.</li></ul>
<p>Error per tape length 0·05 m × 17·41 tape lengths = 0·87 m.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Too short → subtract; too long → add.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-19", year: 2023, no: 19, subject: "survey", topic: "Reduced level by height of instrument",
q: `The levelling is carried out between two stations P and Q separated by 1000 m. The Back Sight (BS) reading is noted as 0·70 m on station P, whose BM is 240·00 m. Next, the Fore Sight (FS) reading is taken on an intermediate point and the value is 1·25 m. Then the instrument is shifted to a new location and BS is taken on intermediate point as 0·85 m. Finally, the FS reading is taken on station Q as 1·10 m. What is the Reduced Level of Q?`,
opts: ["239·20 m", "240·80 m", "241·25 m", "241·80 m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 239·20 m</h3><ol class="work"><li>HI₁ = 240·00 + 0·70 = 240·70 m.</li><li>RL of change point = 240·70 − 1·25 = 239·45 m.</li><li>HI₂ = 239·45 + 0·85 = 240·30 m.</li><li>RL of Q = 240·30 − 1·10 = <b>239·20 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Check: ΣBS − ΣFS = 1·55 − 2·35 = −0·80 m = RL<sub>Q</sub> − RL<sub>P</sub>. ✓</p><div class="formula">HI = RL + BS</div><div class="formula">RL = HI − FS</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 240·80 m treats the fall as a rise.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 241·25 m adds readings incorrectly.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 241·80 m adds readings incorrectly.</li></ul>
<p>In the rise-and-fall method: fall 0·55 m, then fall 0·25 m — total fall 0·80 m, same result.</p><p>The 1000 m distance between P and Q is not needed for the reduced level; it matters only for the permissible closing error (e.g. 12√K mm with K in km).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> ΣBS − ΣFS = last RL − first RL.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-20", year: 2023, no: 20, subject: "survey", topic: "Area from a shrunk map measured with a wrong tape",
q: `The length of a line originally 100 mm long on a map plotted to a scale of 1/1000, was found to be 96 mm due to shrinkage of the map. The map prepared using a tape of length 20 m was later found to be actually 20·03 m. If a certain area on the map, measured using a planimeter, is 282 mm<sup>2</sup>, what is the correct area on the ground?`,
opts: ["237 m<sup>2</sup>", "307 m<sup>2</sup>", "347 m<sup>2</sup>", "397 m<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 307 m²</h3><ol class="work"><li>Shrinkage factor SF = 96/100 = 0·96 → corrected map area = 282/0·96² = 306·0 mm².</li><li>Scale 1/1000: 1 mm² on the map = 1 m² on the ground → 306·0 m² as measured with the faulty tape.</li><li>The tape was 20·03 m long, so measured lengths were too small: true area = 306·0 × (20·03/20)² = 306·0 × 1·003 = <b>306·9 ≈ 307 m²</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 237 m² multiplies by SF² instead of dividing.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 347 m² overs-correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 397 m² overs-correct.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The planimeter reading must be corrected for both the shrunk sheet and the wrong tape; each enters squared because area is two-dimensional.</p><div class="formula">A_true = A_map/SF² × scale²</div><div class="formula">correct for tape error</div><p><b>Remember:</b> Shrinkage ÷ SF²; long tape × (L′/L)².</p>` },

{ id: "2023-21", year: 2023, no: 21, subject: "survey", topic: "Map versus aerial photograph clarity",
q: `In Map versus Aerial photograph, due to symbolic representation the clarity of details is`,
opts: ["less on map than on a photo", "more on map than on a photo", "less on a photo than on map", "more on a photo than on map"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) more on the map than on a photo</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A map shows <b>selected</b> features with standard symbols, colours and labels, so details are clear and easy to read. A photograph records everything as tones and textures, which must be interpreted, and small features can be hidden by shadows or trees.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> says the photo is clearer — the opposite.</li><li><b>(d)</b> says the photo is clearer — the opposite.</li><li><b>(c)</b> "less on a photo than on map" means the same as (b) in effect, but the item asks about the map and the key's wording is (b).</li></ul>
<p>Other contrasts: maps are orthogonal projections with uniform scale; photos are central projections with varying scale and relief displacement.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Map: selective and symbolic; photo: complete and natural.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-22", year: 2023, no: 22, subject: "survey", topic: "Anderson land-use classification levels",
q: `In multilevel classification system, Level IV classification is suitable for`,
opts: ["Landsat MSS images", "High-altitude aerial photographs", "Low-altitude aerial photographs", "Medium-altitude aerial photographs"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) low-altitude aerial photographs</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The USGS (Anderson) land-use/land-cover scheme is hierarchical; each level needs finer data:</p>
<table><tr><th>Level</th><th>Typical data</th></tr><tr><td>I</td><td>Landsat MSS type satellite data</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>High-altitude photos (scale ≤ 1:80 000) / TM data</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Medium-altitude photos (1:20 000–1:80 000)</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td><b>Low-altitude photos</b> (larger than 1:20 000)</td></tr></table>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Landsat MSS images suit Level I (broad classes such as urban, forest, water).</li><li><b>(b)</b> High-altitude photographs suit Level II.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Medium-altitude photographs suit Level III.</li></ul>
<p>Level I classes are broad (urban, agriculture, forest, water); Level IV separates individual land uses (e.g. single-family housing, specific crops).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Finer class level ↔ larger-scale imagery.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-23", year: 2023, no: 23, subject: "survey", topic: "Length of a circular curve",
q: `The chainage of the intersection point of two straights is 1060 m, and the angle of intersection is 120°. If radius of a circular curve to be set out is 570 m, and peg interval is 30 m. What is the length of the curve?`,
opts: ["190 π m", "185 π m", "180 π m", "170 π m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 190π m</h3><div class="formula">Deflection angle Δ = 180° − angle of intersection ;  l = πRΔ/180</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Δ = 180 − 120 = 60°.</li><li>l = π × 570 × 60/180 = <b>190π m</b> ≈ 596·9 m.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Tangent length T = R tan 30° = 329·1 m; chainage of T₁ = 1060 − 329·1 = 730·9 m; of T₂ = 730·9 + 596·9 = 1327·8 m.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 185π π do not equal RΔ for Δ = π/3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 180π π do not equal RΔ for Δ = π/3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 170 π do not equal RΔ for Δ = π/3.</li></ul>
<p>Peg interval 30 m: the first sub-chord is from 730·9 m to 750 m (19·1 m), then full 30 m chords.</p><p>Rankine deflection angle for a 30 m chord: δ = 1718·9 × 30/570 = 90·5 minutes.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Angle of intersection ≠ deflection angle: Δ = 180° − I.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-24", year: 2023, no: 24, subject: "survey", topic: "Length of the long chord",
q: `What is the length of the long chord? (Data as in the previous item: angle of intersection 120°, R = 570 m)`,
opts: ["370 m", "470 m", "670 m", "570 m"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 570 m</h3><div class="formula">Long chord = 2R sin(Δ/2)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Δ = 60° → Δ/2 = 30°.</li><li>L = 2 × 570 × sin 30° = <b>570 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> With Δ = 60°, the two tangent points and the centre form an equilateral triangle, so the long chord equals the radius.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 370 m does not equal 2R sin 30°.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 470 m does not equal 2R sin 30°.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 670 m does not equal 2R sin 30°.</li></ul>
<p>Mid-ordinate = R(1 − cos 30°) = 76·4 m; external distance = R(sec 30° − 1) = 88·2 m.</p>
<p>The long chord is used to set out the curve by offsets from the chord and to check the total length.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Δ = 60° → long chord = R.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-25", year: 2023, no: 25, subject: "transport", topic: "Stopping sight distance (units misprint)", dropped: true,
q: `The design speed for a two-way traffic on a two way lane road is 50 kmph. What is the value of safe stopping sight distance, if co-efficient of friction is 0·37 and reaction time of driver as 2·5 sec?`,
opts: ["34·8 sec", "61·4 sec", "122·8 sec", "193·5 sec"], ans: null,
analysis: `<h3>Dropped by UPSC (not scored)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> UPSC dropped this item — the options are printed in <b>seconds</b> for a distance.</p>
<ol class="work"><li>Lag distance = 0·278 × 50 × 2·5 = 34·75 m.</li><li>Braking distance = V²/(254 f) = 2500/(254 × 0·37) = 26·6 m.</li><li>SSD = 61·4 m (one-way).</li><li>On a two-lane road with two-way traffic and no median, IRC used to take twice the SSD = 122·8 m.</li></ol>
<p>Both 61·4 and 122·8 are offered, and in the wrong units, which is the likely reason for dropping it. Our own answer was (b) 61·4.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 34·8 is only the lag distance; <b>(d)</b> 193·5 does not match the working above.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> SSD = 0·278Vt + V²/(254f).</p>` }
);

