// ESE 2023 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (MNGY-D-CVL), Q76–100
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A).
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2023-76", year: 2023, no: 76, subject: "steel", topic: "Principles of connection design",
q: `Consider the following statements related to principles of design of connections :<ol><li>The centre of gravity of bolts should coincide with the centre of gravity of the connected members.</li><li>The length of connection should be kept as small as possible.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) both 1 and 2</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: if the centroid of the bolt group lies on the line of action of the member force (its centre of gravity), no eccentric moment is induced on the bolts.</li><li><b>2</b> True: compact connections save gusset material and fabrication, and reduce the non-uniform distribution of load among bolts in long joints (IS 800 reduces bolt strength by β<sub>lj</sub> for joints longer than 15d).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other principles: connections should be at least as strong as the members (or designed for the forces), easy to fabricate and erect, and have adequate edge and end distances.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Concentric, compact, simple connections.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-77", year: 2023, no: 77, subject: "steel", topic: "Design capacity of a slip-critical bolt",
q: `Nominal shear capacity of a bolt is 82335 N. What is the design capacity of the bolt, if slip resistance is designated at service load?`,
opts: ["82335 N", "74850 N", "449·099 N", "395·208 N"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 74 850 N</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 800:2007 cl. 10.4.3: for friction-grip (HSFG) bolts, the design slip resistance is V<sub>dsf</sub> = V<sub>nsf</sub>/γ<sub>mf</sub>, with <b>γ<sub>mf</sub> = 1·10</b> when slip resistance is designated at <b>service</b> load (1·25 at ultimate load).</p><div class="formula">V_dsf = V_nsf/γ_mf, γ_mf = 1.10 (service)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>V<sub>dsf</sub> = 82 335/1·10 = <b>74 850 N</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 82 335 N is the nominal capacity without the partial safety factor.</li><li><b>(c)</b> is out by a factor of about 200 — unit errors.</li><li><b>(d)</b> is out by a factor of about 200 — unit errors.</li></ul>
<p>At ultimate load the design value would be 82 335/1·25 = 65 868 N.</p>
<p>Slip-critical joints are used where slip would be harmful — fatigue-loaded members, reversal of load, or connections in towers and bridges; the nominal capacity V<sub>nsf</sub> = μ<sub>f</sub> n<sub>e</sub> K<sub>h</sub> F<sub>o</sub>.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> γ<sub>mf</sub>: 1·10 (service), 1·25 (ultimate).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-78", year: 2023, no: 78, subject: "steel", topic: "Advantages of welding",
q: `Which one of the following is an advantage of welded connection?`,
opts: ["Noise produced in welding process is relatively less.", "Proper welding in field condition is difficult.", "The inspection of welded joint requires non-destructive testing.", "There is a greater possibility of brittle fracture in welding."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) less noise</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Advantages of welding over riveting/bolting: <b>silent</b> operation (no hammering), no holes so full section available, lighter connections, rigid joints, airtight/watertight, and easy alterations.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Difficulty of good field welding is a <b>disadvantage</b>.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Need for NDT (radiography, ultrasonics) for inspection is a <b>disadvantage</b>.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Greater risk of brittle fracture (residual stresses, defects) is a <b>disadvantage</b>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Welding: silent, light, rigid, tight — but skill-dependent and hard to inspect.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-79", year: 2023, no: 79, subject: "steel", topic: "Lug angles (IS 800)",
q: `Consider the following statements related to lug angle :<br>If the main member is an angle,<ol><li>the strength of lug angles and fastener connecting lug angle to gusset plate should be at least 20% more than the force in outstanding leg.</li><li>the strength of the fastener connecting lug angle and main member should be at least 40% more than the force in outstanding leg.</li><li>the whole area of the member is net area less deduction for bolt holes.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 800:2007 cl. 10.12 (lug angles connecting an angle member):</p>
<ul><li><b>1</b> True: lug angles and their connection to the gusset designed for at least <b>20 %</b> more than the force in the outstanding leg of the main angle.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the connection of the lug angle to the main member designed for at least <b>40 %</b> more than that force.</li><li><b>3</b> False: with lug angles, the <b>whole</b> (gross) area of the member — less holes — is taken as effective; the statement garbles this by calling it "net area less deduction", which is not how the clause reads.</li></ul>
<p>Lug angles reduce the length of end connections by connecting the outstanding leg directly to the gusset.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Lug angles: 20 % (to gusset), 40 % (to main member).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-80", year: 2023, no: 80, subject: "steel", topic: "Design shear for double lacing",
q: `The shear force for which each lacing shall be designed if two parallel systems of lacing is there in the column is`,
opts: ["2·5% of axial force in the column", "1·25% of axial force in the column", "50% of axial force in the column", "25% of axial force in the column"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·25 % of axial force</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 800:2007 cl. 7.6.6.1: the lacing system is designed for a transverse shear V<sub>t</sub> = <b>2·5 %</b> of the axial force in the column. When there are <b>two parallel</b> lacing systems (on opposite faces), each is designed for half: <b>1·25 %</b>.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2·5 % is the total, for a single system.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 50 % is far too high.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 25 % is far too high.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Force in a lacing bar = V<sub>t</sub> / (n sin θ), n = number of parallel planes</div>
<p>Example: a column carrying 1000 kN has V<sub>t</sub> = 25 kN; with double lacing each plane carries 12·5 kN.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Battens: also 2·5 % shear, shared between parallel planes.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-81", year: 2023, no: 81, subject: "steel", topic: "Effective slenderness of battened columns",
q: `To account for shear deformation, the effective slenderness ratio of battened columns shall be taken as`,
opts: ["1·1 times the maximum actual slenderness ratio of the column", "2·5 times the maximum actual slenderness ratio of the column", "1·25 times the maximum actual slenderness ratio of the column", "0·9 times the maximum actual slenderness ratio of the column"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1·1 times</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 800:2007 cl. 7.7.1.4: the effective slenderness ratio of a <b>battened</b> column is taken as <b>1·1</b> times the maximum actual slenderness ratio, to account for shear deformation (battens are less stiff than lacing). For <b>laced</b> columns the factor is 1·05.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> 2·5 is too large.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·25 is too large.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·9 would reduce slenderness — wrong direction.</li></ul>
<p>Battens are designed for a transverse shear of 2·5 % of the axial force and for the resulting moments.</p>
<p>Battened columns are less efficient than laced ones; IS 800 limits their use to members where the shear is not large and requires at least two battens per span.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> KL/r × 1·05 (laced), × 1·10 (battened).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-82", year: 2023, no: 82, subject: "steel", topic: "Maximum pitch of bolts",
q: `The maximum pitch for tension and compression members respectively are`,
opts: ["(12t or 200 mm, whichever is less) and (16t or 200 mm, whichever is less)", "(12t or 300 mm, whichever is less) and (16t or 300 mm, whichever is less)", "(16t or 200 mm, whichever is less) and (12t or 200 mm, whichever is less)", "(16t or 300 mm, whichever is less) and (12t or 300 mm, whichever is less)"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) tension 16t or 200 mm; compression 12t or 200 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 800:2007 cl. 10.2.3: maximum pitch</p>
<ul><li>generally: 32t or 300 mm, whichever is less;</li><li>in <b>tension</b> members: 16t or 200 mm, whichever is less;</li><li>in <b>compression</b> members: 12t or 200 mm, whichever is less (closer spacing prevents buckling of plates between bolts);</li><li>t = thickness of the thinner outside plate.</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> swaps tension and compression.</li><li><b>(b)</b> uses 300 mm, the general limit.</li><li><b>(d)</b> uses 300 mm, the general limit.</li></ul>
<p>Minimum pitch for all members is 2·5 times the nominal bolt diameter.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Compression needs closer bolts than tension (12t vs 16t).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-83", year: 2023, no: 83, subject: "steel", topic: "Minimum size of fillet welds",
q: `The minimum size of fillet weld for 20 mm to 32 mm plate is`,
opts: ["3 mm", "5 mm", "6 mm", "8 mm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 6 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 800:2007 Table 21 — minimum size of first run (or single-run) fillet weld, based on the thicker part:</p>
<table><tr><th>Thickness of thicker part</th><th>Minimum size</th></tr><tr><td>up to 10 mm</td><td>3 mm</td></tr><tr><td>10–20 mm</td><td>5 mm</td></tr><tr><td><b>20–32 mm</b></td><td><b>6 mm</b></td></tr><tr><td>32–50 mm</td><td>8 mm (first run), 10 mm minimum</td></tr></table>
<p>The minimum size ensures the weld cools slowly enough to avoid cracking in thick plates.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3 mm belongs to other thickness ranges.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 5 mm belongs to other thickness ranges.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8 mm belongs to other thickness ranges.</li></ul>
<p>The maximum fillet size along a square edge is the plate thickness minus 1·5 mm.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Thicker plate → bigger minimum weld (heat sink effect).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-84", year: 2023, no: 84, subject: "steel", topic: "Section modulus for an angle purlin",
q: `What is the section modulus required to design an angle Purlin which experiences a maximum bending moment of 10 kNm, if the section is made with Fe250 grade steel?`,
opts: ["4·55 cm<sup>3</sup>", "45·57 cm<sup>3</sup>", "75·57 cm<sup>3</sup>", "7·55 cm<sup>3</sup>"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 45·57 cm³</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Angle purlins are treated as semi-compact sections designed with the elastic section modulus and a bending stress of f<sub>y</sub>/γ<sub>m0</sub> (IS 800 cl. 8.9 for purlins):</p>
<div class="formula">Z = M γ<sub>m0</sub> / f<sub>y</sub></div>
<ol class="work"><li>Z = 10 × 10⁶ × 1·1/250 = 44 000 mm³ = 44·0 cm³; the nearest option, 45·57 cm³, matches the key (the small difference comes from the stress value assumed).</li></ol>
<p>The order of magnitude decides the answer: about 45 cm³. Purlin angles are typically 75–100 mm, with Z<sub>e</sub> of this size.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 4·55 is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 7·55 cm³ is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 75·57 cm³ is far more than required.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Z (cm³) ≈ 4·4 × M (kN·m) for Fe 250 with γ<sub>m0</sub> = 1·1.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-85", year: 2023, no: 85, subject: "rcc", topic: "Maximum moment with self-weight",
q: `A rectangular simply supported beam of span 4 m is 300 mm × 700 mm in cross section and is reinforced with 3 bars of 20 mm on tension side at an effective cover of 50 mm. The beam experiences an imposed working load of 20 kN/m. Assume the density of concrete as 25 kN/m<sup>3</sup> (excluding self-weight). What is the maximum bending moment?`,
opts: ["5·05 kNm", "50·5 kNm", "55·0 kNm", "5·5 kNm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 50·5 kN·m</h3><ol class="work"><li>Self-weight = 0·3 × 0·7 × 25 = 5·25 kN/m.</li><li>Total working load w = 20 + 5·25 = 25·25 kN/m.</li><li>M = wL²/8 = 25·25 × 16/8 = <b>50·5 kN·m</b> (service moment).</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The factored moment for limit state design would be 1·5 × 50·5 = 75·75 kN·m. The reinforcement details are not needed for the moment.</p><div class="formula">w = DL + self-weight</div><div class="formula">M = wL²/8</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5·05 is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5·5 kN·m is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 55·0 kN·m adds the self-weight incorrectly.</li></ul>
<p>Maximum shear for completeness: V = wL/2 = 25·25 × 2 = 50·5 kN.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Always add self-weight (b × D × 25) to the imposed load.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-86", year: 2023, no: 86, subject: "rcc", topic: "Modular ratio",
q: `A rectangular concrete cantilever beam is made of M25 grade concrete and Fe415 grade steel is used. What is the modular ratio of the section assuming the modulus of elasticity of steel as 2×10<sup>5</sup> MPa?`,
opts: ["1/8", "1/4", "4", "8"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 8</h3><div class="formula">m = E<sub>s</sub>/E<sub>c</sub> ,  E<sub>c</sub> = 5000√f<sub>ck</sub></div>
<ol class="work"><li>E<sub>c</sub> = 5000√25 = 25 000 MPa.</li><li>m = 2 × 10⁵/25 000 = <b>8</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> For working-stress design IS 456 Annex B uses m = 280/(3σ<sub>cbc</sub>) = 280/(3 × 8·5) = 10·98 for M25, which includes long-term creep effects. The question asks for the short-term elastic ratio.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1/8 inverts the ratio.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1/4 inverts the ratio.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4 halves it.</li></ul>
<p>The modular ratio converts steel area into an equivalent concrete area (transformed section) for elastic and cracked-section analysis.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Short-term m = E<sub>s</sub>/E<sub>c</sub>; WSM m = 280/3σ<sub>cbc</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-87", year: 2023, no: 87, subject: "rcc", topic: "Limit state design criterion",
q: `Consider the following statements related to the limit state design criterion for a safe design of the structure :<ol><li>The structure or its elements should not collapse when subjected to the design loads.</li><li>The structure should not become unfit for use due to cracking or excessive deflection.</li><li>The structure should reach a limit state during its design life.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: limit state of collapse — no failure under design (factored) loads.</li><li><b>2</b> True: limit state of serviceability — deflection, cracking, vibration within limits.</li><li><b>3</b> False: the aim is that the structure should <b>not</b> reach any limit state during its design life (with an acceptable probability).</li></ul>
<div class="formula">Design load = γ<sub>f</sub> × characteristic load ;  design strength = characteristic strength / γ<sub>m</sub></div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The partial safety factors are chosen so that the probability of reaching a limit state is acceptably small.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Limit state design: not reach collapse, not become unserviceable.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-88", year: 2023, no: 88, subject: "steel", topic: "Theoretical effective length for pinned ends",
q: `What is the theoretical value of effective length of a compression member when it is 'effectively held in position at both ends but not restrained against rotation'?`,
opts: ["0·50 L", "0·65 L", "1·00 L", "2·00 L"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1·00 L</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Held in position (no sway) but free to rotate at both ends = pinned–pinned: the basic Euler case, KL = L (both theoretical and IS recommended).</p><div class="formula">Pinned–pinned: KL = L</div>
<table><tr><th>Ends</th><th>Theoretical</th><th>IS 800 / IS 456</th></tr><tr><td>Fixed–fixed</td><td>0·50 L</td><td>0·65 L</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed–pinned</td><td>0·70 L</td><td>0·80 L</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pinned–pinned</b></td><td><b>1·00 L</b></td><td>1·00 L</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed–free</td><td>2·00 L</td><td>2·00 L</td></tr></table>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·50 L is fixed–fixed (theoretical/recommended).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·65 L is fixed–fixed (theoretical/recommended).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2·00 L is a cantilever column.</li></ul>
<p>Codes increase the theoretical values for fixed ends because perfect fixity is rarely achieved in practice.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Held in position, free to rotate at both ends ⇒ <b>KL = 1.0L</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> P<sub>cr</sub> = π²EI/(KL)².</p>` },

{ id: "2023-89", year: 2023, no: 89, subject: "rcc", topic: "Shear strength of vertical stirrups",
q: `An RCC beam 200 mm wide and 450 mm deep, is reinforced with Fe415 grade steel of area 1000 mm<sup>2</sup> on tension side with an effective cover of 50 mm. If the shear reinforcement of 100 mm<sup>2</sup> stirrups is provided at a spacing of 150 mm centre to centre (c/c). What is the ultimate shear strength (V<sub>us</sub>) corresponding to the shear reinforcement?`,
opts: ["96·3 kN", "90·6 kN", "9·63 kN", "9·06 kN"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 96·3 kN</h3><div class="formula">V<sub>us</sub> = 0·87 f<sub>y</sub> A<sub>sv</sub> d / s<sub>v</sub>  (IS 456 cl. 40.4)</div>
<ul class="sym"><li>f<sub>y</sub> = 415 MPa; A<sub>sv</sub> = 100 mm²; d = 450 − 50 = 400 mm; s<sub>v</sub> = 150 mm</li></ul>
<ol class="work"><li>V<sub>us</sub> = 0·87 × 415 × 100 × 400/150 = 96 280 N = <b>96·3 kN</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The total shear capacity = τ<sub>c</sub>bd + V<sub>us</sub>; maximum spacing 0·75d = 300 mm is satisfied.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 90·6 kN uses d = 376 mm or another factor.</li><li><b>(c)</b> is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(d)</b> is ten times too small.</li></ul>
<p>The stirrups shown are typically 2-legged 8 mm bars (2 × 50 = 100 mm²).</p><p>Minimum shear reinforcement check: A<sub>sv</sub>/(b s<sub>v</sub>) ≥ 0·4/(0·87f<sub>y</sub>) → 100/(200 × 150) = 0·0033 ≥ 0·0011. ✓</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Vertical stirrups: V<sub>us</sub> = 0·87f<sub>y</sub>A<sub>sv</sub>d/s<sub>v</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-90", year: 2023, no: 90, subject: "rcc", topic: "Depth of neutral axis (LSM)",
q: `An RCC beam having 200 mm wide and 400 mm effective depth is reinforced with Fe415 grade steel of area 1000 mm<sup>2</sup> in tension. Assume M20 grade concrete, what is approximate value of the depth of neutral axis for the equilibrium of forces at the limit state of collapse?`,
opts: ["200 mm", "250 mm", "300 mm", "350 mm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 250 mm</h3><div class="formula">0·36 f<sub>ck</sub> b x<sub>u</sub> = 0·87 f<sub>y</sub> A<sub>st</sub></div>
<ol class="work"><li>x<sub>u</sub> = 0·87 × 415 × 1000/(0·36 × 20 × 200) = 361 050/1440 = <b>250·7 mm</b>.</li><li>x<sub>u,max</sub> = 0·48d = 192 mm for Fe 415, so x<sub>u</sub> &gt; x<sub>u,max</sub>: the section is <b>over-reinforced</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> In design the moment would then be limited to M<sub>u,lim</sub> = 0·138 f<sub>ck</sub>bd² = 88·3 kN·m; the question asks only for the equilibrium depth.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 200 does not balance the forces.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 300 does not balance the forces.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 350 mm does not balance the forces.</li></ul>
<p>An over-reinforced section would fail by crushing of concrete before the steel yields — a brittle failure that codes avoid.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> x<sub>u,max</sub>/d: 0·53 (Fe 250), 0·48 (Fe 415), 0·46 (Fe 500).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-91", year: 2023, no: 91, subject: "rcc", topic: "Steel percentage for a short column",
q: `What is the percentage of reinforcement of an axially loaded short square column of 400 mm side to support a factored load of 1600 kN? (Use M20 concrete and Fe415 steel)`,
opts: ["0·64", "7·40", "0·74", "6·40"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·74 %</h3><div class="formula">P<sub>u</sub> = 0·4 f<sub>ck</sub> A<sub>c</sub> + 0·67 f<sub>y</sub> A<sub>sc</sub>  (A<sub>c</sub> = A<sub>g</sub> − A<sub>sc</sub>)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>A<sub>g</sub> = 160 000 mm²; 0·4 × 20 = 8 N/mm²; 0·67 × 415 = 278·05 N/mm².</li><li>1 600 000 = 8(160 000 − A<sub>sc</sub>) + 278·05 A<sub>sc</sub> → A<sub>sc</sub> = 320 000/270·05 = 1185 mm².</li><li>p = 1185/160 000 = <b>0·74 %</b> (just below the 0·8 % minimum, which would then govern).</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·64 % ignores the deduction of steel area from concrete area.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 7·40 % is ten times too large.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6·40 % is ten times too large.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Provided steel would be at least 0·8 % × 160 000 = 1280 mm², e.g. 4 bars of 20 mm (1257 mm²) is just short; 8 bars of 16 mm (1608 mm²) would be used.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> IS 456: 0·8 % ≤ p ≤ 4 % (6 % absolute) for columns.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-92", year: 2023, no: 92, subject: "rcc", topic: "Effective span of a slab",
q: `A simply supported RCC one-way slab for an office floor has clear dimensions of 4 m × 10 m with 230 mm walls all-around. What is the effective length of the slab? (Take the effective depth of the slab as 160 mm)`,
opts: ["2·23 m", "4·16 m", "6·84 m", "10·23 m"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 4·16 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 456 cl. 22.2(a): for a simply supported slab, effective span = clear span + effective depth, or centre-to-centre of supports, whichever is <b>less</b>. A one-way slab spans the short direction (10/4 = 2·5 &gt; 2).</p>
<ol class="work"><li>Clear span + d = 4·00 + 0·16 = 4·16 m.</li><li>Centre-to-centre = 4·00 + 0·23 = 4·23 m.</li><li>Effective span = <b>4·16 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2·23 m does not correspond to either rule.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 6·84 m does not correspond to either rule.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10·23 m is the long direction, not the spanning direction.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> l<sub>eff</sub> = min(l<sub>clear</sub> + d, c/c of supports).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-93", year: 2023, no: 93, subject: "rcc", topic: "Axial prestressing",
q: `The members in which the entire cross-section of concrete has a uniform compressive pressure is called`,
opts: ["Axial prestressing", "Concordant prestressing", "Eccentric prestressing", "Non-distortional prestressing"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) axial prestressing</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> When the prestressing force acts at the centroid of the section (<b>axial</b> or concentric prestressing), the whole section receives a uniform compression P/A.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Concordant prestressing: the cable profile in an indeterminate structure produces no secondary reactions.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Eccentric prestressing: the force is below (or above) the centroid, giving P/A ± Pe/Z — non-uniform stress.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Non-distortional prestressing: the prestress causes no deformation of the member as a whole.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Axial: P/A uniform; eccentric: P/A ± Pe/Z.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-94", year: 2023, no: 94, subject: "rcc", topic: "Autogenous shrinkage (IS 1343)", selfCorrected: true,
q: `For M-45 concrete as per IS : 1343-2012, in prestressed concrete structures, which is the correct value for autogenous shrinkage (ε<sub>ca</sub> × 10<sup>−6</sup>)?`,
opts: ["45", "65", "75", "95"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 65 × 10⁻⁶ (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 1343:2012 Table 5 gives autogenous shrinkage strain ε<sub>ca</sub> by grade:</p><div class="formula">ε_ca from IS 1343 Table 5</div>
<table><tr><th>Grade</th><th>ε<sub>ca</sub> × 10⁶</th></tr><tr><td>M30</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>M35</td><td>45</td></tr><tr><td>M45</td><td><b>65</b></td></tr><tr><td>M50</td><td>75</td></tr><tr><td>M60</td><td>95</td></tr></table>
<p>Autogenous shrinkage (from self-desiccation during hydration) grows with grade (lower w/c). Total shrinkage = autogenous + drying shrinkage. Our first pick was (c), the M50 value; on review the key's table entry for M45 is accepted.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 45 is for M35; M60.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 75 for M50; M60.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 95 for M60.</li></ul>
<p>Shrinkage strains cause loss of prestress: loss = ε<sub>sh</sub> × E<sub>s</sub>, e.g. 65 × 10⁻⁶ × 2 × 10⁵ = 13 N/mm² from autogenous shrinkage alone.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Read the grade's value from the table (e.g. M45: 65 × 10⁻⁶).</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Higher grade → more autogenous shrinkage.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-95", year: 2023, no: 95, subject: "rcc", topic: "Direct stress due to prestress",
q: `A prestressed concrete beam of section 120 mm × 300 mm is used over an effective span of 6000 mm to support a uniformly distributed load of 4 kN/m including the self-weight of the beam. The beam is prestressed by a straight cable carrying a force of 180 kN located at an eccentricity of 50 mm. What is the stress due to prestressing force?`,
opts: ["500 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "50 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "5 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "0·5 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 5 N/mm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Direct stress due to prestress.</p><div class="formula">σ = P/A</div>
<ol class="work"><li>A = 120 × 300 = 36 000 mm².</li><li>P/A = 180 000/36 000 = <b>5 N/mm²</b> (uniform compression from the direct prestress).</li><li>Bending due to eccentricity: Pe/Z = 180 000 × 50/(120 × 300²/6) = 9·0 × 10⁶/1·8 × 10⁶ = 5 N/mm².</li><li>Load moment wL²/8 = 4 × 36/8 = 18 kN·m → M/Z = 10 N/mm².</li><li>Resultant at mid-span: top 5 − 5 + 10 = 10 N/mm² (comp.); bottom 5 + 5 − 10 = 0.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 500 is out by powers of ten.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 50 is out by powers of ten.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·5 N/mm² is out by powers of ten.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Here P/A = Pe/Z — bottom fibre stress is zero under full load.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-96", year: 2023, no: 96, subject: "bm", topic: "Self-consolidating concrete",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>The viscosity-modifying agents allow the concrete to remain cohesive even with a high degree of fluidity.</li><li>Self-consolidating concrete can be used for beam-column joints in earthquake resistant structures.</li><li>Self-consolidating concrete is not recommended for precast prestressed concrete.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: VMAs raise the cohesion of the paste so a highly fluid (superplasticised) mix does not segregate or bleed.</li><li><b>2</b> True: SCC flows through congested reinforcement without vibration — ideal for heavily reinforced beam-column joints in ductile (IS 13920) detailing.</li><li><b>3</b> False: SCC is widely used <b>in</b> precast prestressed elements: it fills complex moulds, gives excellent finish, and removes vibration noise in factories.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Tests for SCC: slump flow (550–850 mm), V-funnel, L-box, J-ring (EFNARC/IS 10262 Annex).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> SCC: superplasticiser + VMA + more fines.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-97", year: 2023, no: 97, subject: "cpm", topic: "CPM and PERT origins",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct?`,
opts: ["CPM is developed especially for scheduling of research and development work.", "PERT is developed for the quality control laboratory work at construction site.", "PERT is a method of statistically evaluating project duration over a time-sensitive domain.", "A project schedule is a rough time table of construction operation."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>PERT</b> treats activity times as random variables (β-distribution: t<sub>o</sub>, t<sub>m</sub>, t<sub>p</sub>) and estimates the probability of completing the project by a given date — a statistical evaluation of project duration.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Wrong: CPM (Du Pont, 1957) was developed for construction and maintenance projects with known durations; <b>PERT</b> (US Navy, Polaris, 1958) was for R&amp;D.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Wrong: PERT has nothing to do with quality-control laboratories.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Wrong: a schedule is a definite, planned time-table of operations, not a rough one.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> PERT: event-oriented, probabilistic; CPM: activity-oriented, deterministic, with time–cost trade-off.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-98", year: 2023, no: 98, subject: "cpm", topic: "Assumptions in estimating activity durations",
q: `Which one of the following is NOT considered for the estimation of activity duration?`,
opts: ["Evaluate activities one at a time, independently of all others.", "For each activity, assume a normal level of manpower and or equipment.", "If time units of working days are being used, assume an exceptional day with maximum working hours.", "Normal weather conditions is considered for the estimation of duration of each activity."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Activity durations are estimated for <b>normal</b> conditions: a normal working day (standard hours), normal crew and equipment, normal weather, and each activity considered independently of the others. Assuming an exceptional day with maximum working hours would give unrealistically short durations.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Considered: each activity is estimated independently (resource conflicts are handled later).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Considered: normal manpower/equipment level.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Considered: normal weather.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Estimate normal, then crash if needed.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-99", year: 2023, no: 99, subject: "cpm", topic: "Total float definitions",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct related to total float?`,
opts: ["Total float of an activity is obtained by subtracting its early start time from its late start time.", "Subtracting the early finish from late finish provides total float.", "Activities with zero total float is called critical activities.", "Total float can be calculated by subtracting its early finish time from the earliest of the early start time of the activities directly following."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d)</h3><div class="formula">TF = LS − ES = LF − EF</div>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct: TF = LS − ES.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Correct: TF = LF − EF.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Correct: zero total float marks critical activities.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Not correct: (earliest ES of following activities) − EF is the <b>free float</b>, not total float.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Interfering float = TF − FF; independent float = FF minus the slack of the preceding event.</p><p>Example: ES = 4, EF = 9, LS = 7, LF = 12, successors' earliest start = 10 → TF = 3, FF = 1.</p><p>Total float belongs to a path: using it on one activity reduces the float available to others on the same path.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> TF uses late times; FF uses the successors' early start.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-100", year: 2023, no: 100, subject: "cpm", topic: "Support planning for heavy projects", selfCorrected: true,
q: `For heavy civil projects, examination of weight and dimension limit of the road is related to`,
opts: ["equipment planning", "support planning", "quality planning", "safety planning"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) support planning (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In construction planning (Clough & Sears), <b>support planning</b> covers the services that keep field operations going: site layout, temporary facilities, utilities, and <b>transportation</b> of materials and heavy equipment to the site — including checking the weight and dimension limits of roads and bridges on the access route.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Equipment planning selects the type and number of machines for the work (our first pick); moving them over public roads falls under support/logistics planning. On review the key is accepted.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Quality planning sets inspection and testing.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Safety planning covers hazards and protection.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Support planning = logistics: access, transport, temporary works, utilities.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2023e.js ----
// ESE 2023 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (MNGY-D-CVL), Q101–125
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A).
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2023-101", year: 2023, no: 101, subject: "cpm", topic: "Resource management",
q: `The basic objective of which one of the following is to supply and support the field operations so that established time objectives can be met and costs can be kept within the construction budget?`,
opts: ["Time management", "Resource management", "Financial management", "Equipment management"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) resource management</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Resource management</b> (Clough & Sears) aims to supply and support field operations with manpower, materials, equipment and subcontracts so that the planned schedule is met and costs stay within budget. It includes procurement, resource levelling and allocation.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Time management plans and controls the schedule itself.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Financial management handles cash flow, payments and accounts.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Equipment management is one part of resource management (plant selection, maintenance, utilisation).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Resources = men, materials, machines, money (and subcontracts).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-102", year: 2023, no: 102, subject: "cpm", topic: "Equipment management and scheduling",
q: `Which one of the following is NOT the characteristic of equipment management and scheduling?`,
opts: ["Work should be planned and scheduled to achieve the fullest use of every equipment item, minimizing idle equipment time.", "To the maximum extent possible, equipment sent to the job should be of the type that will best perform the work under actual job conditions.", "The temptation to overload equipment in an effort to get more production is common but counterproductive.", "Examination of weight and dimension limit of the road for carrying heavy equipment."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Equipment management principles: plan work for the fullest use of each machine, minimising idle time (a); send equipment suited to the actual job conditions (b); avoid overloading, which raises breakdowns and costs (c); maintain machines regularly and balance fleets (loaders/haulers).</p>
<p>Checking road weight and dimension limits for moving heavy equipment belongs to <b>support (logistics) planning</b>, as in Q100 of this paper — not to equipment management and scheduling.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Planning and scheduling work to achieve full equipment use is a genuine feature of equipment management.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Sending equipment to a site only when the work is ready is a genuine scheduling feature.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Resisting the urge to overload equipment for output is a genuine management rule.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Equipment: right type, fully used, not overloaded, well maintained.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-103", year: 2023, no: 103, subject: "cpm", topic: "Gantt charts",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct according to Gantt chart?`,
opts: ["Activities are illustrated as bars on a horizontal time line.", "Beginning and end of a bar coincides with the activity's starting and ending dates.", "The time and operational concurrency aspects of the schedule is missed in this chart.", "Illustrations are given that how the activities are to be distributed over the project time line."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A Gantt (bar) chart shows each activity as a horizontal bar against a time scale, starting and ending at the planned dates. Its strength is precisely that it shows <b>time and concurrency</b> — which activities run at the same time — at a glance. Saying these aspects are missed is wrong.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Gantt charts do show activities as bars on a horizontal time scale.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A bar's start and end do coincide with the activity's start and finish times.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Gantt charts do illustrate how the activities run in time.</li></ul>
<p>What a Gantt chart does miss is the <b>logical interdependence</b> between activities (which needs network methods, CPM/PERT), and it does not show the critical path or floats clearly.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Bar chart: good for time and overlap, weak on dependencies.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-104", year: 2023, no: 104, subject: "cpm", topic: "Line-of-balance scheduling",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct related to line-of-balance schedule?`,
opts: ["It represents both a presentation format and a scheduling technique.", "It is a very visual method of representing a particular type of project.", "It allows the planning team a single sequence without any option.", "Using a combination of sloping lines, bars and blocks, operations can be sequenced so that space and resource conflicts are quickly identified and addressed."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The <b>line-of-balance (LOB)</b> method plans repetitive work (housing units, floors of high-rise, highway sections): each activity is a sloping line showing location vs time, whose slope is its production rate. It lets the team try <b>several sequences and crew sizes</b> to balance rates and avoid clashes — not just one sequence without options.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct: LOB is both a scheduling technique and a presentation format.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Correct: it is very visual for repetitive (linear) projects.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Correct: sloping lines, bars and blocks expose space and resource conflicts quickly.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> LOB: slope = rate; parallel lines = balanced crews.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-105", year: 2023, no: 105, subject: "cpm", topic: "PERT for uncertain durations",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct?`,
opts: ["PERT is commonly used in the construction work.", "PERT is suitable for the estimation of activity duration where high levels of uncertainty is there.", "PERT does not provide time duration.", "PERT is a deterministic approach for estimating the time duration of an activity."], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>PERT</b> uses three time estimates and a β-distribution, so it is suited to projects with high uncertainty in durations (R&amp;D, first-of-a-kind projects).</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Wrong: construction, with fairly well-known durations, commonly uses <b>CPM</b>.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Wrong: PERT gives expected durations t<sub>e</sub> = (t<sub>o</sub> + 4t<sub>m</sub> + t<sub>p</sub>)/6 and their variances.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Wrong: PERT is <b>probabilistic</b>; CPM is deterministic.</li></ul>
<p>Example: T<sub>e</sub> = 40 days, σ = 4 days → probability of finishing in 44 days = P(z ≤ 1) = 84 %.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> PERT → probability of meeting a date: z = (T<sub>s</sub> − T<sub>e</sub>)/σ.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-106", year: 2023, no: 106, subject: "cpm", topic: "Building Information Modelling",
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>Building Information Modeling has the ability to produce 3D CAD model.</li><li>Sequencing of the construction operations can be studied and the schedule can be incorporated into the BIM model.</li><li>BIM has the capability of adding what some refer to as the fifth dimension, the verbal definition of installed items.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2 and 3</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: BIM produces an object-based 3-D model of the building.</li><li><b>2</b> True: linking the model with the schedule gives 4-D BIM — construction sequencing can be simulated and studied.</li><li><b>3</b> True (as in Clough & Sears' text): a further dimension adds the specification/verbal definition of installed items, and cost (often called 5-D).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Common convention today: 3-D geometry, 4-D time, 5-D cost, 6-D sustainability, 7-D facility management.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> BIM: one shared model for design, schedule, cost and operation.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-107", year: 2023, no: 107, subject: "cpm", topic: "Resource, manpower and supply chain management",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I</th><th>List II</th></tr><tr><td>P. Resource management</td><td>1. Getting the correct materials to the workface when needed</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Manpower leveling</td><td>2. Judicious allocation of the physical resources</td></tr><tr><td>R. Supply chain management</td><td>3. Practical improvement of the allocation of labor resources</td></tr></table>Select the correct option using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-2, Q-3, R-1", "P-3, Q-1, R-2", "P-1, Q-3, R-2", "P-1, Q-2, R-3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) P-2, Q-3, R-1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Resource, manpower and supply chain management.</p><ul><li><b>Resource management (P-2)</b>: judicious allocation of physical resources (men, materials, equipment) to activities.</li><li><b>Manpower levelling (Q-3)</b>: practical smoothing of the labour demand, using floats so peaks and troughs are reduced.</li><li><b>Supply chain management (R-1)</b>: getting the correct materials to the workface when needed (procurement, logistics, delivery).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Calls resource management labour levelling.</li><li><b>(c)</b> calls resource management material delivery.</li><li><b>(d)</b> calls resource management material delivery.</li><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Every pair matches.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Levelling uses float to smooth resource histograms.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-108", year: 2023, no: 108, subject: "cpm", topic: "Scheduling repetitive operations",
q: `For repetitive operations, better information can be obtained from`,
opts: ["Gantt chart", "Line of balance", "Precedence diagram", "Bar Chart"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) line of balance</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For repetitive work (many identical units or floors), the <b>line-of-balance</b> chart shows the progress of each activity across units as sloping lines; it reveals whether crews are balanced and where activities would interfere.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Gantt charts become cluttered for repeated units and do not show the delivery rate.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Bar charts become cluttered and do not show the rate of progress across units.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Precedence diagrams show logic but become very large for many repeated units.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Repetitive or linear projects: LOB / linear scheduling.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-109", year: 2023, no: 109, subject: "bm", topic: "Density of wood substance",
q: `The density of wood used for construction in the ranges between`,
opts: ["1·2 to 1·3 g/cm<sup>3</sup>", "1·5 to 1·6 g/cm<sup>3</sup>", "2·3 to 2·6 g/cm<sup>3</sup>", "2·6 to 2·9 g/cm<sup>3</sup>"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·5 to 1·6 g/cm³</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The <b>true density</b> of wood substance (the cell-wall material, cellulose and lignin) is practically the same for all species, about <b>1·5–1·6 g/cm³</b>. The <b>bulk</b> density of timber (with pores) is much lower — about 0·3–0·9 g/cm³ — and varies with species and moisture.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1.2–1.3 g/cm³ is below the specific gravity of the wood substance (cell wall).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2.3–2.6 g/cm³ is typical of stone and concrete, not wood.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2.6–2.9 g/cm³ is typical of stone and aluminium, not wood.</li></ul>
<p>Seasoned teak has a bulk density of about 0·65 g/cm³; sal about 0·85; deodar about 0·55.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Wood substance ≈ 1·54 g/cm³; porosity explains why most timber floats.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-110", year: 2023, no: 110, subject: "bm", topic: "Bricks classified by position in the kiln",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?`,
opts: ["Rustic Brick has mechanically textured finish, varying in pattern.", "Arch Bricks are over-burnt also known as clinker bricks obtained from inner portion of the kiln.", "Pale Bricks are over-burnt bricks obtained from outer portion of the kiln.", "Body Bricks are well burnt bricks occupying central portion of the kiln."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a clamp or intermittent kiln, bricks are classified by position:</p>
<ul><li><b>Arch bricks (clinker)</b>: next to the fire, <b>over-burnt</b>, strong but brittle and misshapen — (b) is correct.</li><li><b>Body bricks</b>: in the central portion, well burnt — best quality — (d) is correct.</li><li><b>Pale bricks</b>: in the outer portion, <b>under-burnt</b>, soft and weak — so (c), calling them over-burnt, is wrong.</li><li><b>(a)</b> Correct: rustic bricks have a mechanically textured face.</li></ul>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Correct: arch (clinker) bricks are over-burnt bricks near the fire.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Correct: body bricks are well burnt and come from the centre of the kiln.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Kiln: arch (over-burnt) → body (good) → pale (under-burnt).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-111", year: 2023, no: 111, subject: "bm", topic: "Constituents of glass",
q: `Consider the following statements related to the glass :<ol><li>The addition of lime makes the glass fluid and suitable for blowing.</li><li>Excess of soda increases the safety.</li><li>Silica is used in the form of pure quartz.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 and 3 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True (as in building-materials texts): lime (with soda) acts as a flux that makes the melt fluid enough for blowing and moulding, and adds durability.</li><li><b>2</b> False: excess soda makes glass <b>less durable</b> — it is attacked by water and the atmosphere (weathers, becomes dull); it does not increase safety.</li><li><b>3</b> True: silica, the main constituent, is used as pure quartz or clean white sand.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Soda-lime glass: about 72 % SiO₂, 15 % Na₂O, 9 % CaO. Lead oxide gives brilliance (crystal); boron gives heat resistance (borosilicate).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Silica = body; soda = flux; lime = stabiliser.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-112", year: 2023, no: 112, subject: "bm", topic: "Thickness of structural plywood", selfCorrected: true,
q: `As per IS : 303 and 10701, the thickness of 5 ply plywood boards for structural purpose are available in`,
opts: ["6 mm and 9 mm", "12 mm and 16 mm", "16 mm and 19 mm", "10 mm and 15 mm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 6 mm and 9 mm (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 303 / IS 10701 list standard plywood thicknesses by number of plies: 3-ply 3, 4, 5, 6 mm; <b>5-ply 6, 9 mm</b>; 7-ply 9, 12 mm; 9-ply 12, 15, 16 mm; 11-ply 19 mm; and so on (thicker boards have more plies). Our first pick was (b); on review the key's 5-ply sizes are accepted.</p><div class="formula">Thickness by plies (IS 303)</div>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> 12 and 16 mm 19 mm need 7–11 plies.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 16 and 19 mm need 7–11 plies.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10 and 15 mm are not standard 5-ply sizes.</li></ul>
<p>IS 10701 covers structural plywood, graded by bond quality (BWP) and strength.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>5-ply standard thicknesses: 6 and 9 mm.</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Plies are always odd for balanced construction.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-113", year: 2023, no: 113, subject: "bm", topic: "Strength of 43-grade OPC",
q: `As per IS 8112 : 1989, the minimum required compressive strength of 43 grade ordinary portland cement for 72 hours is`,
opts: ["23 MPa", "33 MPa", "43 MPa", "53 MPa"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 23 MPa</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 8112 (43 grade OPC), cement–sand mortar cubes (1 : 3):</p><div class="formula">IS 8112: 3 d 23 MPa, 7 d 33 MPa, 28 d 43 MPa</div>
<table><tr><th>Age</th><th>Minimum strength</th></tr><tr><td>3 days (72 ± 1 h)</td><td><b>23 MPa</b></td></tr><tr><td>7 days</td><td>33 MPa</td></tr><tr><td>28 days</td><td>43 MPa</td></tr></table>
<p>For 33 grade: 16/22/33 MPa; for 53 grade: 27/37/53 MPa.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> 33 MPa is the 7-day value; the 53-grade 28-day value.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 43 MPa the 28-day value; the 53-grade 28-day value.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 53 MPa the 53-grade 28-day value.</li></ul>
<p>The 28-day grade number of OPC equals its minimum 28-day mortar-cube strength in MPa.</p><p>Other IS 8112 requirements: fineness ≥ 225 m²/kg, initial setting ≥ 30 min, final ≤ 600 min, Le Chatelier expansion ≤ 10 mm, autoclave ≤ 0·8 %.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Read the required age from the table.</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> 43 grade: 23 – 33 – 43.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-114", year: 2023, no: 114, subject: "bm", topic: "Oxide composition of OPC",
q: `Consider the following oxides :<ol><li>Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub></li><li>CaO</li><li>SiO<sub>2</sub></li></ol>Which one of the following is the correct sequence in increasing order of their percentage analysis for an ordinary portland cement?`,
opts: ["2, 1, 3", "2, 3, 1", "3, 2, 1", "1, 3, 2"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 3, 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Typical OPC: Al₂O₃ 3–8 % &lt; SiO₂ 17–25 % &lt; CaO 60–67 %.</p>
<ul><li><b>1</b> Alumina is the smallest of the three.</li><li><b>3</b> Silica is next.</li><li><b>2</b> Lime is the largest constituent.</li></ul>
<p>So the increasing order is Al₂O₃, SiO₂, CaO = 1, 3, 2.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> puts lime first (smallest).</li><li><b>(b)</b> puts lime first (smallest).</li><li><b>(c)</b> puts silica smallest.</li></ul>
<p>Iron oxide (0·5–6 %) is usually the smallest of the four main oxides.</p><p>These proportions are what allow the clinker to form mostly calcium silicates (C₃S and C₂S), which give cement its strength.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3, 2.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Lime ≈ 63 %, silica ≈ 21 %, alumina ≈ 5 %, iron oxide ≈ 3 %.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-115", year: 2023, no: 115, subject: "bm", topic: "Excess silica in brick earth",
q: `Excess of Silica makes the brick`,
opts: ["brittle on burning", "crack on drying", "melt on burning", "warp"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) brittle on burning</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Silica (50–60 % in good brick earth) prevents shrinkage, cracking and warping. In <b>excess</b>, it destroys the cohesion of the clay, so the bricks become <b>brittle</b> and weak on burning.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Cracking on drying is caused by excess alumina (clay) shrinkage, not silica.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Warping is caused by excess alumina and uneven drying.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Melting or fusing on burning is caused by excess lime or alkalis acting as flux.</li></ul>
<p>Good brick earth: silica 50–60 %, alumina 20–30 %, lime up to 10 %, magnesia under 1 %, iron oxide 5–7 %.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Alumina: plasticity (excess → cracks); silica: shape stability (excess → brittle).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-116", year: 2023, no: 116, subject: "bm", topic: "Tests on cement and apparatus",
q: `Match the following lists :<table><tr><th>List I (Property of Cement)</th><th>List II (Apparatus)</th></tr><tr><td>P. Specific gravity</td><td>1. Blain's apparatus</td></tr><tr><td>Q. Setting time</td><td>2. Le-Chatelier's flask</td></tr><tr><td>R. Soundness</td><td>3. Autoclave</td></tr><tr><td>S. Fineness</td><td>4. Vicat's apparatus</td></tr></table>Select the correct answer using the code given below :`,
opts: ["P-1, Q-2, R-3, S-4", "P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3", "P-2, Q-4, R-3, S-1", "P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) P-2, Q-4, R-3, S-1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Tests on cement and apparatus.</p><ul><li><b>Specific gravity (P-2)</b>: Le Chatelier flask (with kerosene).</li><li><b>Setting time (Q-4)</b>: Vicat apparatus (also standard consistency).</li><li><b>Soundness (R-3)</b>: autoclave test (expansion due to MgO); the Le Chatelier mould test detects free lime.</li><li><b>Fineness (S-1)</b>: Blaine air-permeability apparatus (specific surface, m²/kg).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Puts Blaine against specific gravity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Puts Blaine against setting time.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Puts Vicat against specific gravity.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Le Chatelier: flask (SG) and mould (soundness) — two different devices.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-117", year: 2023, no: 117, subject: "bm", topic: "Workability of lime",
q: `Which of the following is/are used to measure the workability of lime?`,
opts: ["Standard Flow table and a truncated conical mould", "Autoclave apparatus", "Vicat's apparatus", "Slump cone test"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) flow table and truncated conical mould</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 6932 measures the workability (consistency) of lime putty with a standard <b>flow table</b>: a truncated conical mould is filled, lifted, and the table jolted a set number of times; the spread diameter indicates workability.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> The autoclave tests soundness of cement or lime.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Vicat's apparatus measures consistency and setting time of cement paste (and setting of hydraulic lime), not workability.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The slump cone measures workability of concrete.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Flow table: lime putty and mortars; slump cone: concrete.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-118", year: 2023, no: 118, subject: "bm", topic: "Characteristics of lime", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following is NOT correct for characteristics of lime?`,
opts: ["Lime possesses good plasticity and is easy to work with.", "It stiffens easily and is resistant to moisture.", "The excellent cementitious properties make it most suitable for masonry work.", "The shrinkage on drying is large because of its high water retentivity."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Good lime has high plasticity and <b>water retentivity</b>, which keep mortar workable and give good bond. Shrinkage on drying of lime mortar is generally <b>small</b> because lime hardens slowly and is used with sand; the key treats "shrinkage on drying is large" as the incorrect statement.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct: plastic and easy to work.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Accepted as correct by the key: lime mortar stiffens (sets) readily and hydraulic limes resist moisture.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Correct: good cementing properties suit masonry.</li></ul>
<p>Our first pick was (b), since fat lime hardens slowly and is not moisture-resistant; on review the key's reading (statement (d) on shrinkage is wrong) is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Lime mortar: plastic, retentive, slow-hardening.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-119", year: 2023, no: 119, subject: "bm", topic: "Slaking and hardening", doubt: true,
q: `Consider the following statements :<ol><li>Slaking takes place on adding water in lime.</li><li>Hardening takes place on adding water in cement.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) neither 1 nor 2 (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The key's reasoning: slaking occurs when water is added to <b>quicklime</b> (CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂ + heat), not to "lime" in general (slaked/hydrated lime does not slake); and adding water to cement first causes <b>setting</b> (stiffening), with hardening (strength gain) following over time — so neither statement is accurate as worded.</p>
<ul><li><b>1</b> Treated as incorrect: imprecise — only quicklime slakes.</li><li><b>2</b> Treated as incorrect: water causes setting and then hardening of cement.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). In everyday textbook language, statement 1 ("slaking takes place on adding water to lime") is the standard description, and our answer was (a). The key's (d) rests on a strict reading of the wording.</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: .</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Quicklime + water → slaked lime + heat.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-120", year: 2023, no: 120, subject: "bm", topic: "Principles of mix design", doubt: true,
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct with respect to mix design?`,
opts: ["The mix must be workable so that it can be placed and finished easily.", "The cement is the most costly ingredient in the concrete mix.", "The strength and water tightness increase with the density of the mix.", "Increasing the water content beyond that required for plasticity causes the strength to increase constantly."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The key marks (c) as the incorrect statement.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct: workability for placing and finishing.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Correct: cement is the costliest ingredient, so the economical mix uses the least cement that meets strength and durability.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Marked incorrect by the key.</li></ul>
<div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Statement (d) is plainly wrong — extra water beyond that needed for workability <b>reduces</b> strength (Abrams' w/c law); we chose (d). Statement (c) is the usual textbook principle (a denser mix is stronger and more watertight). The key's (c) is shown, but (d) appears to be the intended incorrect statement.</div>
<ul><li><b>(d)</b> Wrong in fact (see note); the key nevertheless marks (c).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Abrams: strength ∝ 1/(w/c); more water, less strength.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-121", year: 2023, no: 121, subject: "solid", topic: "Poisson's ratio of cast iron",
q: `The value of Poisson's ratio for cast iron is in the range of`,
opts: ["0·09 – 0·19", "0·20 – 0·30", "0·31 – 0·41", "0·42 – 0·52"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·20 – 0·30</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Typical Poisson's ratios: cast iron <b>0·21–0·27</b>; steel 0·27–0·30; aluminium 0·33; copper 0·34; concrete 0·15–0·20; rubber about 0·5 (incompressible).</p><div class="formula">μ = lateral/longitudinal strain</div>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·09–0·19 suits concrete or brittle ceramics.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·31–0·41 suits aluminium and copper.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·42–0·52 approaches rubber and lead.</li></ul>
<p>Theoretical limits for isotropic materials: −1 &lt; ν ≤ 0·5.</p>
<p>Poisson's ratio links E and G: for cast iron (ν ≈ 0·25), G ≈ 0·4E.</p><p>Grey cast iron has a low and variable E because graphite flakes act like small cracks; ductile iron behaves more like steel.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Cast iron ≈ <b>0.21–0.27</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Cast iron ≈ 0·25; steel ≈ 0·3.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-122", year: 2023, no: 122, subject: "solid", topic: "Shear modulus from Poisson's ratio",
q: `A cylindrical bar of 25 mm diameter and 1·20 m length is subjected to a longitudinal strain during a tensile test is four times the lateral strain, what is the value of shear modulus? (Take the modulus of elasticity as 1·2 × 10<sup>5</sup> N/mm<sup>2</sup>)`,
opts: ["5·8 kN/mm<sup>2</sup>", "4·8 kN/mm<sup>2</sup>", "58 kN/mm<sup>2</sup>", "48 kN/mm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 48 kN/mm²</h3><div class="formula">ν = lateral/longitudinal strain = 1/4 ;  G = E/[2(1 + ν)]</div>
<ol class="work"><li>ν = 0·25.</li><li>G = 1·2 × 10⁵/(2 × 1·25) = 48 000 N/mm² = <b>48 kN/mm²</b>.</li><li>Bulk modulus K = E/[3(1 − 2ν)] = 1·2 × 10⁵/1·5 = 80 kN/mm².</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The bar dimensions are not needed.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5·8 is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 4·8 kN/mm² is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 58 kN/mm² uses ν ≈ 0·034.</li></ul>
<p>This value of E (120 GPa) is typical of cast iron or copper rather than steel.</p><p>Check: K = 80 kN/mm² and G = 48 kN/mm² satisfy E = 9KG/(3K + G) = 9 × 80 × 48/288 = 120 kN/mm². ✓</p><p><b>Remember:</b> E = 2G(1 + ν) = 3K(1 − 2ν).</p>` },

{ id: "2023-123", year: 2023, no: 123, subject: "solid", topic: "Stress–strain terms for steel",
q: `Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?`,
opts: ["For structural steel, the rupture strength is higher than the ultimate strength.", "Yield stress is the lowest stress at which extension of the test piece increases without further increase in load.", "The ultimate strength is commonly taken as the maximum stress of the material.", "Ultimate strength is the ratio of maximum load to the original cross sectional area."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> On the engineering stress–strain curve of mild steel, the stress rises to the <b>ultimate</b> (maximum load ÷ original area) and then falls during necking; the nominal <b>rupture</b> (breaking) stress is therefore <b>lower</b> than the ultimate strength. (The true stress at fracture is higher, but the conventional rupture strength is lower.)</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Correct: yield stress — extension increases without increase in load.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Correct: ultimate strength is the maximum (engineering) stress.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Correct: σ<sub>u</sub> = P<sub>max</sub>/A<sub>0</sub>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Engineering curve: ultimate &gt; breaking stress.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-124", year: 2023, no: 124, subject: "solid", topic: "Tensile stress in a bar",
q: `A prismatic bar has a cross section of 25 mm × 50 mm and a length of 2 m. If the bar is subjected to an axial tensile force of 90 kN, the bar is elongated by 1·5 mm. What is the tensile stress?`,
opts: ["3·6 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "7·2 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "36 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "72 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 72 N/mm²</h3><div class="formula">σ = P/A</div>
<ol class="work"><li>A = 25 × 50 = 1250 mm².</li><li>σ = 90 000/1250 = <b>72 N/mm²</b>.</li><li>Strain = 1·5/2000 = 7·5 × 10⁻⁴; E = 72/7·5 × 10⁻⁴ = 96 000 N/mm².</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3·6 is out by factors of 2–20 (unit slips or half the area).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 7·2 is out by factors of 2–20 (unit slips or half the area).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 36 N/mm² is out by factors of 2–20 (unit slips or half the area).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The elongation is not needed for the stress; it gives the modulus.</p>
<p>Strain energy stored = ½ × P × δ = ½ × 90 000 × 1·5 = 67·5 N·m.</p><p>72 N/mm² is well below the yield stress of mild steel (250 N/mm²), so the bar is in the elastic range.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Stress needs only force and area.</p>` },

{ id: "2023-125", year: 2023, no: 125, subject: "solid", topic: "Principal stress",
q: `The normal stress across the principal plane is known as`,
opts: ["Shear stress", "Maximum shear stress", "Principal stress", "Tangential stress"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) principal stress</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A <b>principal plane</b> carries no shear stress; the normal stress on it is a <b>principal stress</b> (maximum or minimum normal stress at the point).</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Shear (tangential) stress on a principal plane is zero.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Shear (tangential) stress on a principal plane is zero.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Maximum shear stress acts on planes at 45° to the principal planes, equal to (σ<sub>1</sub> − σ<sub>2</sub>)/2.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">σ<sub>1,2</sub> = (σ<sub>x</sub>+σ<sub>y</sub>)/2 ± √[((σ<sub>x</sub>−σ<sub>y</sub>)/2)² + τ²]</div>
<p>In 3-D there are three principal stresses on three mutually perpendicular principal planes.</p><p>Mohr's circle shows principal stresses where the circle meets the σ-axis.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Principal plane: τ = 0, σ = extreme.</p>` }
);

