window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2022-101", year: 2022, no: 101, subject: "transport", topic: "Frost heave",
q: `The held water in subgrade soil forms ice crystals at some spots if the freezing temperatures continue for a certain period. These ice crystals grow further in size if there is a continuous supply of water due to capillary action and the depressed temperature continues. This results in raising of portion of the pavement structure known as`,
opts: ["Frost heave", "Frost melting", "Alternate freeze-thaw cycle", "Frost action"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) frost heave</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> When subgrade water freezes and ice lenses keep growing by drawing water up through capillarity, the pavement surface is <b>raised</b> unevenly — <b>frost heave</b>. On thawing, the excess water weakens the subgrade (frost boil), causing break-up of the pavement.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Frost melting (thaw) follows heave and causes loss of strength.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Freeze–thaw cycles are the repeated process, not the raising itself.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Frost action is the general term for all these effects.</li></ul>
<p>Conditions: frost-susceptible soil (silts), freezing temperatures, and a high water table. Remedies: lower the water table, use non-frost-susceptible base materials, insulation layers.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Heave needs cold + water supply + silty soil.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-102", year: 2022, no: 102, subject: "transport", topic: "CBR test (IRC 37-1970)",
q: `Consider the following statements related to IRC recommendations for the CBR method of design (IRC : 37-1970) :<ol><li>The CBR tests should be performed on remoulded soils on the field.</li><li>For the design of new roads, the subgrade soil sample should be compacted at OMC to proctor density.</li><li>In new constructions, the CBR test samples may be soaked in water for four days period before testing.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> False: IRC recommends CBR tests on remoulded soil in the <b>laboratory</b> (in-situ tests may be used for existing roads), not "on remoulded soils on the field".</li><li><b>2</b> True: for new roads the specimens are compacted at OMC to standard Proctor density (the density expected in the field).</li><li><b>3</b> True: samples are soaked for 4 days before testing to simulate the worst moisture condition.</li></ul>
<div class="formula">CBR = load at 2·5 or 5 mm penetration / standard load × 100</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> IRC 37 has since been revised several times (2001, 2012, 2018); current designs use resilient modulus from CBR and traffic in msa.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Design CBR: soaked, at field density.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-103", year: 2022, no: 103, subject: "transport", topic: "Maintenance of rolling stock",
q: `Which one of the following measures should be taken for maintaining rolling stock?`,
opts: ["The different parts of rolling stock need not be cleaned every day.", "All axles completed service life need not be replaced.", "The parts of rolling stock which get worn out need not be replaced.", "Lubrication of all the reciprocating parts and bearings with a suitable lubricant should be done."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) lubricate reciprocating parts and bearings</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Rolling-stock maintenance includes regular cleaning, inspection, lubrication of all moving/reciprocating parts and bearings, and timely replacement of worn components and axles that have reached the end of their service life.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Wrong: parts must be cleaned regularly.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Wrong: axles at the end of their service life must be replaced (fatigue safety).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Wrong: worn parts must be replaced.</li></ul>
<p>Rolling stock maintenance is scheduled (trip, monthly, periodical overhaul) to ensure safety and reduce track wear.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Each wrong option denies a basic maintenance rule.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-104", year: 2022, no: 104, subject: "transport", topic: "Space-mean speed",
q: `Space-mean speed represents`,
opts: ["the instantaneous speed of a vehicle at a specified section or location", "the effective speed with which a vehicle traverses a particular route between two terminals", "the average speed of vehicles in a certain road length at any time", "the average of instantaneous speeds of observed vehicles at the spot"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) average speed of vehicles over a road length at an instant</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Space-mean speed</b> is the mean speed of all vehicles occupying a length of road at a given instant (as from an aerial photograph). From spot speeds it is the <b>harmonic</b> mean:</p>
<div class="formula">v<sub>s</sub> = n / Σ(1/v<sub>i</sub>) ;  v<sub>t</sub> = v<sub>s</sub> + σ<sub>s</sub>²/v<sub>s</sub></div>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Instantaneous speed at a location is the <b>spot speed</b>.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Effective speed over a route between terminals is the <b>overall (journey) speed</b>.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The arithmetic mean of spot speeds at a point is the <b>time-mean speed</b>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Time-mean ≥ space-mean; q = k × v<sub>s</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-105", year: 2022, no: 105, subject: "transport", topic: "Off-tracking",
q: `A vehicle has a wheel base of 6·5 m. What is the off tracking while negotiating a curved path with a mean radius 32 m?`,
opts: ["1·32 m", "1·15 m", "0·86 m", "0·66 m"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·66 m</h3><div class="formula">Off-tracking = l² / (2R)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>l = 6·5 m; R = 32 m.</li><li>l²/2R = 42·25/64 = <b>0·66 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> On a curve the rear wheels follow a path inside the front wheels; this off-tracking is the basis of mechanical widening (W<sub>m</sub> = n l²/2R for n lanes).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1·32 m is l²/R (double).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1·15 m does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·86 m does not match the working above.</li></ul>
<p>Psychological widening V/(9·5√R) is added to the mechanical widening on roads.</p><p>Longer wheelbases (trailers, buses) need wider carriageways on sharp curves.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Mechanical widening per lane = l²/2R.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-106", year: 2022, no: 106, subject: "transport", topic: "Basis of pavement thickness design", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The Thickness design of pavement mainly depends on`,
opts: ["the pavement materials", "the climatic factors", "the design wheel road", "the subgrade soil"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) the design wheel load (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The thickness of a pavement is chosen so that the stresses from the <b>design wheel load</b> (magnitude, contact pressure, repetitions) are spread over the subgrade to a safe level. Load is the primary input of every design method (CBR curves for given loads, IRC 37 msa charts, Burmister/Westergaard analysis).</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Material properties decide layer moduli and composition.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Climate affects material behaviour (temperature, moisture).</li><li><b>(d)</b> The subgrade strength is the second key input; our first pick was (d), but the thickness is fundamentally governed by the load the pavement must carry for a given subgrade. On review the key is accepted.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Thickness = f(wheel load and repetitions, subgrade CBR).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-107", year: 2022, no: 107, subject: "transport", topic: "Return postcard O–D method", selfCorrected: true,
q: `For traffic surveys using origin and destination studies, the most suitable method in case of heavy traffic and absence of skilled or trained personnel is`,
opts: ["Road side interview method", "License plate method", "Work spot or home interview method", "Return post card method"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) return postcard method (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the <b>return postcard</b> method, pre-addressed questionnaire cards are handed to drivers (at a check-point, without stopping traffic for long) to be filled and posted back. It suits heavy traffic where stopping vehicles for interviews would cause congestion, and it needs few skilled staff; the drawback is a low and biased return rate.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Roadside interviews need trained interviewers and stop traffic.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The licence-plate method — our first pick — needs observers at every station and careful matching, and gives only O–D between stations.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Home interviews are the most complete but need large trained staff.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Postcards: cheap, little staff, poor response.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-108", year: 2022, no: 108, subject: "transport", topic: "Speed of progression",
q: `In traffic control, the speed at which vehicles are presumed to travel through the coordinated signal system is known as`,
opts: ["Signal coordination", "Speed of progression", "Cycle", "Through band"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) speed of progression</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a progressive (green-wave) signal system, offsets between signals are set so that a platoon moving at the design <b>speed of progression</b> meets successive greens.</p>
<div class="formula">Offset = spacing / speed of progression</div>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Signal coordination is the overall scheme.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The cycle is the time for one complete sequence of signal indications.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The through band is the time window on the time–space diagram in which vehicles can pass all signals without stopping.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Green wave: offset = distance ÷ progression speed.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-109", year: 2022, no: 109, subject: "fluid", topic: "Pressure inside a droplet",
q: `The pressure outside the droplet of water of diameter 0·04 mm is 10·32 N/cm<sup>2</sup> (atmospheric pressure). What is the pressure within the droplet if surface tension is 0·0725 N/m of water?`,
opts: ["11·045 N/cm<sup>2</sup>", "10·32 N/cm<sup>2</sup>", "9·45 N/cm<sup>2</sup>", "8·595 N/cm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 11·045 N/cm²</h3><div class="formula">Δp = 4σ/d (droplet, one surface)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Δp = 4 × 0·0725/(0·04 × 10⁻³) = 7250 N/m² = 0·725 N/cm².</li><li>p<sub>inside</sub> = 10·32 + 0·725 = <b>11·045 N/cm²</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> For a soap bubble (two surfaces) the excess pressure is 8σ/d; for a liquid jet, 2σ/d.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> equals the outside pressure (ignores surface tension).</li><li><b>(c)</b> is lower than outside — surface tension always raises the inside pressure.</li><li><b>(d)</b> is lower than outside — surface tension always raises the inside pressure.</li></ul>
<p>Surface tension of water falls with temperature (0·0728 N/m at 20 °C); the excess pressure is significant only for very small drops.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Smaller drops → higher internal pressure.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-110", year: 2022, no: 110, subject: "fluid", topic: "Dynamic from kinematic viscosity",
q: `What is the viscosity of a liquid whose kinematic viscosity is 6 stokes and specific gravity is 1·90?`,
opts: ["1·14 poise", "11·40 poise", "0·114 Ns/m<sup>2</sup>", "11·40 Ns/m<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 11·40 poise</h3><div class="formula">μ = ρ ν</div>
<ol class="work"><li>ρ = 1·90 g/cm³; ν = 6 cm²/s (stokes).</li><li>μ = 1·9 × 6 = <b>11·4 g/(cm·s) = 11·4 poise</b> = 1·14 N·s/m².</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1·14 poise has the right digits but the wrong unit (1·14 is in N·s/m²).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·114 N·s/m² is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 11·40 N·s/m² is ten times too large.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Glycerine-like liquids have viscosities of this order; water is about 0·01 poise at 20 °C.</p><p>Kinematic viscosity matters for Reynolds number; dynamic viscosity for shear stress τ = μ du/dy.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 1 poise = 0·1 N·s/m²; 1 stoke = 10⁻⁴ m²/s.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-111", year: 2022, no: 111, subject: "fluid", topic: "Force on a wall with two liquids",
q: `A 2 m wide tank contains water upto a height of 0·50 m above its base. An immiscible liquid of specific gravity 0·80 is filled on the top of the water upto 1 m height. What is the total pressure force on one side of the tank? (Take density of water as 1000 kg/m<sup>3</sup> and g = 9·81 m/s<sup>2</sup>)`,
opts: ["7·85 kN", "24·52 kN", "10·3 kN", "18·15 kN"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 18·15 kN</h3><ol class="work"><li>Oil layer 1 m thick on top: pressure at interface = 0·8 × 9·81 × 1 = 7·848 kN/m²; force = ½ × 7·848 × 1 × 2 = 7·85 kN.</li><li>Water layer 0·5 m: pressure from 7·848 to 7·848 + 9·81 × 0·5 = 12·753 kN/m²; force = ½(7·848 + 12·753) × 0·5 × 2 = 10·30 kN.</li><li>Total = 7·85 + 10·30 = <b>18·15 kN</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The options (7·85 and 10·3) are the two partial forces, confirming the oil is 1 m deep above the water.</p><div class="formula">F = ½γh² per layer, plus rectangle from upper layer</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 7·85 kN is the oil part only.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 10·3 kN is the water part only.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 24·52 kN treats the whole 1·5 m as water (½ × 9·81 × 1·5² × 2 = 22·1) or similar.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Layered liquids: draw the pressure diagram layer by layer.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-112", year: 2022, no: 112, subject: "fluid", topic: "Head loss in a tapering pipe",
q: `A pipeline of uniformly varying cross section carries an oil of specific gravity 0·87. The diameter of pipe is 200 mm at end A and 500 mm at end B. The end B is located at 4 m higher than A. What is the loss of head in the pipeline if the pressure reading at A is 9·81 N/cm<sup>2</sup> and at B is 5·886 N/cm<sup>2</sup>? (Take discharge is 200 litres/s and g = 9·81 m/s<sup>2</sup>)`,
opts: ["2·609 m", "26·09 cm", "2·109 m", "21·09 cm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2·609 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Head loss in a tapering pipe.</p><ol class="work"><li>V<sub>A</sub> = 0·2/(π/4 × 0·04) = 6·366 m/s → V²/2g = 2·066 m.</li><li>V<sub>B</sub> = 0·2/(π/4 × 0·25) = 1·019 m/s → V²/2g = 0·053 m.</li><li>ρg (oil) = 0·87 × 9810 = 8535 N/m³; p<sub>A</sub>/ρg = 98 100/8535 = 11·494 m; p<sub>B</sub>/ρg = 58 860/8535 = 6·897 m.</li><li>H<sub>A</sub> = 11·494 + 2·066 + 0 = 13·560 m; H<sub>B</sub> = 6·897 + 0·053 + 4 = 10·950 m.</li><li>Loss = 13·560 − 10·950 = <b>2·61 m</b>, flow from A to B.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 26·09 cm is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 21·09 cm is ten times too small.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2·109 m omits part of the velocity head.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Use the oil's specific weight for pressure heads.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-113", year: 2022, no: 113, subject: "fluid", topic: "Laminar boundary layer thickness",
q: `Air at standard conditions flows over a flat plate. The free stream speed is 3 m/s. What is the thickness of boundary layer at a distance of 1 m from the leading edge of the flat plate? (Take the kinematic viscosity of air is 1·5×10<sup>−5</sup> m<sup>2</sup>/s and density is 1·23 kg/m<sup>3</sup>)`,
opts: ["1·80 mm", "1·80 cm", "10·3 cm", "10·3 mm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 10·3 mm</h3><ol class="work"><li>Re<sub>x</sub> = Vx/ν = 3 × 1/1·5 × 10⁻⁵ = 2 × 10⁵ — laminar (&lt; 5 × 10⁵).</li><li>Blasius: δ = 4·64x/√Re<sub>x</sub> (Pohlhausen cubic profile) = 4·64/447·2 = 0·0104 m ≈ <b>10·3 mm</b>.</li><li>(With the exact Blasius 5·0 factor: δ = 11·2 mm.)</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The density is not needed for the thickness; it would be needed for the wall shear stress.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1·80 mm does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1·80 cm does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 10·3 cm is ten times too large.</li></ul>
<p>Transition to turbulence would occur at about x = 5 × 10⁵ × 1·5 × 10⁻⁵/3 = 2·5 m.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Laminar BL: δ ∝ √x; turbulent: δ ∝ x<sup>0·8</sup>.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-114", year: 2022, no: 114, subject: "fluid", topic: "Water hammer with gradual closure",
q: `The water is flowing with a velocity of 1·5 m/s in a pipe of length 2500 m and diameter 500 mm. A valve is provided at the end of the pipe. What is the rise in pressure if the valve is closed in 25 seconds? (Take velocity of pressure wave is 1460 m/s)`,
opts: ["15 N/cm<sup>2</sup>", "1500 N/cm<sup>2</sup>", "150 N/m<sup>2</sup>", "15 kN/m<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 15 N/cm²</h3><ol class="work"><li>Critical time 2L/C = 5000/1460 = 3·42 s &lt; 25 s → gradual closure.</li><li>p = ρLV/t = 1000 × 2500 × 1·5/25 = 150 000 N/m² = <b>15 N/cm²</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Sudden closure would give ρCV = 1000 × 1460 × 1·5 = 219 N/cm² — almost 15 times larger.</p><div class="formula">T &gt; 2L/C ⇒ gradual</div><div class="formula">p = ρLV/T</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 1500 N/cm² is 100 times too large.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 150 N/m² is 1000 and 10 times too small.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 15 kN/m² is 1000 and 10 times too small.</li></ul>
<p>Gradual closure keeps the pressure rise proportional to 1/t, so slower valves greatly reduce surges.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> The same problem appeared in ESE 2026 (Q148).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-115", year: 2022, no: 115, subject: "fluid", topic: "Critical depth",
q: `The depth of flow of a channel section at which the specific energy is minimum, is called`,
opts: ["Critical velocity", "Critical depth", "Critical energy", "Subcritical flow"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) critical depth</h3><div class="formula">E = y + q²/(2gy²) ;  dE/dy = 0 ⇒ y<sub>c</sub> = (q²/g)<sup>1/3</sup>, E<sub>min</sub> = 1·5y<sub>c</sub></div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The depth at which the specific energy is minimum for a given discharge is the <b>critical depth</b>; there the Froude number is 1.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Critical velocity is the velocity at critical depth, V<sub>c</sub> = √(g y<sub>c</sub>).</li><li><b>(c)</b> "Critical energy" is the minimum specific energy value itself, not the depth.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Sub-critical flow occurs at depths <b>greater</b> than critical.</li></ul>
<p>For a rectangular channel with q = 2 m³/s/m, y<sub>c</sub> = (4/9·81)<sup>1/3</sup> = 0·74 m.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Critical depth: E minimum for given Q; Q maximum for given E.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-116", year: 2022, no: 116, subject: "fluid", topic: "Kaplan turbine velocities",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct with respect to Kaplan Turbine?`,
opts: ["The peripheral velocity at inlet is more than peripheral velocity at outlet.", "Velocity of flow at inlet is more than velocity of flow at outlet.", "The peripheral velocity at inlet and outlet are equal.", "Velocity of flow at outlet is more than velocity of flow at inlet."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) peripheral velocities at inlet and outlet are equal</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Kaplan is an <b>axial-flow</b> turbine: water enters and leaves the runner at the same radius, so u<sub>1</sub> = u<sub>2</sub> = πDN/60. The flow (axial) velocity is also equal at inlet and outlet, V<sub>f1</sub> = V<sub>f2</sub>, because the flow area is the same.</p>
<div class="formula">Q = (π/4)(D<sub>o</sub>² − D<sub>b</sub>²) V<sub>f</sub></div>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> True for radial (Francis) turbines, not axial ones.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Flow velocities are equal in an axial machine.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Flow velocities are equal in an axial machine.</li></ul>
<p>Kaplan blades are adjustable, keeping efficiency high over a wide range of loads.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Axial machines: u<sub>1</sub> = u<sub>2</sub>, V<sub>f1</sub> = V<sub>f2</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-117", year: 2022, no: 117, subject: "fluid", topic: "Synchronous speed",
q: `The speed of the generator can be maintained constant only if the speed of the turbine runner is constant equal to the one given by equation N = 60f/p and it is known as`,
opts: ["Synchronous speed", "Asynchronous speed", "Derived speed", "Measured variable speed"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) synchronous speed</h3><div class="formula">N = 120f/P (P = number of poles) = 60f/p (p = pairs of poles)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A turbine coupled to an alternator must run at the <b>synchronous speed</b> so that the generated frequency stays at 50 Hz. Example: 50 Hz, 12 pole pairs → N = 3000/12 = 250 rpm. Governors keep the turbine at this speed when the load changes.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> An asynchronous (induction) generator runs with slip, so it cannot hold a constant synchronous frequency.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 'Derived speed' is not a turbine-governing term.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A variable speed would vary the frequency; the governor keeps the speed constant.</li></ul>
<p>Turbines are chosen with a specific speed that suits the head and the synchronous speed of available generators.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 50 Hz: N = 3000/(pairs of poles).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-118", year: 2022, no: 118, subject: "fluid", topic: "Negative slip of a reciprocating pump", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements related to negative slip of the reciprocating pump :<ol><li>The actual discharge of a reciprocating pump is more than the theoretical discharge.</li><li>The co-efficient of discharge will be more than unity.</li><li>When the suction pipe is short and delivery pipe is long and pump is running at slow speed, then negative slip of the pump occurs.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only (official key)</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: negative slip means actual discharge exceeds the theoretical.</li><li><b>2</b> True: hence C<sub>d</sub> &gt; 1.</li><li><b>3</b> False: negative slip occurs when the suction pipe is <b>long</b>, the delivery pipe <b>short</b> and the pump runs at <b>high</b> speed — the momentum of water in the long suction line keeps the delivery valve open before the delivery stroke begins. The statement reverses all three conditions.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first pick was (d), missing the reversal in statement 3; on review the key is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Negative slip: long suction, short delivery, high speed.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-119", year: 2022, no: 119, subject: "fluid", topic: "Coefficient of discharge of a reciprocating pump",
q: `A single acting reciprocating pump has a plunger of diameter 250 mm and stroke of 350 mm. If the speed of the pump is 60 rpm and it delivers 16·5 lit/sec of water against a suction head of 5 m and a delivery head of 20 m, what is the co-efficient of discharge?`,
opts: ["0·72", "0·79", "0·86", "0·96"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·96</h3><div class="formula">Q<sub>th</sub> = A L N/60  (single acting)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>A = π/4 × 0·25² = 0·04909 m²; L = 0·35 m; N = 60 rpm.</li><li>Q<sub>th</sub> = 0·04909 × 0·35 × 1 = 0·01718 m³/s = 17·18 L/s.</li><li>C<sub>d</sub> = 16·5/17·18 = <b>0·96</b>; slip = 4 %.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Power (theoretical) = ρgQ<sub>th</sub>(H<sub>s</sub> + H<sub>d</sub>) = 9810 × 0·01718 × 25 = 4·2 kW; the heads are needed only for that.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·72 corresponds to larger slips.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·79 corresponds to larger slips.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·86 corresponds to larger slips.</li></ul>
<p>Slip in good pumps is usually 2–10 %, mostly from valve leakage and delayed closing.</p><p>Air vessels on suction and delivery would reduce friction and acceleration heads but not change C<sub>d</sub> much.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> C<sub>d</sub> = Q<sub>actual</sub>/Q<sub>theoretical</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-120", year: 2022, no: 120, subject: "fluid", topic: "Velocity from a stream function",
q: `The stream function is given by the expression ψ = 2x<sup>2</sup> − y<sup>2</sup>. What is the resultant velocity at a point denoted by x = 2 and y = 3?`,
opts: ["10", "12", "15", "18"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 10 units</h3><div class="formula">u = −∂ψ/∂y ;  v = ∂ψ/∂x  (or the opposite signs)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>∂ψ/∂y = −2y = −6 → |u| = 6.</li><li>∂ψ/∂x = 4x = 8 → |v| = 8.</li><li>V = √(6² + 8²) = <b>10</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The sign convention does not change the magnitude. The flow is rotational here (∇²ψ = 4 − 2 ≠ 0), so no velocity potential exists.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 12 does not equal √(36 + 64).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15 does not equal √(36 + 64).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 18 does not equal √(36 + 64).</li></ul>
<p>Streamlines are the curves ψ = constant: 2x² − y² = C, a family of hyperbolas.</p><p>Velocity components are tangent to streamlines, so the resultant is along the hyperbola at (2, 3).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Resultant velocity = √(u² + v²).</p>` },

{ id: "2022-121", year: 2022, no: 121, subject: "hydro", topic: "Optimum number of rain gauges",
q: `A catchment has six rain gauge stations. In a year, the annual rainfall recorded by the gauges are as follows : A 82·6, B 102·9, C 180·3, D 110·3, E 98·8, F 136·7 cm. For a 10% error in the estimation of the mean rainfall, what is the optimum number of stations in the catchment? (Take P̄ = 118·6, σ<sub>m−1</sub> = 35·04 and ε = 10)`,
opts: ["10", "9", "8", "7"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 9</h3><div class="formula">N = (C<sub>v</sub>/ε)² ,  C<sub>v</sub> = 100 σ<sub>m−1</sub>/P̄</div>
<ol class="work"><li>C<sub>v</sub> = 100 × 35·04/118·6 = 29·54 %.</li><li>N = (29·54/10)² = 8·73 → <b>9 stations</b>.</li><li>So 3 more gauges are needed beyond the existing 6.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 10 is more than needed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 8 downs below 8·73.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 7 round downs below 8·73.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> IS 4987 also recommends minimum densities: 1 gauge per 520 km² in plains, 260–390 km² in moderately elevated regions.</p>
<p>The coefficient of variation measures how unevenly rain falls over the catchment.</p><p>The extra stations should be placed where rainfall varies most, e.g. near station C (180 cm).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Always round N up.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-122", year: 2022, no: 122, subject: "hydro", topic: "Transpiration",
q: `Which one of the following is defined as the process by which the water leaves a living plant during photosynthesis, through its leaves, to enter the atmosphere as water vapour?`,
opts: ["Transpiration", "Evapotranspiration", "Stomata", "Evaporation"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) transpiration</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Transpiration</b> is the loss of water from living plants, mainly through the stomata of leaves, as water vapour. It is measured with a phytometer.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Evapotranspiration is the combined loss by evaporation from soil and water surfaces <b>plus</b> transpiration.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Stomata are the leaf pores through which transpiration occurs — not the process.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Evaporation is from open water, soil and wet surfaces.</li></ul>
<p>Transpiration depends on temperature, wind, humidity, sunlight and soil moisture.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> ET = evaporation + transpiration.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-123", year: 2022, no: 123, subject: "hydro", topic: "Canal breach",
q: `Which one of the following is a gap developed in the canal bank due to erosion of some portion of the bank?`,
opts: ["Canal breach", "Sub canal", "Temporary outlet used for irrigation", "Fault"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) canal breach</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A <b>breach</b> is a gap formed in a canal bank by erosion — from overtopping, piping through weak or rat-holed banks, or deliberate cuts by cultivators. It releases water, floods fields and must be closed quickly (ring bunds, sandbags).</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> A sub-canal (distributary) is a channel taking off from a canal.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A temporary outlet is a regulated opening to fields.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A fault is a geological fracture.</li></ul>
<p>Prevention: adequate freeboard, proper compaction, regular patrolling, and filling of rat holes.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Breach: overtopping or piping through banks.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-124", year: 2022, no: 124, subject: "hydro", topic: "Boulder stage of rivers",
q: `Which one of the following stages does the river bed consist of a mixture of boulders, gravels, shingles and alluvial sand deposits created by itself?`,
opts: ["Rocky stage", "Incised river stage", "Boulder river stage", "Rivers in alluvial flood plains stage"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) boulder river stage</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Rivers pass through stages from source to mouth:</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Rocky (incised) stage in the mountains — steep, bed of rock, little deposit.</li><li><b>(b)</b> "Incised" describes rivers cutting deep, narrow valleys (rocky reach).</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Boulder stage</b> in the sub-mountain (foothill) reach — the bed is a mixture of boulders, gravel, shingle and sand that the river itself has deposited; channels are wide and shifting.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Alluvial stage in the plains — fine sand and silt, meandering channels, then the delta.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Hill → boulder → alluvial → tidal/delta.</p>` },

{ id: "2022-125", year: 2022, no: 125, subject: "hydro", topic: "Storage capacity of reservoirs",
q: `Which of the following are the only two factors which govern the storage capacity of the reservoir?`,
opts: ["Inflow to reservoir and the outflow from the reservoir", "Inflow and catchment area", "Catchment area and outflow", "Height of reservoir and catchment area"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) inflow and outflow</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The required storage of a reservoir is fixed by the time pattern of <b>inflow</b> (stream flow) and <b>outflow</b> (demand plus losses): storage must fill the gap between them during the critical dry period (mass curve, sequent-peak methods).</p>
<div class="formula">Storage needed = max cumulative (demand − inflow)</div>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Catchment area affects inflow but is not itself the controlling pair.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Catchment area affects inflow but is not itself the controlling pair.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Height of the dam fixes the <b>available</b> capacity at a site, not the required capacity.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Storage = buffer between supply (inflow) and demand (outflow).</p>` }
);

