// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A).
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2021-26", year: 2021, no: 26, subject: "fluid", topic: "Pitot tube",
q: `Which one of the following is a device used for measuring the velocity of flowing water in pipes or open channels?`,
opts: ["Pitot tube", "Piezometer", "Venturimeter", "Venturi tube"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Pitot tube</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A <b>Pitot tube</b> is a bent tube facing the flow; water rises in it to the stagnation head. The rise above the static level gives the velocity head, so V = C√(2gΔh) with C ≈ 0·98–1·0. It measures the <b>local (point) velocity</b> in pipes and open channels.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> A piezometer measures static pressure head only.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A venturimeter measures discharge, not point velocity.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A venturi tube measures discharge, not point velocity.</li></ul>
<p>A Pitot-static tube combines the stagnation and static openings in one probe; traversing it across a pipe gives the velocity distribution and, by integration, the discharge. In open channels the current meter is the usual alternative for velocity.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Pitot → velocity at a point; venturi/orifice → discharge.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-27", year: 2021, no: 27, subject: "env", topic: "Nitrification",
q: `Which one of the following is the process in which ammonia is oxidised to nitrites and then to nitrates by aerobic bacteria?`,
opts: ["Nitrification", "Denitrification", "Adsorption", "Regeneration"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) nitrification</h3><div class="formula">NH₄⁺ → NO₂⁻ (Nitrosomonas) → NO₃⁻ (Nitrobacter)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Nitrification</b> is the two-step aerobic oxidation of ammonia by autotrophic bacteria; it consumes about 4·57 g O₂ per g of ammonia-N and some alkalinity. It occurs in extended aeration plants, trickling filters and in streams.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Denitrification is the anoxic reduction of nitrate to nitrogen gas by heterotrophic bacteria using organic carbon.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Adsorption is a physical surface process (e.g. activated carbon).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Regeneration refers to restoring adsorbents or ion-exchange resins.</li></ul>
<p>Nitrification followed by denitrification is the standard biological nitrogen-removal route in wastewater treatment.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Nitrification: aerobic, ammonia → nitrate; denitrification: anoxic, nitrate → N₂.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-28", year: 2021, no: 28, subject: "env", topic: "Dosing (holding) tank",
q: `A tank into which raw or partly treated sewage is collected, left to stay, and discharged at such a rate as may be necessary for subsequent treatment, is called`,
opts: ["Dosing tank", "Sedimentation tank", "Skimming tank", "Settling tank"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) dosing tank</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A <b>dosing tank</b> stores raw or partly treated sewage and releases it intermittently (often by a siphon) at the rate required by the next unit — for example, to dose a trickling filter or a sand filter at controlled intervals.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> A sedimentation tank removes settleable solids.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A settling tank removes settleable solids.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A skimming tank removes oil, grease and floating matter.</li></ul>
<p>Dosing tanks allow intermittent loading, which lets filter media rest and re-aerate between doses and avoids continuous flooding of the bed.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Dosing tank: store and release at the rate needed downstream.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-29", year: 2021, no: 29, subject: "env", topic: "Elutriation of sludge",
q: `In the context of sludge conditioning, Elutriation is synonymous to`,
opts: ["Washing", "Heating", "Compacting", "Filtering"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) washing</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Elutriation</b> is washing digested sludge with water (or treated effluent) to remove dissolved alkalinity (bicarbonates and ammonia) before chemical conditioning. The alkalinity would otherwise consume a large dose of coagulant (ferric chloride), so elutriation cuts chemical costs.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Heating (heat treatment) is another conditioning method that breaks down the gel structure of sludge.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Compacting is not a conditioning process.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Filtering (vacuum, pressure, belt filters) is dewatering after conditioning.</li></ul>
<p>Conditioning prepares sludge for dewatering; the main methods are chemical (lime, FeCl₃, polymers), elutriation, heat treatment and freezing.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Elutriation = washing out alkalinity before chemical conditioning.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-30", year: 2021, no: 30, subject: "env", topic: "Sludge thickening",
q: `Sludge thickening is commonly achieved by the following methods :<ol><li>Gravity thickening</li><li>Air flotation</li><li>Centrifugation</li></ol>Which of the above methods are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> Gravity thickening: sludge settles and compacts in a circular tank with slow-moving pickets; suits primary sludge (raises solids from about 1–2 % to 5–10 %).</li><li><b>2</b> Dissolved-air flotation: fine air bubbles float the light activated sludge to the surface, where it is skimmed (to about 4 %).</li><li><b>3</b> Centrifugation: centrifugal force separates solids from water quickly in a small space.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Thickening reduces the volume of sludge before digestion or dewatering, saving tank space and energy; halving the moisture from 98 % to 96 % halves the sludge volume.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Thickening: gravity, flotation, centrifuge, gravity-belt.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-31", year: 2021, no: 31, subject: "env", topic: "Coagulation",
q: `Which one of the following is the process whereby chemicals are added to a wastewater resulting in a reduction of the forces tending to keep suspended particles apart?`,
opts: ["Coagulation", "Flocculation", "Clarification", "Sedimentation"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) coagulation</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Coagulation</b> is the addition of chemicals (alum, ferric salts, polymers) that neutralise the surface charges of colloids and compress their electrical double layer, removing the repulsive forces that keep them apart (destabilisation). It needs rapid mixing.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Flocculation is the following slow mixing that brings the destabilised particles together into larger flocs.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Clarification is the overall removal of turbidity (coagulation + flocculation + settling).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Sedimentation is gravity settling of the flocs.</li></ul>
<p>Optimum coagulant dose is found by the jar test.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Coagulate (destabilise) → flocculate (agglomerate) → settle.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-32", year: 2021, no: 32, subject: "env", topic: "Detritus tank",
q: `Which one of the following is a grit-removal unit which also removes silt as well as some organic matter along with grit?`,
opts: ["Detritus Tank", "Skimming Tank", "Detention Tank", "Suspension Tank"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) detritus tank</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A <b>detritus tank</b> is a grit chamber with a longer detention time (3–4 minutes) and lower velocity than a normal grit channel, so it removes finer silt and some organic matter along with the grit. The deposit (detritus) therefore needs washing to remove putrescible organics before disposal.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> A skimming tank removes floating oil and grease.</li><li><b>(c)</b> "Detention tank" is a general term for a storage tank.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Suspension tank" is not a standard treatment unit.</li></ul>
<p>A normal grit chamber is designed for a horizontal velocity of about 0·3 m/s and detention of 30–60 s to remove only inorganic grit.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Detritus tank = slow grit chamber removing silt and some organics too.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-33", year: 2021, no: 33, subject: "env", topic: "Volatile solids",
q: `The domestic sewage of a town was tested for total solids and the following results were obtained : Weight of sample of sewage = 1000 gm; Weight of solids after evaporation of liquid = 0·952 gm; Weight of dry residue after ignition = 0·516 gm. What is the value of volatile solids?`,
opts: ["952 ppm", "516 ppm", "436 ppm", "694 ppm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 436 ppm</h3><ol class="work"><li>Total solids = 0·952 g in 1000 g = 952 ppm.</li><li>Fixed solids (after ignition at 550 °C) = 0·516 g → 516 ppm.</li><li>Volatile solids = 952 − 516 = <b>436 ppm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Volatile solids approximate the organic matter; here about 46 % of the solids are organic, typical of domestic sewage.</p><div class="formula">VS = TS − FS</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 952 ppm is the total solids.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 516 ppm is the fixed (inorganic) solids.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 694 ppm does not match the working above.</li></ul>
<p>Volatile solids are burnt off at 550 °C; the BOD and the sludge digestion design are related to the volatile fraction, since only organic matter decomposes.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> TS = VS + FS.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-34", year: 2021, no: 34, subject: "env", topic: "Albuminoid nitrogen",
q: `The quantity of nitrogen present in wastewater before the decomposition of organic matter has started, is indicated by`,
opts: ["Albuminoid Nitrogen", "Free Ammonia", "Organic Nitrogen", "Nitrate Nitrogen"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) albuminoid nitrogen</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Nitrogen in sewage indicates its stage of decomposition:</p>
<ul><li><b>Albuminoid (organic) nitrogen</b> — nitrogen still in undecomposed organic matter, measured by boiling with alkaline KMnO₄; it shows the quantity present <b>before</b> decomposition has started.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Free ammonia shows that decomposition has <b>started</b> (fresh pollution).</li><li><b>(c)</b> "Organic nitrogen" in the Kjeldahl sense is total organic-bound N; the textbook term for "before decomposition" is albuminoid nitrogen.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Nitrate nitrogen indicates fully oxidised (old) pollution.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Albuminoid → ammonia → nitrite → nitrate (fresh → old).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-35", year: 2021, no: 35, subject: "hydro", topic: "Wilting point",
q: `Which one of the following is that (low) water content of the soil at which plants can no longer extract sufficient water for their growth?`,
opts: ["Wilting point", "Tail water", "Irrigating head", "Capillary water"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) wilting point</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The <b>permanent wilting point</b> is the moisture content at which plants can no longer extract water (soil suction about 15 bar) and wilt permanently unless water is added. Available water = field capacity − wilting point.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Tail water is water downstream of a structure or the runoff from the end of a field.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Irrigating head is the flow rate delivered to the field.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Capillary water is held by surface tension and is the water available to plants (above the wilting point).</li></ul>
<p>Irrigation is usually applied when about half of the available water has been used, well before the wilting point.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Available water = FC − PWP.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-36", year: 2021, no: 36, subject: "env", topic: "Activated carbon",
q: `Which one of the following is the advantage of using activated carbon for water treatment?`,
opts: ["When used in powdered form after coagulation, it does not aid in coagulation.", "It increases the chlorine demand of treated water.", "It removes organic matter present in water.", "Its overdose is harmful."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) it removes organic matter</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Activated carbon has a huge internal surface (500–1500 m²/g) that adsorbs dissolved organic compounds causing taste, odour and colour, as well as pesticides and THM precursors.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Powdered carbon actually <b>aids</b> coagulation (it adds weight to flocs); "does not aid" is not an advantage.</li><li><b>(b)</b> It <b>reduces</b> chlorine demand by removing organics.</li><li><b>(d)</b> An overdose is harmless (it is removed by settling and filtration) — saying it is harmful is wrong and not an advantage.</li></ul>
<p>It is used as powdered carbon dosed before filters, or as granular carbon in adsorbers that are periodically regenerated.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Activated carbon: taste, odour, colour, organics.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-37", year: 2021, no: 37, subject: "geo", topic: "Water content after partial drying",
q: `A soil has bulk density of 20·1 kN/m<sup>3</sup> and water content 15%. What is the water content if the soil partially dries to a density of 19·4 kN/m<sup>3</sup> and the void ratio remains unchanged?`,
opts: ["10·86%", "10·76%", "10·68%", "10·66%"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 10·86 % (nearest)</h3><div class="formula">γ<sub>d</sub> = γ/(1 + w)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>γ<sub>d</sub> = 20·1/1·15 = 17·48 kN/m³ — unchanged since the void ratio (and solids) are unchanged.</li><li>New w = 19·4/17·48 − 1 = 0·110 → about 11·0 %.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The computed value (about 11·0 %) is closest to option (a) 10·86 %; the small difference comes from rounding of γ<sub>d</sub> in the source solution.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 10·76 is further from the computed value.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 10·68 is further from the computed value.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10·66 % is further from the computed value.</li></ul>
<p>Drying at constant void ratio lowers only the water in the pores; the soil skeleton, and hence γ<sub>d</sub>, does not change. Degree of saturation falls from wG/e accordingly.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> With constant void ratio, dry density is constant.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-38", year: 2021, no: 38, subject: "geo", topic: "Liquidity index",
q: `A fine grained soil is found to have a liquid limit of 90% and a plasticity index of 50. The natural water content is 28%. What is the liquidity index?`,
opts: ["−0·34", "−0·14", "−0·24", "−2·40"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) −0·24</h3><div class="formula">I<sub>L</sub> = (w − PL)/PI ,  PL = LL − PI</div>
<ol class="work"><li>PL = 90 − 50 = 40 %.</li><li>I<sub>L</sub> = (28 − 40)/50 = <b>−0·24</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A negative liquidity index means the natural water content is below the plastic limit: the soil is in a hard, semi-solid state (probably desiccated or overconsolidated). Consistency index I<sub>c</sub> = 1 − I<sub>L</sub> = 1·24.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> −0·34 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(b)</b> −0·14 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> −2·40 does not match the working above.</li></ul>
<p>The plasticity index of 50 indicates a highly plastic clay (CH), likely containing montmorillonite; such soils swell strongly when wetted from this dry state.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> I<sub>L</sub> &lt; 0 hard; 0–1 plastic; &gt; 1 liquid (sensitive).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-39", year: 2021, no: 39, subject: "geo", topic: "Boussinesq stress under a point load",
q: `A concentrated load of 2000 kN is applied at the ground surface. What is the vertical stress at a point 6 m directly below the load?`,
opts: ["16·42 kN/m<sup>2</sup>", "26·53 kN/m<sup>2</sup>", "36·12 kN/m<sup>2</sup>", "40·51 kN/m<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 26·53 kN/m²</h3><div class="formula">σ<sub>z</sub> = (3Q/2πz²)[1/(1 + (r/z)²)]<sup>5/2</sup> ; on the axis (r = 0): σ<sub>z</sub> = 0·4775 Q/z²</div>
<ol class="work"><li>σ<sub>z</sub> = 0·4775 × 2000/36 = <b>26·53 kN/m²</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Westergaard's solution (for layered soils) would give 0·3183Q/z² = 17·7 kN/m² on the axis.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 16·42 is close to Westergaard's value, not Boussinesq.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 36·12 uses wrong coefficients.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 40·51 kN/m² uses wrong coefficients.</li></ul>
<p>The stress falls off as 1/z²: at 12 m depth it would be only a quarter, 6·6 kN/m². Boussinesq assumes an elastic, homogeneous, isotropic, semi-infinite medium.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Below a point load: σ<sub>z</sub> = 0·4775Q/z² (Boussinesq), 0·3183Q/z² (Westergaard).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-40", year: 2021, no: 40, subject: "geo", topic: "Local shear failure", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following is a characteristic of local shear failure?`,
opts: ["Failure pattern is not clearly defined.", "Failure surfaces reach ground surfaces.", "There is no bulging of soil around the footing.", "Failure is not sudden and there is no tilting of footing."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) failure is not sudden and no tilting (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In <b>local shear failure</b> (loose sands, soft clays) the slip surfaces develop only beneath the footing and do not reach the ground surface clearly; large settlements occur, failure is gradual (no sudden collapse), there is only slight bulging, and the footing does not tilt.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> A poorly defined pattern is also typical, and was our first pick; the key chooses (d), the most complete characteristic. On review the key is accepted.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Failure surfaces reaching the ground with heave is <b>general</b> shear failure (dense sand, stiff clay).</li><li><b>(c)</b> Some bulging does occur in local shear; none is not correct.</li></ul>
<p>Terzaghi accounts for local shear by reducing c and tan ϕ to 2/3 of their values. Punching shear is the third mode (very loose soil, deep footings).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> General: sudden, tilting, heave; local: gradual, partial slip surfaces.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-41", year: 2021, no: 41, subject: "geo", topic: "Void ratio from mass and volume",
q: `A sample of silty clay has a volume of 14·88 cm<sup>3</sup>, a total mass of 28·81 gm, a dry mass of 24·83 gm and a specific gravity of solids 2·7. What is the void ratio?`,
opts: ["0·412", "0·521", "0·618", "0·663"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·618</h3><ol class="work"><li>Volume of solids V<sub>s</sub> = 24·83/2·7 = 9·196 cm³.</li><li>Volume of voids V<sub>v</sub> = 14·88 − 9·196 = 5·684 cm³.</li><li>e = V<sub>v</sub>/V<sub>s</sub> = <b>0·618</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other properties: w = (28·81 − 24·83)/24·83 = 16 %; degree of saturation S = wG/e = 0·16 × 2·7/0·618 = 70 %.</p><div class="formula">V_s = M_s/(G ρ_w)</div><div class="formula">e = V_v/V_s</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·412 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·521 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·663 does not match the working above.</li></ul>
<p>Porosity n = e/(1 + e) = 0·382; bulk density = 28·81/14·88 = 1·94 g/cm³.</p><p>Silty clays typically have void ratios of 0·5–1·0 and specific gravities of 2·65–2·75.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> e = V<sub>v</sub>/V<sub>s</sub>; get V<sub>s</sub> from dry mass/(Gρ<sub>w</sub>).</p>` },

{ id: "2021-42", year: 2021, no: 42, subject: "geo", topic: "Constant-head permeability test",
q: `A constant head permeability test is carried out on a cylindrical sample of sand 10 cm diameter and 15 cm height. 160 cm<sup>3</sup> of water is collected in 1·75 minutes, under a head of 30 cm. What is the coefficient of permeability in m/year?`,
opts: ["1257 m/year", "2111 m/year", "3060 m/year", "3332 m/year"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3060 m/year</h3><div class="formula">k = QL/(Aht)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>A = π/4 × 10² = 78·54 cm²; t = 105 s.</li><li>k = 160 × 15/(78·54 × 30 × 105) = 9·70 × 10⁻⁴ cm/s.</li><li>In m/year: 9·70 × 10⁻⁶ m/s × 3·1536 × 10⁷ s = <b>3060 m/year</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> k ≈ 10⁻³ cm/s is typical of fine to medium sand.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1257 uses wrong time or area.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2111 uses wrong time or area.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3332 m/year uses wrong time or area.</li></ul>
<p>The constant-head test is suitable for coarse soils (k &gt; 10⁻³ cm/s); for finer soils the falling-head test is used.</p><p>Water temperature should be noted, as k is reported at 27 °C after viscosity correction.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 1 cm/s ≈ 3·15 × 10⁵ m/year.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-43", year: 2021, no: 43, subject: "geo", topic: "Assumptions of Rankine's theory", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following is the correct assumption of Rankine's theory?`,
opts: ["The soil mass is infinite.", "The soil mass is non homogeneous.", "The soil mass is cohesive.", "The ground surface is a plane which may be horizontal or inclined."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) the ground surface is a plane, horizontal or inclined (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Rankine's earth pressure theory assumes: a <b>semi-infinite</b>, homogeneous, dry, cohesionless soil mass; a <b>plane ground surface</b> (horizontal or inclined); a vertical, smooth back of wall; and a state of plastic equilibrium throughout.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> The soil is <b>semi-infinite</b> (bounded by the ground surface), not infinite; our first pick, but strictly incorrect wording. On review the key is accepted.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The soil is assumed homogeneous.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The basic theory is for cohesionless soil (Bell extended it to c–ϕ soils).</li></ul>
<div class="formula">K<sub>a</sub> = (1 − sin ϕ)/(1 + sin ϕ) for horizontal backfill</div>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Rankine: smooth wall, semi-infinite soil, plane surface.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-44", year: 2021, no: 44, subject: "geo", topic: "Earth pressure at rest",
q: `If a retaining wall 5 m high is restrained from yielding, what is the at-rest earth pressure per meter length of wall? (Consider the backfill is cohesionless soil having ϕ = 30° and γ = 18 kN/m<sup>3</sup>)`,
opts: ["108 kN/m", "112·5 kN/m", "115 kN/m", "124 kN/m"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 112·5 kN/m</h3><div class="formula">K<sub>0</sub> = 1 − sin ϕ (Jaky) ;  P<sub>0</sub> = ½ K<sub>0</sub> γ H²</div>
<ol class="work"><li>K<sub>0</sub> = 1 − 0·5 = 0·5.</li><li>P<sub>0</sub> = ½ × 0·5 × 18 × 25 = <b>112·5 kN/m</b>, acting at H/3 = 1·67 m above the base.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> For comparison the active thrust would be ½ × (1/3) × 18 × 25 = 75 kN/m; walls that cannot yield (basement walls, bridge abutments with fixed tops) must be designed for the at-rest value.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 108 uses other K<sub>0</sub> values.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 115 uses other K<sub>0</sub> values.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 124 kN/m uses other K<sub>0</sub> values.</li></ul>
<p>For overconsolidated backfill, K<sub>0</sub> is larger (K<sub>0,OC</sub> = K<sub>0,NC</sub>√OCR roughly), so the thrust would be greater.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> K<sub>a</sub> &lt; K<sub>0</sub> &lt; K<sub>p</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-45", year: 2021, no: 45, subject: "geo", topic: "Construction with geotextiles",
q: `Consider the following steps related to construction with the use of geotextiles :<ol><li>Start with an adequate working surface and staging area.</li><li>Lay a geotextile sheet of proper width on the ground surface.</li><li>Construction equipment must work from the soil backfill and be kept off the unprotected geotextile.</li></ol>Which of the above steps are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: prepare an adequate working surface (clear stumps and sharp objects) and a staging area for rolls.</li><li><b>2</b> True: unroll the geotextile of proper width, with specified overlaps or seams, directly on the ground.</li><li><b>3</b> True: spread the fill by end-dumping and pushing it forward; equipment must run on the fill, never directly on the bare fabric, to avoid tearing and displacement.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Further steps: place the first lift thick enough (about 150–300 mm) to protect the fabric, then compact carefully; repair any damaged area with a patch overlapping the tear.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Geotextile installation: prepare, unroll, cover — never drive on bare fabric.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-46", year: 2021, no: 46, subject: "geo", topic: "Bulk unit weight",
q: `The void ratio of a clay sample is 0·5 and the degree of saturation is 70%. What is the bulk unit weight of the soil? (Assume G = 2·7)`,
opts: ["10·46 kN/m<sup>3</sup>", "14·32 kN/m<sup>3</sup>", "17·77 kN/m<sup>3</sup>", "19·95 kN/m<sup>3</sup>"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 19·95 kN/m³</h3><div class="formula">γ = (G + S e) γ<sub>w</sub>/(1 + e)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>G + Se = 2·7 + 0·7 × 0·5 = 3·05.</li><li>γ = 3·05 × 9·81/1·5 = <b>19·95 kN/m³</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Dry unit weight = 2·7 × 9·81/1·5 = 17·66 kN/m³; saturated = (2·7 + 0·5) × 9·81/1·5 = 20·93 kN/m³. Water content w = Se/G = 13 %.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 10·46 is close to the submerged weight.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 14·32 uses wrong relationships (17·77 ≈ dry unit weight).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 17·77 kN/m³ uses wrong relationships (17·77 ≈ dry unit weight).</li></ul>
<p>The submerged unit weight is γ<sub>sat</sub> − γ<sub>w</sub> = 20·93 − 9·81 = 11·1 kN/m³.</p><p>Water content can be found from Se = wG: w = 0·35/2·7 = 0·13.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> γ<sub>d</sub> &lt; γ<sub>bulk</sub> &lt; γ<sub>sat</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-47", year: 2021, no: 47, subject: "geo", topic: "Coefficient of volume change",
q: `What is the coefficient of volume change (using change in void ratio method) for pressure range 100 kN/m<sup>2</sup> to 200 kN/m<sup>2</sup>? (Consider σ′<sub>0</sub> = 100 kN/m<sup>2</sup>, e<sub>0</sub> = 1·121, σ′ = 200 kN/m<sup>2</sup>, e = 0·964, Δσ′ = 100 kN/m<sup>2</sup> and Δe = −0·157)`,
opts: ["0·25 m<sup>2</sup>/MN", "0·48 m<sup>2</sup>/MN", "0·69 m<sup>2</sup>/MN", "0·74 m<sup>2</sup>/MN"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·74 m²/MN</h3><div class="formula">m<sub>v</sub> = (Δe/Δσ′) / (1 + e<sub>0</sub>) = a<sub>v</sub>/(1 + e<sub>0</sub>)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>a<sub>v</sub> = 0·157/100 = 1·57 × 10⁻³ m²/kN.</li><li>m<sub>v</sub> = 1·57 × 10⁻³/2·121 = 7·40 × 10⁻⁴ m²/kN = <b>0·74 m²/MN</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> This indicates a highly compressible clay (m<sub>v</sub> &gt; 0·3 m²/MN). Compression index C<sub>c</sub> = 0·157/log 2 = 0·52.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·25 uses the wrong initial void ratio or Δe.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·48 uses the wrong initial void ratio or Δe.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·69 m²/MN uses the wrong initial void ratio or Δe.</li></ul>
<p>Settlement of a 4 m layer under Δσ′ = 100 kPa would be 0·74 × 10⁻³ × 100 × 4 = 0·30 m.</p><p>m<sub>v</sub> is not a constant: it decreases as the effective stress increases, so it must be taken for the actual stress range.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Consolidation settlement = m<sub>v</sub> Δσ′ H.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-48", year: 2021, no: 48, subject: "geo", topic: "Seepage problems in earth dams",
q: `Which one of the following problems is required to be studied in the design of earth dams?`,
opts: ["The prediction of the position of the line of seepage in the longitudinal section.", "The computation of seepage loss.", "The seepage line should cut the down-stream slope.", "The seepage loss through the dam should be maximum."], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) computation of seepage loss</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Seepage studies for earth dams include: locating the phreatic line in the <b>cross</b>-section (Casagrande/Kozeny parabola), computing the <b>seepage loss</b> (q = kHN<sub>f</sub>/N<sub>d</sub>), checking pore pressures for slope stability, and ensuring safety against piping at the exit.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> The phreatic line is studied in the <b>cross-section</b>, not the longitudinal section.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The seepage line must <b>not</b> cut the downstream slope; a toe filter or chimney drain keeps it inside the dam.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Seepage should be <b>minimum</b>, not maximum.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Earth dam seepage: phreatic line, discharge, exit gradient, filters.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-49", year: 2021, no: 49, subject: "survey", topic: "Instruments for setting out right angles",
q: `Which one of the following is <i>not</i> an instrument for setting out right angles?`,
opts: ["Cross staff", "Site square", "Prism square", "Optical staff"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) optical staff</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Instruments used in chain surveying for setting out right angles (offsets): the <b>cross staff</b> (open, French, adjustable), the <b>optical square</b> (two mirrors at 45°), the <b>prism square</b> (a prism replacing the mirrors) and the <b>site square</b> (two telescopes at right angles, for building setting-out).</p>
<p>"Optical staff" is not such an instrument (staffs are used for levelling or stadia readings).</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> A cross staff is a standard right-angle instrument.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A site square is a standard right-angle instrument.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A prism square is a standard right-angle instrument.</li></ul>
<p>Right angles can also be set out with a tape alone using the 3-4-5 method.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Optical square and prism square: reflection principle, angle 90°.</p>` },

{ id: "2021-50", year: 2021, no: 50, subject: "survey", topic: "Prismatic compass",
q: `Which one of the following is correct for Prismatic Compass?`,
opts: ["The graduated ring rotates with line of sight.", "Instrument cannot be used without tripod.", "The graduations are engraved inverted.", "The readings can directly be taken by seeing through the top of the glass."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) graduations are engraved inverted</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a <b>prismatic compass</b> the graduated ring is attached to the needle and stays fixed in the magnetic meridian; readings are taken through a prism, which inverts the image, so the graduations are engraved <b>inverted</b> (and the zero is at the south end). It reads whole-circle bearings 0°–360°.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> The ring does <b>not</b> rotate with the line of sight (that is the surveyor's compass, where the ring is fixed to the box).</li><li><b>(b)</b> It can be held in the hand; a tripod is optional.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Readings are taken through the prism, not by looking down through the top glass (that is the surveyor's compass).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Prismatic: WCB, ring fixed to needle, inverted graduations, read via prism.</p>` }
);

