window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2020-1", year: 2020, no: 1, subject: "bm", topic: "Efflorescence rating of bricks",
q: `When the deposit of efflorescence is more than 10% but less than 50% of the exposed area of the brick, the presence of efflorescence is`,
opts: ["Moderate", "Slight", "Heavy", "Serious"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) moderate</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 3495 (Part 3) grades efflorescence after the brick is soaked and dried by the area covered with salt deposit:</p>
<table><tr><th>Rating</th><th>Deposit</th></tr><tr><td>Nil</td><td>imperceptible</td></tr><tr><td>Slight</td><td>up to 10 % of exposed area</td></tr><tr><td><b>Moderate</b></td><td>10–50 %, no flaking</td></tr><tr><td>Heavy</td><td>&gt; 50 %, no flaking</td></tr><tr><td>Serious</td><td>heavy deposit with powdering/flaking of the surface</td></tr></table>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Slight efflorescence covers up to 10 % of the exposed area.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Heavy efflorescence covers more than 50 % of the area, without flaking.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Serious efflorescence is a heavy deposit together with powdering or flaking of the surface.</li></ul>
<p>Efflorescence comes from soluble salts (sulphates of Na, K, Mg) in the clay or water; first-class bricks should show no more than slight efflorescence.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Slight ≤ 10 %, moderate 10–50 %, heavy &gt; 50 %.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-2", year: 2020, no: 2, subject: "bm", topic: "Mohs scale",
q: `Mohs scale is used for stones to determine`,
opts: ["Flakiness index", "Durability", "Strength", "Hardness"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) hardness</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Mohs scale</b> ranks the scratch hardness of minerals from 1 (talc) to 10 (diamond): talc, gypsum, calcite, fluorite, apatite, orthoclase, quartz, topaz, corundum, diamond. A mineral scratches all those below it. A good building stone should have a hardness of at least about 6–7 for surfaces subject to wear.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Flakiness index is a shape test on aggregates with a thickness gauge.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Durability is checked by soundness and weathering tests.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Strength is measured by crushing tests on cubes or cylinders.</li></ul>
<p>For aggregates, hardness is measured as resistance to abrasion (Los Angeles test); the Dorry test gives a coefficient of hardness for stones.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Mohs: scratch hardness, 1 talc to 10 diamond.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-3", year: 2020, no: 3, subject: "bm", topic: "Uses of sulphate resisting cement",
q: `Which of the following conditions are recommended for using sulphate resisting cement?<ol><li>Concrete to be used in foundation and basement, where soil is not infested with sulphates</li><li>Concrete used for fabrication of pipes which are likely to be buried in marshy region or sulphate bearing soils</li><li>Concrete to be used in the construction of sewage treatment works</li></ol>`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2 and 3 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> Not a condition: where the soil is <b>not</b> infested with sulphates, ordinary cement is adequate; SRC is for sulphate-bearing soils.</li><li><b>2</b> Recommended: pipes buried in marshy or sulphate-bearing soils.</li><li><b>3</b> Recommended: sewage treatment works, where sulphates and H₂S-derived sulphuric acid attack concrete.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Sulphate-resisting cement (IS 12330) limits C₃A to 5 % (and 2C₃A + C₄AF ≤ 25 %), so little ettringite forms when sulphates penetrate. It is also used in marine works, canal linings and culverts in alkaline soils.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> SRC: low C₃A.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-4", year: 2020, no: 4, subject: "bm", topic: "Calcium chloride cement",
q: `Which one of the following cements is a <i>deliquescent</i>?`,
opts: ["Quick setting Portland cement", "White and Coloured cement", "Calcium Chloride cement", "Water Repellent cement"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) calcium chloride cement</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Calcium chloride absorbs moisture from the air until it dissolves — it is <b>deliquescent</b>. Cement with added CaCl₂ (extra rapid hardening cement, up to 2 %) therefore absorbs moisture during storage and must be kept dry and used quickly (usually within a month).</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Quick-setting cement contains less gypsum and finely ground clinker, but is not deliquescent.</li><li><b>(b)</b> White and coloured cements are ordinary cements with low iron or pigments.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Water-repellent (hydrophobic) cement is ground with water-repellent agents and resists moisture.</li></ul>
<p>CaCl₂ also accelerates setting and hardening, but it promotes corrosion of reinforcement, so it is not used in RCC or prestressed concrete.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> CaCl₂: accelerator, deliquescent, corrosive to steel.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-5", year: 2020, no: 5, subject: "bm", topic: "Cement content from w/c",
q: `Consider the following data for concrete with mild exposure : Water-cement ratio = 0·50; Water = 191·6 litre. The required cement content will be`,
opts: ["561 kg/m<sup>3</sup>", "472 kg/m<sup>3</sup>", "383 kg/m<sup>3</sup>", "294 kg/m<sup>3</sup>"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 383 kg/m³</h3><div class="formula">C = W / (w/c)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>C = 191·6/0·50 = <b>383·2 kg/m³</b>.</li><li>Check against IS 456 Table 5 (mild exposure, RCC): minimum cement 300 kg/m³ and maximum w/c 0·55 — satisfied.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> IS 456 also caps cement at 450 kg/m³ (OPC) to limit shrinkage and heat.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 561 corresponds to w/c of 0·34 and 0·41.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 472 kg/m³ corresponds to w/c of 0·34 and 0·41.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 294 kg/m³ is below the minimum and corresponds to w/c 0·65.</li></ul>
<p>In IS 10262 mix design the free water content is chosen first from the maximum aggregate size and slump, and the cement content then follows from the adopted w/c ratio, as here. If the result fell below the IS 456 minimum, the minimum would govern.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Cement = water ÷ w/c ratio.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-6", year: 2020, no: 6, subject: "bm", topic: "Plowman's maturity–strength relation",
q: `The strength of a fully matured concrete sample is 500 kg/cm<sup>2</sup>. When cured at an average temperature of 20°C in day, 10°C in night, datum temperature T<sub>0</sub> is −11°C. If Plowman constants A is 32 and B is 54, the strength of identical concrete at 7 days will be nearly`,
opts: ["333 kg/cm<sup>2</sup>", "312 kg/cm<sup>2</sup>", "272 kg/cm<sup>2</sup>", "243 kg/cm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 333 kg/cm²</h3><div class="formula">Maturity M = Σ(T − T<sub>0</sub>) Δt ;  % strength = A + B log<sub>10</sub>(M/1000)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Per day: 12 h × (20 + 11) + 12 h × (10 + 11) = 372 + 252 = 624 °C·h.</li><li>7 days: M = 4368 °C·h.</li><li>% strength = 32 + 54 log(4·368) = 32 + 54 × 0·640 = 66·6 %.</li><li>Strength = 0·666 × 500 = <b>333 kg/cm²</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 312 uses wrong maturity (e.g. datum 0 °C or 24 h at one temperature).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 272 uses wrong maturity (e.g. datum 0 °C or 24 h at one temperature).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 243 kg/cm² uses wrong maturity (e.g. datum 0 °C or 24 h at one temperature).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Maturity concepts let engineers estimate in-place strength from the temperature history, e.g. for formwork removal in cold weather.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Datum −11 °C: concrete gains no strength below it.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-7", year: 2020, no: 7, subject: "bm", topic: "Gel–space ratio strength",
q: `A sample of concrete is prepared by using 500 g of cement with water cement ratio of 0·55 and 240 N/mm<sup>2</sup> intrinsic strength of gel. The theoretical strength of concrete on full hydration will be nearly`,
opts: ["148 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "126 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "104 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "82 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 104 N/mm²</h3><div class="formula">x = 0·657 α / (0·319 α + w/c) ;  f = 240 x³</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Full hydration α = 1: x = 0·657/(0·319 + 0·55) = 0·756.</li><li>f = 240 × 0·756³ = 240 × 0·432 = <b>103·7 N/mm²</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The theoretical value exceeds real concrete strength because the theory assumes a flawless paste (see ESE 2021 Q145). The cement mass (500 g) cancels out.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 148 corresponds to lower w/c.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 126 N/mm² corresponds to lower w/c.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 82 N/mm² corresponds to partial hydration.</li></ul>
<p>The gel/space ratio x is the volume of hydrated gel divided by the space available to it (gel plus capillary pores); lowering w/c or increasing the degree of hydration raises x and the strength very sharply because of the cube law.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Powers: strength ∝ (gel/space)³.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-8", year: 2020, no: 8, subject: "bm", topic: "Laitance",
q: `The cement and water slurry coming on the top and setting on the surface is called`,
opts: ["Crazing", "Efflorescence", "Sulphate deterioration", "Laitance"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) laitance</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Laitance</b> is a weak layer of cement paste and fines carried to the surface by bleeding water; it sets as a soft, dusty skin that bonds poorly with the next lift and wears quickly. It must be removed (wire brushing, green cutting) before placing new concrete at construction joints.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Crazing is a network of fine surface cracks from rapid drying or over-trowelling.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Efflorescence is white salt deposits brought out by water evaporation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Sulphate attack causes expansion and cracking from ettringite formation.</li></ul>
<p>Laitance is reduced by low w/c, good grading, avoiding over-vibration and by air entrainment.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Bleeding → laitance.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-9", year: 2020, no: 9, subject: "bm", topic: "Uses of polymer concrete",
q: `Polymer concrete is most suitable for`,
opts: ["Sewage disposal works", "Mass concreting works", "Insulating exterior walls of an air-conditioned building", "Road repair works"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) sewage disposal works</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Polymer concrete</b> (aggregate bound with a polymer resin, or polymer-impregnated concrete) is dense, almost impermeable and highly resistant to acids and chemicals. It is therefore suited to sewer pipes, sewage treatment units, chemical plants and industrial floors, as well as precast elements and quick repairs.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Mass concreting needs cheap, low-heat concrete — polymer concrete is far too costly.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Thermal insulation is better provided by lightweight/aerated concrete.</li><li><b>(d)</b> It is used for rapid repairs, but the item's key use is chemical resistance in sewage works.</li></ul>
<p>Other forms: polymer-modified concrete (latex added to the mix) for overlays and repairs.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Polymer concrete: impermeable, acid-resistant, high early strength, costly.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-10", year: 2020, no: 10, subject: "bm", topic: "Lime for finishing coats",
q: `Which one of the following limes will be used for finishing coat in plastering and white washing?`,
opts: ["Semi Hydraulic lime", "Kankar lime", "Magnesium/Dolomitic lime", "Eminently Hydraulic lime"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) magnesium/dolomitic lime</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 712 classes of building lime and uses:</p>
<table><tr><th>Class</th><th>Type</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Eminently hydraulic</td><td>structural masonry, concrete</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Semi-hydraulic</td><td>masonry mortar</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>Fat lime</td><td>finishing coat, white wash, composite mortar</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td><b>Magnesium/dolomitic</b></td><td><b>finishing coat in plastering, white washing</b></td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>Kankar lime</td><td>masonry mortar</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>Siliceous dolomitic</td><td>undercoat and finishing coat of plaster</td></tr></table>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Semi-hydraulic lime sets partly under water; used for masonry mortar.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Kankar lime is impure hydraulic lime, used for masonry.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Eminently hydraulic lime sets under water; used for masonry in damp work.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Finishing and whitewash: classes C and D.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-11", year: 2020, no: 11, subject: "bm", topic: "Lithium in X-ray shielding mortar", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following light weight element will be added to enhance the protective properties for X-ray shielding mortars?`,
opts: ["Sodium", "Potassium", "Lithium", "Calcium"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) lithium (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Shielding mortars use heavy aggregates (barytes, magnetite) to attenuate X- and gamma rays; light elements such as <b>lithium</b>, boron and hydrogen are added because they absorb neutrons and capture the secondary radiation, improving overall protection. The key identifies lithium (lithium salts are added to barium plasters) as the light element used.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Sodium compounds are soluble and not used for shielding.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Potassium compounds are soluble and not used for shielding.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Calcium is already the main constituent of cement; it adds no special shielding.</li></ul>
<p>Shielding concrete is often designed with high density (&gt; 3500 kg/m³) and high hydrogen (water) content.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Heavy elements stop gamma; light elements (Li, B, H) stop neutrons.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-12", year: 2020, no: 12, subject: "bm", topic: "Artificial stones", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following stone is produced by moulding a mixture of iron slag and Portland cement?`,
opts: ["Imperial stone", "Garlic stone", "Ransom stone", "Victoria stone"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Garlic stone (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Artificial (cast) stones in building-materials texts:</p>
<ul><li><b>Garlic stone</b>: made by moulding a mixture of iron slag and Portland cement — used for steps, kerbs and paving.</li><li><b>(a)</b> Imperial stone: finely crushed granite with Portland cement, treated with silicate of soda.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Ransom stone (chemically formed): soda silicate with cement, giving decorative floor finishes.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Victoria stone: granite pieces with surface hardened by soaking in soda silicate.</li></ul>
<p>Our first pick was (d); on review the key's classification (Rangwala) is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Garlic: iron slag + cement.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-13", year: 2020, no: 13, subject: "solid", topic: "Bulk modulus from E and G",
q: `When a round bar material with diameter of 37·5 mm, length of 2·4 m, Young's modulus of 110 GN/m<sup>2</sup> and shear modulus of 42 GN/m<sup>2</sup> is stretched for 2·5 mm, its Bulk modulus will be nearly`,
opts: ["104 GN/m<sup>2</sup>", "96 GN/m<sup>2</sup>", "84 GN/m<sup>2</sup>", "76 GN/m<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 96 GN/m²</h3><div class="formula">ν = E/(2G) − 1 ;  K = E/[3(1 − 2ν)] = EG/[3(3G − E)]</div>
<ol class="work"><li>ν = 110/84 − 1 = 0·310.</li><li>K = 110 × 42/[3(126 − 110)] = 4620/48 = <b>96·25 GN/m²</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The bar size and stretch are not needed for the elastic constants. E = 110 GPa with G = 42 GPa is typical of copper or bronze.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 104 does not satisfy E = 9KG/(3K + G).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 84 does not satisfy E = 9KG/(3K + G).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 76 GN/m² does not satisfy E = 9KG/(3K + G).</li></ul>
<p>Physically, K measures resistance to uniform volume change; as ν approaches 0·5 the material becomes incompressible and K tends to infinity, while ν = 0 gives K = E/3.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> E = 2G(1 + ν) = 3K(1 − 2ν).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-14", year: 2020, no: 14, subject: "solid", topic: "Punching shear stress",
q: `A punch of 20 mm diameter is used to punch a hole in 8 mm thick plate. If the force required to create a hole is 110 kN, the average shear stress in the plate will be nearly`,
opts: ["410 MPa", "320 MPa", "220 MPa", "140 MPa"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 220 MPa</h3><div class="formula">τ = P/(π d t)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Sheared area = π × 20 × 8 = 502·7 mm².</li><li>τ = 110 000/502·7 = <b>218·8 ≈ 220 MPa</b>.</li><li>Compressive stress in the punch = 110 000/(π × 20²/4) = 350 MPa.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 410 uses a smaller area.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 320 MPa uses a smaller area.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 140 MPa uses too large an area.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The shear acts on the cylindrical surface around the hole, not on the circular face.</p>
<p>Plates are punched only when the hole diameter is at least about the plate thickness; the punch stress (here 350 MPa) must stay below the punch's working stress, otherwise the hole is drilled.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Punching: shear area = perimeter × thickness.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-15", year: 2020, no: 15, subject: "solid", topic: "Composite RC column (elastic)",
q: `A reinforced concrete circular section of 50,000 mm<sup>2</sup> cross-sectional area carries 6 reinforcing bars whose total area is 500 mm<sup>2</sup>. If the concrete is not to be stressed more than 3·5 MPa and modular ratio for steel and concrete is 18, the safe load the column can carry will be nearly`,
opts: ["225 kN", "205 kN", "180 kN", "160 kN"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 205 kN</h3><div class="formula">P = σ<sub>c</sub>(A<sub>c</sub> + m A<sub>s</sub>)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>A<sub>c</sub> = 50 000 − 500 = 49 500 mm².</li><li>Steel stress = mσ<sub>c</sub> = 18 × 3·5 = 63 MPa.</li><li>P = 3·5 × 49 500 + 63 × 500 = 173 250 + 31 500 = 204 750 N ≈ <b>205 kN</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The transformed (equivalent concrete) area is 49 500 + 18 × 500 = 58 500 mm².</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 225 kN ignores the deduction of steel area from concrete.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 180 ignores the steel contribution or use m wrongly.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 160 kN ignores the steel contribution or use m wrongly.</li></ul>
<p>This is the elastic (modular-ratio) method of the old working-stress codes; IS 456 limit state design would instead use P<sub>u</sub> = 0·4f<sub>ck</sub>A<sub>c</sub> + 0·67f<sub>y</sub>A<sub>sc</sub>, which accounts for steel yielding at collapse.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Strain compatibility: σ<sub>s</sub> = m σ<sub>c</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-16", year: 2020, no: 16, subject: "solid", topic: "Bending strain energy of a cantilever",
q: `The strain energy U stored due to bending of the cantilever beam due to point load at the free end will be (where W = concentrated load, l = length of a cantilever, EI = flexural rigidity)`,
opts: ["W<sup>2</sup>l<sup>3</sup>/6EI", "W<sup>2</sup>l<sup>2</sup>/6EI", "W<sup>3</sup>l<sup>3</sup>/36EI", "W<sup>2</sup>l<sup>3</sup>/36EI"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) W²l³/6EI</h3><div class="formula">U = ∫₀ˡ M² dx/(2EI) ,  M = Wx</div>
<ol class="work"><li>U = ∫ W²x² dx/(2EI) = W²l³/(6EI).</li><li>Check by work: U = ½Wδ = ½W × Wl³/(3EI) = W²l³/(6EI). ✓</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Castigliano: ∂U/∂W = Wl³/3EI = tip deflection.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> l² instead of l³ makes it dimensionally not an energy.</li><li><b>(c)</b> W³ is dimensionally wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 36 is the wrong constant; U = W²l³/(6EI).</li></ul>
<p>For a cantilever with UDL w, the bending strain energy is w²l⁵/(40EI); shear strain energy is usually negligible for slender beams.</p><p>Strain energy methods (Castigliano, unit load) find deflections of beams, frames and trusses without integrating the elastic curve.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Strain energy = ½ × load × deflection.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-17", year: 2020, no: 17, subject: "solid", topic: "Volume change of a bar in tension",
q: `A steel bar 2 m long, 20 mm wide and 15 mm thick is subjected to a tensile load of 30 kN. If Poisson's ratio is 0·25 and Young's modulus is 200 GPa, an increase in volume will be`,
opts: ["160 mm<sup>3</sup>", "150 mm<sup>3</sup>", "140 mm<sup>3</sup>", "130 mm<sup>3</sup>"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 150 mm³</h3><div class="formula">ΔV = (σ/E)(1 − 2ν) V</div>
<ol class="work"><li>σ = 30 000/300 = 100 MPa; ε = 100/200 000 = 5 × 10⁻⁴.</li><li>ε<sub>v</sub> = 5 × 10⁻⁴ × 0·5 = 2·5 × 10⁻⁴.</li><li>V = 2000 × 20 × 15 = 600 000 mm³ → ΔV = <b>150 mm³</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 160 uses other Poisson's ratios.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 140 uses other Poisson's ratios.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 130 mm³ uses other Poisson's ratios.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Note ΔV = (P/E)(1 − 2ν)L — independent of the cross-section: 30 000 × 0·5 × 2000/200 000 = 150 mm³.</p>
<p>Volume increases in tension because the axial extension (ε) outweighs the lateral contraction (2νε) whenever ν &lt; 0·5.</p><p>The bar's length grows by 1 mm, while width and thickness each shrink by ε<sub>lat</sub> = 1·25 × 10⁻⁴ of their size.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Uniaxial: ε<sub>v</sub> = ε(1 − 2ν).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-18", year: 2020, no: 18, subject: "solid", topic: "Bolt diameter by maximum principal stress theory",
q: `A bolt is under an axial thrust of 9·6 kN together with a transverse force of 4·8 kN. If factor of safety is 3, yield strength of bolt material is 270 N/mm<sup>2</sup> and Poisson's ratio is 0·3, its diameter as per maximum principal stress theory will be nearly`,
opts: ["13 mm", "15 mm", "17 mm", "19 mm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 13 mm</h3><ol class="work"><li>σ = 9600/A, τ = 4800/A (both on the same section).</li><li>σ<sub>1</sub> = σ/2 + √(σ²/4 + τ²) = (4800 + √(4800² + 4800²))/A = 11 588/A.</li><li>Allowable = 270/3 = 90 MPa → A = 128·8 mm².</li><li>d = √(4 × 128·8/π) = <b>12·8 ≈ 13 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Poisson's ratio would be needed only for the maximum strain theory.</p><div class="formula">σ₁ = σ/2 + √(σ²/4 + τ²) ≤ σ_y/FS</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 15 is larger than needed (would be required by the more conservative shear theories).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 17 is larger than needed (would be required by the more conservative shear theories).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 19 mm is larger than needed (would be required by the more conservative shear theories).</li></ul>
<p>The maximum shear stress theory (Tresca) would require τ<sub>max</sub> = √(σ²/4 + τ²) ≤ 45 MPa, giving a larger bolt of about 15 mm; Rankine's theory is the least conservative here.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Rankine: σ<sub>1</sub> ≤ f<sub>y</sub>/FOS.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-19", year: 2020, no: 19, subject: "solid", topic: "Total strain energy per unit volume",
q: `In a material the principal stresses are 60 MN/m<sup>2</sup>, 48 MN/m<sup>2</sup> and −36 MN/m<sup>2</sup>. When the values of E = 200 GN/m<sup>2</sup> and 1/m = 0·3, the total strain energy per unit volume will be nearly`,
opts: ["43·5 kNm/m<sup>3</sup>", "35·5 kNm/m<sup>3</sup>", "27·5 kNm/m<sup>3</sup>", "19·5 kNm/m<sup>3</sup>"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 19·5 kN·m/m³</h3><div class="formula">U = [σ₁² + σ₂² + σ₃² − 2ν(σ₁σ₂ + σ₂σ₃ + σ₃σ₁)] / (2E)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Σσ² = 3600 + 2304 + 1296 = 7200.</li><li>Σσσ = 2880 − 1728 − 2160 = −1008 → −2ν × (−1008) = +604·8.</li><li>U = 7804·8/(2 × 200 000) MN/m² = 0·01951 MN·m/m³ = <b>19·5 kN·m/m³</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> uses wrong signs for the negative stress or omit ν.</li><li><b>(b)</b> uses wrong signs for the negative stress or omit ν.</li><li><b>(c)</b> uses wrong signs for the negative stress or omit ν.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The strain energy splits into volumetric and distortional parts; the distortional part (von Mises) is what governs yielding of ductile metals, while the total energy is Haigh's criterion.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Keep signs: compressive stresses enter as negative.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-20", year: 2020, no: 20, subject: "solid", topic: "Condition for a zero principal stress",
q: `At a point in a two dimensional stress system, the normal stress on two mutually perpendicular planes are σ<sub>xx</sub> and σ<sub>yy</sub> and shear stress is τ<sub>xy</sub>. One of the principal stresses will become zero when the value of shear stress τ<sub>xy</sub> is`,
opts: ["±(σ<sub>xx</sub>σ<sub>yy</sub>)", "±√(σ<sub>xx</sub> − σ<sub>yy</sub>)", "±√(σ<sub>xx</sub> + σ<sub>yy</sub>)", "±√(σ<sub>xx</sub>σ<sub>yy</sub>)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) τ = ±√(σ<sub>xx</sub>σ<sub>yy</sub>)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The product of the principal stresses is the second invariant:</p>
<div class="formula">σ₁σ₂ = σ<sub>xx</sub>σ<sub>yy</sub> − τ<sub>xy</sub>²</div>
<p>One principal stress is zero when σ₁σ₂ = 0, i.e. τ<sub>xy</sub>² = σ<sub>xx</sub>σ<sub>yy</sub> → τ = ±√(σ<sub>xx</sub>σ<sub>yy</sub>). The other principal stress is then σ<sub>xx</sub> + σ<sub>yy</sub>.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A product without √ has dimensions of stress².</li><li><b>(b)</b> √(σxx − σyy) has dimensions of √stress.</li><li><b>(c)</b> √(σxx + σyy) has dimensions of √stress.</li></ul>
<p>Example: σ<sub>xx</sub> = 80 and σ<sub>yy</sub> = 20 MPa give τ = 40 MPa for one principal stress to vanish; the other is then 100 MPa, so the element is in uniaxial tension when referred to its principal axes.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Determinant of the 2-D stress tensor = σ₁σ₂.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-21", year: 2020, no: 21, subject: "solid", topic: "Close-coiled spring deflection",
q: `The deflection δ of the closed coil helical spring is (where W axial load, R radius of coil, n number of turns, C modulus of rigidity, d wire diameter)`,
opts: ["WR<sup>2</sup>n/8Cd<sup>3</sup>", "64WR<sup>3</sup>n/Cd<sup>4</sup>", "128WR<sup>3</sup>n/Cd<sup>2</sup>", "64WR<sup>2</sup>n/Cd<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 64WR³n/(Cd⁴)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Close-coiled spring deflection.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The wire of length 2πRn is twisted by T = WR.</li><li>Angle of twist θ = TL/(CJ), J = πd⁴/32, and δ = Rθ:</li></ol>
<div class="formula">δ = WR · 2πRn · R · 32/(C π d⁴) = 64WR³n/(Cd⁴)</div>
<p>Stiffness k = Cd⁴/(64R³n).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> R² and d³ are the wrong powers (δ ∝ R³/d⁴).</li><li><b>(c)</b> d² is the wrong power of the wire diameter.</li><li><b>(d)</b> R² and d² are the wrong powers.</li></ul>
<p>The derivation assumes a small helix angle (close coils), so the wire is in pure torsion; open-coiled springs also have bending of the wire.</p><p>Springs in series add deflections; in parallel they share the load in proportion to stiffness.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> With mean diameter D: δ = 8WD³n/(Cd⁴).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-22", year: 2020, no: 22, subject: "solid", topic: "Spring deflection (numerical)",
q: `A closely-coiled helical spring of round steel wire 5 mm in diameter having 12 complete coils of 50 mm mean diameter is subjected to an axial load of 100 N. If modulus of rigidity is 80 GPa, the deflection of the spring will be`,
opts: ["36 mm", "32 mm", "28 mm", "24 mm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 24 mm</h3><div class="formula">δ = 8WD³n/(Gd⁴)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>8 × 100 × 50³ × 12 = 1·2 × 10⁹.</li><li>G d⁴ = 80 000 × 625 = 5 × 10⁷.</li><li>δ = <b>24 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Shear stress τ = 8WD/(πd³) = 8 × 100 × 50/(π × 125) = 102 MPa; stiffness k = 100/24 = 4·17 N/mm.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 36 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 32 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 28 mm does not match the working above.</li></ul>
<p>The spring index C = D/d = 10 is a common practical value; the Wahl factor (≈ 1·14) would raise the maximum shear stress to about 116 MPa.</p><p>Energy stored = ½ × 100 × 24 = 1200 N·mm.</p><p>Doubling the wire diameter would make the spring 16 times stiffer.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> δ ∝ D³n/d⁴.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-23", year: 2020, no: 23, subject: "solid", topic: "Power of a hollow shaft",
q: `A hollow shaft of external and internal diameters as 100 mm and 40 mm respectively is transmitting power at 120 rpm. If the shearing stress is not to exceed 50 MPa, the power the shaft can transmit will be`,
opts: ["100 kW", "120 kW", "140 kW", "160 kW"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 120 kW</h3><div class="formula">T = τ π (D⁴ − d⁴)/(16D) ;  P = 2πNT/60</div>
<ol class="work"><li>D⁴ − d⁴ = 10⁸ − 2·56 × 10⁶ = 9·744 × 10⁷ mm⁴.</li><li>T = 50 × π × 9·744 × 10⁷/(16 × 100) = 9·566 × 10⁶ N·mm.</li><li>P = 2π × 120 × 9566/60 = 120 200 W ≈ <b>120 kW</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A solid shaft of 100 mm would carry only 2·6 % more torque — hollow shafts save material with little loss of strength.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 100 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 140 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 160 kW does not match the working above.</li></ul>
<p>Power in kW can also be written P = T(kN·m) × N/9·549; T = 9·566 kN·m at 120 rpm gives about 120 kW.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Hollow shaft: Z<sub>p</sub> = π(D⁴ − d⁴)/(16D).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-24", year: 2020, no: 24, subject: "solid", topic: "Maximum shear in a circular beam",
q: `A circular beam of 100 mm diameter is subjected to a shear force of 30 kN. The maximum shear stress will be nearly`,
opts: ["5·1 MPa", "6·3 MPa", "7·5 MPa", "8·7 MPa"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 5·1 MPa</h3><div class="formula">τ<sub>max</sub> = (4/3) V/A</div>
<ol class="work"><li>A = π × 100²/4 = 7854 mm²; τ<sub>avg</sub> = 3·82 MPa.</li><li>τ<sub>max</sub> = 4/3 × 3·82 = <b>5·09 MPa</b>, at the neutral axis.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 6·3 MPa uses a factor of about 1·65.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 7·5 MPa uses 2 (thin tube).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8·7 MPa is not a standard factor.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> For a rectangle the factor would be 1·5; for a thin tube 2.</p>
<p>The shear stress distribution across a circle is parabolic, zero at the top and bottom points and maximum along the horizontal diameter.</p><p>Beams of circular section are usually governed by bending, not shear, except when very short and heavily loaded.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Circle: 4/3; rectangle: 3/2.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-25", year: 2020, no: 25, subject: "solid", topic: "Cantilever tip deflection",
q: `A cantilever beam AB as shown in figure is subjected to a point load of 12 kN over a span of 6 m with E = 2×10<sup>5</sup> N/mm<sup>2</sup> and I<sub>xx</sub> = 6×10<sup>7</sup> mm<sup>4</sup>. The deflection at the free end will be<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2020-25.png" alt="Cantilever AB fixed at A, length l, point load W at free end B">`,
opts: ["80 mm", "72 mm", "64 mm", "56 mm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 72 mm</h3><div class="formula">δ = Wl³/(3EI)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Wl³ = 12 000 × (6000)³ = 2·592 × 10¹⁵ N·mm³.</li><li>3EI = 3 × 2 × 10⁵ × 6 × 10⁷ = 3·6 × 10¹³.</li><li>δ = <b>72 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Slope at the free end = Wl²/2EI = 0·018 rad (1·03°).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 80 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 64 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 56 mm does not match the working above.</li></ul>
<p>The deflection is l/83 here — far more than typical limits (l/150 to l/180 for cantilevers), so such a beam would need a larger section in practice.</p><p>Halving the span would cut the deflection to one-eighth (9 mm).</p><p>The fixed end carries the maximum moment Wl = 72 kN·m.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Cantilever tip load: δ = Wl³/3EI.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2020b.js ----
// ESE 2020 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (KJL-S-CVL), Q26–50
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A).
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2020-26", year: 2020, no: 26, subject: "steel", topic: "Spacing of floor joists",
q: `A floor has to carry a load of 12 kN/m<sup>2</sup>. The floor is supported on rectangular joists each 100 mm wide, 300 mm deep and 5 m long. If maximum stress in the joists should not exceed 8 MN/m<sup>2</sup>, the centre to centre distance of joists will be`,
opts: ["430 mm", "400 mm", "360 mm", "320 mm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 320 mm</h3><ol class="work"><li>Z = 100 × 300²/6 = 1·5 × 10⁶ mm³; allowable M = 8 × 1·5 × 10⁶ = 12 kN·m.</li><li>Each joist carries 12s kN/m (s = spacing, m); M = 12s × 5²/8 = 37·5s kN·m.</li><li>37·5s = 12 → s = 0·32 m = <b>320 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The joist is treated as simply supported over 5 m with the floor load collected from a strip equal to the spacing. Deflection (span/360 with brittle finishes) and bearing at the supports should also be checked for timber.</p><div class="formula">M = σZ, Z = bd²/6</div><div class="formula">M = wL²/8</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 430 would overstress the joists (above 8 MPa).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 400 would overstress the joists (above 8 MPa).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 360 mm would overstress the joists (above 8 MPa).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Joist load per metre = floor load × spacing.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-27", year: 2020, no: 27, subject: "steel", topic: "Maximum shear in a wooden beam",
q: `A simply supported wooden beam 100 mm wide, 250 mm deep and 3 m long is carrying a uniformly distributed load of 40 kN/m. The maximum shear stress will be`,
opts: ["2·4 MPa", "2·8 MPa", "3·2 MPa", "3·6 MPa"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3·6 MPa</h3><ol class="work"><li>Maximum shear V = wL/2 = 40 × 3/2 = 60 kN at the supports.</li><li>τ<sub>avg</sub> = 60 000/(100 × 250) = 2·4 MPa.</li><li>τ<sub>max</sub> = 1·5 × 2·4 = <b>3·6 MPa</b> at the neutral axis.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Timber has low shear strength along the grain (horizontal shear, often 0·6–1·0 MPa permissible), so short, heavily loaded timber beams are frequently governed by shear rather than bending; this beam would actually fail in horizontal shear.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2·4 MPa is the average shear stress.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2·8 uses wrong factors.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3·2 MPa uses wrong factors.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Rectangle: τ<sub>max</sub> = 1·5V/(bd).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-28", year: 2020, no: 28, subject: "sa", topic: "Yield of a prop to equalise reactions",
q: `A simply supported beam of span 8 m carries a uniformly distributed load of 24 kN/m run over the whole span. The beam is propped at the middle of the span. The values of E = 200×10<sup>6</sup> kN/m<sup>2</sup> and I = 20×10<sup>−5</sup> m<sup>4</sup>. The amount by which the prop should yield in order to make all three reactions equal will be nearly`,
opts: ["20 mm", "15 mm", "10 mm", "5 mm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 15 mm</h3><ol class="work"><li>Total load = 24 × 8 = 192 kN; equal reactions → each 64 kN, so the prop carries 64 kN.</li><li>EI = 200 × 10⁶ × 20 × 10⁻⁵ = 40 000 kN·m².</li><li>Deflection at mid-span from UDL alone = 5wL⁴/384EI = 5 × 24 × 4096/(384 × 40 000) = 0·0320 m.</li><li>Upward deflection from the 64 kN prop force = PL³/48EI = 64 × 512/(48 × 40 000) = 0·0171 m.</li><li>Net deflection (= prop yield) = 0·0320 − 0·0171 = 0·0149 m ≈ <b>15 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A rigid prop would carry 5wL/8 = 120 kN; letting it sink reduces its share to 64 kN.</p><div class="formula">Prop force = R making reactions equal</div><div class="formula">δ_prop = 5wL⁴/(384EI) − RL³/(48EI)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 20 gives other prop reactions.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 10 gives other prop reactions.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5 mm gives other prop reactions.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Prop yield = free deflection − deflection recovered by the prop force.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-29", year: 2020, no: 29, subject: "solid", topic: "Stepped cantilever deflection",
q: `A cantilever beam ACB has end A fixed and subjected to a point load P at free end B. The point C is mid-point of AB and the moment of inertia of AC is twice that of CB. The deflection at the free end will be<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2020-29.png" alt="Stepped cantilever: AC (length l/2) has 2I, CB (length l/2) has I; load P at free end B">`,
opts: ["Pl<sup>3</sup>/3EI", "Pl<sup>3</sup>/48EI", "5Pl<sup>3</sup>/96EI", "9Pl<sup>3</sup>/48EI"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 9Pl³/48EI</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> By moment-area (or unit load), with x measured from the free end and M = Px:</p>
<div class="formula">δ<sub>B</sub> = ∫ M·x dx / (EI<sub>x</sub>)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>CB (0 to l/2, I): ∫ Px² dx/EI = P(l/2)³/(3EI) = Pl³/(24EI) = 2Pl³/48EI.</li><li>AC (l/2 to l, 2I): ∫ Px² dx/(2EI) = P(l³ − l³/8)/(6EI) = 7Pl³/48EI.</li><li>δ<sub>B</sub> = (2 + 7)Pl³/48EI = <b>9Pl³/48EI</b> = 0·1875 Pl³/EI.</li></ol>
<p>A uniform cantilever with I throughout would give Pl³/3EI = 16Pl³/48EI; stiffening the root half reduces the deflection by 44 %.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Pl³/3EI is the deflection at B, not at C.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Pl³/48EI is the simply-supported central deflection.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 5Pl³/96EI is the deflection at C, not the free-end value asked.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Stiffness matters most near the fixed end.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-30", year: 2020, no: 30, subject: "sa", topic: "Equal deflection: simple vs fixed beam",
q: `A beam of uniform cross-section, simply supported at ends carries a concentrated load W at midspan. If the ends of the beam are fixed and only load P is applied at the midspan such that the deflection at the centre remains the same, the value of the load P will be`,
opts: ["6W", "4W", "2W", "W"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 4W</h3><div class="formula">Simply supported: WL³/48EI ;  fixed–fixed: PL³/192EI</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Equal deflections: PL³/192 = WL³/48 → P = 4W.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Fixing both ends makes the beam four times stiffer for a central load (and halves the maximum moment from WL/4 to WL/8 for the same load). For a UDL, fixed ends make it five times stiffer (wL⁴/384 vs 5wL⁴/384).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 6W would need a stiffness ratio of 6 (fixed vs SS is 4).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2W would need a stiffness ratio of 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> W would mean fixing the ends changes nothing.</li></ul>
<p>The extra stiffness comes from the fixing moments (WL/8 at each end), which reverse the curvature near the supports and greatly reduce mid-span sag. In practice full fixity is hard to achieve, so real beams lie between the two cases.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Central load: fixed beam is 4× stiffer.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-31", year: 2020, no: 31, subject: "sa", topic: "Maximum moment from two wheel loads",
q: `Two wheel loads 200 kN and 80 kN spaced at 2 m apart move on the span of girder of 16 m. If any wheel load can lead the other, the maximum bending moment that can occur at a section of 6 m from the left end will be`,
opts: ["1050 kNm", "990 kNm", "870 kNm", "750 kNm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 990 kN·m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Influence line for moment at C (a = 6 m, b = 10 m): peak ordinate ab/L = 60/16 = 3·75 m at C, decreasing linearly to zero at both supports.</p><div class="formula">M_C = Σ P × ILD ordinate</div><div class="formula">ordinate = ab/L at C</div>
<ol class="work"><li>200 kN at C, 80 kN 2 m to the right (ordinate 3·75 × 8/10 = 3·0): M = 200 × 3·75 + 80 × 3·0 = 750 + 240 = 990 kN·m.</li><li>200 kN at C, 80 kN 2 m to the left (ordinate 3·75 × 4/6 = 2·5): M = 750 + 200 = 950 kN·m.</li><li>80 kN at C, 200 kN 2 m right: 300 + 600 = 900; 200 kN 2 m left: 300 + 500 = 800.</li><li>Maximum = <b>990 kN·m</b>.</li></ol>
<p>The general rule: the maximum occurs with a heavy load at the section, placed so the average load to the left equals that to the right as nearly as possible.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1050 kN·m exceeds every arrangement.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 870 is lower arrangements.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 750 kN·m is lower arrangements.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Use the ILD: M = Σ W × ordinate.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-32", year: 2020, no: 32, subject: "sa", topic: "FEM for loads at third points",
q: `A beam of length l is fixed at its both ends and carries two concentrated loads of W each at a distance of l/3 from both ends. The fixed end moment at A will be`,
opts: ["−Wl/3", "−2Wl/9", "−6Wl/15", "−4Wl/27"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) −2Wl/9</h3><div class="formula">FEM for one load: Wab²/l²</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Load at l/3 from A (a = l/3, b = 2l/3): W(l/3)(4l²/9)/l² = 4Wl/27.</li><li>Load at 2l/3 from A (a = 2l/3, b = l/3): W(2l/3)(l²/9)/l² = 2Wl/27.</li><li>M<sub>A</sub> = 6Wl/27 = <b>2Wl/9</b> (hogging, shown negative).</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The free moment between the loads is Wl/3; mid-span moment of the fixed beam = Wl/3 − 2Wl/9 = Wl/9.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> −Wl/3 is the simply supported moment.</li><li><b>(c)</b> −6Wl/15 is not the sum.</li><li><b>(d)</b> −4Wl/27 is only one load's contribution.</li></ul>
<p>The bending moment diagram is constant (Wl/9 sagging) between the loads and varies linearly to −2Wl/9 at the supports; points of contraflexure lie within the end thirds.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Superpose Wab²/l² for each load.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-33", year: 2020, no: 33, subject: "sa", topic: "Natural frequency of a cantilever with tip mass",
q: `The natural frequency of a mass m at the end of the cantilever beam of negligible mass with usual notations will be`,
opts: ["(1/2π)(3EI/mL<sup>3</sup>)<sup>1/2</sup>", "(1/π)(6EI/mL<sup>3</sup>)<sup>1/2</sup>", "(1/2π)(6EI/mL<sup>3</sup>)<sup>1/2</sup>", "(1/π)(3EI/mL<sup>3</sup>)<sup>1/2</sup>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) f = (1/2π)√(3EI/mL³)</h3><ol class="work"><li>Tip stiffness of a cantilever: k = P/δ = 3EI/L³.</li><li>f = (1/2π)√(k/m) = (1/2π)√(3EI/(mL³)).</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other cases: simply supported beam with central mass k = 48EI/L³; fixed–fixed with central mass k = 192EI/L³. The circular frequency is ω = √(k/m) rad/s, and the period T = 1/f.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Uses 1/π and 6EI/L³ – both wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Factor must be 1/(2π), not 1/π.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Cantilever stiffness is 3EI/L³, not 6EI/L³.</li></ul>
<p>Example: a 2 m steel cantilever (EI = 2 × 10⁶ N·m²) with a 100 kg tip mass has k = 750 kN/m and f = 13·8 Hz. Including the beam's own mass would lower the frequency slightly (add about 0·24 of its mass at the tip).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> f = (1/2π)√(k/m); find k for the support case.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-34", year: 2020, no: 34, subject: "sa", topic: "Natural frequency of SDOF oscillator",
q: `The simple oscillator under idealized conditions of no-damping, once excited will oscillate indefinitely with constant amplitude at its natural frequency f will be`,
opts: ["(1/2π)√(m/k)", "(1/π)√(k/m)", "(1/2π)√(k/m)", "(1/π)√(m/k)"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) f = (1/2π)√(k/m)</h3><div class="formula">m ẍ + k x = 0 ⇒ x = A sin(ωt + φ), ω = √(k/m)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The natural frequency in hertz is f = ω/2π = (1/2π)√(k/m); without damping, the motion continues indefinitely with constant amplitude.</p>
<p>In terms of the static deflection δ<sub>st</sub> = mg/k: f = (1/2π)√(g/δ<sub>st</sub>) ≈ 15·76/√δ<sub>st</sub> (δ in mm).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> √(m/k) is the inverse ratio (period-like).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Both the factor and the ratio are wrong.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Factor must be 1/(2π), not 1/π.</li></ul>
<p>Real structures have damping, so free vibration decays; the damped frequency is ω<sub>d</sub> = ω√(1 − ξ²), almost equal to ω for typical ξ of 2–5 %.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Stiffer → higher frequency; heavier → lower.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-35", year: 2020, no: 35, subject: "sa", topic: "Ratio of maximum to minimum cable tension",
q: `A cable carrying a load of 10 kN/m run of horizontal span is stretched between supports of 100 m apart. If the supports are at same level and central dip of 8 m, the ratio of greatest and least tensions in the cable will be`,
opts: ["1·05", "1·35", "1·65", "1·95"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1·05</h3><ol class="work"><li>H (least tension, at the lowest point) = wL²/(8d) = 10 × 10 000/64 = 1562·5 kN.</li><li>V at supports = wL/2 = 500 kN.</li><li>T<sub>max</sub> = √(1562·5² + 500²) = 1640·5 kN.</li><li>Ratio = 1640·5/1562·5 = <b>1·05</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> In general T<sub>max</sub>/H = √(1 + (4d/L)²) = √(1 + 0·1024) = 1·05: flat cables (small dip ratio) have nearly uniform tension.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> corresponds to much larger dip ratios (about 0·2–0·4).</li><li><b>(c)</b> corresponds to much larger dip ratios (about 0·2–0·4).</li><li><b>(d)</b> corresponds to much larger dip ratios (about 0·2–0·4).</li></ul>
<p>The minimum tension equals the horizontal component H and occurs at the lowest point; the maximum occurs at the supports where the cable slope is steepest.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> T<sub>max</sub>/T<sub>min</sub> = √(1 + 16d²/L²).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-36", year: 2020, no: 36, subject: "sa", topic: "Minimum dip for limited tension",
q: `A cable is suspended between two points 75 m apart at the same level. It carries a uniformly distributed load of 12·5 kN per horizontal meter. If the maximum tension in the cable is limited to 1000 kN, the minimum central dip will be nearly`,
opts: ["14 m", "12 m", "10 m", "8 m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 10 m</h3><ol class="work"><li>V = wL/2 = 12·5 × 75/2 = 468·75 kN.</li><li>H = √(1000² − 468·75²) = 883·3 kN.</li><li>d = wL²/(8H) = 12·5 × 5625/(8 × 883·3) = <b>9·95 ≈ 10 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A smaller dip would raise H and the maximum tension above 1000 kN; a larger dip lowers tension but needs taller towers.</p><div class="formula">V = wL/2, T² = V² + H²</div><div class="formula">d = wL²/(8H)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 14 is safe but not the minimum.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 12 m is safe but not the minimum.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8 m would give H ≈ 1099 kN and T &gt; 1000 kN.</li></ul>
<p>The dip-to-span ratio here is about 1/7·5; suspension bridges commonly use 1/8 to 1/12 as a compromise between cable tension and tower height.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Minimum dip ↔ maximum allowable tension.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-37", year: 2020, no: 37, subject: "steel", topic: "Factor of safety from a test specimen",
q: `A tie bar 50 mm × 8 mm is to carry a load of 80 kN. A specimen of the same quality steel of cross-sectional area is 250 mm<sup>2</sup>. For a maximum load of 125 kN carried by the specimen, the factor of safety in the design will be`,
opts: ["3·0", "2·5", "2·0", "1·5"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2·5</h3><ol class="work"><li>Ultimate stress = 125 000/250 = 500 MPa.</li><li>Working stress in the tie = 80 000/(50 × 8) = 200 MPa.</li><li>FOS = 500/200 = <b>2·5</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Factor of safety based on ultimate strength is typical of older working-stress practice; modern limit state design uses partial factors on loads (1·5) and materials (1·1 for yield, 1·25 for rupture).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3·0 does not equal 500/200.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2·0 does not equal 500/200.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1·5 does not equal 500/200.</li></ul>
<p>The tie area 400 mm² is 1·6 times the specimen area, and it carries 80 kN against the specimen's 125 kN breaking load per 250 mm²; the stress ratio gives the factor of safety directly.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> FOS = ultimate stress ÷ working stress.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-38", year: 2020, no: 38, subject: "steel", topic: "Hanger connections",
q: `Hanger connections are made when`,
opts: ["Beam as well as girder is meeting at different level. A plate or hanger is interposed between the beam and the girder and finally interconnected by means of angle cleats or bolts and rods.", "Beam as well as girder is meeting at same level. A plate is interposed between the beam and the girder", "The beams are meeting at different levels. A hanger is interposed between the beams and finally interconnected by means of angle cleats or bolts and rods", "The girders are meeting at same level. A plate is interposed between the girders and finally interconnected by means of bolts and rods."], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A <b>hanger connection</b> is used when a beam is to be supported <b>below</b> a girder (different levels): a hanger (plate, angles or rods) hangs from the girder, and the beam is attached to it with angle cleats or bolts. The connection carries the beam reaction in tension.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Same-level meeting uses framed (web-cleat) connections, not hangers.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Same-level girders use framed connections.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Hangers are for beams meeting girders at different levels; this wording is incomplete.</li></ul>
<p>Hanger bolts are in direct tension, so prying action must be considered in their design.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Hanger: beam hung below girder, bolts in tension.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-39", year: 2020, no: 39, subject: "steel", topic: "Column splices",
q: `The splicing of a column becomes necessary, where`,
opts: ["The available length of structural steel section is less than the required length of the column", "Section remains same throughout at all floors", "Only riveted columns are to be designed", "Splices should be designed to carry axial loads only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A <b>splice</b> joins two lengths of a column when the available rolled length is shorter than the column, or when the section size changes between storeys (heavier sections below). Splices are placed just above floor level, where moments are small.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> If the section and length are the same throughout, no splice is needed for that reason.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Splices apply to bolted, riveted and welded columns alike.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Splices must carry axial load, moment and shear at the splice location; IS 800 requires them to be designed for the actual forces (or a proportion of the member capacity).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Splice where length runs out or section changes.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-40", year: 2020, no: 40, subject: "steel", topic: "Gauge length of the test specimen", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A tie bar 50 mm × 8 mm is to carry a load of 80 kN. A specimen of same quality steel of cross-sectional area is 250 mm<sup>2</sup>. If the maximum load carried by the specimen is 125 kN, the gauge length will be`,
opts: ["133 mm", "126 mm", "113 mm", "106 mm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 113 mm (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For geometrically similar tensile specimens (Unwin/IS 1608), the gauge length is proportional to √A. The key's value corresponds to</p>
<div class="formula">L<sub>0</sub> = k√A<sub>0</sub> ,  k ≈ 7·14 (older proportional specimen)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>√250 = 15·81 mm.</li><li>L<sub>0</sub> ≈ 7·14 × 15·81 = 113 mm.</li></ol>
<p>Modern IS 1608 uses L<sub>0</sub> = 5·65√A (= 89 mm here, not offered). Our first pick was (a); on review the key's 113 mm (proportional gauge length on the older basis) is accepted. The maximum load is not needed for the gauge length itself.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 133 does not correspond to standard proportionality constants.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 126 does not correspond to standard proportionality constants.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 106 mm does not correspond to standard proportionality constants.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Proportional specimen: L<sub>0</sub> = k√A<sub>0</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-41", year: 2020, no: 41, subject: "steel", topic: "Imperfections affecting column strength",
q: `The strength of a column depends on which of the following imperfections?<ol><li>The material being isotropic and homogeneous</li><li>Geometric variations of columns</li><li>Eccentricity of load</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> Not an imperfection: isotropic, homogeneous material is an <b>ideal</b> assumption; the imperfection would be non-homogeneity (residual stresses).</li><li><b>2</b> Imperfection: geometric variations — initial crookedness, out-of-straightness, variation in section.</li><li><b>3</b> Imperfection: eccentricity of the applied load.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Together with residual stresses, these imperfections make real columns weaker than the Euler load; IS 800 captures them with imperfection factors α = 0·21 to 0·76 for buckling classes a–d.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Imperfections: crookedness, eccentricity, residual stress.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-42", year: 2020, no: 42, subject: "steel", topic: "Failure modes of beam-columns",
q: `Which of the following types of failures occur in the beam-column connections?<ol><li>Failure by lateral-torsional buckling</li><li>Failure by combined instability in both the principal directions</li><li>Failure by combined twisting and bending on the torsionally weak sections</li><li>Failure by combined twisting and bending when plane of bending does not contain the shear centre</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 3 and 4 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> Lateral-torsional buckling of the compression flange under moment.</li><li><b>3</b> Combined twisting and bending of torsionally weak open sections.</li><li><b>4</b> Combined twisting and bending when the load plane does not pass through the shear centre (channels, angles).</li><li><b>2</b> Not listed: biaxial instability is treated as a member (overall buckling) mode of the beam-column rather than among these flexural–torsional failure types in the key.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Beam-columns are checked for section strength and for overall member buckling with the interaction equations of IS 800 cl. 9.3.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Open thin-walled sections twist easily — watch the shear centre.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-43", year: 2020, no: 43, subject: "steel", topic: "Non-dimensional LTB slenderness",
q: `In a design of beam columns, the values of plastic section ratio β<sub>b</sub> = 1, the plastic sectional modulus Z<sub>pz</sub> = 3948812 mm<sup>3</sup>, the yield stress f<sub>y</sub> = 250 N/mm<sup>2</sup> and critical moment of M<sub>cr</sub> = 16866×10<sup>6</sup> N.mm. The non-dimensional lateral torsional slenderness ratio will be nearly`,
opts: ["0·141", "0·242", "0·323", "0·424"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·242</h3><div class="formula">λ<sub>LT</sub> = √(β<sub>b</sub> Z<sub>p</sub> f<sub>y</sub> / M<sub>cr</sub>)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>β<sub>b</sub>Z<sub>p</sub>f<sub>y</sub> = 3 948 812 × 250 = 9·872 × 10⁸ N·mm.</li><li>λ<sub>LT</sub> = √(9·872 × 10⁸/1·6866 × 10¹⁰) = √0·0585 = <b>0·242</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Since λ<sub>LT</sub> &lt; 0·4, IS 800 cl. 8.2.2 allows lateral-torsional buckling to be ignored: the beam can reach its full plastic moment.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·141 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·323 does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·424 does not match the working above.</li></ul>
<p>For λ<sub>LT</sub> between 0·4 and about 2, the bending strength is reduced by the factor χ<sub>LT</sub> from the buckling curve with α<sub>LT</sub> = 0·21 (rolled) or 0·49 (welded).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> λ<sub>LT</sub> ≤ 0·4: no LTB reduction.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-44", year: 2020, no: 44, subject: "steel", topic: "Railway impact factor (CDA)",
q: `As per Indian Railway Board, the impact factor i (also known as coefficient of dynamic augment, CDA) in steel girders for single track span is (L is span)`,
opts: ["[0·15 + 8/(6 + L)] ≯ 1·0", "[0·75 + 6/(8 + L)] ≯ 1·0", "[0·15 + 6/(8 + L)] ≯ 1·0", "[0·75 + 8/(6 + L)] ≯ 1·0"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) CDA = 0·15 + 8/(6 + L), not more than 1·0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Indian Railways Bridge Rules give the coefficient of dynamic augment for steel bridges:</p>
<div class="formula">Single track: CDA = 0·15 + 8/(6 + L) ;  main girders of double/multiple tracks: reduced to 0·72 × that value (for two tracks), etc.</div>
<p>For a 10 m span, CDA = 0·15 + 0·5 = 0·65 — the live load is multiplied by 1·65 for design. Shorter spans have larger impact effects (CDA capped at 1·0).</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Single track: CDA = 0.15 + 8/(6 + L).</li><li>Substitute L and compare with the options.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Constants 0.75 and 6/(8 + L) are not in the Bridge Rules.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 6/(8 + L) is not the Bridge Rules term.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.75 is not the Bridge Rules constant.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Railway CDA: 0·15 + 8/(6 + L).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-45", year: 2020, no: 45, subject: "steel", topic: "Embedment depth of a column in a pocket base", selfCorrected: true,
q: `An ISHB 300 with plastic section modulus of 921·68×10<sup>3</sup> mm<sup>3</sup>, flange width of 250 mm, the yield stress of 250 N/mm<sup>2</sup> is embedded in a pocket base to develop its strength with M 25 concrete in design of beam-column. The required depth will be nearly`,
opts: ["475 mm", "425 mm", "375 mm", "325 mm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 475 mm (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A column embedded in a concrete pocket develops its plastic moment through a couple of bearing stresses on the concrete over the embedded depth d:</p>
<div class="formula">M<sub>p</sub> = Z<sub>p</sub> f<sub>y</sub> = bearing couple on the concrete over depth d</div>
<ol class="work"><li>M<sub>p</sub> = 921·68 × 10³ × 250 = 230·4 kN·m.</li><li>With a bearing stress of 0·45f<sub>ck</sub> = 11·25 N/mm² on the flange width and an elastic distribution, d = √(6M<sub>p</sub>/(σ b<sub>f</sub>)) = √(6 × 230·4 × 10⁶/(11·25 × 250)) = 702 mm; with the plastic (rectangular-couple) distribution d = √(4M/(σb)) = 573 mm; the key's 475 mm corresponds to a higher bearing stress (about 0·67f<sub>ck</sub>).</li></ol>
<p>Our first pick was (b); the result depends on the assumed bearing stress distribution, and the key's (a) is accepted.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> 425 gives insufficient embedment for the key's assumptions.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 375 gives insufficient embedment for the key's assumptions.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 325 mm gives insufficient embedment for the key's assumptions.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Pocket bases: embedment from the bearing couple on the concrete.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-46", year: 2020, no: 46, subject: "steel", topic: "Elastic critical stress",
q: `In beam-columns or eccentric loaded columns, an elastic critical stress in compression f<sub>cc</sub> is (where E = modulus of elasticity of steel, λ = slenderness ratio in the plane of bending)`,
opts: ["πE/λ", "π<sup>2</sup>E/λ<sup>2</sup>", "πE/λ<sup>2</sup>", "π<sup>2</sup>E/λ"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) f<sub>cc</sub> = π²E/λ²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Dividing Euler's load by the area: f<sub>cc</sub> = P<sub>cr</sub>/A = π²EI/(A(KL)²) = π²E/(KL/r)² = π²E/λ².</p><div class="formula">f_cc = π²E/λ²</div>
<p>Example: λ = 100, E = 2 × 10⁵ MPa → f<sub>cc</sub> = 197 MPa. IS 800 uses f<sub>cc</sub> to compute the non-dimensional slenderness λ = √(f<sub>y</sub>/f<sub>cc</sub>) and the moment amplification for beam-columns.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Divide P_cr by A: π²EI/(A(KL)²).</li><li>With r² = I/A: <b>f_cc = π²E/(KL/r)²</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> π and λ must both be squared.</li><li><b>(c)</b> π must be squared.</li><li><b>(d)</b> λ must be squared.</li></ul>
<p>For beam-columns IS 800 Annex B uses f<sub>cc</sub> in the amplification factor C<sub>m</sub>/(1 − f<sub>ac</sub>/0·6f<sub>cc</sub>), which grows rapidly as the axial stress approaches the Euler stress.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Euler stress = π²E/λ².</p>` },

{ id: "2020-47", year: 2020, no: 47, subject: "solid", topic: "Shape factor of a diamond section",
q: `A square of side a is placed such that its diagonal is horizontal. The shape factor of the square will be<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2020-47.png" alt="Square of side a placed with one diagonal horizontal (diamond), bending about the horizontal diagonal">`,
opts: ["3·2", "2·0", "1·5", "1·0"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2·0</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Bending about a diagonal: the section is two triangles of base d = a√2 and height h = d/2.</p>
<ol class="work"><li>Elastic: I = a⁴/12; y<sub>max</sub> = a/√2 → Z = a³√2/12 = 0·1179a³.</li><li>Plastic: Z<sub>p</sub> = 2 × (triangle area d²/4) × (centroid distance h/3) = 2 × (a²/2) × (a/(3√2)) = a³/(3√2) = 0·2357a³.</li><li>Shape factor = Z<sub>p</sub>/Z = <b>2·0</b>.</li></ol>
<p>Shape factors: rectangle 1·5, circle 1·7, diamond 2·0, I-section about 1·12–1·15, triangle 2·34.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3·2 is too high for any common section.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·5 is a rectangle (square with sides horizontal).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1·0 would mean no plastic reserve.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Diamond 2·0; rectangle 1·5; circle 1·7.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-48", year: 2020, no: 48, subject: "steel", topic: "Assumptions for plastic moment",
q: `Which one of the following is the correct assumption made in evaluation of fully plastic moment?`,
opts: ["The upper and lower yield stresses and the modulus of elasticity have different values in compression and tension", "The material is homogeneous and isotropic in both the elastic and plastic states", "There will be resultant axial force on the beam", "Some layers of the material are not free to expand and contract longitudinally and laterally under stress"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) homogeneous and isotropic in both states</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Assumptions of plastic theory: the material is homogeneous and isotropic, elastic–perfectly plastic (no strain hardening); yield stress and E are the <b>same</b> in tension and compression; plane sections remain plane; there is <b>no</b> resultant axial force (so the plastic neutral axis divides the area equally); each layer is free to expand and contract.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Values are assumed equal in tension and compression.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Pure bending — no axial force.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Layers are assumed free to deform independently.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Plastic NA: equal-area axis.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-49", year: 2020, no: 49, subject: "rcc", topic: "Limiting cracking in concrete",
q: `As per IS-456:2000, cracking of concrete in tension zone cannot be avoided but can be limited by<ol><li>Adhering to the codal requirements of minimum steel area</li><li>Proper and prolonged curing of concrete</li><li>Increasing water cement ratio to increase workability</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: minimum steel and spacing limits distribute cracks into many fine ones (IS 456 cl. 26.5, 35.3.2).</li><li><b>2</b> True: good, prolonged curing reduces plastic and drying shrinkage cracking.</li><li><b>3</b> False: a higher w/c increases shrinkage and lowers tensile strength, <b>increasing</b> cracking; workability should be obtained with admixtures, not extra water.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> IS 456 limits surface crack widths to 0·3 mm for normal exposure and 0·2 mm or less for aggressive exposure.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Fine distributed steel + good curing = controlled cracks.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-50", year: 2020, no: 50, subject: "solid", topic: "Assumptions of Euler's theory",
q: `Which of the following assumptions are made with respect to Euler's theory applied to columns?<ol><li>The section of the column is uniform</li><li>The length of the column is very large compared to the lateral dimensions</li><li>The direct stress is large when compared with the bending stress</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 2 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 and 2 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: uniform cross-section.</li><li><b>2</b> True: long column (length ≫ lateral dimensions), so failure is by buckling.</li><li><b>3</b> False: Euler assumes the direct stress is <b>small</b> compared with the bending stress at buckling (crushing is ignored).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other assumptions: initially straight, concentric load, elastic homogeneous isotropic material, self-weight neglected.</p>
<p>For short and intermediate columns, Rankine–Gordon's formula 1/P = 1/P<sub>c</sub> + 1/P<sub>E</sub> combines crushing and buckling, overcoming Euler's limitation.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Euler: long, straight, elastic — buckling dominates crushing.</p>` }
);

