// ESE 2020 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (KJL-S-CVL), Q51–75
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A).
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2020-51", year: 2020, no: 51, subject: "rcc", topic: "Equivalent shear for torsion",
q: `A rectangular beam with b = 200 mm and effective depth d = 300 mm is subjected to limit state shear of 80 kN and torsional moment of 6 kNm. The equivalent value of shear will be`,
opts: ["128 kN", "116 kN", "104 kN", "92 kN"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 128 kN</h3><div class="formula">V<sub>e</sub> = V<sub>u</sub> + 1·6 T<sub>u</sub>/b  (IS 456 cl. 41.3.1)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>1·6 T<sub>u</sub>/b = 1·6 × 6/0·2 = 48 kN.</li><li>V<sub>e</sub> = 80 + 48 = <b>128 kN</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The nominal equivalent shear stress τ<sub>ve</sub> = 128 000/(200 × 300) = 2·13 MPa must not exceed τ<sub>c,max</sub> (2·8 MPa for M20); if it exceeds τ<sub>c</sub>, closed stirrups and longitudinal steel are designed for torsion. The equivalent moment is M<sub>e1</sub> = M<sub>u</sub> + T<sub>u</sub>(1 + D/b)/1·7.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 116 uses other factors than 1·6/b.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 104 uses other factors than 1·6/b.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 92 kN uses other factors than 1·6/b.</li></ul>
<p>IS 456 also requires that when τ<sub>ve</sub> exceeds τ<sub>c</sub>, transverse reinforcement is provided as closed two-legged stirrups with A<sub>sv</sub> = T<sub>u</sub>s<sub>v</sub>/(b<sub>1</sub>d<sub>1</sub>·0·87f<sub>y</sub>) + V<sub>u</sub>s<sub>v</sub>/(2·5d<sub>1</sub>·0·87f<sub>y</sub>).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> IS 456 torsion: V<sub>e</sub> = V + 1·6T/b.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-52", year: 2020, no: 52, subject: "rcc", topic: "Strain limit in axial compression",
q: `As per IS-456:2000, the value of maximum compression strain in concrete in axial compression for limit state of collapse is`,
opts: ["0·001", "0·002", "0·003", "0·004"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·002</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 456 cl. 39.1: in members under pure axial compression, the maximum compressive strain in concrete is taken as <b>0·002</b> (where the parabolic stress–strain curve reaches its peak). In flexure, the extreme fibre strain at collapse is 0·0035; for combined axial load and bending with the neutral axis outside the section, the strain at the highly compressed edge is 0·0035 − 0·75 × strain at the least compressed edge.</p><div class="formula">ε_cu (axial compression) = 0.002</div>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·001 is too low.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·003 is the ACI flexural limit.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0·004 exceeds code values.</li></ul>
<p>At ε = 0·002, Fe 415 steel has a stress of about 0·79f<sub>y</sub> ≈ 0·67f<sub>y</sub> design value — hence the 0·67f<sub>y</sub> term in the column formula.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>IS 456 cl. 39.1: pure axial compression strain limit <b>0.002</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Axial: 0·002; flexure: 0·0035.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-53", year: 2020, no: 53, subject: "rcc", topic: "BM coefficient for end span",
q: `The positive bending moment coefficient at the middle of the end-span of a continuous one way slab is (w<sub>l</sub> = live load, w<sub>d</sub> = dead load)`,
opts: ["(w<sub>l</sub>/10 + w<sub>d</sub>/12) L<sup>2</sup>", "(w<sub>l</sub>/9 + w<sub>d</sub>/10) L<sup>2</sup>", "(w<sub>l</sub>/12 + w<sub>d</sub>/16) L<sup>2</sup>", "(w<sub>l</sub>/9 + w<sub>d</sub>/12) L<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) (w<sub>l</sub>/10 + w<sub>d</sub>/12)L²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 456 Table 12 — bending moment coefficients for continuous beams and slabs:</p>
<table><tr><th>Location</th><th>Dead load</th><th>Imposed load</th></tr><tr><td><b>Near middle of end span</b></td><td><b>+1/12</b></td><td><b>+1/10</b></td></tr><tr><td>At support next to end support</td><td>−1/10</td><td>−1/9</td></tr><tr><td>Middle of interior span</td><td>+1/16</td><td>+1/12</td></tr><tr><td>Interior supports</td><td>−1/12</td><td>−1/9</td></tr></table>
<p>Imposed loads have larger coefficients because pattern loading can produce worse effects.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> 1/9 and 1/10 are support (negative) coefficients.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1/12 and 1/16 are for interior spans.</li><li><b>(d)</b> mixes support and span values.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> End span: DL/12 + LL/10.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-54", year: 2020, no: 54, subject: "rcc", topic: "Design requirements of retaining walls", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which of the following are the general design requirements of retaining wall?<ol><li>The factor of safety against sliding should be at least 1·5</li><li>The factor of safety against overturning should be at least 2·0</li><li>The bearing pressure at toe should be less than the bearing capacity of the soil</li><li>The length of retaining wall to be cast in one go should not exceed 10 m otherwise cracks may develop</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 3 and 4 only (official key)</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: FOS against sliding ≥ 1·5 (IS 456 cl. 20.2 — 1·4 for dead load with 0·9 factor).</li><li><b>3</b> True: maximum base pressure at the toe must not exceed the safe bearing capacity, and there should be no tension at the heel.</li><li><b>4</b> True (as keyed): long walls are cast in lengths with contraction/expansion joints (about 10 m or less) to control shrinkage and temperature cracking.</li><li><b>2</b> Not counted by the key: IS 456 cl. 20.1 requires the stabilising moment to be at least 1·4 times (with 0·9 × dead load), i.e. a factor of about 1·55, not 2·0; the value 2·0 is from older practice.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first pick was (a); on review the key's reading of current IS 456 provisions is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> IS 456: overturning and sliding both checked with factor 1·4 on 0·9DL.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-55", year: 2020, no: 55, subject: "rcc", topic: "Movement joints in water tanks",
q: `Which of the following are the desirable properties for efficient functioning in design for movement joint of water tank?<ol><li>The joint should accommodate repeated movement of the structure without loss of water-tightness</li><li>The design should provide for exclusion of grit and debris which would prevent the closing of the joint</li><li>The material used in the construction of movement joints should not slump unduly in hot weather or become brittle in cold weather</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: joints must take repeated expansion/contraction without leaking (water bars, sealants).</li><li><b>2</b> True: grit and debris must be excluded, or the joint cannot close and may spall.</li><li><b>3</b> True: sealing compounds must stay stable — not slump in heat or become brittle in cold.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> IS 3370 (Part 1) lists these requirements and classifies joints as contraction, expansion and sliding joints, with water bars of PVC, rubber or copper.</p>
<p>Typical joint spacing in water tanks is 7·5 m for contraction joints and about 30 m for expansion joints, with sealing compounds such as polysulphide or bitumen-based mastics.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Movement joints: watertight, clean, durable sealants.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-56", year: 2020, no: 56, subject: "rcc", topic: "Nominal shear stress",
q: `A simply supported beam having 200 mm width and 450 mm effective depth supports a total uniformly distributed load of 2,00,000 N. The nominal shear stress will be nearly`,
opts: ["0·8 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "1·1 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "1·8 N/mm<sup>2</sup>", "2·2 N/mm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·1 N/mm²</h3><div class="formula">τ<sub>v</sub> = V<sub>u</sub>/(b d)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Maximum shear at the supports V = W/2 = 100 000 N.</li><li>τ<sub>v</sub> = 100 000/(200 × 450) = <b>1·11 N/mm²</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> This is compared with τ<sub>c</sub> from IS 456 Table 19 (depending on p<sub>t</sub> and f<sub>ck</sub>); if τ<sub>v</sub> &gt; τ<sub>c</sub>, shear reinforcement is designed for (τ<sub>v</sub> − τ<sub>c</sub>)bd.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·8 uses wrong areas.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1·8 N/mm² uses wrong areas.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2·2 N/mm² uses the total load W instead of W/2.</li></ul>
<p>For M20 concrete τ<sub>c,max</sub> = 2·8 N/mm², so 1·11 N/mm² is acceptable; the section need not be enlarged, and only shear stirrups for the excess over τ<sub>c</sub> are needed.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Simply supported UDL: V<sub>max</sub> = W/2.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-57", year: 2020, no: 57, subject: "rcc", topic: "Rules for cover", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which of the following are correct for cover to reinforcement?<ol><li>The reinforcement shall have a minimum clear cover of 20 mm or diameter of such bar whichever is more</li><li>At each end of reinforcing bar not less than 25 mm nor less than twice the diameter of such bar</li><li>Increased cover thickness may be provided when surface of concrete is exposed to the action of harmful chemicals.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3 (official key)</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: older IS 456 rule — clear cover not less than 20 mm (or as per exposure) nor less than the bar diameter.</li><li><b>2</b> True: at each end of a bar, cover not less than 25 mm nor twice the bar diameter (IS 456:1978 cl. 25.4; retained in practice).</li><li><b>3</b> True: increased cover for aggressive chemicals (IS 456 Table 16 — up to 75 mm for extreme exposure).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Our first pick was (c), doubting statement 2; on review it is a standard code rule for bar ends, and the key is accepted. Current IS 456 relates nominal cover to exposure class (20–75 mm).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Cover ≥ bar diameter; ends ≥ 25 mm or 2φ.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-58", year: 2020, no: 58, subject: "rcc", topic: "Steel area for a given moment",
q: `A beam of size 250 mm width and 460 mm effective depth is subjected to limit state moment of 146 kNm. If M 20 grade concrete and Fe 415 steel are used, the area of steel required will be`,
opts: ["435 mm<sup>2</sup>", "935 mm<sup>2</sup>", "1100 mm<sup>2</sup>", "1235 mm<sup>2</sup>"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1100 mm²</h3><div class="formula">A<sub>st</sub> = (0·5f<sub>ck</sub>/f<sub>y</sub>)[1 − √(1 − 4·6M<sub>u</sub>/(f<sub>ck</sub>bd²))] bd</div>
<ol class="work"><li>M<sub>u,lim</sub> = 0·138 × 20 × 250 × 460² = 146·0 kN·m — the beam is exactly balanced.</li><li>4·6M/(f<sub>ck</sub>bd²) = 4·6 × 146 × 10⁶/(20 × 250 × 460²) = 0·635.</li><li>A<sub>st</sub> = 0·02410 × (1 − 0·604) × 115 000 = <b>1096 ≈ 1100 mm²</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Balanced steel for Fe 415, M20: p<sub>t,lim</sub> = 0·96 % → 0·0096 × 115 000 = 1104 mm². ✓</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 435 is too little for 146 kN·m.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 935 mm² is too little for 146 kN·m.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1235 mm² would make the section over-reinforced.</li></ul>
<p>Because the moment equals M<sub>u,lim</sub>, the neutral axis depth reaches x<sub>u,max</sub> = 0·48d = 221 mm; any greater moment would need a doubly reinforced section.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> M<sub>u,lim</sub> = 0·138f<sub>ck</sub>bd² (Fe 415).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-59", year: 2020, no: 59, subject: "bm", topic: "Air permeability method",
q: `Air permeability method is used to determine`,
opts: ["Soundness of cement", "Setting time", "Fineness of cement", "Resistance of cement"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) fineness of cement</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The <b>Blaine air-permeability</b> apparatus (IS 4031 Part 2) measures the time for a fixed volume of air to pass through a compacted bed of cement; the finer the cement, the greater the resistance. The result is the specific surface (m²/kg) — at least 225 m²/kg for OPC.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Soundness: Le Chatelier and autoclave tests.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Setting time: Vicat apparatus.</li><li><b>(d)</b> "Resistance" (strength) is measured by mortar cube compression tests.</li></ul>
<p>Fineness can also be estimated by sieving on the 90 μm sieve (residue ≤ 10 %).</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Blaine → specific surface.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-60", year: 2020, no: 60, subject: "steel", topic: "LTB assumptions",
q: `Which of the following assumptions are correct for the lateral torsional buckling of an I-section beam?<ol><li>The beam is initially distorted</li><li>Its behaviour is elastic</li><li>It is loaded by equal and opposite end moments in the plane of the web</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> False: the classical theory assumes a beam initially <b>straight and undistorted</b> (no imperfections).</li><li><b>2</b> True: elastic behaviour.</li><li><b>3</b> True: uniform moment from equal and opposite end moments in the plane of the web.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Also assumed: simple (fork) supports preventing twist but free to warp, no residual stresses, loads applied at the shear centre. See ESE 2024 Q82 for the same assumptions.</p>
<p>The elastic critical moment for this case is M<sub>cr</sub> = (π/L)√(EI<sub>y</sub>GJ)·√(1 + π²EΓ/(GJL²)), which grows with lateral stiffness, torsional stiffness and warping stiffness.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Basic LTB case: perfect, elastic, uniform moment.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-61", year: 2020, no: 61, subject: "cpm", topic: "Duration of excavation",
q: `In an excavation of 3,000 cub.mtr of common earth for a canal project, 6 men can be effectively employed on the job. If an output of a man is taken as 100 cub.mtr per day, the duration of excavation activity will be`,
opts: ["5 days", "6 days", "7 days", "8 days"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 5 days</h3><ol class="work"><li>Gang output = 6 × 100 = 600 m³/day.</li><li>Duration = 3000/600 = <b>5 days</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Activity durations are estimated as quantity ÷ (crew size × productivity), using the maximum crew that can work effectively on the job (here 6 men). (The output of 100 m³ per man-day is unusually high — typical manual excavation is 2–3 m³ per man-day — the item is purely arithmetical.)</p><div class="formula">Duration = quantity/(gang size × output per man)</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 6 imply lower productivity or smaller gangs.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 7 imply lower productivity or smaller gangs.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8 days imply lower productivity or smaller gangs.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Duration = quantity ÷ (crew × productivity).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-62", year: 2020, no: 62, subject: "cpm", topic: "Functions of the project plan",
q: `The project plan for construction<ol><li>Clearly defines project's scope of work. It breaks down project objectives into clear, identifiable, attainable and verifiable goals</li><li>Identifies critical activities, thus enabling management of projects by exceptions</li><li>Provides the basis for co-ordinating the efforts of clients, consultants, architects, designers, quality surveyors, specialists, suppliers, contractors and project staff</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 3</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the plan defines scope and breaks objectives into verifiable goals (WBS).</li><li><b>2</b> True: it identifies critical activities, so management attention can focus on exceptions.</li><li><b>3</b> True: it coordinates all parties — client, consultants, designers, QS, specialists, suppliers, contractors and staff.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The project plan also sets the baseline for monitoring, resource allocation, cash flow and risk management.</p>
<p>Without such a plan, there is no reference against which progress, cost and quality can be measured, and responsibility between the parties becomes unclear.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Plan = scope + schedule + coordination baseline.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-63", year: 2020, no: 63, subject: "cpm", topic: "Network analysis techniques",
q: `Which one of the following techniques is <i>not</i> covered in Project Network Analysis?`,
opts: ["Critical Path Method", "Program Evaluation and Review Techniques", "Procedure Network Analysis", "Measurement Book"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) measurement book</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Network analysis techniques include CPM, PERT, precedence (activity-on-node) networks and related methods. The <b>measurement book</b> is a record of quantities of work done, used for payment of contractors — a contract-administration document, not a network technique.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> CPM is a core network-analysis method.</li><li><b>(b)</b> PERT is a core network-analysis method.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Precedence/procedure network analysis is a network method.</li></ul>
<p>MB entries are the basis for running bills and final bills in Indian public works practice.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> MB = record of measurements for payment.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-64", year: 2020, no: 64, subject: "cpm", topic: "Critical path properties",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct for Network Critical Path?<ol><li>The path of critical activities, which links the start and end events is critical path</li><li>It is the path of activities having zero float</li><li>It is the path of events having zero slack</li><li>The sum of the duration of the critical activities along a critical path gives the duration of the project</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) all four</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: it is the chain of critical activities from start to end.</li><li><b>2</b> True: critical activities have zero total float.</li><li><b>3</b> True: events on it have zero slack (TL = TE).</li><li><b>4</b> True: its length (sum of durations) is the project duration — the longest path.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> There can be more than one critical path; any delay on a critical activity delays the whole project unless it is compensated by crashing.</p>
<p>Non-critical activities have positive total float and can be delayed within it; critical activities cannot. Monitoring therefore concentrates on the critical path.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Critical path = longest path = zero float.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-65", year: 2020, no: 65, subject: "cpm", topic: "Independent float",
q: `Independent float is an amount of time by which the start of an activity may be delayed without affecting<ol><li>the preceding or the following activity</li><li>the start of a following activity</li><li>the completion of the project</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Independent float</b> = E<sub>j</sub> − L<sub>i</sub> − t: the delay possible even if predecessors finish as late as possible and successors start as early as possible, so it affects neither the preceding nor the following activities.</p>
<ul><li><b>2</b> Delay without affecting the start of a following activity defines <b>free float</b>.</li><li><b>3</b> Delay without affecting completion of the project defines <b>total float</b>.</li></ul>
<p><b>1</b> True: this is the defining property of independent float, which can be negative in calculation and is then taken as zero.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> TF ⊃ FF ⊃ IF.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-66", year: 2020, no: 66, subject: "cpm", topic: "Critical path from a table",
q: `Consider the following activity for the total project : A (–, 10), B (–, 9), C (A, 9), D (A, 8), E (B, 7), F (B, 11), G (D, E; 5). The total project duration for the critical path will be`,
opts: ["25 days", "23 days", "21 days", "19 days"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 23 days</h3><ol class="work"><li>Paths: A–C = 19; A–D–G = 10 + 8 + 5 = 23; B–E–G = 9 + 7 + 5 = 21; B–F = 20.</li><li>Longest = A–D–G = <b>23 days</b> (critical path).</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Floats: C has 4 days, F 3 days, B–E 2 days.</p><div class="formula">Critical path = longest path</div>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 25 days is longer than any path.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 21 days is B–E–G.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 19 days is A–C.</li></ul>
<p>Any delay to A, D or G directly extends the project, while activity C could be delayed up to 4 days without effect.</p><p>Total floats: C = 23 − 19 = 4 days; B and E = 23 − 21 = 2 days; F = 23 − 20 = 3 days; A, D and G have zero float and form the critical path.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Enumerate paths; the longest is critical.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-67", year: 2020, no: 67, subject: "cpm", topic: "Functions of a construction manager",
q: `By performing which of the following functions the construction manager can achieve the project goals?<ol><li>Envisioning the task ahead</li><li>Setting targets and monitoring performance</li><li>Motivating the work force</li><li>Building the line supervisors team</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) all four</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> Envisioning: foreseeing the work, risks and resources.</li><li><b>2</b> Setting targets and monitoring performance: planning and control.</li><li><b>3</b> Motivating the workforce: leadership for productivity.</li><li><b>4</b> Building the team of line supervisors: organising and delegating.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> These correspond to the classical management functions — planning, organising, directing (leading/motivating) and controlling — applied to construction.</p>
<p>Good construction managers also ensure safety, quality and communication with clients, but the four listed functions are the core of achieving project goals.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Construction manager: plan, organise, lead, control.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-68", year: 2020, no: 68, subject: "cpm", topic: "Present worth of instalment purchase",
q: `The cost of the machine is ₹20,00,000, and if it is purchased under instalment basis; the company has to pay 25% of the cost at the time of purchase and the remaining amount in 10 annual equal instalments of ₹2,50,000 each. If rate of interest is 18%, compounded annually the present worth of the machine will be`,
opts: ["₹17,01,000", "₹16,22,500", "₹15,43,000", "₹14,64,500"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) ₹16,22,500</h3><div class="formula">PW = down payment + A × [(1 + i)ⁿ − 1]/[i(1 + i)ⁿ]</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Down payment = 25 % × 20,00,000 = ₹5,00,000.</li><li>P/A factor (18 %, 10 yr) = (1 − 1·18⁻¹⁰)/0·18 = 4·494.</li><li>PW of instalments = 2,50,000 × 4·494 = ₹11,23,500.</li><li>Total PW = 5,00,000 + 11,23,500 = <b>₹16,23,500 ≈ ₹16,22,500</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> So paying by instalments is cheaper in present-worth terms than paying ₹20 lakh cash, at an 18 % cost of money.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> uses other annuity factors.</li><li><b>(c)</b> uses other annuity factors.</li><li><b>(d)</b> uses other annuity factors.</li></ul>
<p>The factor (P/A, 18 %, 10) can also be checked as 1/0·18 × (1 − 1/5·234) = 5·556 × 0·809 = 4·494.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Discount every future payment to the present.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-69", year: 2020, no: 69, subject: "cpm", topic: "Components of a bid price",
q: `Which of the following relations are correct for determining different components of a bid price?<ol><li>Bid price = Direct cost + Indirect cost + Mark up amount</li><li>Direct cost = Project overheads + Common plant and equipment cost + Common work men cost</li><li>Mark up amount = Profit + Contingency + Allowances for risks + General overheads</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: bid price = direct cost + indirect (project overhead) cost + mark-up.</li><li><b>2</b> False: project overheads, common plant and common workmen are <b>indirect</b> costs; direct costs are labour, materials, equipment and subcontracts for specific work items.</li><li><b>3</b> True: mark-up covers profit, contingency, risk allowances and general (head office) overheads.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A contractor's estimate is usually built bottom-up: quantities × unit rates give direct costs, site overheads are added as indirect costs, and finally the mark-up percentage is applied.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Direct (items) + indirect (site overheads) + mark-up (HO, risk, profit).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-70", year: 2020, no: 70, subject: "cpm", topic: "Resource smoothing",
q: `Resource smoothing is`,
opts: ["An optimization and economical utilization of resources", "An adjustment of resources without affecting project duration", "A gradual increase in resources", "A gradual decrease in resources"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) adjustment of resources without affecting duration</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Resource smoothing</b> shifts non-critical activities within their floats to reduce peaks and valleys in the resource histogram, <b>keeping the project duration fixed</b>. In contrast, <b>resource levelling</b> keeps resource usage within a limit, and may extend the duration.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Optimisation is the broad aim, not the specific definition.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A gradual increase describes resource build-up, not smoothing.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A gradual decrease describes run-down, not smoothing.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Smoothing: fixed time; levelling: fixed resources.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-71", year: 2020, no: 71, subject: "cpm", topic: "PERT distribution",
q: `In PERT technique, the time estimate of activities and probability of their occurrence follow`,
opts: ["Binomial distribution", "Normal distribution", "Poisson distribution", "β distribution"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) β distribution</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> PERT assumes each activity's duration follows a <b>beta distribution</b> defined by the optimistic, most likely and pessimistic times; this gives t<sub>e</sub> = (t<sub>o</sub> + 4t<sub>m</sub> + t<sub>p</sub>)/6 and σ = (t<sub>p</sub> − t<sub>o</sub>)/6.</p>
<p>The <b>project</b> duration (sum of many activities) is then taken as <b>normally</b> distributed by the central limit theorem, used to find the probability of meeting a deadline.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Binomial is a discrete distribution.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Poisson is a discrete distribution.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Normal applies to the total project duration (CLT), not each activity.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Activity: beta; project: normal.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-72", year: 2020, no: 72, subject: "cpm", topic: "Indirect costs of accidents",
q: `Indirect cost due to accidents includes`,
opts: ["Legal charges", "Medical expenses for the injured", "Compensation amount to the injured", "Over time payment to make up the loss of time"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) overtime to make up lost time</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Accident costs: <b>direct</b> (insured) costs — medical expenses, compensation, legal charges; <b>indirect</b> (hidden) costs — lost working time of the injured and others, damage to plant and materials, overtime to recover lost production, retraining, reduced morale and productivity, investigation. Heinrich estimated indirect costs at about four times the direct costs.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Legal charges arising from an accident are a direct cost.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Medical expenses of the injured are a direct cost.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Compensation paid to the injured is a direct cost.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Hidden accident costs exceed the visible ones.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-73", year: 2020, no: 73, subject: "fluid", topic: "Equivalent water head",
q: `An oil of specific gravity 0·9 contained in a vessel. At a point the height of oil is 40 m and for the density of water = 1000 kg/m<sup>3</sup>, the corresponding height of water at the point will be`,
opts: ["28 m", "32 m", "36 m", "40 m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 36 m</h3><div class="formula">h<sub>w</sub> = S h<sub>oil</sub></div>
<ol class="work"><li>h<sub>w</sub> = 0·9 × 40 = <b>36 m</b> of water.</li><li>Pressure = 1000 × 9·81 × 36 = 353 kPa.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 28 uses lower specific gravities.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 32 m uses lower specific gravities.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 40 m ignores the density difference.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A lighter liquid needs a taller column for the same pressure, so the equivalent water column is shorter.</p>
<p>The general relation is p = ρgh, so for equal pressure ρ<sub>oil</sub>h<sub>oil</sub> = ρ<sub>w</sub>h<sub>w</sub>, giving h<sub>w</sub> = 900 × 40/1000 = 36 m.</p><p>Mercury (S = 13·6) works the other way: 40 m of oil would equal only 2·65 m of mercury, which is why manometers use dense liquids for large pressures.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Same pressure: h<sub>1</sub>S<sub>1</sub> = h<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-74", year: 2020, no: 74, subject: "fluid", topic: "Similar velocity triangles in pumps",
q: `When speed changes in case of centrifugal pump, which of the following points are correct?<ol><li>The shape of the velocity triangle will remain same</li><li>Various angles will remain same</li><li>Magnitude of velocity will change proportionately</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> When the speed of a given pump changes at the same flow coefficient (homologous points), all velocities (u, V<sub>f</sub>, V<sub>w</sub>, V<sub>r</sub>) change in proportion to N, so the velocity triangles stay geometrically <b>similar</b>: same shape and angles, magnitudes scaled.</p>
<div class="formula">Q ∝ N ,  H ∝ N² ,  P ∝ N³ (affinity laws)</div>
<ul><li><b>1</b>, <b>2</b> and <b>3</b> all follow from similarity.</li></ul>
<p>Specific speed N<sub>s</sub> = N√Q/H<sup>3/4</sup> stays the same at these homologous points, which is why model testing of pumps is possible.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Affinity laws come from similar velocity triangles.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-75", year: 2020, no: 75, subject: "fluid", topic: "Use of flow nets",
q: `Which one of the following is the use of flow net analysis in fluid mechanics?`,
opts: ["To determine the streamlines and equipotential lines", "To determine downward lift pressure above hydraulic structure", "To determine the viscosity for given boundaries of flow", "To design the hydraulic structure"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) to determine streamlines and equipotential lines</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A <b>flow net</b> is the grid of streamlines and equipotential lines for 2-D irrotational (potential) flow; they cross at right angles forming curvilinear squares. From it one obtains velocities, pressures, seepage discharge, uplift pressures and exit gradients.</p>
<ul><li><b>(b)</b> Flow nets give <b>uplift</b> (upward) pressure below structures, not "downward lift above".</li><li><b>(c)</b> Flow nets apply to ideal (inviscid) flow and cannot determine viscosity.</li><li><b>(d)</b> They help in the design but are not themselves the design.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Flow net: ψ and φ lines at right angles.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2020d.js ----
// ESE 2020 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (KJL-S-CVL), Q76–100
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A).
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2020-76", year: 2020, no: 76, subject: "fluid", topic: "Mach number",
q: `A jet propelled aircraft is flying at a speed of 1100 km/hour at t = 20°C, k = 1·4 and R = 287 J/kg K. The Mach number at a point on the jet will be nearly`,
opts: ["0·3", "0·5", "0·7", "0·9"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·9</h3><div class="formula">c = √(kRT) ,  M = V/c</div>
<ol class="work"><li>V = 1100/3·6 = 305·6 m/s.</li><li>c = √(1·4 × 287 × 293) = √117 727 = 343·1 m/s.</li><li>M = 305·6/343·1 = <b>0·89 ≈ 0·9</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The flow is therefore high-subsonic (0·8 &lt; M &lt; 1 is the transonic range), where compressibility effects are significant — the density change is far above the 5 % tolerated in incompressible analysis (M &lt; 0·3).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·3 is the usual incompressibility limit, not this value.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·5 results from arithmetic slips (e.g. using T in °C).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·7 results from arithmetic slips (e.g. using T in °C).</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Always use absolute temperature in c = √(kRT).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-77", year: 2020, no: 77, subject: "fluid", topic: "Terminal velocity",
q: `When the drag force becomes equal to the weight of the body, the acceleration ceases and the net external force acting in the body becomes`,
opts: ["Zero and the body will move at constant speed", "Light and the body will move forward", "Zero and the body will move fast", "High and the body will move at constant speed"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) zero, body moves at constant speed</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A body falling through a fluid accelerates until drag (plus buoyancy) balances its weight. The net force is then <b>zero</b>, acceleration ceases (Newton's first law), and the body moves at a constant <b>terminal velocity</b>. For a small sphere in Stokes flow, V<sub>t</sub> = (ρ<sub>s</sub> − ρ)g d²/(18μ) — the basis of the falling-sphere viscometer and of settling analysis.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> "Light" is meaningless as a force description.</li><li><b>(c)</b> With zero net force the speed stays constant; it does not keep increasing.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The net force is zero, not high.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Terminal velocity: weight = drag + buoyancy.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-78", year: 2020, no: 78, subject: "fluid", topic: "Specific energy diagram",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct regarding flow in open channel?`,
opts: ["The curve for kinetic energy is a parabola", "The curve for potential energy is a parabola", "Specific energy is asymptotic to the vertical axis", "At critical depth the specific energy is maximum"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) the kinetic energy curve is a parabola</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a rectangular channel, E = y + q²/(2gy²). On the E–y diagram, the potential (depth) term E<sub>p</sub> = y is a straight line at 45°, while the kinetic term q²/(2gy²) is a curve of hyperbolic type (y²·E<sub>k</sub> = constant), commonly described in texts as a "parabola". The total specific energy curve approaches the 45° line for large depths and the horizontal (E) axis as y → 0.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The potential energy curve is a straight 45° line.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The specific energy curve is asymptotic to the 45° line and to the horizontal axis, not the vertical axis.</li><li><b>(d)</b> At critical depth the specific energy is a <b>minimum</b> for the given discharge.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> E–y curve: asymptotes are E = y and the E-axis.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-79", year: 2020, no: 79, subject: "fluid", topic: "Critical flow",
q: `Which one of the following statement is correct regarding critical state of flow through a channel section?`,
opts: ["Specific energy is a minimum for a given discharge", "Specific energy is a maximum for a given discharge", "The Froude number is greater than two", "The discharge is a minimum for a given specific force"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) specific energy is a minimum for a given discharge</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At critical flow: dE/dy = 0 ⇒ Q²T/(gA³) = 1, i.e. Froude number F = 1. Properties of critical flow:</p><ul><li>specific energy is minimum for the given discharge;</li><li>discharge is maximum for the given specific energy;</li><li>specific force (momentum function) is minimum for the given discharge;</li><li>for a rectangular channel, y<sub>c</sub> = (q²/g)<sup>1/3</sup> and E<sub>min</sub> = 1·5y<sub>c</sub>.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Specific energy is a minimum, not a maximum.</li><li><b>(c)</b> F = 1 at critical flow.</li><li><b>(d)</b> For a given specific force the discharge is a maximum, not a minimum.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Critical: E<sub>min</sub>, Q<sub>max</sub>, F = 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-80", year: 2020, no: 80, subject: "fluid", topic: "Centrifugal pump features",
q: `Which one of the following statement is correct regarding centrifugal pumps?`,
opts: ["The discharge is fluctuating and pulsating", "It is used for large discharge through smaller heads", "The efficiency is low", "It runs at low speed"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) large discharge at smaller heads</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Centrifugal pumps, compared with reciprocating pumps:</p><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Centrifugal</th><th>Reciprocating</th></tr><tr><td>Discharge</td><td>large, continuous, smooth</td><td>small, pulsating</td></tr><tr><td>Head</td><td>low–moderate</td><td>high</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>high (direct motor drive)</td><td>low</td></tr><tr><td>Efficiency</td><td>high at design point</td><td>high but drops with wear</td></tr></table>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Pulsating discharge is a feature of reciprocating pumps.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Efficiency of centrifugal pumps is high (70–90 %).</li><li><b>(d)</b> They run at high speed.</li></ul>
<p>Centrifugal pumps must be primed before starting, unlike reciprocating pumps, and their head–discharge curve falls as discharge rises.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Centrifugal: high Q, low H, high N.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-81", year: 2020, no: 81, subject: "fluid", topic: "Hydraulic press",
q: `A hydraulic press has a ram of 300 mm diameter and a plunger of 45 mm diameter. When the force applied at the plunger is 50 N, the weight lifted by the hydraulic press will be nearly`,
opts: ["2133 N", "2223 N", "2316 N", "2406 N"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2223 N</h3><div class="formula">W/A<sub>ram</sub> = F/A<sub>plunger</sub>  (Pascal's law)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>W = F (D/d)² = 50 × (300/45)² = 50 × 44·44.</li><li>W = <b>2222 N</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Pascal's law: pressure applied to a confined fluid is transmitted equally in all directions, so the force is magnified by the area ratio. Work is conserved: the ram moves 44·4 times less than the plunger.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2133 N does not equal 50 × (20/3)².</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2316 N does not equal 50 × (20/3)².</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2406 N does not equal 50 × (20/3)².</li></ul>
<p>In practice friction at the ram packing reduces the lift slightly, and the mechanical advantage is often increased further by a lever on the plunger.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Force ratio = (diameter ratio)².</p>` },

{ id: "2020-82", year: 2020, no: 82, subject: "fluid", topic: "Hydraulic efficiency of Francis turbine",
q: `Hydraulic efficiency of Francis turbine is`,
opts: ["Directly proportional to velocity of whirl at inlet and inversely proportional to net head on turbine", "Directly proportional to velocity of whirl at inlet and net head on turbine", "Inversely proportional to velocity of whirl at inlet and net head on turbine", "Inversely proportional to velocity of whirl at inlet and directly proportional to net head on turbine"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a)</h3><div class="formula">η<sub>h</sub> = V<sub>w1</sub>u<sub>1</sub>/(gH)  (radial discharge, V<sub>w2</sub> = 0)</div>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The runner work per unit weight is (V<sub>w1</sub>u<sub>1</sub> − V<sub>w2</sub>u<sub>2</sub>)/g; for a Francis turbine designed for radial (zero-whirl) discharge, V<sub>w2</sub> = 0. Dividing by the available head H gives η<sub>h</sub> ∝ V<sub>w1</sub> and ∝ 1/H.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> η<sub>h</sub> is inversely, not directly, proportional to H.</li><li><b>(c)</b> η<sub>h</sub> increases with V<sub>w1</sub>.</li><li><b>(d)</b> reverses both relations.</li></ul>
<p>Typical Francis turbines achieve η<sub>h</sub> of 90–95 %.</p>
<p>Overall efficiency η<sub>o</sub> = η<sub>h</sub> × η<sub>m</sub> × η<sub>v</sub>, combining hydraulic, mechanical and volumetric losses.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> η<sub>h</sub> = V<sub>w1</sub>u<sub>1</sub>/gH.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-83", year: 2020, no: 83, subject: "fluid", topic: "Specific speed of turbine",
q: `A turbine develops 7225 kW power under a head of 25 m at 135 rpm. The specific speed of the turbine will be nearly`,
opts: ["245 rpm", "225 rpm", "205 rpm", "185 rpm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 205 rpm</h3><div class="formula">N<sub>s</sub> = N√P/H<sup>5/4</sup>  (P in kW)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>√7225 = 85.</li><li>25<sup>5/4</sup> = 25 × 25<sup>1/4</sup> = 25 × 2·236 = 55·9.</li><li>N<sub>s</sub> = 135 × 85/55·9 = <b>205 rpm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> N<sub>s</sub> ≈ 205 (in kW units) lies in the Francis range (about 60–300); Pelton wheels have N<sub>s</sub> below ~35 and Kaplan turbines 300–1000.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 245 comes from wrong powers of H.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 225 comes from wrong powers of H.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 185 rpm comes from wrong powers of H.</li></ul>
<p>Specific speed is the speed of a geometrically similar turbine producing 1 kW under 1 m head, used to select the turbine type.</p><p>With P = 7225 kW, the discharge at 90 % efficiency is about Q = P/(ηρgH) ≈ 32·7 m³/s, a typical medium-head Francis installation.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Turbine N<sub>s</sub>: H<sup>5/4</sup>; pump N<sub>s</sub>: H<sup>3/4</sup>.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-84", year: 2020, no: 84, subject: "fluid", topic: "Bodies producing lift and drag", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following is an example of bodies where both drag and lift forces are produced?`,
opts: ["Hydrofoils", "A tall chimney exposed to wind", "Flow of water past a bridge pier", "Motion of aeroplanes, submarines, torpedoes"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) hydrofoils (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A body produces lift when the flow around it is asymmetric — an inclined or cambered foil. A <b>hydrofoil</b> is designed specifically to generate lift (with accompanying drag) in water, just as an aerofoil does in air.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> A tall chimney is a symmetric bluff body; wind exerts essentially only drag.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A bridge pier in a stream is also symmetric about the flow — only drag.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Groups aeroplanes with submarines and torpedoes. Submarines and torpedoes are symmetric streamlined bodies moving along their axis, so they experience mainly drag; the option as a whole is therefore not a clean example. Textbooks (e.g. Bansal) list "submarines, torpedoes, aeroplanes" under bodies experiencing drag in uniform flow.</li></ul>
<p>Our first pick was (d), counting aeroplanes; on review the key's pure example, hydrofoils, is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Lift needs asymmetry — foils and inclined bodies.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-85", year: 2020, no: 85, subject: "hydro", topic: "Relative humidity",
q: `The relative humidity h is a measure of air's capacity, at its existing temperature, to absorb further moisture, and is defined by the relation (e = vapour pressure, e<sub>s</sub> = saturation vapour pressure)`,
opts: ["(e/e<sub>s</sub>) × 100", "(e<sub>s</sub>/e) × 100", "(2e<sub>s</sub>/e) × 100", "(2e/e<sub>s</sub>) × 100"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) (e/e<sub>s</sub>) × 100</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Relative humidity is the ratio of the actual vapour pressure to the saturation vapour pressure at the same temperature, in percent. It is 100 % at saturation and lower for drier air. Since e<sub>s</sub> rises steeply with temperature, the same moisture content gives a lower RH on a warmer afternoon.</p><div class="formula">RH = p_v/p_sat × 100</div>
<p>RH is used in evaporation formulae such as Dalton's law E = C(e<sub>w</sub> − e<sub>a</sub>), where the deficit depends on humidity.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Ratio of actual to saturation vapour pressure at the same temperature, in %.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> e_s/e inverts the ratio and exceeds 100 %.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The factor 2 has no place in the definition RH = e/e_s.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The factor 2 has no place in the definition RH = e/e_s.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> RH = e/e<sub>s</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-86", year: 2020, no: 86, subject: "hydro", topic: "Deterrents in tank water harvesting",
q: `Which one of the following is <i>not</i> a major deterrent in water harvesting through water tanks?`,
opts: ["Deforestation mainly due to population pressure in the catchments of tank systems", "Siltation", "Lack of maintenance and repairs and breaches of tank embankments", "Shallow depth of water tanks"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) shallow depth of water tanks</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Traditional irrigation tanks (South India) are naturally shallow and wide; shallow depth is a design feature, not a cause of their decline. The main deterrents are:</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Deforestation in the catchments increases runoff erosion and reduces dependable inflow.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Siltation steadily reduces storage capacity.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Neglect of maintenance, encroachments and breaches of bunds.</li></ul>
<p>Other problems include encroachment of tank beds, breakdown of community management and choking of supply channels. Revival schemes focus on desilting, catchment treatment and restoration of bunds and sluices.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Tanks decline from silt, neglect and catchment damage.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-87", year: 2020, no: 87, subject: "hydro", topic: "Well screen requirements",
q: `Which one of the following is <i>not</i> a basic requirement for any well screen?`,
opts: ["Resistance to corrosion, incrustation and deterioration", "Enough structural strength to prevent collapse", "Suitability for excessive movement of sand into the well", "Minimum resistance to flow of water into the well"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) suitability for excessive sand movement</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A well screen must <b>prevent</b> the entry of sand into the well, not allow it. Slot sizes are chosen (e.g. to retain 40–60 % of the aquifer or gravel pack) so that, after development, fines near the screen are removed and the rest is held back.</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Resistance to corrosion and incrustation gives a long life.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Structural strength resists collapse under earth pressure.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Minimum resistance to inflow (large open area, entrance velocity below about 3 cm/s) reduces head loss and drawdown.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Screen: pass water freely, keep sand out.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-88", year: 2020, no: 88, subject: "geo", topic: "Geophysical methods",
q: `Which one of the following methods is <i>not</i> the category of Geophysical methods of sub-surface investigation?`,
opts: ["Electrical resistivity method", "Electric logging", "Gamma-ray logging", "Electrical response surveying"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) electrical response surveying</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Geophysical sub-surface methods include:</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Electrical resistivity surveys (Wenner, Schlumberger arrays) — surface methods for depth of rock and water table.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Electric logging — resistivity and spontaneous potential measured down a borehole.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Gamma-ray logging — natural radioactivity of strata (clays read high), used to identify layers.</li></ul>
<p>Seismic refraction/reflection, gravity and magnetic surveys are also geophysical. "Electrical response surveying" is not a recognised method.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Geophysics: resistivity, seismic, gravity, magnetic, well logs.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-89", year: 2020, no: 89, subject: "env", topic: "Industrial water demand",
q: `In which one of the following industries, the water requirement in kilo litres per unit of production is very high?`,
opts: ["Paper industry", "Steel industry", "Sugar industry", "Fertilizer industry"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) paper industry</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Typical water requirements per tonne of product:</p><table><tr><th>Industry</th><th>Water (kL per tonne)</th></tr><tr><td><b>Paper</b></td><td><b>200–400</b></td></tr><tr><td>Steel</td><td>200–250 (much recirculated, modern plants ≈ 4–10)</td></tr><tr><td>Fertilizer</td><td>80–200</td></tr><tr><td>Sugar</td><td>1–2 per tonne of cane</td></tr></table>
<p>Pulp and paper uses water for pulping, washing, bleaching and sheet formation, making it one of the most water-intensive industries.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Steel needs much less water per unit product than paper.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Sugar needs much less water per unit product than paper.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Fertiliser needs much less water per unit product than paper.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Paper industry is the classic water-intensive one.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-90", year: 2020, no: 90, subject: "hydro", topic: "Drip emitters (ASAE)", selfCorrected: true,
q: `In drip irrigation system, which one of the following emitters is <i>not</i> based on definitions by American Society of Agricultural Engineers (ASAE)?`,
opts: ["Emitter", "Pulsating emitter", "Long path emitter", "Multi-outlet emitter"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) pulsating emitter (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> ASAE Standard EP405 (design of trickle irrigation) defines: <b>emitter</b> (a device to discharge water at a controlled rate), <b>long-path emitter</b> (head dissipated by a long narrow flow path), <b>multi-outlet emitter</b> (one emitter supplying several outlets), <b>orifice emitter</b>, vortex emitter, line-source emitter and porous pipe. "Pulsating emitter" is not one of the defined categories — pulse irrigation is an operating mode rather than an emitter type.</p>
<p><b>Why the others are ASAE terms</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Emitter — the general definition.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Long-path emitter — friction-type.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Multi-outlet emitter — one device, several delivery tubes.</li></ul>
<p>Our first pick was (d); on review multi-outlet emitters appear in the ASAE list, and the key is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> ASAE emitter types: long-path, orifice, vortex, multi-outlet, line-source.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-91", year: 2020, no: 91, subject: "hydro", topic: "Depth of irrigation",
q: `A Persian wheel with an average discharge of 230 litre/minute irrigates 1 hectare wheat crop in 50 hours. The average depth of irrigation will be nearly`,
opts: ["4 cm", "5 cm", "6 cm", "7 cm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 7 cm</h3><ol class="work"><li>Volume = 230 L/min × 60 × 50 = 690 000 L = 690 m³.</li><li>Depth = 690/10 000 = 0·069 m = <b>6·9 cm ≈ 7 cm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The general relation is Q·t = A·d: discharge × time = area × depth of water applied. In duty terms, one cumec irrigating for one day spreads 8·64 hectare-metres.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 4 cm unders-estimate the 690 m³ volume.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 5 cm unders-estimate the 690 m³ volume.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 6 cm unders-estimate the 690 m³ volume.</li></ul>
<p>A Persian wheel is a traditional animal-driven chain of buckets lifting water from open wells, with typical outputs of 200–300 L/min.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Depth = Q t / A.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-92", year: 2020, no: 92, subject: "hydro", topic: "Causes of salinity and sodicity",
q: `Which one of the following is <i>not</i> the main cause for soil salinity and sodicity?`,
opts: ["Irrigation mismanagement", "Poor land levelling", "Use of heavy machinery, resulting in no soil compaction", "Leaching without adequate drainage"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) heavy machinery with no compaction</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Salinity and sodicity develop when salts brought by irrigation water or rising groundwater accumulate in the root zone because evaporation exceeds leaching. Causes include:</p>
<ul><li><b>(a)</b> Over-irrigation and seepage raise the water table (waterlogging), and capillary rise then deposits salts.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Poor levelling leaves high spots where salts accumulate and low spots that waterlog.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Leaching without drainage simply moves salts down to a rising water table that returns them.</li></ul>
<p>Option (c) is self-contradictory: heavy machinery causes compaction, which impedes drainage; "no soil compaction" would not cause salinity. Hence it is not a cause.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Salinity: high water table + evaporation + no drainage.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-93", year: 2020, no: 93, subject: "hydro", topic: "Factors in canal seepage", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following is <i>not</i> the major factor influencing seepage from a canal?`,
opts: ["Characteristics of the soil traversed by the canal system", "Area wetted by the canal", "Location of the canal", "Frequencies of canal usage"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) location of the canal (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Seepage loss from a canal depends on:</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Soil permeability along the canal — sandy soils lose far more than clay.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Wetted area (perimeter × length) — loss is often expressed per m² of wetted area (e.g. cumec per million m²).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Frequency and duration of running — a canal that runs intermittently loses more, as the dry bed has to be re-wetted each time.</li></ul>
<p>Other factors are the depth of the water table, water depth in the canal, sediment load (silting reduces seepage) and the age of the canal. The location as such is not a direct factor; its effect appears only through the soil and water table.</p>
<p>Our first pick was (d); on review frequency of running is listed in texts as a factor, and the key is accepted.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Seepage: soil, wetted area, water table, running period.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-94", year: 2020, no: 94, subject: "hydro", topic: "Causes of failure of weirs",
q: `Which of the following are the causes of failure of weirs?<ol><li>Rupture of floor due to uplift</li><li>Rupture of floor due to suction caused by standing wave</li><li>Scour on the upstream and downstream of the weir</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2 and 3</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: uplift pressure from seepage beneath the floor can burst a floor that is too thin.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the hydraulic jump (standing wave) reduces pressure on top of the floor, adding to the net uplift and causing rupture.</li><li><b>3</b> True: deep scour upstream and downstream can undermine the cut-offs and the floor.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Another cause is <b>piping</b> (undermining) when the exit gradient exceeds the safe value. Bligh's and Khosla's theories address these modes: adequate creep length, floor thickness, cut-off depths and protection (launching aprons, inverted filters).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Weir failures: piping, uplift, jump suction, scour.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-95", year: 2020, no: 95, subject: "hydro", topic: "Choice of canal lining",
q: `Which of the following are the principal factors influencing the choice of a particular method of lining?<ol><li>Availability and cost of the material at the site or within easy reach</li><li>Velocity of flow in the channel</li><li>Cost of maintenance</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2 and 3</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: local material availability (stone, bricks, aggregates) largely decides the economics.</li><li><b>2</b> True: high velocities need hard surface linings (concrete), while low velocities permit earth or soil-cement linings.</li><li><b>3</b> True: maintenance cost over the canal's life is weighed against initial cost.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Other factors include the seepage loss expected, durability, water-tightness required, climate, and the degree of structural strength needed against earth pressure and groundwater uplift.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Lining choice: economy, velocity, maintenance.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-96", year: 2020, no: 96, subject: "hydro", topic: "Objectives of river training",
q: `Which of the following are the objectives for river training?<ol><li>High flood discharge may pass safely and quickly through the reach</li><li>To make the river course stable and reduce bank erosion to minimum</li><li>To check flow through canal</li><li>To provide a sufficient draft for navigation as well as good course for it</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2 and 4 only</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: passing flood discharge safely (high-stage training).</li><li><b>2</b> True: stabilising the course and minimising bank erosion.</li><li><b>3</b> False: checking flow through a canal is a function of regulators, not of river training.</li><li><b>4</b> True: maintaining navigable depth (low-stage training).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> River training is classified as high-water (flood) training, low-water training and mean-water training (for sediment and channel stability). Works include guide bunds, spurs/groynes, levees, pitching and cut-offs.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> River training: flood, stability, navigation.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-97", year: 2020, no: 97, subject: "hydro", topic: "Capillary fringe",
q: `The transition region between unsaturated zone and saturated zone is called`,
opts: ["Capillary fringe", "Water table", "Yadose water zone", "Confining bed"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) capillary fringe</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Above the water table, water rises by capillarity into a zone that is nearly saturated but held at negative pressure — the <b>capillary fringe</b>. It forms the transition between the unsaturated (vadose) zone above and the saturated zone below. Its thickness ranges from a few cm in gravel to over 1 m in silts and clays.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The water table is the surface at atmospheric pressure, the top of the saturated zone.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The vadose zone is the whole unsaturated region.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A confining bed is an impermeable layer bounding a confined aquifer.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Vadose zone: soil water, intermediate, capillary fringe.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-98", year: 2020, no: 98, subject: "env", topic: "Organic content parameters",
q: `Which of the following chemical parameters are associated with the organic content of water?<ol><li>Biological Oxygen Demand (BOD)</li><li>Chemical Oxygen Demand (COD)</li><li>Total Organic Carbon (TOC) and Total Oxygen Demand (TOD)</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> BOD: oxygen used by microbes to oxidise biodegradable organics (5-day, 20 °C).</li><li><b>2</b> COD: oxygen equivalent of organics oxidised by dichromate in acid — includes non-biodegradable matter; COD &gt; BOD.</li><li><b>3</b> TOC measures organic carbon (by combustion to CO₂); TOD measures total oxygen demand by high-temperature catalytic combustion.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Typical ordering: TOD ≥ COD ≥ BOD<sub>u</sub> &gt; BOD<sub>5</sub>. BOD/COD &gt; 0·5 indicates readily biodegradable wastewater.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> BOD, COD, TOC, TOD — all organic indicators.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-99", year: 2020, no: 99, subject: "env", topic: "Chlorine species vs pH",
q: `When chlorine is dissolved in water, it reacts to form hypochlorous acid and hypochlorite ions. At pH &lt; 5, chlorine exists in water as`,
opts: ["Elemental or molecular chlorine", "Remains in the form of hypochlorous acid", "Remains in the form of hypochlorite ions", "Remains in the form of both hypochlorous acid and hypochlorite ions"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) elemental or molecular chlorine</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Cl₂ + H₂O ⇌ HOCl + H⁺ + Cl⁻ ; HOCl ⇌ H⁺ + OCl⁻ (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 7·5).</p>
<ul><li>At very low pH (textbook statement: pH below about 5, strictly below 2), the hydrolysis is pushed back and chlorine remains largely as molecular Cl₂.</li><li>Between pH 5 and 6, HOCl dominates almost completely.</li><li>At pH 7·5, HOCl and OCl⁻ are equal.</li><li>Above pH 9·5, nearly all is OCl⁻.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> HOCl dominates at pH 5–7.</li><li><b>(c)</b> OCl⁻ dominates above pH 9.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Both coexist around pH 6–9.</li></ul>
<p>HOCl is about 80 times more effective a disinfectant than OCl⁻, so chlorination is more efficient at slightly acidic pH.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> pH ↑: Cl₂ → HOCl → OCl⁻.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-100", year: 2020, no: 100, subject: "env", topic: "Reactive hazardous wastes",
q: `Reactive substances are`,
opts: ["Unstable under normal conditions. They can cause explosions and/or liberate toxic fumes, gases, and vapors when mixed with water", "Easily ignited and burn vigorously and persistently", "Liquids with pH less than 2 or greater than 12·5, and those that are capable of corroding metal containers", "Harmful or fatal when ingested or absorbed"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Hazardous wastes are characterised (US EPA RCRA) by four properties:</p>
<ul><li><b>Reactivity</b> — unstable, may explode or release toxic gases when mixed with water (option a).</li><li><b>(b)</b> Ignitability — flash point below 60 °C, burn vigorously.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Corrosivity — pH ≤ 2 or ≥ 12·5, or corrode steel at more than 6·35 mm/yr.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Toxicity — harmful when ingested or absorbed (TCLP test).</li></ul>
<p>India's Hazardous and Other Wastes Rules, 2016 use similar characteristics for classifying industrial wastes.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Hazardous: ignitable, corrosive, reactive, toxic.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2020e.js ----
// ESE 2020 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (KJL-S-CVL), Q101–125
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A).
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2020-101", year: 2020, no: 101, subject: "env", topic: "Factors in noise value",
q: `The noise value of sound waves depends upon:<ol><li>The frequency of sound waves</li><li>The intensity of sound waves</li><li>The time of exposure of sound waves</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the ear is most sensitive at 2–5 kHz, so the A-weighted level dB(A) depends on frequency.</li><li><b>2</b> True: intensity (sound pressure) sets the decibel level, L = 10 log(I/I₀).</li><li><b>3</b> True: the annoyance and damage value depends on duration — hence L<sub>eq</sub>, L<sub>dn</sub> and permissible exposure limits (90 dB(A) for 8 h, halving with every 5 dB increase under OSHA).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Noise is assessed by these three together: noise rating curves combine frequency and level, while dose criteria include time.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Noise impact: level, frequency and duration.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-102", year: 2020, no: 102, subject: "env", topic: "Neutralising alkaline effluent",
q: `Which one of the following type of treatments will be used for neutralization of alkaline effluent?`,
opts: ["Lime stone treatment", "Caustic lime treatment", "Carbon dioxide treatment", "Hydrochloric acid treatment"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) carbon dioxide treatment</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Alkaline wastes are neutralised by acids: sulphuric or hydrochloric acid, flue gas or <b>CO₂</b> (forming carbonic acid, H₂CO₃). CO₂ (often from boiler flue gas) is cheap, self-buffering and cannot over-acidify the effluent below about pH 6, which makes it the standard listed method in environmental engineering texts.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Limestone is a base – it neutralises acidic, not alkaline, effluent.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Caustic lime is a base – used for acidic effluent.</li><li><b>(d)</b> HCl works but is corrosive and adds chlorides; CO₂ (flue gas) is preferred.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Acid waste → lime; alkaline waste → CO₂ or H₂SO₄.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-103", year: 2020, no: 103, subject: "env", topic: "Flocculation",
q: `Flocculation is the process of`,
opts: ["Gently mixing the water and coagulant allowing the formation of large particles of floc", "Removing relatively large floating and suspended debris", "Flow, which is slowed enough so that gravity will cause the floc to settle", "Mixture of solids and liquids collected from the settling tank are dewatered and disposed of"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) gentle mixing to form large flocs</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> After rapid mixing disperses the coagulant (alum), <b>flocculation</b> uses slow, gentle stirring (velocity gradient G ≈ 20–75 s⁻¹, Gt ≈ 10⁴–10⁵, 20–40 min) to bring destabilised particles into contact so they grow into settleable flocs.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> describes screening.</li><li><b>(c)</b> describes sedimentation (clarification).</li><li><b>(d)</b> describes sludge treatment and disposal.</li></ul>
<p>Too high a G breaks the flocs; too low allows them to settle in the flocculator.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Coagulation (fast) → flocculation (slow) → settling.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-104", year: 2020, no: 104, subject: "env", topic: "Waste utilisation",
q: `In solid waste management, waste utilization is achieved by`,
opts: ["Recover, Reclamation and Reproduce", "Reuse, Reclamation and Recycling", "Recover, Recycling and Reproduce", "Reuse, Reproduce and Recycling"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) reuse, reclamation and recycling</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Waste utilisation turns waste into a resource by:</p><ul><li><b>Reuse</b> — using an item again in its original form (bottles, containers).</li><li><b>Reclamation</b> — recovering materials or energy (composting, incineration with energy recovery, land reclamation).</li><li><b>Recycling</b> — reprocessing materials into new products (paper, metals, plastics).</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 'Recover' and 'reproduce' are not the standard three Rs.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 'Recover' and 'reproduce' are not the standard three Rs.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 'Reproduce' is not a waste-management term.</li></ul>
<p>The waste hierarchy places reduction first, then reuse, recycling and recovery, with disposal last.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Hierarchy: reduce → reuse → recycle → recover → dispose.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-105", year: 2020, no: 105, subject: "env", topic: "Audible frequency range",
q: `The frequency range for hearing the sound by a human ear is in the range of`,
opts: ["20 Hz – 200 kHz", "10 Hz – 20 kHz", "20 Hz – 20 kHz", "10 Hz – 20 Hz"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 20 Hz – 20 kHz</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The normal human ear hears sound from about <b>20 Hz to 20 000 Hz</b>. Below 20 Hz is infrasound; above 20 kHz is ultrasound. The ear is most sensitive at 2–5 kHz, which is why A-weighting reduces low-frequency contributions. With age, the upper limit falls (presbycusis), and noise exposure first damages hearing near 4 kHz.</p><div class="formula">Audible range ≈ 20 Hz – 20 kHz</div>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Below 20 Hz infrasound; above 20 kHz ultrasound.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 200 kHz is far into the ultrasonic range.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 10 Hz is infrasonic.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10–20 Hz is entirely infrasonic.</li></ul>
<p>Sound levels are measured in decibels on a logarithmic scale: the threshold of hearing is 0 dB (20 μPa) and the threshold of pain is about 120–140 dB.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Audible: 20 Hz – 20 kHz.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-106", year: 2020, no: 106, subject: "env", topic: "Physiological effects of noise", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Physiological responses accompanying response and other noise exposures include:<ol><li>A vascular response characteristic by peripheral vasoconstriction, changes in heart beat rate and blood pressure</li><li>Various glandular charges such as increased output of adrenaline evidenced by chemical changes in blood</li><li>Slow, deep breathing</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3 (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Peavy, Rowe and Tchobanoglous list the physiological responses accompanying the startle response and other noise exposures as:</p>
<ul><li><b>1</b> A vascular response — peripheral vasoconstriction, changes in heart rate and blood pressure.</li><li><b>2</b> Glandular changes — increased output of adrenaline, seen as chemical changes in the blood.</li><li><b>3</b> Changes in breathing — the text describes "slow, deep breathing" as one of the responses.</li></ul>
<p>Our first pick was (a), expecting faster breathing under stress; on review the source text lists slow, deep breathing, and the key is accepted. Other effects are muscle tension, dilation of pupils and gastro-intestinal changes.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Noise stress: vascular, glandular, respiratory changes.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-107", year: 2020, no: 107, subject: "env", topic: "Electrostatic precipitators",
q: `Electrostatic precipitators are used for removal of:<ol><li>Gaseous contaminants</li><li>Liquid contaminants</li><li>Particulate contaminants</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 only</h3><ul><li><b>3</b> True: an ESP charges particles with a corona discharge and collects them on oppositely charged plates; efficiency exceeds 99 % even for fine particles (0·1–10 μm). Used for fly ash in thermal power plants and cement kilns.</li><li><b>1</b> False: gases pass through; gaseous pollutants need absorption (scrubbers), adsorption or combustion.</li><li><b>2</b> False: liquid effluents are treated in water treatment units, not ESPs (wet ESPs can capture mists, but that is still particulate matter in the gas).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Collection efficiency follows the Deutsch equation η = 1 − exp(−wA/Q).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ESP → particulates only.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-108", year: 2020, no: 108, subject: "env", topic: "Autecology",
q: `Which one of the following type of ecology is dealt with autecology?`,
opts: ["Synecology", "Community ecology", "Ecosystem ecology", "Individual species ecology"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) individual species ecology</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Autecology</b> studies an individual organism or a single species in relation to its environment — its life history, adaptations, tolerance and behaviour. <b>Synecology</b> studies groups of organisms (populations, communities, ecosystems) and their interactions.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Synecology studies communities – the opposite branch.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Community ecology is part of synecology.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Ecosystem ecology is part of synecology.</li></ul>
<p>Example: studying the temperature tolerance of one fish species is autecology; studying the food web of a lake is synecology.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Auto = self/one species; syn = together.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-109", year: 2020, no: 109, subject: "geo", topic: "Bulk unit weight",
q: `A soil sample has a porosity of 40%, and the specific gravity of solid is 2·70. If the soil is 50% saturated, the unit weight will be nearly`,
opts: ["22 kN/m<sup>3</sup>", "20 kN/m<sup>3</sup>", "18 kN/m<sup>3</sup>", "16 kN/m<sup>3</sup>"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 18 kN/m³</h3><div class="formula">γ = (G + S e) γ<sub>w</sub>/(1 + e)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>e = n/(1 − n) = 0·4/0·6 = 0·667.</li><li>Se = 0·5 × 0·667 = 0·333.</li><li>γ = (2·70 + 0·333) × 9·81/1·667 = <b>17·9 kN/m³</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Alternatively γ = [(1 − n)G + nS]γ<sub>w</sub> = (0·6 × 2·7 + 0·4 × 0·5) × 9·81 = 1·82 × 9·81 = 17·9 kN/m³. Dry: 15·9; saturated: 19·8 kN/m³.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 22 exceed even the saturated value.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 20 exceed even the saturated value.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 16 is the dry unit weight.</li></ul>
<p>Remember that a 50 % degree of saturation means half the void volume is filled with water; the water then adds Se·γ<sub>w</sub>/(1 + e) to the dry unit weight.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> n = e/(1 + e).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-110", year: 2020, no: 110, subject: "geo", topic: "Shrinkage limit",
q: `Oven dry mass of a pat of clay is 10·8 gm and mass of mercury displaced on immersion is 84·2 gm. If the specific gravity of solids is 2·72 and the density of the mercury is 13·6 g/cm<sup>3</sup>, the shrinkage limit of the soil will be nearly`,
opts: ["12%", "15%", "18%", "21%"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 21 %</h3><div class="formula">w<sub>s</sub> = (V<sub>d</sub> − V<sub>s</sub>)ρ<sub>w</sub>/M<sub>d</sub></div>
<ol class="work"><li>V<sub>d</sub> = 84·2/13·6 = 6·19 cm³ (dry pat volume).</li><li>V<sub>s</sub> = 10·8/2·72 = 3·97 cm³.</li><li>w<sub>s</sub> = (6·19 − 3·97)/10·8 = 0·206 = <b>20·6 % ≈ 21 %</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Equivalently w<sub>s</sub> = (1/G<sub>m</sub> − 1/G)×100, with G<sub>m</sub> = 10·8/6·19 = 1·745: (0·573 − 0·368) = 20·6 %. The shrinkage limit is the water content below which further drying causes no volume change.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 12 % comes from arithmetic slips.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 15 % comes from arithmetic slips.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 18 % comes from arithmetic slips.</li></ul>
<p>Mercury is used because it does not wet or penetrate the dry soil pat, giving its true external volume; the method is specified in IS 2720 Part 6.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Dry pat volume by mercury displacement.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-111", year: 2020, no: 111, subject: "geo", topic: "Suitability number",
q: `The suitability number of a backfill for D<sub>50</sub> = 1 mm, D<sub>20</sub> = 0·5 mm and D<sub>10</sub> = 0·08 mm will be nearly`,
opts: ["16", "18", "20", "22"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 22</h3><div class="formula">S<sub>N</sub> = 1·7 √(3/D<sub>50</sub>² + 1/D<sub>20</sub>² + 1/D<sub>10</sub>²)  (Brown)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>3/1 = 3; 1/0·25 = 4; 1/0·0064 = 156·25.</li><li>Sum = 163·25; √ = 12·78.</li><li>S<sub>N</sub> = 1·7 × 12·78 = <b>21·7 ≈ 22</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Ratings for vibroflotation backfill: 0–10 excellent, 10–20 good, 20–30 fair, 30–50 poor, &gt;50 unsuitable. This backfill is "fair".</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 16 omits or misuse terms.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 18 omits or misuse terms.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 20 omits or misuse terms.</li></ul>
<p>The D<sub>10</sub> term dominates the sum, showing that fines control the suitability of a backfill for vibro-compaction.</p><p>Brown's suitability number is used in vibroflotation design (Brown, 1977); IS and most texts quote the same formula and rating table.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Smaller S<sub>N</sub> = better backfill.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-112", year: 2020, no: 112, subject: "geo", topic: "Porosity–void ratio",
q: `The porosity of a soil n is (e = void ratio)`,
opts: ["e/(1 + e)", "e/(1 − e)", "(e + 1)/e", "(e − 1)/e"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) e/(1 + e)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Porosity–void ratio.</p><div class="formula">Porosity n = V<sub>v</sub>/V, void ratio e = V<sub>v</sub>/V<sub>s</sub></div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Taking V<sub>s</sub> = 1 gives V<sub>v</sub> = e and V = 1 + e, so n = e/(1 + e); inversely e = n/(1 − n).</li></ol>
<p>Porosity is always below 1 (100 %), while e can exceed 1 (soft clays e ≈ 1–3).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> e/(1 − e) has no defined meaning (n/(1 − n) = e is the inverse form).</li><li><b>(c)</b> (e + 1)/e = 1/n.</li><li><b>(d)</b> (e − 1)/e has no physical meaning and can be negative.</li></ul>
<p>Example: e = 0·667 gives n = 0·4 (40 %); e = 1 gives n = 0·5. Porosity is used in hydrology, void ratio in consolidation.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> n = e/(1 + e), e = n/(1 − n).</p>` },

{ id: "2020-113", year: 2020, no: 113, subject: "geo", topic: "Critical hydraulic gradient",
q: `A coarse-grained soil has a void ratio of 0·78 and specific gravity as 2·67. The critical gradient at which a quick sand condition occurs will be`,
opts: ["0·62", "0·74", "0·82", "0·94"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·94</h3><div class="formula">i<sub>c</sub> = (G − 1)/(1 + e) = γ′/γ<sub>w</sub></div>
<ol class="work"><li>i<sub>c</sub> = (2·67 − 1)/(1 + 0·78) = 1·67/1·78 = <b>0·938</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> At this upward gradient the seepage force equals the submerged weight, effective stress becomes zero, and cohesionless soil loses strength (quick condition / boiling). Typical i<sub>c</sub> ≈ 1; designs keep the exit gradient below i<sub>c</sub>/FOS (4–5).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·62 uses wrong values of G or e.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0·74 uses wrong values of G or e.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0·82 uses wrong values of G or e.</li></ul>
<p>Equivalently, i<sub>c</sub> = γ′/γ<sub>w</sub>, where γ′ = (G − 1)γ<sub>w</sub>/(1 + e) = 9·2 kN/m³ here.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> i<sub>c</sub> ≈ 1 for most soils.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-114", year: 2020, no: 114, subject: "geo", topic: "Rankine theory assumptions",
q: `Which of the following assumptions of the Rankine theory of lateral earth pressure are correct?<ol><li>The soil mass is semi-infinite, homogeneous, dry and cohesionless</li><li>The ground surface is a plane which may be horizontal or inclined</li><li>The wall yields about the base and thus satisfies the deformation condition for plastic equilibrium</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2 and 3</h3><ul><li><b>1</b> True: originally for semi-infinite, homogeneous, dry, cohesionless soil (later extended to c–φ soils by Bell).</li><li><b>2</b> True: the surface is a plane, horizontal or inclined at β.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the wall moves (rotates about its base) enough to bring the soil into active or passive plastic equilibrium.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Rankine also assumes a smooth vertical back (no wall friction), so the resultant acts parallel to the surface. Coulomb's wedge theory relaxes this by including wall friction δ.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Rankine: smooth wall, plastic equilibrium.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-115", year: 2020, no: 115, subject: "geo", topic: "Coefficient of earth pressure",
q: `The ratio of the horizontal stress to the vertical stress is called coefficient of`,
opts: ["Active earth pressure", "Passive earth pressure", "Earth pressure", "Plastic earth pressure"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) earth pressure</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The general ratio K = σ<sub>h</sub>/σ<sub>v</sub> is the <b>coefficient of earth pressure</b>. It takes particular values:</p><ul><li>K<sub>0</sub> (at rest) ≈ 1 − sin φ′ with no wall movement;</li><li>K<sub>a</sub> (active) = (1 − sin φ)/(1 + sin φ) when the wall moves away;</li><li>K<sub>p</sub> (passive) = (1 + sin φ)/(1 − sin φ) when it moves into the soil.</li></ul>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> K_a is the limiting value when the wall moves away.</li><li><b>(b)</b> K_p is the limiting value when the wall is pushed into the soil.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 'Plastic earth pressure' is not a standard coefficient.</li></ul>
<p>For a normally consolidated sand with φ′ = 30°, K<sub>0</sub> ≈ 0·5, K<sub>a</sub> = 0·33 and K<sub>p</sub> = 3·0, showing how strongly the coefficient depends on wall movement.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> K<sub>a</sub> &lt; K<sub>0</sub> &lt; K<sub>p</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-116", year: 2020, no: 116, subject: "geo", topic: "Field consolidation time",
q: `A bed consists of compressible clay of 4 m thickness with pervious sand on top and impervious rock at the bottom. In a consolidation test on an undisturbed specimen of clay from this deposit 90% settlement was reached in 4 hours. The specimen was 20 mm thick. The time for the building founded over this deposit to reach 90% of its final settlement will be`,
opts: ["91 years", "82 years", "73 years", "64 years"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 73 years</h3><div class="formula">t ∝ d²  (same T<sub>v</sub>, same c<sub>v</sub>)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Lab specimen: double drainage, d = 20/2 = 10 mm.</li><li>Field: single drainage (rock impervious), d = 4 m = 4000 mm.</li><li>t<sub>field</sub> = 4 h × (4000/10)² = 4 × 160 000 = 640 000 h.</li><li>= 640 000/(24 × 365) = <b>73 years</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 91 uses wrong drainage paths or conversions (double drainage in the field would give about 18 years).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 82 uses wrong drainage paths or conversions (double drainage in the field would give about 18 years).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 64 years uses wrong drainage paths or conversions (double drainage in the field would give about 18 years).</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The key point is that T<sub>v</sub> = c<sub>v</sub>t/d² is the same for 90 % consolidation (T<sub>90</sub> = 0·848) in both cases.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Check drainage on both sides: lab double, field single here.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-117", year: 2020, no: 117, subject: "geo", topic: "Plate load test — sand",
q: `A 30 cm square bearing plate settles by 8 mm in the plate load test on cohesionless soil when the intensity of loading is 180 kN/m<sup>2</sup>. The settlement of a shallow foundation of 1·5 m square under the same intensity of loading will be nearly`,
opts: ["30 mm", "26 mm", "22 mm", "18 mm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 22 mm</h3><div class="formula">S<sub>f</sub>/S<sub>p</sub> = [B<sub>f</sub>(B<sub>p</sub> + 0·3)/(B<sub>p</sub>(B<sub>f</sub> + 0·3))]²  (Terzaghi–Peck, sand)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Ratio inside = 1·5 × 0·6/(0·3 × 1·8) = 0·9/0·54 = 1·667.</li><li>Squared = 2·778.</li><li>S<sub>f</sub> = 8 × 2·778 = <b>22·2 mm</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> For clays, settlement is proportional to width (S<sub>f</sub> = S<sub>p</sub>B<sub>f</sub>/B<sub>p</sub> = 40 mm); for sands the ratio is limited — the footing can settle at most about 4 times the plate.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 30 does not match the working above the formula.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 26 does not match the working above the formula.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 18 mm does not match the working above the formula.</li></ul>
<p>Plate load tests are done as per IS 1888; the test is reliable only to a depth of about twice the plate width, so deeper soft layers below a large footing may be missed.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Sand: (B(B<sub>p</sub>+0·3)/(B<sub>p</sub>(B+0·3)))².</p>` },

{ id: "2020-118", year: 2020, no: 118, subject: "geo", topic: "Dilatancy correction to SPT",
q: `When the observed value of N exceeds 15, the corrected penetration number N<sub>c</sub> as per Terzaghi and Peck recommendation in the silty fine sands will be (N<sub>R</sub> = recorded value)`,
opts: ["15 − ½(N<sub>R</sub> − 15)", "15 − ½(N<sub>R</sub> + 15)", "15 + ½(N<sub>R</sub> − 15)", "15 + ½(N<sub>R</sub> + 15)"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 15 + ½(N<sub>R</sub> − 15)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In saturated fine or silty sands, rapid driving produces negative pore pressure (dilatancy), which temporarily raises resistance. Terzaghi and Peck recommend reducing values above 15: N<sub>c</sub> = 15 + ½(N<sub>R</sub> − 15). E.g. N<sub>R</sub> = 35 → N<sub>c</sub> = 25.</p><div class="formula">N′ = 15 + ½(N − 15) for N &gt; 15 (fine saturated sand)</div>
<p>This dilatancy correction is applied after the overburden correction.</p>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Apply only when N exceeds 15.</li><li>Halve the excess over 15.</li></ol><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Subtracting reduces N below 15 – the opposite of the correction.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Gives values below 15.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Increases N above the recorded value.</li></ul>
<p>IS 2131 adopts the same correction for fine sands and silts below the water table.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Dilatancy: halve the excess over 15.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-119", year: 2020, no: 119, subject: "geo", topic: "Sudden drawdown — Taylor's number",
q: `A canal of 4 m deep has side slopes of 1 : 1. The properties of the soil are c = 15 kN/m<sup>2</sup>, φ = 15°, e = 0·76 and G = 2·7. Taylor's stability number for that sudden drawdown = 0·136. The factor of safety with respect to cohesion in the case of sudden drawdown will be`,
opts: ["0·64", "1·43", "2·22", "3·01"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·43</h3><div class="formula">F<sub>c</sub> = c/(S<sub>n</sub> γ H)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>γ<sub>sat</sub> = (G + e)γ<sub>w</sub>/(1 + e) = (2·7 + 0·76) × 9·81/1·76 = 19·3 kN/m³ (soil remains saturated after drawdown).</li><li>F<sub>c</sub> = 15/(0·136 × 19·3 × 4) = 15/10·5 = <b>1·43</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> For sudden drawdown the weighted friction angle φ<sub>w</sub> = (γ′/γ<sub>sat</sub>)φ is used to read S<sub>n</sub> from Taylor's chart, while the saturated unit weight is used in the denominator — the worst condition for a canal or dam upstream slope.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0·64 uses other unit weights.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2·22 uses other unit weights.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3·01 uses other unit weights.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Sudden drawdown: γ<sub>sat</sub> with φ<sub>w</sub>.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-120", year: 2020, no: 120, subject: "geo", topic: "Electro-osmosis",
q: `The stability or shear strength of fine-grained soils can be increased by draining them with the passage of direct current through them. This process is known as`,
opts: ["Electro-osmosis", "Zeta potential", "Electro-chemical hardening", "Consolidation"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) electro-osmosis</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In <b>electro-osmosis</b> a DC field is applied between electrodes; pore water (with cations in the diffuse double layer) migrates to the cathode, where it is pumped out. This drains low-permeability silts and clays, raising effective stress and strength. Casagrande used it for slope stabilisation.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Zeta potential is the electrical potential at the shear plane of the double layer — a property, not a process.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Electro-chemical hardening uses metal electrodes (e.g. aluminium) that dissolve and form cementing compounds; the drainage effect described is electro-osmosis.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Consolidation is drainage under load.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> DC current → water to cathode → drainage.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-121", year: 2020, no: 121, subject: "survey", topic: "Combined curvature and refraction",
q: `The combined correction for curvature and refraction for a distance of 3400 m will be nearly`,
opts: ["0·2 m", "0·4 m", "0·6 m", "0·8 m"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·8 m</h3><div class="formula">C = 0·0673 d²  (d in km, C in m)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>C = 0·0673 × 3·4² = 0·0673 × 11·56 = <b>0·778 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Curvature alone is 0·0785d² (= 0·907 m), refraction is 1/7 of that and opposite in sign (−0·130 m), so the combined correction is (6/7) × 0·0785d². It is subtractive from staff readings.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.2 m would correspond to about 1.7 km (0.0673d²).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.4 m would correspond to about 2.4 km.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.6 m would correspond to about 3.0 km.</li></ul>
<p>The correction matters in long sights, such as reciprocal levelling across rivers; reciprocal observations cancel it automatically.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> 0·0673d² combined; 0·0785d² curvature only.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-122", year: 2020, no: 122, subject: "survey", topic: "Sag correction",
q: `A 100 m tape is suspended between the ends under a pull of 200 N. If the weight of the tape is 30 N, the correct distance between the tape ends will be nearly`,
opts: ["100·5 m", "100·3 m", "100·1 m", "99·9 m"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 99·9 m</h3><div class="formula">C<sub>s</sub> = L W²/(24 P²)  (subtractive)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>C<sub>s</sub> = 100 × 30²/(24 × 200²) = 90 000/960 000 = 0·094 m.</li><li>Horizontal distance = 100 − 0·094 = <b>99·906 m ≈ 99·9 m</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> A suspended tape hangs in a catenary, so the chord is shorter than the tape length; the sag correction is always negative. The normal tension at which sag and pull corrections cancel is P<sub>n</sub> = 0·204W√(AE)/√(P<sub>n</sub> − P<sub>0</sub>).</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> is longer than the tape, which is impossible for sag.</li><li><b>(b)</b> is longer than the tape, which is impossible for sag.</li><li><b>(c)</b> is longer than the tape, which is impossible for sag.</li></ul>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Sag correction is always subtractive.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-123", year: 2020, no: 123, subject: "survey", topic: "Anallactic lens", selfCorrected: true,
q: `In horizontal distance measurement, the basic formula for distance in stadia tacheometry has an additive constant. An anallatic lens is inserted in the tacheometer to make this additive constant zero. This lens is`,
opts: ["Convex lens inserted between object glass and diaphragm", "Plano-convex lens between object glass and diaphragm", "Plano-convex lens between diaphragm and eye piece", "Convex lens inserted between diaphragm and eye piece"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) plano-convex lens between object glass and diaphragm (official key)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In an external-focusing telescope D = (f/i)s + (f + d); the additive constant (f + d) is removed by an <b>anallactic lens</b> — a convex lens of the plano-convex type placed between the objective and the diaphragm at a fixed distance from the objective — so that the vertex of the measuring angle falls at the instrument centre and D = Ks.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A plain convex lens is not the anallatic lens form.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Between diaphragm and eyepiece is the wrong position.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Wrong lens form and position.</li></ul>
<p>Drawbacks: some loss of brightness and extra cost; internal-focusing telescopes have a very small additive constant without it.</p>
<p><b>Remember:</b> Anallactic lens: objective–diaphragm, makes C = 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-124", year: 2020, no: 124, subject: "survey", topic: "LMT to GMT",
q: `If the LMT is 8<sup>h</sup> 12<sup>m</sup> 16<sup>s</sup> AM at 38° 45′ W longitude, the GMT will be`,
opts: ["11<sup>h</sup> 12<sup>m</sup> 16<sup>s</sup> AM", "10<sup>h</sup> 47<sup>m</sup> 16<sup>s</sup> AM", "9<sup>h</sup> 29<sup>m</sup> 46<sup>s</sup> AM", "5<sup>h</sup> 29<sup>m</sup> 46<sup>s</sup> AM"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 10<sup>h</sup> 47<sup>m</sup> 16<sup>s</sup> AM</h3><div class="formula">GMT = LMT + longitude (W) in time</div>
<ol class="work"><li>38° 45′ = 38·75° × 4 min = 155 min = 2<sup>h</sup> 35<sup>m</sup>.</li><li>West of Greenwich, local time is behind, so GMT = 8<sup>h</sup>12<sup>m</sup>16<sup>s</sup> + 2<sup>h</sup>35<sup>m</sup> = <b>10<sup>h</sup>47<sup>m</sup>16<sup>s</sup> AM</b>.</li></ol>
<p><b>Concept:</b> Rule: "longitude east, Greenwich least; longitude west, Greenwich best". 15° = 1 h, 1° = 4 min, 1′ = 4 s.</p>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> adds 3 h.</li><li><b>(c)</b> subtracts or misconvert the longitude.</li><li><b>(d)</b> subtracts or misconvert the longitude.</li></ul>
<p>Places west of Greenwich see the sun later, so their local time lags Greenwich; adding the time equivalent of longitude converts LMT to GMT.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> West: add; east: subtract.</p>` },

{ id: "2020-125", year: 2020, no: 125, subject: "survey", topic: "Flying height from photo scale",
q: `A section line AB appears to be 10·16 cm on a photograph for which the focal length is 16 cm. The corresponding line measures 2·54 cm on a map, which is to a scale 1/50,000. The terrain has an average elevation of 200 m above mean sea level. The flying altitude of the aircraft above mean sea level during photograph will be`,
opts: ["1800 m", "2000 m", "2200 m", "2400 m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2200 m</h3><div class="formula">Photo scale = f/(H − h)</div>
<ol class="work"><li>Ground length = 2·54 cm × 50 000 = 1270 m.</li><li>Photo scale = 0·1016/1270 = 1/12 500.</li><li>H − h = 0·16 × 12 500 = 2000 m.</li><li>H = 2000 + 200 = <b>2200 m</b> above MSL.</li></ol>
<p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1800 m subtracts the terrain height.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2000 m is the height above the ground, not above MSL.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2400 m adds 400 m.</li></ul>
<p><b>Concept:</b> The method uses a map of known scale to find the photo scale, which avoids needing ground control measurements.</p><p>A check: at 2000 m above ground, a 16 cm lens gives 1 mm on the photo for every 12·5 m on the ground.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Photo/map ratio gives the scale; then H = f·S + h.</p>` }
);

