// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; Q10 and Q32 dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2019-1", year: 2019, no: 1, subject: "bm", topic: "Broken-brick aggregate", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which of the following statements are wholly correct regarding broken-brick aggregate useable in concretes?<ol><li>Broken-brick aggregate is obtained by crushing waste bricks; and it has a density varying between 1000 kg/m³ – 1200 kg/m³.</li><li>Such aggregate is usable in concrete for foundation in light buildings, floorings and walkways.</li><li>Such aggregate may also be used in light-weight reinforced concrete floors.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Broken-brick (brick-bat) aggregate is made by crushing over-burnt or waste bricks. It is porous and lighter than stone, so it is used in low-grade and light-weight concrete: foundations and floors of light buildings, walkways, and light-weight reinforced floor slabs.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Not wholly correct: the official key treats this statement as faulty (the density band stated is not the accepted one), so any option containing 1 is out.</li><li><b>2</b> Correct: low-strength uses such as foundation concrete, floorings and walkways.</li><li><b>3</b> Correct: its low density suits light-weight concrete floors.</li></ul><p>Hence 2 and 3 only. (a), (c) and (d) all include statement 1.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Brick aggregate: low-grade and light-weight concrete only, not for heavy or high-strength work.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-2", year: 2019, no: 2, subject: "bm", topic: "Air-entraining admixtures",
q: `In handling air-entraining admixtures the beneficial amount of entrained air depends upon certain factors like<ol><li>Type and quantity of air-entraining agent</li><li>Water-cement ratio of the mix</li><li>Strength of aggregates</li><li>Extent of compaction of concrete</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The air actually retained in a mix depends on the agent used and its dose, the water–cement ratio (wetter mixes entrain more), the grading and fineness of the sand, the cement fineness and content, temperature, mixing time and the compaction (over-vibration drives air out).</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Yes: each agent and its dose gives a different air content.</li><li><b>2</b> Yes: higher w/c entrains more air.</li><li><b>3</b> No: air content is governed by the grading and fines of the aggregate, not by its strength.</li><li><b>4</b> Yes: prolonged vibration or heavy compaction expels air.</li></ul><p>So (b) is correct; (a), (c) and (d) wrongly include the strength of aggregates.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Air content: agent dose, w/c, sand grading, mixing and compaction; not aggregate strength.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-3", year: 2019, no: 3, subject: "bm", topic: "Fly ash in concrete", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Which one of the following statements is <b>not</b> correct with respect to fly ash?`,
opts: ["As part replacement of cement in the range of 15% – 30%, fly ash reduces the strength in the initial period, but once the Pozzolanic process sets in, higher strength can be obtained.", "Fly ash as a part replacement of sand has a beneficial effect on strength even at early age.", "Fly ash as a part replacement of sand is economical.", "A simultaneous replacement of cement and fine aggregates enables the strength at a specified age to be equalled depending upon the water content."], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) (c) is the incorrect statement</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Fly ash is a pozzolana: it reacts slowly with the lime released by cement hydration, so cement-replacement mixes are weaker at early ages and stronger later. Used in place of sand it acts as a very fine filler that improves packing and workability, and the extra pozzolanic reaction gives higher strength.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct: standard description of early-age strength loss and later gain.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Correct as accepted: ash used as part of the fine aggregate adds fines and strength even early.</li><li><b>(c)</b> This is the statement the key marks as not correct: sand replacement is not presented as an economy measure, because processed fly ash costs more than natural sand and its use is justified by strength and durability.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Correct: with cement and sand both partly replaced, the strength at a given age can be matched by adjusting the water content.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Fly ash: cement replacement gives slow early strength; sand replacement is a quality measure, not a cost saving.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-4", year: 2019, no: 4, subject: "bm", topic: "Pozzolanas in cement",
q: `Which one of the following statements is <b>not</b> correct with respect to the properties of cement?`,
opts: ["Highly reactive Pozzolanas enhance the early age strength of the composite cement.", "Pozzolanic activity refines pore structure which decreases electrolytic resistance of concrete.", "The expansion due to alkali-silica reaction can be controlled by replacement of as high as 60% of OPC with high-calcium Pozzolana.", "Such high amounts of replacement cements result in higher accelerated carbonation depths compared to pure use of OPC only."], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) (b) is the incorrect statement</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Pozzolanic reaction produces extra C–S–H that fills and refines the capillary pores. A finer, less connected pore structure makes it harder for ions to move through the concrete, so its electrical (electrolytic) resistivity <b>increases</b>, which is why blended cements resist chloride corrosion.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Correct: very reactive pozzolanas (silica fume, metakaolin) raise early strength.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Incorrect: refining the pores increases, not decreases, the electrolytic resistance. This is the answer.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Correct: large replacement levels suppress alkali–silica expansion.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Correct: with less portlandite, high-replacement cements carbonate faster in accelerated tests.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Finer pores mean higher resistivity and better chloride resistance.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-5", year: 2019, no: 5, subject: "bm", topic: "Hydration compounds and strength",
q: `Hydration of which compound is responsible for increase in strength of cement in later age?`,
opts: ["Tri-calcium Aluminate (C₃A)", "Tetra-calcium Aluminoferrite (C₄AF)", "Tri-calcium Silicate (C₃S)", "Di-calcium Silicate (C₂S)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) di-calcium silicate (C₂S)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> C₃S hydrates quickly and supplies the early strength (up to about 28 days). C₂S hydrates slowly, so it contributes little in the first weeks but is responsible for the strength gain after 28 days, up to a year.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> C₃A reacts within minutes (flash set, controlled by gypsum) and adds little strength.</li><li><b>(b)</b> C₄AF hydrates slowly but contributes very little strength; it gives the grey colour.</li><li><b>(c)</b> C₃S gives the early-age strength, not the later gain.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> C₃S early, C₂S later strength.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-6", year: 2019, no: 6, subject: "bm", topic: "Creep of cement paste",
q: `The creep strain of cement attains its terminal value by`,
opts: ["1 year", "2 years", "5 years", "6 months"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 5 years</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Creep under sustained load is rapid at first and then slows. About half of the final creep occurs in the first 2–3 months and about 75 % in the first year; the creep strain approaches its terminal value only after several years. The accepted answer is about 5 years.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> About 1 year gives roughly 75–80 % of the final creep.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2 years is still short of the terminal value.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6 months gives only about two-thirds of the final creep.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Creep: ~50 % in 3 months, ~75 % in 1 year, terminal in about 5 years.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-7", year: 2019, no: 7, subject: "bm", topic: "Segregation in concrete",
q: `Which of the following methods will help in reducing segregation in concrete?<ol><li>Not using vibrator to spread the concrete</li><li>Reducing the continued vibration</li><li>Improving the cohesion of a lean dry mix through addition of a further small quantity of water</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Segregation is the separation of coarse particles from the mortar. It is reduced by careful handling and placing and by keeping the mix cohesive.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Vibrators are for compaction; using them to move concrete sideways makes the coarse aggregate separate.</li><li><b>2</b> Over-vibration makes the heavy aggregate sink and the paste rise.</li><li><b>3</b> A lean, dry, harsh mix lacks cohesion; a little more water (or more fines) binds it and reduces segregation.</li></ul><p>All three are accepted, so (a).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Segregation: avoid over-vibration, avoid spreading with vibrators, keep the mix cohesive.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-8", year: 2019, no: 8, subject: "bm", topic: "Slump loss", selfCorrected: true,
q: `On an average, in a 125 mm slump, the concrete may lose about (in first one hour)`,
opts: ["15 mm of slump", "25 mm of slump", "40 mm of slump", "50 mm of slump"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 50 mm of slump</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Fresh concrete stiffens with time as water is absorbed by aggregate, evaporates and is used in hydration. A mix of about 125 mm slump may lose about 50 mm (roughly 40 % of its slump) in the first hour at ordinary temperatures, and the loss is larger for higher initial slump and hotter weather. The key gives 50 mm.</p><div class="formula">Slump loss = initial slump − slump after time t</div><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 15 mm is far too small for a 125 mm mix.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 25 mm is about 20 % and underestimates the usual loss.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 40 mm is close, but the accepted figure is 50 mm.</li></ul><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>About 50 mm loss is typical over 1 h in hot weather for a 125 mm slump mix (key's value).</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> 125 mm slump loses about 50 mm in the first hour.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-9", year: 2019, no: 9, subject: "bm", topic: "Permeability of concrete",
q: `Permeability in concrete is studied towards providing for, or guarding against, which of the following features?<ol><li>The penetration by materials in solution may adversely affect the durability of concrete; moreover, aggressive liquids 'attack' the concrete.</li><li>In case of reinforced concrete, ingress of moisture and air will result in corrosion of steel leading to an increase in volume of steel, resulting in cracking and spalling of the concrete cover.</li><li>The moisture penetration depends on permeability and if the concrete can become saturated with water it is less vulnerable to frost action.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Permeability controls how easily water, air and dissolved salts enter concrete.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: dissolved sulphates, chlorides and acids enter through permeable concrete and attack it.</li><li><b>2</b> True: rust occupies more volume than steel and bursts the cover.</li><li><b>3</b> False: saturated concrete is <b>more</b> vulnerable to frost, because freezing water expands in the pores. Low permeability keeps the concrete from saturating.</li></ul><p>Hence 1 and 2 only. The other options include statement 3.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Saturated concrete is the one that fails in frost.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-10", year: 2019, no: 10, subject: "bm", topic: "Poisson ratio from pulse velocity", dropped: true,
q: `Poisson's ratio of concrete μ can be determined using the formula (V is pulse velocity, n resonant frequency of longitudinal vibration, L distance between transducers)`,
opts: ["(V/2nL) = (1 − μ)/((1 − 2μ)(1 + μ))", "(V/2nL) = (1 + μ)/((1 − 2μ)(1 + μ))", "(V²/2nL) = (1 − μ)/((1 − 2μ)(1 + μ))", "(V²/2nL) = (1 − μ²)/((1 − 2μ)(1 + μ))"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Dropped by UPSC (not scored)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> UPSC dropped this item. The pulse velocity in a bulk medium is V² = (E/ρ)(1 − μ)/((1 + μ)(1 − 2μ)) and the longitudinal resonant frequency gives E/ρ = (2nL)². Dividing, (V/2nL)² = (1 − μ)/((1 + μ)(1 − 2μ)). Option (c) is the nearest, but it prints V² over 2nL instead of (V/2nL)², and the other options lack the square or the right numerator, so none is exactly right; this is the likely reason for dropping it.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Pulse velocity and resonance together give μ without knowing E or ρ.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-11", year: 2019, no: 11, subject: "bm", topic: "Concreting in caissons and coffer dams",
q: `Which one of the following methods/techniques will be used for placing of concrete in dewatered 'Caissons or Coffer' dams?`,
opts: ["Tremie method", "Placing in bags", "Prepacked concrete", "In-the-dry practice"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) in-the-dry practice</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> When a caisson or coffer dam has been pumped out, the work area is dry, so concrete is placed as in ordinary construction (in the dry) and compacted by vibration.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Tremie is used for concrete placed under water, not in a dewatered cell.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Bagged concrete is a special under-water technique for small repairs.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Prepacked concrete grouts pre-placed aggregate, used under water or in repairs, not for the dry case.</li></ul><p>Typical practice: in a coffer dam the cell is pumped dry, the foundation is cleaned and blinded, and the concrete is placed in layers and vibrated like any other foundation work. Tremie and bagged placing are used only where the water cannot be removed, and a tremie seal course may be cast first so that the pumping out can be done.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Dewatered cell means ordinary placing in the dry.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-12", year: 2019, no: 12, subject: "bm", topic: "Minimum cement content",
q: `The minimum cement content (kg/m³) for a pre-specified strength of concrete (using standard notations) premised on 'free water-cement ratio' will be as`,
opts: ["1 − C/(1000·S<sub>C</sub>) − W/1000", "Water Content / Water Cement ratio", "Water Content × Water Cement ratio", "100F/(C + F)"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) water content / water–cement ratio</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The w/c ratio is free water ÷ cement. For a chosen strength the w/c ratio is fixed, so the cement content is C = W/(w/c): water content divided by the water–cement ratio. Example: W = 186 kg/m³ and w/c = 0.5 give C = 372 kg/m³.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This is the form for the fraction of volume left for aggregate (absolute volume method), not the cement content.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Multiplying water content by w/c has the wrong dimensions and gives no useful quantity.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 100F/(C+F) is a percentage of fines in the paste, not cement content.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Cement = water ÷ (w/c).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-13", year: 2019, no: 13, subject: "solid", topic: "Poisson ratio from tension test",
q: `A bar specimen of 36 mm diameter is subjected to a pull of 90 kN during a tension test. The extension on a gauge length of 200 mm is measured to be 0·089 mm and the change in diameter to be 0·0046 mm. The Poisson's ratio will be`,
opts: ["0·287", "0·265", "0·253", "0·241"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·287</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Poisson ratio from tension test.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> ν = lateral strain ÷ longitudinal strain</div><ul class="sym"><li>δL = 0.089 mm, L = 200 mm (gauge length)</li><li>δd = 0.0046 mm, d = 36 mm</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>Longitudinal strain = 0.089/200 = 4.45 × 10⁻⁴.</li><li>Lateral strain = 0.0046/36 = 1.278 × 10⁻⁴.</li><li>ν = 1.278 × 10⁻⁴ / 4.45 × 10⁻⁴ = 0.287.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0.265 would need a diameter change of 0.0042 mm.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.253 would need a diameter change of 0.0041 mm.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.241 would need a diameter change of 0.0039 mm.</li></ul><p>The load is not needed for ν.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> ν = lateral strain/axial strain; the load is a distractor.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-14", year: 2019, no: 14, subject: "solid", topic: "Thermal stress in a rod",
q: `A steel rod 15 m long is at a temperature of 15°C. The values of α = 12 × 10⁻⁶/°C and E = 200 GN/m² are adopted. When the temperature is raised to 65°C, what is the free expansion of the length; and if this expansion of the length is fully prevented, what is the temperature stress produced?`,
opts: ["5 mm and 120 MN/m²", "9 mm and 120 MN/m²", "5 mm and 150 MN/m²", "9 mm and 150 MN/m²"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 9 mm and 120 MN/m²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Thermal stress in a rod.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formulae:</b> free expansion δ = αLΔT; restrained stress σ = EαΔT</div><ul class="sym"><li>α = 12 × 10⁻⁶ /°C, L = 15 m, ΔT = 65 − 15 = 50 °C, E = 200 GN/m²</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>δ = 12 × 10⁻⁶ × 15000 × 50 = 9 mm.</li><li>σ = 200 × 10³ MPa × 12 × 10⁻⁶ × 50 = 120 MPa = 120 MN/m².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5 mm is wrong for δ; 5 mm would need ΔT ≈ 28 °C.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Both values are wrong (5 mm, 150 MN/m²).</li><li><b>(d)</b> The expansion 9 mm is right but 150 MN/m² would need ΔT = 62.5 °C.</li></ul><p>This is the reason expansion joints and sliding bearings are provided in bridges, rails and pipelines: if the movement is prevented, a rise of only 50 °C in steel develops a stress of 120 MPa, about half of the yield stress of mild steel. Steel and concrete have nearly the same α, which is why reinforced concrete does not tear itself apart with temperature.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Stress = Eα ΔT, independent of length.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-15", year: 2019, no: 15, subject: "solid", topic: "Volumetric strain of a bar",
q: `A bar of uniform rectangular section of area A is subjected to an axial tensile load P; its Young's modulus is E and its Poisson's ratio is 1/m. Its volumetric strain e<sub>v</sub> is`,
opts: ["(P/AE)(1 + 3/m)", "(P/AE)(1 + 2/m)", "(P/AE)(1 − 2/m)", "(P/AE)(1 − 1/2m)"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) (P/AE)(1 − 2/m)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Volumetric strain of a bar.</p><div class="formula">Axial strain ε = P/AE</div><div class="formula">Each lateral strain is −νε = −ε/m</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Volumetric strain = ε − 2ε/m = (P/AE)(1 − 2/m) = (P/AE)(1 − 2ν).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Adds the lateral strains with the wrong sign and count.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Lateral contraction reduces the volume change, so the sign is minus.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The factor is 2ν (two lateral directions), not ν/2.</li></ul><p>As a check, for ν = 0.5 the volumetric strain is zero (an incompressible material such as rubber), and for ν = 0 the volume changes by the full axial strain. The bulk modulus follows from K = E/3(1 − 2ν), which gives the same relation between the pull and the volume change.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> e_v = ε(1 − 2ν); zero at ν = 0.5.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-16", year: 2019, no: 16, subject: "solid", topic: "Shear stress from maximum shear",
q: `The normal stresses on two mutually perpendicular planes are 140 N/mm² (Tensile) and 70 N/mm² (Tensile). If the maximum shear stress is 45 N/mm², the shear stress on these planes will be nearly`,
opts: ["20·9 N/mm²", "24·6 N/mm²", "28·3 N/mm²", "32·0 N/mm²"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 28·3 N/mm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Shear stress from maximum shear.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> τ<sub>max</sub> = √[((σx − σy)/2)² + τ²]</div><ul class="sym"><li>σx = 140, σy = 70, (σx − σy)/2 = 35 N/mm²</li><li>τ<sub>max</sub> = 45 N/mm²</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>45² = 35² + τ² → τ² = 2025 − 1225 = 800.</li><li>τ = 28.3 N/mm².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 20.9 gives τmax = 40.8, not 45.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 24.6 gives τmax = 42.8.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 32.0 gives τmax = 47.4.</li></ul><p>The same Mohr's circle reasoning gives the principal stresses here: centre = 105 N/mm², radius = 45, so σ₁ = 150 and σ₂ = 60 N/mm². The maximum shear stress is always the radius of the circle and acts on planes at 45° to the principal planes, so these data are consistent.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> τmax is the radius of Mohr’s circle.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-17", year: 2019, no: 17, subject: "solid", topic: "Principal stresses",
q: `The normal stresses on the two mutually perpendicular planes at a point are 120 MPa (Tensile) and 60 MPa (Tensile). If the shear stress across these planes is 30 MPa, the principal stresses will be nearly`,
opts: ["124 MPa (Tensile) and 24 MPa (Compressive)", "132 MPa (Tensile) and 24 MPa (Compressive)", "124 MPa (Tensile) and 48 MPa (Tensile)", "132 MPa (Tensile) and 48 MPa (Tensile)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 132 MPa and 48 MPa (both tensile)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> σ₁,₂ = (σx + σy)/2 ± √[((σx − σy)/2)² + τ²].</p><ul class="sym"><li>(σx + σy)/2 = 90 MPa; (σx − σy)/2 = 30 MPa; τ = 30 MPa</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>R = √(30² + 30²) = 42.43 MPa.</li><li>σ₁ = 90 + 42.4 = 132.4 MPa; σ₂ = 90 − 42.4 = 47.6 MPa (tensile).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Both numbers are wrong; σ₂ cannot be compressive because the centre 90 MPa exceeds the radius 42.4 MPa.</li><li><b>(b)</b> σ₂ cannot be compressive for the same reason.</li><li><b>(c)</b> σ₁ would be 124 only if R = 34.</li></ul><p>Mohr's circle check: centre at 90, radius 42.4; the circle lies wholly on the tension side because 90 > 42.4, so the minor principal stress stays tensile. The principal planes lie at tan 2θ = 2τ/(σx − σy) = 1, so θ = 22.5° from the 120 MPa plane.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> If centre > radius, both principals have the same sign.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-18", year: 2019, no: 18, subject: "solid", topic: "Maximum shear stress",
q: `At a point in a material, the stresses acting on two planes at right angles to each other are: σ<sub>z</sub> = 120 MPa and σ<sub>y</sub> = −200 MPa and τ<sub>zy</sub> = −80 MPa. The maximum shear stress on the element will be nearly`,
opts: ["142 MPa", "155 MPa", "167 MPa", "179 MPa"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 179 MPa</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Maximum shear stress.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> τ<sub>max</sub> = √[((σz − σy)/2)² + τ²] (radius of Mohr's circle)</div><ol class="work"><li>(σz − σy)/2 = (120 + 200)/2 = 160 MPa.</li><li>τ<sub>max</sub> = √(160² + 80²) = √32000 = 178.9 MPa ≈ 179 MPa.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 142 MPa does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 155 MPa would need τ = 66 MPa.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 167 MPa would need τ = 47 MPa.</li></ul><p>Principal stresses for the same data are σ₁,₂ = −40 ± 178.9, that is +138.9 and −218.9 MPa, and the maximum shear stress is half their difference, (138.9 + 218.9)/2 = 178.9 MPa. The planes of maximum shear are at 45° to the principal planes.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> τmax = half the difference of the principal stresses.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-19", year: 2019, no: 19, subject: "solid", topic: "Stress on an inclined plane",
q: `The principal stresses in the wall of a container are 40 MN/mm² and 80 MN/mm². The normal makes an angle of 30° with a direction of maximum principal stress. The resultant stresses (in magnitude) in the plane will be nearly<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2019-19.png" alt="Figure for question 19">`,
opts: ["84 MN/mm²", "72 MN/mm²", "64 MN/mm²", "58 MN/mm²"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 72 MN/mm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Stress on an inclined plane.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formulae:</b> σn = σ₁cos²θ + σ₂sin²θ; τ = ½(σ₁ − σ₂) sin 2θ; resultant = √(σn² + τ²)</div><ul class="sym"><li>σ₁ = 80, σ₂ = 40 MN/mm²; θ = 30° (normal to σ₁ direction)</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>σn = 80(0.75) + 40(0.25) = 70.</li><li>τ = ½ × 40 × sin 60° = 17.32.</li><li>Resultant = √(70² + 17.32²) = 72.1 MN/mm².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 84 exceeds the maximum principal stress 80, which no plane can have.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 64 would need σn ≈ 61.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 58 would need σn ≈ 55; the actual normal stress is 70.</li></ul><p>The element is in biaxial tension with σ₁ = 80 along the horizontal and σ₂ = 40 along the vertical. The stress is a vector with a normal part 70 and a shear part 17.3 on the 30° plane, and its direction makes an angle of tan⁻¹(17.3/70) = 13.9° with the normal. The same Mohr's circle (centre 60, radius 20) gives these values at the point 2θ = 60° from the σ₁ point.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Resultant = √(σn² + τ²); check it lies between σ₂ and σ₁.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-20", year: 2019, no: 20, subject: "solid", topic: "Shear force and load",
q: `The change in shearing force between two points on the beam is equal to the area of`,
opts: ["Loading diagram between the two points", "Shear force diagram between the two points", "Bending moment diagram between the two points", "M/EI diagram between the two points"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) loading diagram</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> From dV/dx = −w, integrating between two sections gives V₂ − V₁ = −∫w dx: the change in shear equals the area of the loading diagram between the points.</p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The area of the shear-force diagram gives the change in bending moment (dM/dx = V).</li><li><b>(c)</b> The area of the bending-moment diagram has no direct meaning except as the M/EI loading in moment-area theorems.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The area of the M/EI diagram gives the change in slope (first moment-area theorem).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Load → shear → moment: each is the area of the one before.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-21", year: 2019, no: 21, subject: "solid", topic: "Shear flow",
q: `Which one of the following statements specifies shear flow?`,
opts: ["Flow of shear force along the beam", "It is the product of the shear stress at any level and the corresponding width b (of the section)", "Unbalanced force on any side of given section divided by area of section", "The deformation at any level due to sudden variation in shear stress"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) τ × b</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Shear flow q = τ·b = VAȳ/I: the shear force per unit length along the beam at a given level. It is used to design bolts, rivets, welds and connectors between parts of a built-up section.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Shear flow is not the flow of the shear force along the beam; it is a force per unit length at one level of the section.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Unbalanced force divided by area is an average shear stress, not shear flow.</li><li><b>(d)</b> This describes shear deformation (strain), not flow.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> q = VAȳ/I = τb, force per unit length.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-22", year: 2019, no: 22, subject: "solid", topic: "Power transmitted by a shaft",
q: `Which one of the following statements is correct for the rotating shafts transmitting power?`,
opts: ["Lower the frequency of shaft lower will be the torque", "Higher the frequency of shaft lower will be the torque", "Frequency of the shaft does not influence the torque", "Higher the frequency of shaft higher will be the torque"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) higher frequency means lower torque</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Power P = 2πNT/60 = ωT. For a given power, T = P/ω, so torque is inversely proportional to the speed (frequency). A faster shaft carries the same power with a smaller torque and so a smaller diameter.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Lower frequency means higher torque for the same power.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Frequency enters directly through ω in T = P/ω.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Higher frequency lowers the torque.</li></ul><p>Examples: a motor delivering 100 kW at 1500 rpm needs a torque of 637 N·m, but through a 10:1 reduction gear the slow shaft at 150 rpm carries 6370 N·m and needs a much larger diameter. That is why the high-speed shaft of a gearbox is thin and the low-speed output shaft is thick.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> T = P/ω: speed up, torque down.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-23", year: 2019, no: 23, subject: "solid", topic: "Maximum shear stress in a shaft",
q: `The maximum shear stress induced in a solid circular shaft of diameter 15 cm, when the shaft transmits 150 kW power at 180 rpm, will be`,
opts: ["16 N/mm²", "14 N/mm²", "12 N/mm²", "10 N/mm²"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 12 N/mm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Maximum shear stress in a shaft.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formulae:</b> T = P/ω with ω = 2πN/60; τ<sub>max</sub> = 16T/(πd³)</div><ul class="sym"><li>P = 150 kW = 150 × 10³ W; N = 180 rpm; d = 150 mm</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>ω = 2π × 180/60 = 18.85 rad/s.</li><li>T = 150000/18.85 = 7957 N·m = 7.957 × 10⁶ N·mm.</li><li>τ = 16 × 7.957 × 10⁶ /(π × 150³) = 127.3 × 10⁶ / 1.0603 × 10⁷ = 12.0 N/mm².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 16 N/mm² would need T = 10.6 kN·m.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 14 N/mm² would need T = 9.3 kN·m.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10 N/mm² would need T = 6.6 kN·m.</li></ul><p>Check on the units: with P in kW and N in rpm, T = 9550P/N N·m = 9550 × 150/180 = 7958 N·m, the same value as above. Using a diameter of 15 cm = 150 mm gives the polar section modulus Z<sub>p</sub> = πd³/16 = 6.627 × 10⁵ mm³, and τ = T/Z<sub>p</sub> = 12.0 N/mm².</p><p><b>Remember:</b> τ = 16T/πd³ with T = 9550·P(kW)/N in N·m.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-24", year: 2019, no: 24, subject: "solid", topic: "Stiffness of a helical spring",
q: `A closely coiled helical spring made of 10 mm diameter steel wire has 15 coils of 100 mm mean diameter. The spring is subjected to an axial load of 100 N. For a modulus of rigidity of 8·16 × 10⁴ N/mm², the stiffness of the spring will be nearly`,
opts: ["5·9 N/mm", "6·8 N/mm", "7·7 N/mm", "8·8 N/mm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 6·8 N/mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Stiffness of a helical spring.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> stiffness k = Gd⁴/(8D³n)</div><ul class="sym"><li>G = 8.16 × 10⁴ N/mm², d = 10 mm, D = 100 mm, n = 15</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>d⁴ = 10⁴; D³ = 10⁶.</li><li>k = 8.16 × 10⁴ × 10⁴ /(8 × 10⁶ × 15) = 8.16 × 10⁸/1.2 × 10⁸ = 6.8 N/mm.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5.9 N/mm would need n = 17.3 coils.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 7.7 N/mm would need n = 13.3 coils.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8.8 N/mm would need n = 11.6 coils.</li></ul><p>The 100 N load only gives the deflection (14.7 mm); it does not change the stiffness.</p><p>The spring deflection for 100 N is δ = P/k = 100/6.8 = 14.7 mm, and the maximum shear stress, with the Wahl factor ignored, is τ = 8PD/(πd³) = 8 × 100 × 100/(π × 1000) = 25.5 N/mm². A series connection of springs halves the stiffness and a parallel connection doubles it.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> k = Gd⁴/8D³n; load not needed.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-25", year: 2019, no: 25, subject: "sa", topic: "Total load from a shear-force diagram",
q: `The shear-force diagram of a beam is shown in the figure. The total of the vertically downward loads on the beam is<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2019-25.png" alt="Figure for question 25">`,
opts: ["2600 N", "3000 N", "3400 N", "3800 N"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3800 N</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Read the diagram from left to right: a downward load makes the shear fall by its magnitude, an upward reaction makes it rise. The levels are −800, +800, −1200 and +1000 N, so the jumps are: 0 to −800 (downward 800 N); up by 1600 N (upward reaction); down by 2000 N (downward load); up by 2200 N (upward reaction); down by 1000 N to zero (downward 1000 N).</p><div class="formula">Jump in SFD = concentrated force</div><ol class="work"><li>Downward loads = 800 + 2000 + 1000 = 3800 N.</li><li>Upward reactions = 1600 + 2200 = 3800 N, so the beam is in equilibrium.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2600 N omits one of the downward steps in the SFD.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3000 N omits the 800 N step.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3400 N omits the 400 N step.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Sum of upward jumps must equal sum of downward jumps.</p>` }
);

