// ESE 2019 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (VBY-S-CVL), Q26–50
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; Q10 and Q32 dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2019-26", year: 2019, no: 26, subject: "solid", topic: "Shear stress in a triangular section",
q: `A beam of triangular cross-section is subjected to a shear force of 50 kN. The base width of the section is 250 mm and the height is 200 mm. The beam is placed with its base horizontal. The shear stress at neutral axis will be nearly`,
opts: ["2·2 N/mm²", "2·7 N/mm²", "3·2 N/mm²", "3·7 N/mm²"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2·7 N/mm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formulae:</b> A = bh/2; average shear stress τ<sub>avg</sub> = F/A. For a triangle (base down) the shear stress is 4/3 τ<sub>avg</sub> at the neutral axis (h/3 from the base) and 3/2 τ<sub>avg</sub> at mid-height (the maximum).</p><ul class="sym"><li>F = 50 kN, b = 250 mm, h = 200 mm</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>A = 250 × 200/2 = 25 000 mm².</li><li>τ<sub>avg</sub> = 50 000/25 000 = 2 N/mm².</li><li>τ at the neutral axis = (4/3) × 2 = 2.67 ≈ 2.7 N/mm².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2.2 N/mm² is close to the average value; the neutral axis value is 4/3 of it.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3.2 N/mm² is not given by any factor of the average.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3.7 N/mm² is not given by any factor of the average.</li></ul><p>The maximum, 3.0 N/mm², occurs at mid-height, not at the neutral axis.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Triangle: 4/3 τavg at the NA, 3/2 τavg at h/2 (maximum).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-27", year: 2019, no: 27, subject: "solid", topic: "Timber beam: bending and shear limits",
q: `A timber beam, 100 mm wide and 150 mm deep, supports a UDL over a span of 2 m. If the safe stresses are not to exceed 28 MPa in bending and 2 MPa in shear, the maximum load that the beam can support is`,
opts: ["16 kN/m", "20 kN/m", "24 kN/m", "28 kN/m"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 20 kN/m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> V = wL/2, τ<sub>max</sub> = 1.5V/(bd).</p><div class="formula"><b>Formulae:</b> M = wL²/8, σ = M/Z with Z = bd²/6;</div><ul class="sym"><li>b = 100, d = 150 mm, L = 2 m, σ<sub>allow</sub> = 28, τ<sub>allow</sub> = 2 MPa</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>Z = 100 × 150²/6 = 3.75 × 10⁵ mm³. M<sub>allow</sub> = 28 × 3.75 × 10⁵ = 10.5 kN·m.</li><li>w from bending: wL²/8 = 0.5w = 10.5 → w = 21 kN/m.</li><li>V<sub>allow</sub> = 2 × 15 000/1.5 = 20 000 N. w from shear: wL/2 = w = 20 kN/m.</li><li>The smaller governs: 20 kN/m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 16 kN/m is safe but not the maximum.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 24 kN/m exceeds both limits.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 28 kN/m exceeds both limits (bending allows 21, shear 20).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Check both bending and shear; the smaller load governs.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-28", year: 2019, no: 28, subject: "steel", topic: "Rankine formula for a column",
q: `A 1·5 m long column has a circular cross-section of 50 mm diameter. Consider Rankine's formula with values of f<sub>c</sub> = 560 N/mm², α = 1/1600 for pinned ends and factor of safety of 3. If one end of the column is fixed and the other end is free, the safe load will be`,
opts: ["9948 N", "9906 N", "9864 N", "9822 N"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 9906 N</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a column fixed at one end and free at the other, L<sub>e</sub> = 2L.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> P<sub>R</sub> = f<sub>c</sub>A/[1 + α(L<sub>e</sub>/k)²]; safe load = P<sub>R</sub>/FS</div><ul class="sym"><li>f<sub>c</sub> = 560 N/mm², α = 1/1600, L = 1500 mm, d = 50 mm, FS = 3</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>A = π × 50²/4 = 1963.5 mm²; k = d/4 = 12.5 mm.</li><li>L<sub>e</sub> = 2 × 1500 = 3000 mm; L<sub>e</sub>/k = 240.</li><li>1 + (240)²/1600 = 1 + 36 = 37.</li><li>P<sub>R</sub> = 560 × 1963.5/37 = 29 718 N; safe load = 29 718/3 = 9906 N.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 9948 N would need 1 + α(Le/k)² slightly below 37, i.e. a rounded area or k.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 9864 N would need the denominator slightly above 37, from a rounded area.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 9822 N would need the denominator near 37.3, from rounding A or k.</li></ul><p>Using L<sub>e</sub> = L (pinned) would give 560 × 1963.5/(1 + 3.6) / 3 ≈ 79.7 kN, far larger.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Fixed-free column: effective length 2L.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-29", year: 2019, no: 29, subject: "sa", topic: "Continuous beam, support moment",
q: `A continuous beam with uniform flexural rigidity is shown in the figure. The moment at B is<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2019-29.png" alt="Figure for question 29">`,
opts: ["18 kNm", "16 kNm", "14 kNm", "12 kNm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 12 kNm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The beam has three simple supports A, B, C, so the end moments are zero and the three-moment (Clapeyron) equation gives the support moment M<sub>B</sub>.</p><div class="formula">Clapeyron: M_AL₁ + 2M_B(L₁ + L₂) + M_CL₂ = −6(A₁x̄₁/L₁ + A₂x̄₂/L₂)</div><p><b>Formula:</b> 2M<sub>B</sub>(L₁ + L₂) = −P a(L₁² − a²)/L₁ (point load P at distance a from A in span L₁; span L₂ unloaded).</p><ul class="sym"><li>P = 40 kN, a = 2 m, L₁ = 4 m, L₂ = 6 m</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>Load term = 40 × 2 × (16 − 4)/4 = 240.</li><li>2M<sub>B</sub> × 10 = −240 → M<sub>B</sub> = −12 kN·m (hogging).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 18 kN·m would be the result if the load term were 360.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 16 kN·m uses the wrong span-load term in Clapeyron's equation.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 14 kN·m uses the wrong span-load term in Clapeyron's equation.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pinned ends: M_A = M_C = 0; solve three-moment equation at B.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-30", year: 2019, no: 30, subject: "solid", topic: "Shear stress in a circular section",
q: `The maximum shear stress across a circular section is`,
opts: ["(4/3) Average shear stress", "(3/2) Average shear stress", "(5/4) Average shear stress", "(9/5) Average shear stress"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 4/3 of the average</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a solid circle τ = (V/3I)(R² − y²)·... which is greatest at the neutral axis, where τ<sub>max</sub> = 4V/(3A) = (4/3) τ<sub>avg</sub>.</p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 3/2 is the factor for a rectangular section.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 5/4 is not the factor for any common section.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 9/5 is not the factor for any common section (a triangle gives 3/2 at mid-height).</li></ul><p>For comparison: a rectangular section has a maximum shear stress of 3/2 of the average, a triangle 3/2 at mid-height, and a thin circular tube 2. In an I-section the web carries nearly all the shear, and the maximum is only about 1.1 to 1.2 times the average. In a circular section the shear stress varies parabolically across the depth and is zero at the top and bottom edges.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Rectangle 3/2, circle 4/3, thin tube 2.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-31", year: 2019, no: 31, subject: "sa", topic: "Temperature effect on a three-hinged arch", doubt: true,
q: `Which of the following statements are correct in respect of temperature effect on a load-carrying three-hinged arch?<ol><li>No stresses are produced in a three-hinged arch due to temperature change alone.</li><li>There is a decrease in horizontal thrust due to rise in temperature.</li><li>There is an increase in horizontal thrust due to rise in temperature.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 only", "3 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A three-hinged arch is statically determinate, so a change of temperature only lets the crown rise or fall; it produces no stresses and does not change the reactions.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: no temperature stresses in a determinate structure.</li><li><b>2</b> The key treats this as true (thrust said to decrease with rise in temperature).</li><li><b>3</b> The key treats this as false.</li></ul><p>The key's (a) is shown. (b) includes 3, (c) and (d) omit the clearly true statement 1.</p><div class="note">In doubt (key followed). For a statically determinate three-hinged arch the horizontal thrust does not change with temperature at all, so statements 2 and 3 are both false and only statement 1 is true; no option says that. The key's (a) is shown.</div><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Three-hinged arch: no temperature stress. Two-hinged arch: thrust changes.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-32", year: 2019, no: 32, subject: "sa", topic: "Horizontal reaction of a pin-jointed frame", dropped: true,
q: `Consider the frame as shown in the figure. The magnitude of the horizontal support reaction at E is<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2019-32.png" alt="Figure for question 32">`,
opts: ["400 kN", "300 kN", "250 kN", "200 kN"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Dropped by UPSC (not scored)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> UPSC dropped this item (the load is given in N while the options are in kN). Solving the frame as a pin-jointed truss: joint A carries 200 N with members AC (horizontal) and AB (slope 2:4); AB = 200/sin θ = 447 N and AC = 400 N (tension). At B, BC = 200 N (tension) and BD = 400 N (compression). At C the members CE and CD must balance CA = 400 and CB = 200: this gives CE = 447 N (tension) and CD = 0. The horizontal component of CE at E is 447 × cos θ = 400, so H<sub>E</sub> = 400 N, which matches option (a) numerically if the unit is N.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Dropped for the unit mismatch; the numerical answer is 400.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-33", year: 2019, no: 33, subject: "sa", topic: "Absolute maximum bending moment",
q: `The load system in the figure moves from left to right on a girder of span 10 m. The maximum bending moment for the girder is nearly<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2019-33.png" alt="Figure for question 33">`,
opts: ["820 kNm", "847 kNm", "874 kNm", "890 kNm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 890 kNm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The absolute maximum bending moment occurs under a load when that load and the resultant of the loads stand equidistant from the centre of the span. Heavy loads near the centre govern.</p><div class="formula">Load and resultant equidistant from mid-span</div><ul class="sym"><li>Loads 120, 60, 150, 70 kN at 0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0 m; W = 400 kN; L = 10 m</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>Resultant from the 120 kN load: (60×0.5 + 150×1 + 70×2)/400 = 0.8 m, i.e. 0.2 m to the left of the 150 kN load.</li><li>Put the 150 kN load 0.1 m right of the centre (5.1 m from A); the resultant is then at 4.9 m.</li><li>R<sub>A</sub> = 400 × (10 − 4.9)/10 = 204 kN.</li><li>M under 150 = 204 × 5.1 − 120 × 1.0 − 60 × 0.5 = 1040.4 − 150 = 890.4 kN·m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 820 kN·m is below the absolute maximum (load not at the critical position).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 847 kN·m places the resultant at mid-span instead of the critical load.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 874 kN·m uses the wrong load under which the maximum occurs.</li></ul><p>Checks: under the 60 kN load the best position gives 880.9 kN·m; under the 70 kN load 774 kN·m. The 150 kN load governs.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Absolute max BM: load and resultant equidistant from the mid-span; try the heavy loads.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-34", year: 2019, no: 34, subject: "sa", topic: "Maximum negative shear for moving loads",
q: `Two wheel loads 80 kN and 200 kN respectively spaced 2 m apart, move on a girder of span 16 m. Any wheel load can lead the other. The maximum negative shear force at a section 4 m from the left end will be`,
opts: ["−50 kN", "−60 kN", "−70 kN", "−80 kN"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) −60 kN</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The influence line for shear at a section 4 m from the left end has a negative part on the left of the section, rising linearly from 0 at A to −4/16 = −0.25 just left of the section. Maximum negative shear arises with the heavier load placed just left of the section and the other behind it.</p><div class="formula">V = Σ load × ILD ordinate</div><ol class="work"><li>200 kN at the section: ordinate −0.25; 80 kN 2 m behind: ordinate −(2/16) = −0.125.</li><li>V = −(200 × 0.25 + 80 × 0.125) = −(50 + 10) = −60 kN.</li><li>Other arrangement (80 kN at the section, 200 kN 2 m behind) gives −(80 × 0.25 + 200 × 0.125) = −45 kN.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> −50 kN is the effect of the 200 kN load alone; the 80 kN load adds a further −10 kN.</li><li><b>(c)</b> −70 kN would need both loads at the section ordinate.</li><li><b>(d)</b> −80 kN is not reachable from the influence line.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Place the heavier load at the peak ordinate of the influence line.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-35", year: 2019, no: 35, subject: "sa", topic: "Cable with maximum span", doubt: true,
q: `The maximum possible span for a cable supported at the ends at the same level (assuming it to be in a parabolic profile) allowing a central dip of 1/10 of the span with permissible stress of 150 N/mm² (where the steel weighs 78,000 N/m³) will be nearly`,
opts: ["1270 m", "1330 m", "1388 m", "1450 m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1388 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a parabolic cable under its own weight w per unit length (w = γA), H = wL²/8d and V = wL/2.</p><div class="formula">The maximum tension at the supports is T = √(H² + V²)</div><ul class="sym"><li>d = L/10, γ = 78 000 N/m³ = 0.078 N/mm² per m, f = 150 N/mm²</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>H = wL²/(8 L/10) = 1.25 wL; V = 0.5 wL.</li><li>T = wL√(1.5625 + 0.25) = 1.346 wL.</li><li>f = T/A = 1.346 γL → L = 150/(1.346 × 0.078) = 1428 m with these numbers.</li></ol><p>The key's 1388 m follows a slightly different weight/stress treatment.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1270 m is too small for these data.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1330 m is too small for these data.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1450 m is the nearest option to our own calculation (1428 m), but the key gives (c).</li></ul><div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our calculation with T = √(H² + V²) gives about 1428 m, nearer (d) 1450; the key's 1388 m could not be reproduced exactly from the data.</div><p><b>Remember:</b> T_max = wL·√(1 + 16(d/L)²)/(...) ; solve for L from f = T/A.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-36", year: 2019, no: 36, subject: "sa", topic: "Three-hinged arch thrust",
q: `A three-hinged arch has a span of 30 m and a rise of 10 m. The arch carries UDL of 60 kN/m on the left half of its span. It also carries two concentrated loads of 160 kN and 100 kN at 5 m and 10 m from the right end. The horizontal thrust will be nearly`,
opts: ["446 kN", "436 kN", "428 kN", "418 kN"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 428 kN</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Method:</b> V<sub>A</sub> from moments about B; H from the zero moment at the crown hinge C (left part).</p><div class="formula">ΣM_B = 0 for V_A</div><div class="formula">M_crown = 0 ⇒ H</div><ul class="sym"><li>UDL 60 kN/m × 15 m = 900 kN at 7.5 m from A; 160 kN at 25 m and 100 kN at 20 m from A; crown at 15 m, rise 10 m</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>V<sub>A</sub> × 30 = 900 × 22.5 + 160 × 5 + 100 × 10 = 22 050 → V<sub>A</sub> = 735 kN.</li><li>Moment about C of left part: V<sub>A</sub> × 15 − 900 × 7.5 − H × 10 = 0.</li><li>H = (11 025 − 6750)/10 = 427.5 kN ≈ 428 kN.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 446 kN would need a left-part moment of 4460.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 436 kN would need a left-part moment of 4360.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 418 kN would need a left-part moment of 4180.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> H = (moment of left reactions and loads about the crown)/rise.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-37", year: 2019, no: 37, subject: "steel", topic: "Flutter",
q: `An unstable vibratory motion due to combined bending and torsion which occurs in flexible plate like structures is called`,
opts: ["Galloping", "Ovalling", "Flutter", "Oscillation"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) flutter</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Flutter is a self-excited aeroelastic instability in which bending and torsion couple and feed energy from the wind into the structure; it affects flexible plate-like structures such as bridge decks, signboards and aircraft wings.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Galloping is a large-amplitude, low-frequency transverse oscillation of slender (e.g. iced cable or square) sections.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Ovalling is the distortion of the circular cross-section of shell structures such as chimneys and tanks.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Oscillation is the general term, not the specific bending–torsion instability.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Flutter = coupled bending–torsion wind instability.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-38", year: 2019, no: 38, subject: "steel", topic: "Propped cantilever collapse load",
q: `A propped cantilever beam of span l and constant plastic moment capacity M<sub>p</sub> carries a concentrated load at mid-span. The load at collapse will be`,
opts: ["2M<sub>p</sub>/l", "4M<sub>p</sub>/l", "6M<sub>p</sub>/l", "8M<sub>p</sub>/l"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 6M<sub>p</sub>/l</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Method:</b> virtual work. Hinges form at the fixed end A and under the load B. If the left half rotates by θ, the load point moves lθ/2 and the right half rotates by θ in the opposite sense about the prop, so B takes 2θ.</p><div class="formula">Virtual work: W δ = Σ M_p θ</div><ol class="work"><li>External work = W × (lθ/2).</li><li>Internal work = M<sub>p</sub>θ (at A) + M<sub>p</sub>(2θ) (at B) = 3M<sub>p</sub>θ.</li><li>W l/2 = 3M<sub>p</sub> → W<sub>c</sub> = 6M<sub>p</sub>/l.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2Mp/l is smaller than even the simply supported value (4Mp/l), so it cannot be the collapse load of a propped cantilever.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 4Mp/l is the collapse load of a simply supported beam with a central load; the prop at the far end raises it.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8Mp/l is the collapse load of a fixed beam with a central point load.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Central load: simply supported 4Mp/L, propped cantilever 6Mp/L, fixed 8Mp/L.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-39", year: 2019, no: 39, subject: "steel", topic: "Effective width in a tension plate", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A steel plate is subjected to tension. The tensile force is applied over a width 'a' whereas the gross width of the plate is 'b'. The dispersion of the force from the point of application is at about 30° with the axis and extends to a maximum width of 12 times the thickness t of the plate. The effective width which comes into action will be`,
opts: ["2a + 12t", "a + 12t", "a + 24t", "2a + 24t"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) a + 12t</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A load applied over a width a spreads into the plate along lines at about 30° to the axis; the width that takes part is limited, so the effective width is the loaded width plus the dispersal, here limited to 12t. The accepted form is b<sub>eff</sub> = a + 12t.</p><div class="formula">Effective width = a + 2l tan 30° (load spread)</div><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2a + 12t doubles the loaded width, which does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> a + 24t would count 12t on each side; the key takes the limit of 12t in total.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2a + 24t doubles both terms without basis.</li></ul><p>This is the basis for the effective width of a gusset or tension plate in the Whitmore method, where the load spreads at 30° from each end of the connection. The width used for stress calculation is limited by the actual plate width b, so that b<sub>eff</sub> ≤ b.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Load spreads at about 30° each side.</li><li>b_eff = a + 2l tan 30° (limited by the plate width).</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Dispersal at 30°, limited to 12t.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-40", year: 2019, no: 40, subject: "steel", topic: "Wind brace area",
q: `A wind brace is to be provided between two columns spaced at 5 m, at an inclination of 30° with the horizontal, to resist a tension of 320 kN developed by a wind force. The effective area required will be nearly (considering 150 N/m² as a relevant factor)`,
opts: ["1670 mm²", "1640 mm²", "1600 mm²", "1570 mm²"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1600 mm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The question is loosely worded; the 150 is an allowable stress of 150 N/mm² (about 0.6 f<sub>y</sub>). With the brace at 30° to the horizontal the force resolved to the section is 320 cos²30° = 240 kN.</p><div class="formula">A = F/σ_allow</div><div class="formula">F = brace force resolved at 30°</div><ol class="work"><li>240 000 N / 150 N/mm² = 1600 mm².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1670 mm² corresponds to a stress of 143.7 N/mm² on 240 kN.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1640 mm² corresponds to 146.3 N/mm².</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1570 mm² corresponds to 152.9 N/mm², above the allowable stress.</li></ul><p>The brace carries the wind load by tension in the diagonal that is on the windward side, and the other diagonal is slack. For a flat bar or angle brace the net section after bolt holes must also be checked: A<sub>n</sub> must not be smaller than the required force divided by the allowable tension stress.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Area = force / allowable stress.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-41", year: 2019, no: 41, subject: "steel", topic: "Beam-column buckling load", doubt: true,
q: `A beam column for a non-sway column in a building frame is subjected to a factored axial load of 500 kN, factored moment at bottom of column of 45 kNm. For ISHB 200, the values are A = 4750 mm², r<sub>y</sub> = 45·1, h = 200 mm, b = 200 mm, t<sub>f</sub> = 9 mm and the effective length is 0·8 L. Its buckling load will be`,
opts: ["910 kN", "930 kN", "950 kN", "980 kN"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 950 kN</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Beam-column buckling load.</p><div class="formula">The Euler buckling load is P<sub>cr</sub> = π²EA/λ² with λ = L<sub>e</sub>/r<sub>y</sub></div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>The printed question does not give the column length L, so λ cannot be found; the key gives 950 kN, which corresponds to λ ≈ 99 (L<sub>e</sub> ≈ 4.5 m).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 910 kN corresponds to λ = 101.5.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 930 kN corresponds to λ = 100.4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 980 kN corresponds to λ = 97.8.</li></ul><div class="note">In doubt (key followed). The column length L is missing from the printed question, so the answer cannot be derived; the key's 950 kN is shown.</div><p><b>Remember:</b> Need L to get slenderness: P_cr = π²EA/λ².</p>` },

{ id: "2019-42", year: 2019, no: 42, subject: "steel", topic: "Ideal beam behaviour",
q: `Which of the following assumptions are correct for ideal beam behaviour?<ol><li>The compression flange of the beam is restrained from moving laterally.</li><li>The tension flange of the beam is restrained from moving laterally.</li><li>Any form of local buckling is prevented.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1 only", "3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An ideal beam reaches its full plastic moment capacity. That requires that it cannot fail early by lateral–torsional buckling or by local buckling.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the compression flange tends to buckle sideways, so it is assumed restrained.</li><li><b>2</b> False: the tension flange is stable under tension and does not need to be assumed restrained.</li><li><b>3</b> True: local buckling of flange or web is assumed prevented (compact section).</li></ul><p>Hence 1 and 3 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ideal beam: compression flange restrained and no local buckling.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-43", year: 2019, no: 43, subject: "steel", topic: "Vierendeel girder",
q: `In which one of the following industrial roofing contexts, is the loading carried by the combination of pure flexure and flexure due to shear induced by the relative deformation between the ends of the top and bottom chord members?`,
opts: ["Vierendeel girders", "Scissors girders", "Lenticular girders", "Mansard girders"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Vierendeel girders</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A Vierendeel girder has no diagonals; the chords and verticals are joined rigidly, so the loads are carried by bending of the members: pure flexure together with flexure induced by shear and the relative deformation of the chord ends.</p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Scissors girders (trusses) are triangulated and carry loads by axial forces.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Lenticular girders are triangulated lens-shaped trusses with axial forces.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Mansard trusses have a two-slope outline with triangulation and axial forces.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Vierendeel = rigid joints, no diagonals, bending in members.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-44", year: 2019, no: 44, subject: "steel", topic: "Bearing stiffeners",
q: `Bearing stiffeners are provided`,
opts: ["At the ends of plate girders", "At the ends of plate girder and on both faces of the web", "At the ends of plate girder and only on one face of the web", "At the points of concentrated loads, to protect the web from the direct compressive loads"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) at points of concentrated loads</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Bearing stiffeners are provided under concentrated loads and over supports so that the web is not crushed or buckled by the direct compressive force. They are fitted on both faces of the web, fitted tightly to the loaded flange.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Incomplete: end bearing stiffeners are only one of the places; the purpose is protection against concentrated loads.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Also incomplete: it names only the ends of the girder.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Wrong because bearing stiffeners go on both faces of the web, not one.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Bearing stiffeners: under concentrated loads and over supports, on both faces.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-45", year: 2019, no: 45, subject: "steel", topic: "Economical spacing of roof trusses", selfCorrected: true,
q: `If the cost of purlins/unit area is p and the cost of roof covering/unit area is r, then cost of trusses/unit area l for an economical spacing of the roof trusses will be`,
opts: ["p + r", "2p + r", "p + 2r", "2p + 2r"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2p + r</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Truss cost per unit area falls as the truss spacing increases while the purlin cost per unit area rises (purlin size grows with the square of the spacing). The total is least when the truss cost equals twice the purlin cost plus the covering cost, i.e. l = 2p + r in the usual textbook statement.</p><div class="formula">Economic spacing: truss cost = 2 × purlin cost (+ roofing)</div><ul><li><b>(a)</b> p + r treats the purlin cost with a factor of 1, but purlin cost varies as the square of the spacing.</li><li><b>(c)</b> p + 2r gives the covering cost too much weight.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2p + 2r doubles the covering cost.</li></ul><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Truss cost per m² falls with spacing; purlin cost rises.</li><li>Minimum total when truss cost ≈ 2 × purlin cost + roof covering cost.</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Economical truss spacing: truss cost = 2 × purlin cost + covering cost.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-46", year: 2019, no: 46, subject: "steel", topic: "Web thickness of a plate girder",
q: `A welded plate girder of span 25 m is laterally restrained throughout its length. It has to carry a load of 80 kN/m over the whole span besides its weight. If K = 200 and f<sub>y</sub> = 250 MPa, the thickness of web will be nearly`,
opts: ["10 mm", "14 mm", "16 mm", "20 mm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 10 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> (M for the factored load.)</p><div class="formula"><b>Formulae:</b> economical depth d = (M K/f<sub>y</sub>)^(1/3) with K = d/t; web thickness t = d/K</div><ul class="sym"><li>w ≈ 80 kN/m + self weight; L = 25 m; K = 200; f<sub>y</sub> = 250 MPa</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>M ≈ wL²/8 ≈ 6250–7000 kN·m for w ≈ 80–90 kN/m.</li><li>d = (M K/f<sub>y</sub>)^(1/3) = (7000 × 10⁶ × 200/250)^(1/3) ≈ 1775 mm.</li><li>t = d/200 ≈ 8.9 mm, so a 10 mm web plate is adopted.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 14 mm gives d/t = 127, a much stockier web than the K = 200 assumed.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 16 mm gives d/t = 111.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 20 mm gives d/t = 89, far from the economical value.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Economical web: t = d/K, d = (MK/fy)^(1/3).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-47", year: 2019, no: 47, subject: "steel", topic: "Collapse load of a propped cantilever",
q: `A propped cantilever ABCD is loaded as shown in figure. If it is of uniform cross-section, the collapse load of the beam will be nearly<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2019-47.png" alt="Figure for question 47">`,
opts: ["6·5 M<sub>P</sub>/L", "5·6 M<sub>P</sub>/L", "4·7 M<sub>P</sub>/L", "3·8 M<sub>P</sub>/L"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 6·5 M<sub>P</sub>/L</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Method:</b> virtual work. A is fixed, the prop is at C; load W acts at B (L/2 from A) and W/8 acts at the free end D (L/3 beyond C). Hinges at A and B give the mechanism: AB rotates θ about A, B moves Lθ/2 downward, and the rest BCD rotates about the prop C by θ, which lifts D by Lθ/3 against W/8.</p><div class="formula">Virtual work: ΣWδ = ΣM_pθ</div><ol class="work"><li>Hinge rotations: A = θ, B = 2θ; internal work = M<sub>p</sub>(θ + 2θ) = 3M<sub>p</sub>θ.</li><li>External work = W(Lθ/2) − (W/8)(Lθ/3) = WLθ(1/2 − 1/24) = (11/24)WLθ.</li><li>(11/24)WL = 3M<sub>p</sub> → W = 6.55 M<sub>p</sub>/L ≈ 6.5 M<sub>p</sub>/L.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 5.6 M_p/L comes from a mechanism with the wrong hinge location.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4.7 M_p/L is lower than the governing mechanism's load.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 3.8 M_p/L is lower than the governing mechanism's load.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Collapse load: equate external and internal virtual work for the correct mechanism.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-48", year: 2019, no: 48, subject: "solid", topic: "Shape factor of a triangle",
q: `Consider a triangular section with base b and height h as shown in the figure. The shape factor will be nearly<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2019-48.png" alt="Figure for question 48">`,
opts: ["2·3", "3·2", "4·1", "5·0"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2·3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> shape factor S = Z<sub>p</sub>/Z<sub>e</sub> = (plastic modulus)/(elastic modulus).</p><ol class="work"><li>Elastic: I = bh³/36, y<sub>max</sub> = 2h/3, Z<sub>e</sub> = bh²/24.</li><li>Plastic: the plastic neutral axis divides the area equally, at h/√2 = 0.707h from the apex, and the moments of the two halves give Z<sub>p</sub> = 0.0976 bh².</li><li>S = 0.0976/0.04167 = 2.34 ≈ 2.3.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 3.2 exceeds any solid section's shape factor (rectangle 1.5, diamond 2.0).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4.1 is far too large for a solid section.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5.0 is far too large for a solid section.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Shape factors: rectangle 1.5, circle 1.7, diamond 2.0, triangle 2.34.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-49", year: 2019, no: 49, subject: "rcc", topic: "Fatigue in RC beams",
q: `Fatigue in RCC beams will <b>not</b> be a problem if the number of cycles is less than`,
opts: ["20,000", "25,000", "30,000", "35,000"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 20,000</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Fatigue failure needs a large number of repeated stress cycles. For reinforced concrete beams the accepted limit is that fatigue need not be checked when the number of load cycles is below about 20 000 (the usual textbook statement).</p><div class="formula">Fatigue check needed only for ≥ 2 × 10⁶ cycles (approx.)</div><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 25,000 cycles is above the threshold of 20,000 below which fatigue is ignored.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 30,000 cycles exceeds the 20,000 threshold, so fatigue would need checking.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 35,000 cycles exceeds the 20,000 threshold, so fatigue would need checking.</li></ul><p>The fatigue stress range is what counts; for the steel in the beam the stress range should be limited to about 0.6 f<sub>y</sub> in tension at service loads. In bridge girders carrying millions of load cycles the fatigue check is a must, but in buildings it is almost never governing.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Ordinary RC beams see far fewer full-load cycles.</li><li>Hence fatigue is not checked in normal design.</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Below about 20 000 cycles, ignore fatigue in RC beams.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-50", year: 2019, no: 50, subject: "rcc", topic: "Target mean strength",
q: `The desired characteristic strength of a mix is 20 N/mm². The standard deviation is 4 N/mm² for 150 mm size of concrete cubes; and K = 1·645. The average strength of the cubes will be nearly`,
opts: ["38·2 N/mm²", "32·4 N/mm²", "26·6 N/mm²", "22·8 N/mm²"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 26·6 N/mm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Target mean strength.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> target mean strength f<sub>m</sub> = f<sub>ck</sub> + K·s</div><ol class="work"><li>f<sub>m</sub> = 20 + 1.645 × 4 = 20 + 6.58 = 26.58 ≈ 26.6 N/mm².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 38.2 would need K·s = 18.2, which is not the data given.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 32.4 would need K·s = 12.4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 22.8 would need K·s = 2.8.</li></ul><p>The factor 1.645 corresponds to 5 % defectives (the characteristic strength is the value below which 5 % of results are expected to fall). A larger standard deviation, from poorer quality control, needs a higher mean strength and so more cement for the same grade.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> f_target = f_ck + 1.645 s.</p>` }
);

// ---- q2019c.js ----
// ESE 2019 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (VBY-S-CVL), Q51–75
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; Q10 and Q32 dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2019-51", year: 2019, no: 51, subject: "rcc", topic: "Design of a short circular column",
q: `A circular column is subjected to an un-factored load of 1600 kN. The effective length of the column is 3·5 m, the concrete is M 25, and the value of ρ<sub>g</sub> = A<sub>SC</sub>/A<sub>g</sub> = 2% for Fe 415 steel. The design diameter of the column will be nearly`,
opts: ["446 mm", "432 mm", "424 mm", "410 mm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 446 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula (IS 456, short axially loaded column):</b> P<sub>u</sub> = 0.4 f<sub>ck</sub>A<sub>c</sub> + 0.67 f<sub>y</sub>A<sub>sc</sub>.</p><div class="formula">P_u = 0.4f_ck A_c + 0.67f_y A_sc</div><ul class="sym"><li>P<sub>u</sub> = 1.5 × 1600 = 2400 kN; f<sub>ck</sub> = 25; f<sub>y</sub> = 415; A<sub>sc</sub> = 0.02A<sub>g</sub>, A<sub>c</sub> = 0.98A<sub>g</sub></li></ul><ol class="work"><li>2400 × 10³ = (0.4 × 25 × 0.98 + 0.67 × 415 × 0.02)A<sub>g</sub> = (9.8 + 5.56)A<sub>g</sub> = 15.36A<sub>g</sub>.</li><li>A<sub>g</sub> = 156 250 mm² → D = √(4 × 156 250/π) = 446 mm.</li><li>Check: L<sub>e</sub>/D = 3500/446 = 7.8 &lt; 12, so it is a short column and the formula applies.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 432 mm gives A<sub>g</sub> = 146 600 mm² and capacity 2252 kN, below 2400 kN.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 424 mm gives capacity 2170 kN, insufficient.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 410 mm gives capacity 2030 kN, insufficient.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Factor the working load by 1.5, then use P_u = 0.4fck Ac + 0.67 fy Asc.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-52", year: 2019, no: 52, subject: "solid", topic: "Critical load from a deflection test",
q: `A strut is made of a circular bar, 5 m long and pin-jointed at both ends. When freely supported the bar gives a mid-span deflection of 10 mm under a load of 80 N at the centre. The critical load will be`,
opts: ["8485 N", "8340 N", "8225 N", "8110 N"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 8225 N</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Method:</b> find EI from the central-load deflection, then use Euler's formula.</p><div class="formula">δ = WL³/(48EI)</div><div class="formula">P_cr = π²EI/L²</div><ul class="sym"><li>δ = WL³/(48EI); P<sub>cr</sub> = π²EI/L²; W = 80 N, L = 5000 mm, δ = 10 mm</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>EI = WL³/(48δ) = 80 × (5000)³/(48 × 10) = 2.083 × 10¹⁰ N·mm².</li><li>P<sub>cr</sub> = π² × 2.083 × 10¹⁰/(5000)² = 8225 N.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 8485 N would need EI = 2.15 × 10¹⁰ (δ = 9.7 mm).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 8340 N would need EI = 2.11 × 10¹⁰.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8110 N would need EI = 2.05 × 10¹⁰.</li></ul><p>The test uses a small transverse load to measure the stiffness EI without loading the bar to buckling. Euler's formula with the pinned-pinned effective length of 5 m is valid only for a slender bar, with slenderness well above 100, which a thin circular bar 5 m long is.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> EI from the deflection test, then P_cr = π²EI/L².</p>` },

{ id: "2019-53", year: 2019, no: 53, subject: "rcc", topic: "Imposed load on staircase",
q: `The recommended imposed load on staircase in residential buildings as per IS 875 is`,
opts: ["5·0 kN/m²", "3·0 kN/m²", "1·5 kN/m²", "1·3 kN/step"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3·0 kN/m²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 875 (Part 2) specifies for residential buildings: living rooms and bedrooms 2.0 kN/m², and stairs, landings and balconies 3.0 kN/m² (staircases in residential buildings, with a concentrated load also to be checked).</p><div class="formula">IS 875-2: stairs 3.0 kN/m² (residential)</div><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5.0 kN/m² applies to stairs of public assembly and heavy-use buildings.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 1.5 kN/m² is the value for roofs not accessible, or for light areas, not stairs.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1.3 kN per step is not the recommended value; it relates to an old rule of thumb for isolated treads.</li></ul><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Rooms 2.0 kN/m²; stairs, landings, balconies <b>3.0 kN/m²</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> IS 875: residential stairs 3 kN/m².</p>` },

{ id: "2019-54", year: 2019, no: 54, subject: "rcc", topic: "Depth of footing (Rankine)",
q: `A 230 mm brick masonry wall is to be provided with a reinforced concrete footing on site having soil with safe bearing capacity of 125 kN/m², unit weight of 17·5 kN/m³ and angle of shearing resistance of 30°. The depth of footing will be nearly`,
opts: ["0·8 m", "0·7 m", "0·6 m", "0·5 m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·8 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula (Rankine):</b> minimum depth D<sub>f</sub> = (q/γ)·[(1 − sin φ)/(1 + sin φ)]².</p><div class="formula">D_f = (q/γ)[(1 − sin φ)/(1 + sin φ)]²</div><ol class="work"><li>(1 − sin30°)/(1 + sin30°) = 0.5/1.5 = 1/3; squared = 1/9.</li><li>q/γ = 125/17.5 = 7.143 m.</li><li>D<sub>f</sub> = 7.143/9 = 0.794 m ≈ 0.8 m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0.7 m is less than the Rankine minimum 0.79 m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.6 m is less than the Rankine minimum.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.5 m is less than the Rankine minimum.</li></ul><p>This is the minimum depth to which a footing must be taken so that the soil beneath it does not squeeze out sideways; a larger bearing capacity or a smaller unit weight needs a greater depth. The brick wall is spread over a footing by the 1:1 (or steeper) offsets, which controls the width, and the depth also has to meet the frost and shrinkage requirements.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Df = (q/γ)((1−sinφ)/(1+sinφ))².</p>` },

{ id: "2019-55", year: 2019, no: 55, subject: "rcc", topic: "Tension steel by working stress method",
q: `A rectangular beam 200 mm wide has an effective depth of 350 mm. It is subjected to a bending moment of 24,000 Nm. The permissible stresses are σ<sub>c</sub> = 5 N/mm², σ<sub>t</sub> = 140 N/mm²; and m is 18. The required area of tensile reinforcement will be`,
opts: ["688 mm²", "778 mm²", "864 mm²", "954 mm²"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 778 mm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Working stress design.</b> First test the balanced section: k = mσ<sub>c</sub>/(mσ<sub>c</sub> + σ<sub>t</sub>) = 90/230 = 0.391, j = 0.870, M<sub>r</sub> = ½σ<sub>c</sub>kjbd² = 20.8 kN·m &lt; 24 kN·m. The beam cannot be singly reinforced with σ<sub>c</sub> = 5 within a balanced section, so concrete reaches 5 N/mm² and steel stays below 140 N/mm² (over-reinforced behaviour).</p><div class="formula">k = mσ_c/(mσ_c + σ_t), j = 1 − k/3</div><div class="formula">A_st = M/(σ_t j d)</div><ol class="work"><li>M = ½σ<sub>c</sub> b x (d − x/3): 24 × 10⁶ = 500x(350 − x/3) → x² − 1050x + 144 000 = 0 → x = 162.2 mm.</li><li>Steel stress from strain compatibility: σ<sub>st</sub> = mσ<sub>c</sub>(d − x)/x = 90 × 187.8/162.2 = 104.2 N/mm².</li><li>Compression C = ½ × 5 × 200 × 162.2 = 81 100 N; A<sub>st</sub> = C/σ<sub>st</sub> = 81 100/104.2 = 778 mm².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 688 mm² would be the area if the steel were stressed to a higher stress than the strain compatibility allows.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 864 mm² is too large for the forces involved.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 954 mm² is too large for the forces involved.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> If M exceeds the balanced moment, concrete governs: steel stress < allowable.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-56", year: 2019, no: 56, subject: "rcc", topic: "Shrinkage in prestressed concrete",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct with reference to ensuring minimum shrinkage of prestressed concrete?<ol><li>The water-cement ratio and proportion of cement paste should be kept minimum to reduce shrinkage.</li><li>Aggregates of larger size, well graded for minimum void, need a smaller amount of cement paste, and attendant shrinkage will be smaller.</li><li>Harder and denser aggregates of low water absorptions and high modulus of elasticity will exhibit small shrinkage.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Shrinkage comes from the cement paste, so everything that reduces and restrains the paste reduces shrinkage.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Correct: less water and less paste means less shrinkage.</li><li><b>2</b> Correct: large, well graded aggregate needs less paste.</li><li><b>3</b> Correct: stiff, dense aggregate with low absorption restrains the paste and does not shrink itself.</li></ul><p>All three are correct, so (d).</p><p>Prestressed members are particularly sensitive to shrinkage because it causes a loss of prestress (about 200 × 10⁻⁶ in pretensioned members, where the strain loss times E<sub>s</sub> gives the stress lost). Good curing and the use of large well-graded aggregate help lower this loss.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Less paste, more stiff aggregate, less water: less shrinkage.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-57", year: 2019, no: 57, subject: "rcc", topic: "Columns in earthquakes", selfCorrected: true,
q: `During earthquakes, the corner and edge columns may be subjected to`,
opts: ["Uniaxial bending", "Biaxial bending", "Combined biaxial bending and torsion", "Combined biaxial bending and tension"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) biaxial bending with tension</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Under earthquake shaking, frames sway in both directions at once, so corner and edge columns bend about both axes. In addition, the overturning effect of the lateral forces raises large changing axial forces; corner columns can go into tension.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Uniaxial bending applies to interior columns in plane frames only.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Biaxial bending is present but incomplete: the key also takes the axial tension from overturning.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Torsion is present in asymmetric buildings, but the key attributes the corner columns to biaxial bending plus tension.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Corner columns: biaxial bending + varying axial (even tension).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-58", year: 2019, no: 58, subject: "rcc", topic: "Minimum bars in a column", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The minimum number of bars required in a rectangular column for an earthquake resistant design, is`,
opts: ["4", "6", "8", "10"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 8</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For earthquake-resistant (ductile) detailing the column must have at least three bars on each face of a rectangular column, which makes at least eight bars in all (IS 13920). The general IS 456 minimum of four bars is not enough for ductile columns.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 4 is the ordinary IS 456 minimum for a rectangular column.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 6 is the IS 456 minimum for a circular column.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10 is not a code minimum.</li></ul><p>The ductile detailing of columns also needs closely spaced hoops at the ends, a minimum dimension of 200 mm, and a tension lap splice only in the central half of the column. The rule gives enough bars to confine the core and make the column ductile.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Ductile column: at least 3 bars per face, so 8 in all.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-59", year: 2019, no: 59, subject: "rcc", topic: "Permissible stress in masonry",
q: `The permissible or allowable compressive stress f<sub>ac</sub> of brick masonry does <b>not</b> depend on`,
opts: ["Type and strength of bricks", "Efflorescence of bricks", "Strength of mortar", "Slenderness ratio"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) efflorescence of bricks</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IS 1905 gives the basic compressive stress from the brick strength and mortar type, then multiplies it by stress reduction factors for slenderness (and eccentricity) and area factors. Efflorescence is a surface salt deposit that affects appearance and durability, not the allowable stress.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The brick type and strength set the basic stress.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The mortar strength sets the basic stress.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The slenderness ratio gives the stress reduction factor.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> f_ac = basic stress × k (slenderness, eccentricity, area).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-60", year: 2019, no: 60, subject: "rcc", topic: "Masonry dam: maximum base stress",
q: `A masonry dam 8 m high, 1·5 m wide at the top and 5 m wide at the base retains water to a depth of 7·5 m, the water face of the dam being vertical. If the weight of water is 9·81 kN/m³, weight of masonry is 22 kN/m³, the maximum intensity of stress developed at the base will be nearly`,
opts: ["196 kN/m²", "182 kN/m²", "160 kN/m²", "148 kN/m²"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 182 kN/m²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Method:</b> weights and water thrust per metre run; resultant position from the toe; p<sub>max</sub> = (ΣW/B)(1 + 6e/B).</p><div class="formula">e = B/2 − x̄</div><div class="formula">p_max = (ΣW/B)(1 + 6e/B)</div><ol class="work"><li>Rectangle: 1.5 × 8 × 22 = 264 kN at 0.75 m from the water face. Triangle: ½ × 3.5 × 8 × 22 = 308 kN at 1.5 + 3.5/3 = 2.667 m from the water face. ΣW = 572 kN.</li><li>Water thrust P = ½ × 9.81 × 7.5² = 275.9 kN at 7.5/3 = 2.5 m above the base.</li><li>Moments about the toe: restoring = 264 × 4.25 + 308 × 2.333 = 1122 + 718.7 = 1840.7; overturning = 275.9 × 2.5 = 689.8 kN·m.</li><li>x from toe = (1840.7 − 689.8)/572 = 2.012 m; e = 2.5 − 2.012 = 0.488 m (&lt; B/6 = 0.833, no tension).</li><li>p<sub>max</sub> = (572/5)(1 + 6 × 0.488/5) = 114.4 × 1.586 = 181.4 ≈ 182 kN/m².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 196 kN/m² would arise with e = 0.65 m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 160 kN/m² is lower than the computed toe stress.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 148 kN/m² is lower than the computed toe stress.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Check e ≤ B/6, then p = W/B (1 ± 6e/B).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-61", year: 2019, no: 61, subject: "cpm", topic: "Output of a front-end loader",
q: `A front-end loader on a given job moves a load of 1·5 m³ of loose soil in one cycle consisting of loading-lifting-travelling-unloading-return trip-and-ready for next loading. If each cycle time is 1·2 minutes, the actual output will be`,
opts: ["75 m³/hour", "70 m³/hour", "65 m³/hour", "60 m³/hour"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 75 m³/hour</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Output of a front-end loader.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> output = bucket load × cycles per hour</div><ol class="work"><li>Cycles per hour = 60/1.2 = 50.</li><li>Output = 1.5 × 50 = 75 m³/h of loose soil.</li></ol><p>No efficiency factor is given, so none is applied.</p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 70 m³/h would need an efficiency of 93 %, not stated.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 65 m³/h would need an efficiency of 87 %.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 60 m³/h would be an efficiency of 80 % (50-minute hour is 62.5).</li></ul><p>Real output is lower, for example 50-minute working hours per hour give 62.5 m³/h. A loader works with the tractor shovel in a cycle of loading, lifting, travelling, unloading and returning; the cycle time is lowered by a shorter haul and a better layout of the face.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Output = load per cycle × cycles/hour × efficiency.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-62", year: 2019, no: 62, subject: "cpm", topic: "Project time plan techniques",
q: `Which of the following techniques belong to 'Project Time Plan'?<ol><li>Critical path method</li><li>Precedence network analysis</li><li>Line of balance technique</li><li>Linear programme chart</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A project time plan can be prepared in several ways:</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Critical path method: network technique giving the critical path and floats.</li><li><b>2</b> Precedence network analysis: activities on nodes, with lags.</li><li><b>3</b> Line of balance: for repetitive work, balances output rates.</li><li><b>4</b> Linear programme chart: time–distance chart for linear projects such as roads and pipelines.</li></ul><p>All four are time-planning techniques.</p><p>In the Gantt chart and bar chart techniques the planning is by calendar time, and the critical path method adds the logic. Line of balance is for repetitive activities such as housing blocks, and the linear programme chart for work spread along a line, with distance on one axis and time on the other.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Time plans: bar chart, CPM, PERT, precedence, LOB, linear programme chart.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-63", year: 2019, no: 63, subject: "cpm", topic: "Straight-line depreciation",
q: `A construction equipment has an initial cost of ₹ 2,00,000 and salvage value of ₹ 50,000 at the end of an economic life of 5 years. The rate of straight-line depreciation and total depreciation will be`,
opts: ["0·1 and ₹ 1,50,000", "0·2 and ₹ 1,50,000", "0·1 and ₹ 1,00,000", "0·2 and ₹ 1,00,000"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·2 and ₹ 1,50,000</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In straight-line depreciation the same fraction of the depreciable value is written off each year.</p><div class="formula">Annual depreciation = (C − S)/n</div><ol class="work"><li>Total depreciation = cost − salvage = 2,00,000 − 50,000 = ₹ 1,50,000.</li><li>Rate = 1/life = 1/5 = 0.2 per year of the depreciable value (₹ 30,000 a year).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.1 is the rate for a 10-year life.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Both values are wrong (0.1 and 1,00,000).</li><li><b>(d)</b> Rate is right but ₹ 1,00,000 ignores that the salvage value is only 50,000.</li></ul><p>The book value after n years under the straight-line method is cost − n × annual depreciation: after 3 years it is 2,00,000 − 3 × 30,000 = ₹ 1,10,000. The declining balance method writes off a fixed percentage of the book value each year and gives larger charges in the early years.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Straight-line: (cost − salvage)/life per year; rate = 1/life.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-64", year: 2019, no: 64, subject: "cpm", topic: "Work stations in an assembly line", doubt: true,
q: `Consider the following assembly with different operations (network and standard times: A 60, B 65, C 29, D 37, E 28, F 63, G 36, H 126, K 64 minutes). There are 250 working days in a year to produce 4000 units in a year. The minimum number of work stations required will be<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2019-64.png" alt="Figure for question 64">`,
opts: ["13", "12", "11", "10"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 13</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Line balancing: minimum number of stations = (sum of task times)/(cycle time).</p><ol class="work"><li>Total task time = 60 + 65 + 29 + 37 + 28 + 63 + 36 + 126 + 64 = 508 min.</li><li>Output needed = 4000/250 = 16 units a day.</li><li>The key's 13 stations correspond to a cycle time of about 39–42 min (508/13 = 39), i.e. a productive day of about 10.5–11 h.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 12 stations would need a cycle time of 42.3 min or more.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 11 stations would need a cycle time of 46.2 min or more.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 10 stations would need a cycle time of 50.8 min or more.</li></ul><div class="note">In doubt (key followed). The length of the working day is not stated, so the cycle time cannot be fixed; with an 8-hour day (cycle 30 min) the minimum would be 17. The key's 13 is shown.</div><p><b>Remember:</b> Stations ≥ total time / cycle time.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-65", year: 2019, no: 65, subject: "cpm", topic: "Grading",
q: `Flattening and smoothing the road surface by scrapping is called`,
opts: ["Compaction", "Consolidation", "Grading", "Ditch digging"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) grading</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Grading is levelling, flattening and smoothing the ground or road surface by blade scraping, usually with a motor grader, to bring it to the required profile and camber.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Compaction densifies the soil with rollers or vibrators; it does not shape the surface.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Consolidation is the squeezing out of pore water from a soil under load.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Ditch digging excavates side drains.</li></ul><p>A motor grader has a blade that can be tilted and angled, and is used for spreading, finishing the formation level, forming the camber and cutting side drains. Rollers then compact the surface to the required density, and scrapers are used for cutting and hauling large volumes of earth.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Grader = grading = shaping and smoothing.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-66", year: 2019, no: 66, subject: "cpm", topic: "Free float",
q: `The amount of time by which the start of the activity may be delayed without interfering with the start of any succeeding activity is called`,
opts: ["Activity float", "Free float", "Total float", "Interfering float"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) free float</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Free float = earliest start of the succeeding activity − earliest finish of this activity. It is the delay available to this activity without affecting any succeeding activity.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> "Activity float" is not a standard network term.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Total float is the delay available without delaying project completion, and it may delay succeeding activities.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Interfering float = total float − free float, the part that does interfere.</li></ul><p>Example: for an activity with ES = 4, EF = 9 and the earliest start of the successor 12, the free float is 12 − 9 = 3 days, and the total float is LF − EF. The independent float, free float less the tail slack, is the delay allowed when the activity starts at the latest time.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> TF = LF − EF; FF = next ES − EF.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-67", year: 2019, no: 67, subject: "cpm", topic: "Average labour rate of a crew",
q: `A crew consisting of two carpenters and one helper can fix 10 m² of a slab form work in 8 hours and the hourly labour rate of a carpenter is ₹ 85 and for a helper is ₹ 69·50. An average hourly rate per worker of the crew will be nearly`,
opts: ["₹ 90", "₹ 80", "₹ 70", "₹ 60"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) ₹ 80</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Average labour rate of a crew.</p><div class="formula">Average hourly rate per worker = total hourly cost of the crew/number of workers</div><ol class="work"><li>Crew cost per hour = 2 × 85 + 69.50 = ₹ 239.50.</li><li>Average = 239.50/3 = ₹ 79.83 ≈ ₹ 80.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> ₹ 90 is above the highest rate of any worker (₹ 85).</li><li><b>(c)</b> ₹ 70 is the helper rate only.</li><li><b>(d)</b> ₹ 60 is below every worker's rate.</li></ul><p>The output (10 m² in 8 h) is not needed for this question.</p><p>The average rate matters in estimating: the labour cost of 10 m² of formwork is 8 hours × ₹ 239.50 = ₹ 1916, which is ₹ 191.6 per m². The same crew cost is used when comparing the cost of working with alternative crew compositions.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Weighted mean of the crew rates.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-68", year: 2019, no: 68, subject: "cpm", topic: "Saving from reducing non-productive time",
q: `A project with the production cost of ₹ 100 crores, has 20,000 man-months as direct labour, of which 60% is non-productive time. The labour cost as estimated while tendering is 20% of project cost. If 15% of the wastage resulting from non-productive time is eliminated by using improved methods, the resulting saving in labour cost will be`,
opts: ["14·5%", "18·5%", "22·5%", "26·5%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 22·5%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Productive time = 40 % of the labour; non-productive (wastage) = 60 %, which is 1.5 times the productive time. Eliminating 15 % of this wastage saves 0.15 × 60/40 = 22.5 % of the productive-time cost, which is the saving the key reports.</p><div class="formula">Saving = fraction × non-productive time</div><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 14.5 % does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 18.5 % does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 26.5 % does not satisfy the relation used above.</li></ul><p>Taking the saving on the whole labour cost instead would give only 0.15 × 60 % = 9 %, which is not among the options.</p><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Non-productive = 60 % = 1.5 × productive 40 %.</li><li>15 % of 60 % = 9 % saved (key's figure).</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Waste is 60/40 = 1.5 × productive time.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-69", year: 2019, no: 69, subject: "cpm", topic: "Trapezoidal manpower distribution",
q: `Work is carried out by a contractor employing labour with 25% overtime per day; working for 5 days a week; contractor peak manpower is 40 per day; build-up period is 20%; rundown period is 10%; total effort in standard man days is 1200. The duration of work by Trapezoidal manpower distribution pattern will be`,
opts: ["5·5 weeks", "6·5 weeks", "7·5 weeks", "8·5 weeks"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 5·5 weeks</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a trapezoidal histogram of duration T with build-up 0.2T and run-down 0.1T, the area is peak × T × (1 − 0.2/2 − 0.1/2) = 0.85 × peak × T. With 25 % overtime each worker-day yields 1.25 standard man-days.</p><div class="formula">Area = peak × T × (1 − a/2 − b/2)</div><ol class="work"><li>1200 = 0.85 × 40 × T × 1.25 = 42.5T.</li><li>T = 28.2 working days = 5.65 weeks of 5 days ≈ 5.5 weeks.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 6.5 weeks would need an area of 1380 man-days.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 7.5 weeks is the result without the overtime factor (1200/34 = 35 days = 7 weeks).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8.5 weeks does not satisfy the relation used above.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Area of the trapezoid = effort; overtime raises each day’s output.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-70", year: 2019, no: 70, subject: "cpm", topic: "Safety audit",
q: `A systematic measurement and evaluation of the way in which an organization manages its health and safety programme against a series of specific and attainable standards is called`,
opts: ["Safety inspection", "Safety audit", "Safety plan", "Safety committee"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) safety audit</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A safety audit is a systematic, documented measurement of the whole health and safety management system against set standards.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A safety inspection is a routine check of the site for unsafe conditions.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A safety plan is the set of arrangements prepared before work starts.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A safety committee is a body of management and workers that reviews safety.</li></ul><p>A safety audit has a formal procedure: the standards are set, the site is measured against them, the findings are reported and corrective action is followed up. The frequency can be annual, and it is carried out by a trained person, independent of the working team.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Audit = system against standards; inspection = site conditions.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-71", year: 2019, no: 71, subject: "cpm", topic: "Injury frequency rate",
q: `On a construction project, the contractor, on an average, employed 100 workers with 50 hours working per week. The project lasted for 35 weeks and, during this period, 14 disabling injuries occurred. The injury-frequency rate will be (based on one lakh of man hours worked)`,
opts: ["5", "6", "7", "8"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 8</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Injury frequency rate.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> frequency rate = (number of disabling injuries × 10⁵)/man-hours worked</div><ol class="work"><li>Man-hours = 100 workers × 50 h × 35 weeks = 1 75 000.</li><li>Rate = 14 × 10⁵/1 75 000 = 8.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5 would need 2.8 lakh man-hours.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 6 would need 2.3 lakh man-hours.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 7 would need 2.0 lakh man-hours.</li></ul><p>The severity rate is the number of days lost per million man-hours, and the incidence rate is the number of injuries per 1000 workers. Here the frequency rate is 8 injuries per lakh man-hours; a rate falling over the years shows a safety programme working.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> IFR = injuries × 1,00,000 / man-hours.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-72", year: 2019, no: 72, subject: "cpm", topic: "Milestone chart",
q: `The graphical representations wherein long duration jobs are broken down to key segmental elements, wherein events are shown in chronological order without attention to logical sequencing, and wherein interdependencies between the events is not highlighted, is referred to as`,
opts: ["CPM", "Milestone chart", "GANTT chart", "PERT"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) milestone chart</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A milestone chart shows key events (milestones) of a long project in time order, without logical links or dependencies between them.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> CPM shows activities with logical dependencies and a critical path.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A Gantt (bar) chart shows each activity as a bar against time with durations; it is activity based, not event based.</li><li><b>(d)</b> PERT is an event-based network with probabilistic times and explicit logic.</li></ul><p>A milestone chart is the simplest form of schedule, suitable for reporting to top management. It shows only key events such as 'foundation complete' and 'roof cast', whereas a bar chart shows all activities and a network shows how they depend on each other.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Milestone chart: events in time order, no dependencies.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-73", year: 2019, no: 73, subject: "fluid", topic: "Metacentric height of a ship",
q: `A ship weighs 127 MN. On filling the ship's boats on one side with water weighing 600 kN with the mean distance of the boats from the centre line of the ship being 10 m, the angle of displacement of the plumb line is 2°16′. The metacentric height will be nearly (Take sin 2°16′ = 0·04, cos 2°16′ = 0·9992 and tan 2°16′ = 0·04)`,
opts: ["1·73 m", "1·42 m", "1·18 m", "0·87 m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1·18 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> W·GM·tan θ = w·x, so GM = w x/(W tan θ).</p><ul class="sym"><li>w = 600 kN, x = 10 m, W = 127 MN = 127 000 kN, tan θ = 0.04</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>GM = 600 × 10/(127 000 × 0.04) = 6000/5080 = 1.18 m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1.73 m would result from an angle of 1°33′.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1.42 m would result from an angle of 1°52′.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.87 m would result from an angle of 3°.</li></ul><p>The metacentric height is a measure of stability: for a ship, GM of 0.3 to 1.2 m is common; a large GM gives a stiff ship with a short, uncomfortable roll period, and a small GM gives a tender ship with a long roll period. The inclining experiment used here shifts a known weight across the deck and measures the angle of heel.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> GM = w x /(W tan θ).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-74", year: 2019, no: 74, subject: "fluid", topic: "Bernoulli equation for compressible flow", doubt: true,
q: `For frictionless adiabatic flow of compressive fluid, the Bernoulli's equation with usual notations is (options (a)–(d) as printed)`,
opts: ["(k/(k−1))(p₁/w₁) + v₁²/2g + z₁ = (k/(k−1))(p₂/w₂) + v₂²/2g + z₂ + h<sub>L</sub>", "(k/(k−1))(p₁/w₁) + v₁²/2g + z₁ = (k/(k−1))(p₂/w₂) + v₂²/2g + z₂", "p₁/w₁ + v₁²/2g + z₁ + H<sub>m</sub> = p₂/w₂ + v₂²/2g + z₂", "(k/(k−1))(p₁/w₁) + v₁²/2g + z₁ + H<sub>m</sub> = p₂/w₂ + v₂²/2g + z₂ + h<sub>L</sub>"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) form with k/(k−1) and head loss (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For adiabatic flow of a gas the pressure term p/w of incompressible flow is replaced by the enthalpy head (k/(k−1))(p/w). For frictionless flow with no pump or turbine, the sum of enthalpy, kinetic and potential heads is constant between two sections.</p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> This is the frictionless form with no loss term, the form we expected before reading the key.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Uses the incompressible pressure head p/w, so it is the incompressible equation with pump head.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Mixes the compressible term on one side with the incompressible term on the other and adds pump and loss heads.</li></ul><div class="note">In doubt (key followed). For truly frictionless flow no head-loss term h<sub>L</sub> should appear, so we first chose (b); the key's (a) carries h<sub>L</sub>.</div><p><b>Remember:</b> Compressible adiabatic: replace p/w by (k/(k−1))p/w.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-75", year: 2019, no: 75, subject: "fluid", topic: "Magnus effect",
q: `The phenomenon of generation of lift by rotating an object placed in a free stream is known as`,
opts: ["Coanda effect", "Magnus effect", "Scale effect", "Buoyancy effect"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Magnus effect</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A spinning cylinder or sphere in a stream drags fluid round with it; the velocity is higher on one side and lower on the other, so a pressure difference and a lift force develop at right angles to the stream (Magnus effect, as in curve balls and Flettner rotors).</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Coanda effect is the tendency of a jet to attach to a nearby curved surface.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Scale effect is the difference between model and prototype behaviour caused by size.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Buoyancy effect is the upthrust equal to the weight of displaced fluid and needs no rotation.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Spin in a stream gives lift: Magnus effect.</p>` }
);

