// ESE 2019 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (VBY-S-CVL), Q76–100
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; Q10 and Q32 dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2019-76", year: 2019, no: 76, subject: "fluid", topic: "Assumptions in the hydraulic jump analysis", doubt: true,
q: `Which of the following assumptions is/are made in the analysis of hydraulic jump?<ol><li>It is assumed that before and after jump formation the flow is essentially two-dimensional and that the pressure distribution is hydrostatic.</li><li>The length of the jump is small so that the losses due to friction on the channel floor are small and hence neglected.</li><li>The channel floor is horizontal or the slope is so gentle that the weight component of the water mass comprising the jump is very high.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The momentum equation for the jump assumes: horizontal (or gently sloping) floor, hydrostatic pressure at sections 1 and 2, negligible boundary friction over the short length of the jump, and uniform velocity at each section.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> The hydrostatic pressure and parallel-flow assumption is a standard one; the key does not accept the statement as worded (two-dimensional flow).</li><li><b>2</b> Accepted: the jump is short, so floor friction is neglected.</li><li><b>3</b> False as worded: the weight component along the floor is <b>negligible</b>, not very high.</li></ul><p>The key gives (b), 2 only.</p><div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Statement 1 is also a normal assumption of the momentum-equation derivation, and with statement 3 wrongly worded the expected answer would be 1 and 2, which is not an option. The key's (b) is shown.</div><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Jump analysis: short length, negligible friction and weight component.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-77", year: 2019, no: 77, subject: "fluid", topic: "Pumps in series for a deep well",
q: `Water is to be pumped out of a deep well under a total head of 95 m. A number of identical pumps of design speed 1000 rpm and specific speed 900 rpm with a rated capacity of 150 l/s are available. The number of pumps required will be`,
opts: ["1", "3", "5", "7"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a deep well the pumps are placed in series, and each supplies a part of the head. <b>Formula:</b> N<sub>s</sub> = N√Q/H<sup>3/4</sup> (Q in l/s) for one pump.</p><ol class="work"><li>H<sup>3/4</sup> = N√Q/N<sub>s</sub> = 1000 × √150/900 = 13.61.</li><li>Head per pump H = 13.61<sup>4/3</sup> = 32.5 m.</li><li>Number in series = 95/32.5 = 2.9 ≈ 3.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> One pump would have to lift 95 m, but at this specific speed one pump delivers only about 32.5 m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Five pumps would give 162 m, more than needed.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Seven pumps would give 227 m, far more than needed.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Series pumps: head per stage from N_s, then total head ÷ stage head.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-78", year: 2019, no: 78, subject: "fluid", topic: "Power loss in a Pelton nozzle",
q: `Consider the following data from a test on Pelton wheel: head at the base of the nozzle = 32 m; discharge of the nozzle = 0·18 m³/s; area of the jet = 7500 mm²; power available at the shaft = 44 kW; mechanical efficiency = 94%. The power lost in the nozzle will be nearly`,
opts: ["3·9 kW", "4·7 kW", "3·5 kW", "2·3 kW"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 4·7 kW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Method:</b> power at nozzle base − power in the jet = nozzle loss.</p><ul class="sym"><li>H = 32 m, Q = 0.18 m³/s, a = 7500 mm² = 0.0075 m²</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>Power at base = ρgQH = 9.81 × 0.18 × 32 = 56.5 kW.</li><li>Jet velocity v = Q/a = 0.18/0.0075 = 24 m/s.</li><li>Jet power = ½ρQv² = 0.5 × 1000 × 0.18 × 24² = 51.84 kW.</li><li>Nozzle loss = 56.5 − 51.84 = 4.7 kW.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 3.9 kW would need a jet power of 52.6 kW.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3.5 kW would need a jet power of 53.0 kW.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2.3 kW would need a jet power of 54.2 kW.</li></ul><p>Since the shaft power of 44 kW is given at an efficiency of 94 %, the water power delivered to the wheel is 44/0.94 = 46.8 kW. The rest of the power from the jet (51.84 kW) is lost in the wheel by friction in buckets, and so on. The nozzle loss is only one item of the chain.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Nozzle loss = ρgQH − ½ρQv².</p>` },

{ id: "2019-79", year: 2019, no: 79, subject: "fluid", topic: "Run-of-river hydropower plant",
q: `A certain hydropower plant utilizes the flow as it occurs, without any provision for storage. It is premised that a defined minimum dry weather flow is available. Such a plant is classified as`,
opts: ["Diverted-flow plant", "Pooled storage plant", "Base-load plant", "Run-of-river plant"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) run-of-river plant</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A run-of-river plant uses the river flow as it comes, with no (or very small) storage, and is designed around the minimum dry-weather flow.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A diverted-flow plant takes water from the river through a canal or tunnel to gain head; it is a layout, not the storage classification.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A pondage or pooled storage plant has a reservoir to regulate daily or weekly flow.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Base-load describes the position on the load curve, not the water supply.</li></ul><p>A run-of-river plant is designed for the firm power of the minimum dry flow and it spills the surplus flow in the monsoon. Its output varies from season to season, so it is generally a base-load plant in a grid, and a plant with pondage (a small daily storage) can follow the daily load.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> No storage = run-of-river.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-80", year: 2019, no: 80, subject: "fluid", topic: "Installed capacity and load factor",
q: `Two turbo-generators, each of capacity 25,000 kW, have been installed at a hydel power station. The load on the hydel plant varies from 15,000 kW to 40,000 kW. The total installed plant capacity and the load factor are nearly`,
opts: ["40,000 kW and 68·8%", "50,000 kW and 68·8%", "40,000 kW and 62·3%", "50,000 kW and 62·3%"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 50,000 kW and 68·8%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Installed capacity and load factor.</p><div class="formula">Load factor = average load/peak load</div><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Installed capacity = 2 × 25 000 = 50 000 kW.</li><li>Taking the average load as the mean of the minimum and maximum loads, (15 000 + 40 000)/2 = 27 500 kW, so load factor = 27 500/40 000 = 0.6875 = 68.8 %.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The capacity 40 000 kW is the peak load, not the installed capacity.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Both values are wrong.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The capacity is right, but 62.3 % would need an average load of 31 000 kW or division by the capacity (27 500/50 000 = 55 %).</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Load factor = average load / peak load; installed capacity = sum of units.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-81", year: 2019, no: 81, subject: "fluid", topic: "Streamlined body",
q: `An airfoil is a streamlined body as shown in the figure below. Because of the streamlining of the body, the separation occurs only at the extreme rear of the body, resulting in<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2019-81.png" alt="Figure for question 81">`,
opts: ["A very high pressure drag", "A small wake and consequently small pressure drag", "A moderate pressure drag", "No pressure drag"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) small wake and small pressure drag</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The gradual taper keeps the adverse pressure gradient mild, so the boundary layer stays attached almost to the tail. The wake is narrow, the pressure recovers at the rear, and the pressure (form) drag is small. Most of the drag is then skin friction.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A very high pressure drag belongs to bluff bodies with early separation and a wide wake.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Moderate pressure drag would need a wake of moderate size.</li><li><b>(d)</b> No pressure drag occurs only in ideal flow (d’Alembert’s paradox); in a real fluid there is always some wake.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Streamlining delays separation: small wake, small form drag.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-82", year: 2019, no: 82, subject: "fluid", topic: "Dynamic viscosity of a fluid film",
q: `A plate 0·025 mm distant from a fixed plate moves at 60 cm/s and requires a force of 0·2 kgf/m² to maintain this speed. The dynamic viscosity of the fluid between the plates will be nearly`,
opts: ["9·2 × 10⁻¹⁰ kgf·s/cm²", "8·3 × 10⁻¹⁰ kgf·s/cm²", "7·4 × 10⁻¹⁰ kgf·s/cm²", "6·5 × 10⁻¹⁰ kgf·s/cm²"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 8·3 × 10⁻¹⁰ kgf·s/cm²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Dynamic viscosity of a fluid film.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula (Newton's law):</b> τ = μ V/h</div><ul class="sym"><li>τ = 0.2 kgf/m² (force per unit area), V = 0.6 m/s, h = 0.025 mm = 2.5 × 10⁻⁵ m</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>μ = τh/V = 0.2 × 2.5 × 10⁻⁵/0.6 = 8.33 × 10⁻⁶ kgf·s/m².</li><li>1 m² = 10⁴ cm², so μ = 8.33 × 10⁻¹⁰ kgf·s/cm².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 9.2 × 10⁻¹⁰ would need τ = 0.22 kgf/m².</li><li><b>(c)</b> 7.4 × 10⁻¹⁰ would need τ = 0.18 kgf/m².</li><li><b>(d)</b> 6.5 × 10⁻¹⁰ would need τ = 0.156 kgf/m².</li></ul><p>The velocity gradient is V/h = 0.6/(2.5 × 10⁻⁵) = 24 000 s⁻¹, the linear profile assumed for a thin film between a moving and a fixed plate. For comparison, water at 20 °C has μ ≈ 1.02 × 10⁻⁴ kgf·s/m², so the fluid here is about 80 times more viscous than water, like a light oil. Newton's law τ = μ du/dy holds for any Newtonian fluid.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> μ = τ h / V; convert m² to cm² at the end.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-83", year: 2019, no: 83, subject: "fluid", topic: "Oil pressure governor",
q: `Which of the following are component parts for an oil pressure governor in modern turbines?<ol><li>Servomotor, known as relay cylinder</li><li>Oil sump</li><li>Oil pump which is driven by belt connected to turbine main shaft</li><li>Draft tube</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An oil pressure governor keeps the turbine speed constant by moving the gates or needle with oil pressure.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Servomotor (relay cylinder): moves the gate or spear under oil pressure.</li><li><b>2</b> Oil sump: stores the oil from which the pump draws and to which it returns.</li><li><b>3</b> Oil pump: driven from the turbine shaft (by belt), it supplies pressurised oil.</li><li><b>4</b> Draft tube: part of the turbine and not of the governor; it recovers kinetic head at the outlet of a reaction turbine.</li></ul><p>The main parts also include the control valve and the pendulum (centrifugal governor). Hence 1, 2 and 3.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Governor: pendulum, control valve, relay cylinder, oil pump, sump.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-84", year: 2019, no: 84, subject: "fluid", topic: "Double-acting reciprocating pump",
q: `A double-acting reciprocating pump having piston area 0·1 m² has a stroke 0·30 m long. The pump is discharging 2·4 m³ of water per minute at 45 rpm through a height of 10 m. The slip of the pump and power required to drive the pump will be nearly`,
opts: ["0·005 m³/s and 4·8 kW", "0·003 m³/s and 4·8 kW", "0·005 m³/s and 4·4 kW", "0·003 m³/s and 4·4 kW"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·005 m³/s and 4·4 kW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Double-acting reciprocating pump.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formulae:</b> theoretical discharge Q<sub>th</sub> = 2ALN/60; slip = Q<sub>th</sub> − Q<sub>act</sub>; power = ρg Q<sub>th</sub> H</div><ol class="work"><li>Q<sub>th</sub> = 2 × 0.1 × 0.3 × 45/60 = 0.045 m³/s.</li><li>Q<sub>act</sub> = 2.4/60 = 0.04 m³/s, so slip = 0.045 − 0.04 = 0.005 m³/s.</li><li>Power = 9.81 × 0.045 × 10 = 4.41 kW ≈ 4.4 kW (using the volume the piston displaces).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The slip is right but 4.8 kW is too high.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The slip 0.003 m³/s would need Q<sub>th</sub> = 0.043 m³/s, and 4.8 kW is too high.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The power is right but the slip is 0.005, not 0.003 m³/s.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Slip = Q_th − Q_act; double acting uses 2ALN/60.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-85", year: 2019, no: 85, subject: "hydro", topic: "Intensity–duration formula",
q: `In intensity-duration analysis by Sherman, the intensity of rainfall i is represented as (where t is time and a, b, n are constants for the area)`,
opts: ["bⁿ/(t + a)", "aⁿ/(t + b)ⁿ", "(a + t)ⁿ/b", "a/(t + b)ⁿ"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) a/(t + b)ⁿ</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Intensity–duration relations in common use: Talbot i = a/(t + b); Bernard and the generalised form i = a/(t + b)ⁿ; Sherman i = a/tⁿ (b = 0). In the key, the general form i = a/(t + b)ⁿ is the one given for Sherman.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> bⁿ/(t + a) swaps the constants and has the wrong exponent form.</li><li><b>(b)</b> aⁿ/(t + b)ⁿ raises the numerator constant to the power n, which no empirical formula does.</li><li><b>(c)</b> (a + t)ⁿ/b increases with duration, but rainfall intensity decreases with duration.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Intensity must fall with duration: a/(t+b)ⁿ.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-86", year: 2019, no: 86, subject: "hydro", topic: "Siting a rain gauge",
q: `Which one of the following points should be kept in mind while selecting the site for a rain gauge station?`,
opts: ["The site where a rain gauge is set up should be close to a meteorological observatory.", "The rain gauge should be on the top of a hill.", "A fence, if erected to protect the rain gauge from cattle etc. should be located within twice the height of the fence.", "The distance between the rain gauge and the nearest object should be at least twice the height of the object."], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) distance to nearest object at least twice its height</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A rain gauge must stand on level ground, in the open, so that nearby objects do not shield it from the rain. The standard rule is that the distance from the gauge to the nearest obstruction should be at least twice (better, four times) the height of the obstruction.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Gauges are spread over the catchment; being near an observatory is not a siting condition.</li><li><b>(b)</b> A hilltop is exposed to wind, which causes under-catch.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A fence should stand farther away than twice its height (not within it), otherwise it shelters the gauge.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Obstruction distance ≥ 2 × its height.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-87", year: 2019, no: 87, subject: "hydro", topic: "Retarding reservoir", doubt: true,
q: `Which of the following statements relates to a retarding reservoir?<ol><li>There are no gates at the outlets and hence the possibility of human error in reservoir operation is eliminated.</li><li>The high cost of gate installation and also its operation is saved.</li><li>An automatic regulation may cause coincidence of flood crest farther downstream where two or more channels taking off from retarding reservoirs join together.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A retarding reservoir has ungated outlets, so it discharges automatically according to the water level: a flood peak is flattened without any operator.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Advantage: no gates, so no operating error.</li><li><b>2</b> Advantage: no gate cost and no operating cost.</li><li><b>3</b> This is a drawback of a retarding reservoir: automatic regulation can make the flood peaks of several such reservoirs meet downstream. The key does not count it among the statements.</li></ul><p>The key's (b) is shown.</p><div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Statement 3 is a recognised disadvantage of retarding reservoirs and so does 'relate to' them; we first chose all three. The key's (b) is shown.</div><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Retarding reservoir: ungated, automatic; advantages 1 and 2.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-88", year: 2019, no: 88, subject: "hydro", topic: "Transmissibility from a pumping test",
q: `The coefficient of transmissibility T for a confined aquifer can be determined by a pumping-out test together with other relevant observations. The applicable formula is (where Q = Discharge, and ΔS = Difference in drawdowns in two wells)`,
opts: ["Q/(2·72 ΔS)", "Q/(1·72 √ΔS)", "(Q/2·72)ΔS", "(Q/2·72)√ΔS"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) Q/(2·72 ΔS)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Thiem's equilibrium formula for a confined aquifer is Q = 2πT(s₁ − s₂)/ln(r₂/r₁). With logarithms to base 10 and ratios of radii in a 10-fold (one log cycle) interval, T = 2.303 Q/(2π ΔS) = Q/(2.72 ΔS).</p><div class="formula">Q = 2πT(s₁ − s₂)/ln(r₂/r₁)</div><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Thiem's equation has no √ΔS term; 1.72 is not 2π/2.303.</li><li><b>(c)</b> T is inversely, not directly, proportional to ΔS.</li><li><b>(d)</b> T is inversely proportional to ΔS and has no √.</li></ul><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>One log cycle: ln(r₂/r₁) = 2.303.</li><li>T = 2.303Q/(2πΔs).</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> T = Q/(2.72 Δs) per log cycle of distance.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-89", year: 2019, no: 89, subject: "hydro", topic: "Dead storage",
q: `The volume of water below the minimum pool level in a reservoir is known as`,
opts: ["Useful storage", "Surcharge storage", "Dead storage", "Bank storage"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) dead storage</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The volume below the minimum pool level (the lowest sluice) cannot be drawn off by gravity and is used for silt deposition: dead storage.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Useful storage lies between the minimum pool level and the normal pool level.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Surcharge storage lies above the normal pool level up to the maximum water level.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Bank storage is water held in the soil of the banks during a rise of the reservoir.</li></ul><p>The levels in a reservoir, from the bottom: the dead storage below the minimum pool level (also called the low water level); the useful storage up to the full reservoir level (the normal pool level); and the flood or surcharge storage up to the maximum reservoir level. The dead storage is fixed by the silt allowance for the life of the dam and the lowest outlet.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Dead < useful < surcharge.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-90", year: 2019, no: 90, subject: "hydro", topic: "Classification of flow irrigation",
q: `Depending upon the source from which the water is drawn, flow irrigation can be sub-divided into<ol><li>River canal irrigation</li><li>Reservoir or tank irrigation</li><li>Combined storage and lift irrigation</li><li>Combined storage and diversion irrigation</li></ol>Which of the above designations are relevant?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In flow irrigation water reaches the field by gravity. By source it is divided into direct (river) canal irrigation, storage (reservoir or tank) irrigation, and combined storage and diversion canal irrigation.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> River canal irrigation: relevant (diversion without storage).</li><li><b>2</b> Reservoir or tank irrigation: relevant (storage).</li><li><b>3</b> Not relevant: lift irrigation needs pumping and is not flow irrigation.</li><li><b>4</b> Relevant: combined storage and diversion canals.</li></ul><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Flow irrigation = gravity; lift irrigation is separate.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-91", year: 2019, no: 91, subject: "hydro", topic: "Field capacity of soil",
q: `Consider the following data: root zone depth = 2 m; existing water content = 5%; dry density of soil = 15 kN/m³; water applied to the soil = 500 m³; water loss due to evaporation and deep percolation = 10%; area of plot = 1000 m². The field capacity of the soil will be nearly`,
opts: ["16·8%", "17·7%", "18·8%", "19·7%"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 19·7%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Field capacity of soil.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> depth of water stored d = [(FC − MC)/100] × (γ<sub>d</sub>/γ<sub>w</sub>) × D</div><ul class="sym"><li>Stored water = 500 × 0.9 = 450 m³ over 1000 m² → d = 0.45 m</li><li>γ<sub>d</sub>/γ<sub>w</sub> = 15/9.81 = 1.529; D = 2 m; MC = 5 %</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>0.45 = [(FC − 5)/100] × 1.529 × 2.</li><li>FC − 5 = 0.45 × 100/3.058 = 14.7.</li><li>FC = 19.7 %.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 16.8 % would store only 0.36 m.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 17.7 % would store 0.39 m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 18.8 % would store 0.42 m.</li></ul><p>The field capacity is the water content a soil holds after the free (gravity) water has drained, about 2 to 3 days after irrigation. The permanent wilting point lies below it and the water between the two is the available water. The irrigation interval is the readily available water divided by the daily consumptive use.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Stored depth = ΔMC × (γd/γw) × root depth.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-92", year: 2019, no: 92, subject: "hydro", topic: "Sodium absorption ratio",
q: `Consider the following data for irrigation water: Na⁺ = 24 meq/l; Ca⁺⁺ = 3·6 meq/l; Mg⁺⁺ = 2 meq/l. The Sodium-Absorption Ratio (SAR) is nearly`,
opts: ["13·1", "14·3", "15·5", "16·7"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 14·3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> SAR = Na⁺/√[(Ca⁺⁺ + Mg⁺⁺)/2] (concentrations in meq/l).</p><ol class="work"><li>(3.6 + 2)/2 = 2.8; √2.8 = 1.673.</li><li>SAR = 24/1.673 = 14.3.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 13.1 would need Ca + Mg = 6.7 meq/l.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15.5 would need Ca + Mg = 4.8 meq/l.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 16.7 would need Ca + Mg = 4.1 meq/l.</li></ul><p>The classification: SAR below 10 low sodium hazard, 10–18 medium, 18–26 high and above 26 very high. Water with an SAR of 14.3 has a medium sodium hazard and is usable on soils with good drainage and added gypsum. A high SAR causes the soil to disperse and lose permeability.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> SAR = Na/√((Ca+Mg)/2).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-93", year: 2019, no: 93, subject: "hydro", topic: "Type of weir",
q: `Consider the following statements with respect to weir under discussion: 1. Its design corresponds to soft sandy foundation. 2. The difference in weir crest and downstream river bed may not exceed 3 m. 3. When water passes over it, the longitudinal location of the formation of a hydraulic jump is variable. This weir is of the type`,
opts: ["Vertical drop weir", "Masonry or concrete sloping weir", "Dry stone slope weir", "Parabolic weir"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) masonry or concrete sloping weir</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A masonry or concrete sloping weir is designed (Bligh, Khosla) for permeable sandy foundations and is used where the drop (crest to downstream bed) does not exceed about 3 m. Over its sloping apron the hydraulic jump forms at different positions with the discharge, so the location is variable.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A vertical drop weir has a vertical face; the jump forms close to the toe in a fixed position and drops are generally small.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A dry stone slope weir is a rockfill type for very low drops, and not designed on sand like this.</li><li><b>(d)</b> A parabolic weir is a profile used on the downstream face of an overflow spillway, with no such drop limit.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Sloping weir: sandy foundation, drop ≤ 3 m, jump position varies.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-94", year: 2019, no: 94, subject: "hydro", topic: "Mitra’s expansion transition",
q: `Consider the following data while designing an expansion transition for a canal by Mitra's method: length of flume = 16 m; width of throat = 9 m; width of canal = 15 m. If B<sub>x</sub> is the width at any distance x from the flumed section, the values of B<sub>x</sub> at x = 8 m and at x = 16 m are nearly`,
opts: ["10·8 m and 15 m", "11·3 m and 15 m", "10·8 m and 13 m", "11·3 m and 13 m"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 11·3 m and 15 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Mitra’s expansion transition.</p><div class="formula"><b>Mitra's formula:</b> B<sub>x</sub> = B<sub>f</sub>B<sub>c</sub>L/[L B<sub>c</sub> − x(B<sub>c</sub> − B<sub>f</sub>)]</div><ul class="sym"><li>B<sub>f</sub> = 9 m (throat), B<sub>c</sub> = 15 m (canal), L = 16 m</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>x = 8: B<sub>x</sub> = 9 × 15 × 16/(16 × 15 − 8 × 6) = 2160/192 = 11.25 ≈ 11.3 m.</li><li>x = 16: B<sub>x</sub> = 2160/(240 − 96) = 2160/144 = 15 m (the canal width, as it must be at the end).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 10.8 m at the middle is too small for Mitra’s hyperbolic curve.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 10.8 m is wrong, and 13 m at the end disagrees with the canal width (15 m).</li><li><b>(d)</b> The value 13 m at the end disagrees with the canal width.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> In Mitra’s method 1/B varies linearly with x; the end gives the canal width.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-95", year: 2019, no: 95, subject: "hydro", topic: "Drain spacing and rainfall",
q: `Consider the following data for a drain: L = 50 m, a = 10 m, b = 10·3 m, and k = 1 × 10⁻⁵ m/s. If the drains carry 1% of average annual rainfall in 24 hrs, the average annual rainfall for which this system has been designed will be`,
opts: ["78 cm", "84 cm", "90 cm", "96 cm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 84 cm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula (Hooghoudt / steady drainage):</b> discharge per unit area q = 4k(b² − a²)/L².</p><div class="formula">q = 4k(b² − a²)/L²</div><ol class="work"><li>q = 4 × 10⁻⁵ × (10.3² − 10²)/50² = 4 × 10⁻⁵ × 6.09/2500 = 9.74 × 10⁻⁹ m/s.</li><li>In 24 h: 9.74 × 10⁻⁹ × 86 400 = 8.4 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.084 cm of water.</li><li>Equating to the stated fraction of rainfall gives the keyed figure of about 84 cm (the printed 1 % is 0.1 % in this arithmetic).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 78 cm needs q = 7.8 × 10⁻⁴ m/day.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 90 cm needs q = 9.0 × 10⁻⁴ m/day.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 96 cm needs q = 9.6 × 10⁻⁴ m/day.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> q = 4k(b² − a²)/L²; the numbers match 84 cm.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-96", year: 2019, no: 96, subject: "hydro", topic: "Groyne",
q: `The purpose of constructing a 'Groyne' is to`,
opts: ["Expand a river channel to improve its depth", "Encourage meandering", "Train the flow along a certain course", "Reduce the silting in the river bed"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) train the flow along a certain course</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A groyne (spur) is a structure projecting from the bank into the stream. It deflects the current away from the bank, protects the bank from erosion, and trains the flow along a desired course (channel).</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Groynes can contract a channel and deepen it, but their aim is to train and protect, not to expand.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Training works discourage meandering; they do not encourage it.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Silt may well deposit between groynes; reducing silting is not their purpose.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Groyne: protects bank and trains the channel.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-97", year: 2019, no: 97, subject: "env", topic: "Methemoglobinemia",
q: `Which one of the following compounds of nitrogen, when in excessive amounts in water, contributes to the illness known as infant methemoglobinemia?`,
opts: ["Ammoniacal nitrogen", "Albuminoid nitrogen", "Nitrite", "Nitrate"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) nitrate</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Nitrate in drinking water (above 45 mg/l) is reduced to nitrite in the infant’s stomach; nitrite combines with haemoglobin to form methaemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen (blue-baby syndrome).</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Ammoniacal nitrogen indicates recent organic pollution.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Albuminoid nitrogen indicates undecomposed organic matter.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Nitrite is the direct agent, but the compound found in excess in water, which is later reduced to nitrite, is nitrate.</li></ul><p>The limit for nitrate in drinking water is 45 mg/l as NO₃ (IS 10500). Nitrate in groundwater comes from fertilisers, sewage and septic tanks. It cannot be removed by boiling, and is reduced by ion exchange, reverse osmosis or blending.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Blue baby: excess nitrate, limit 45 mg/l.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-98", year: 2019, no: 98, subject: "hydro", topic: "Elementary profile of a gravity dam",
q: `Consider the following data regarding a theoretical profile of a dam: permissible value of compressible stress σ = 350 tonnes/m²; specific gravity of concrete s = 2·4; uplift coefficient c = 0·6 m; the value of γ = 1. The height and base width will be nearly`,
opts: ["125 m and 63 m", "175 m and 63 m", "125 m and 93 m", "175 m and 93 m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 125 m and 93 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formulae (elementary profile with uplift):</b> limiting height H = σ/[γ(s + 1 − c)]; base width B = H/√(s − c).</p><div class="formula">H = σ/[γ(s + 1 − c)]</div><div class="formula">B = H/√(s − c)</div><ol class="work"><li>H = 350/(1 × (2.4 + 1 − 0.6)) = 350/2.8 = 125 m.</li><li>B = 125/√(2.4 − 0.6) = 125/1.342 = 93.2 m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The height is right, but 63 m would need s − c = 3.9.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Both values are wrong: H = 175 m would need σ = 490 t/m².</li><li><b>(d)</b> The base width is right for H = 125 m only; with 175 m it would be 130 m.</li></ul><p>In the elementary profile of a gravity dam the downstream face is a straight line and the upstream face is vertical. The limiting height at which the compressive stress equals the permissible stress is the dividing height between a low and a high dam: above 125 m here, the profile must be widened (a practical profile).</p><p><b>Remember:</b> H = σ/γ(s+1−c); B = H/√(s−c).</p>` },

{ id: "2019-99", year: 2019, no: 99, subject: "env", topic: "Chlorine demand",
q: `Chlorine usage in the treatment of 25,000 m³/day of water has been 9 kg/day. The residual chlorine after 10 minutes contact is 0·2 mg/l. The chlorine demand of water would nearly be`,
opts: ["0·28 mg/l", "0·22 mg/l", "0·16 mg/l", "0·12 mg/l"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·16 mg/l</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Chlorine demand.</p><div class="formula">Chlorine demand = dose applied − residual after the contact time</div><ol class="work"><li>Dose = 9 kg/day ÷ 25 000 m³/day = 0.36 g/m³ = 0.36 mg/l.</li><li>Demand = 0.36 − 0.20 = 0.16 mg/l.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.28 mg/l subtracts a residual of 0.08.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.22 mg/l subtracts a residual of 0.14.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.12 mg/l subtracts a residual of 0.24.</li></ul><p>Chlorine demand is the amount used up by reaction with organics, ammonia and iron, and the residual left after the contact period is the free or combined chlorine remaining. Ammonia gives chloramines, which are slower in disinfection. The usual residual desired is 0.2 mg/l at the consumer end after 10 minutes contact.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Demand = dose − residual.</p>` },

{ id: "2019-100", year: 2019, no: 100, subject: "env", topic: "Plain sedimentation tank area",
q: `The demand of water is 150 litres/head/day in a city of one lakh population. The factor of safety is taken as 1·5, detention time as 4 h and overflow rate as 20,000 litres/day/m². The area of 3 m deep plain sedimentation tank as per surface loading consideration will be`,
opts: ["1025 m²", "1075 m²", "1125 m²", "1175 m²"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1125 m²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Plain sedimentation tank area.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> surface area A = Q/(overflow rate), with Q the design flow</div><ol class="work"><li>Q = 150 × 1 00 000 = 15 × 10⁶ l/day; design flow = 1.5 × 15 × 10⁶ = 22.5 × 10⁶ l/day.</li><li>A = 22.5 × 10⁶/20 000 = 1125 m².</li></ol><p>Check by detention: V = Q t = 22.5 × 10⁶/24 × 4 = 3750 m³ → A = 3750/3 = 1250 m²; the governing area is by surface loading as the question says.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1025 m² would correspond to a flow of 20.5 MLD.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1075 m² would correspond to a flow of 21.5 MLD.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1175 m² would correspond to a flow of 23.5 MLD.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> A = Q/overflow rate.</p>` }
);

