// ESE 2018 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (SKP-D-CVL), Q51–75
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; Q10 dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2018-51", year: 2018, no: 51, subject: "fluid", topic: "Discharge over a weir with velocity of approach",
q: `In a rectangular open channel, 2·0 m wide, water flows at a depth of 0·8 m. It discharges over an aerated sharp-crested weir over the full width, with depth over weir crest being 0·25 m. C<sub>c</sub> = 0·61. Adjusting for velocity head of approach, what would be the discharge through the channel? √2g = 4·43 units`,
opts: ["0·439 m³/sec", "0·445 m³/sec", "0·453 m³/sec", "0·461 m³/sec"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·461 m³/sec</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Discharge over a weir with velocity of approach.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> Q = (2/3)C<sub>d</sub>√(2g) L[(H + h<sub>a</sub>)^1.5 − h<sub>a</sub>^1.5], h<sub>a</sub> = v²/2g (velocity of approach v = Q/(B × 0.8))</div><ol class="work"><li>First estimate without approach velocity: Q = (2/3)(0.61)(4.43)(2)(0.25)^1.5 = 3.603 × 0.125 = 0.450 m³/s.</li><li>v = 0.450/(2 × 0.8) = 0.281 m/s; h<sub>a</sub> = v²/2g = 0.0040 m.</li><li>Q = 3.603[(0.254)^1.5 − (0.004)^1.5] = 3.603(0.1280 − 0.00026) = 0.460 m³/s; repeating once gives 0.461 m³/s.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.439 m³/s is below even the first estimate without approach velocity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.445 m³/s ignores the velocity of approach.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.453 m³/s includes only part of the correction.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Include the velocity head of approach and iterate once.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-52", year: 2018, no: 52, subject: "fluid", topic: "Stagnation point of a flow field",
q: `A steady, two dimensional, incompressible flow field is represented by u = x + 3y + 3 and v = 2x − y − 8. In this flow field, the stagnation point is`,
opts: ["(3, 2)", "(−3, 2)", "(−3, −2)", "(3, −2)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) (3, −2)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At a stagnation point both velocity components are zero.</p><ol class="work"><li>v = 0 → y = 2x − 8.</li><li>u = 0 → x + 3(2x − 8) + 3 = 0 → 7x = 21 → x = 3, then y = −2.</li></ol><p>Check: u = 3 − 6 + 3 = 0; v = 6 + 2 − 8 = 0. Continuity: ∂u/∂x + ∂v/∂y = 1 − 1 = 0, so the flow is incompressible.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> (3, 2) gives v = 6 − 2 − 8 = −4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> (−3, 2) gives u = −3 + 6 + 3 = 6.</li><li><b>(c)</b> (−3, −2) gives u = −3 − 6 + 3 = −6.</li></ul><p>A flow field with these linear velocity components represents a flow in which the velocity changes linearly with position; the stagnation point at (3, −2) is the origin of a new frame in which u = x′ + 3y′ and v = 2x′ − y′. Since ∂u/∂x = −∂v/∂y the flow satisfies continuity. The streamlines near the stagnation point are hyperbolas or ellipses depending on the invariants of the matrix.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Set u = v = 0 and solve.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-53", year: 2018, no: 53, subject: "fluid", topic: "Power of a water jet",
q: `If the energy present in a jet of water of 20 cm diameter and having a velocity of 25 m/s could be extracted by a device with 90% efficiency, the power extracted would be nearly (taking 1/2g = 0·051 sec²/m)`,
opts: ["180 kW", "225 kW", "260 kW", "300 kW"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 225 kW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Power of a water jet.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> jet power = ρgQ(v²/2g)</div><ol class="work"><li>Q = (π/4)(0.2)² × 25 = 0.7854 m³/s.</li><li>Kinetic head v²/2g = 625 × 0.051 = 31.9 m.</li><li>Power = 9.81 × 0.7854 × 31.9 = 245.6 kW; at 90 % efficiency = 221 kW ≈ 225 kW.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 180 kW would need an efficiency of 73 %.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 260 kW is above the jet’s power of 245.6 kW, impossible at 90 %.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 300 kW is above the jet’s power, impossible.</li></ul><p>The kinetic energy flux of a jet is ½ρQv² and it equals ρgQ(v²/2g), the same expression written with the velocity head. For a Pelton wheel the largest fraction that can be taken from the jet is the hydraulic efficiency, of 90 % or more in a well-designed machine; the actual extraction would then be the 221 kW computed above.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> P = ρgQ·v²/2g × η.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-54", year: 2018, no: 54, subject: "fluid", topic: "Pressure at the summit of a siphon",
q: `In a siphon, the summit is 5 m above the water level in the tank from which the flow is being discharged. If the head loss from the inlet to the summit is 2·5 m and the velocity head at the summit is 0·5 m, (taking γ = 10 appropriate units) the pressure head at the summit is`,
opts: ["−80 kPa", "−3 m of water (abs)", "5 m of water (abs)", "18 m of water (abs)"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) −80 kPa</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Apply Bernoulli's equation from the tank surface (pressure atmospheric, velocity zero) to the summit:</p><div class="formula">z₁ + p₁/γ + V₁²/2g = z₂ + p₂/γ + V₂²/2g + h_L</div><ol class="work"><li>0 = 5 + p/γ + 0.5 + 2.5 (gauge pressures).</li><li>p/γ = −8 m of water (gauge).</li><li>p = −8 × 10 = −80 kPa (gauge).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> −3 m (abs) mixes the absolute scale with a loss of only 5 m of the 8.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 5 m (abs) is the elevation of the summit alone.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 18 m (abs) is the pressure head on the upstream side of the summit with the sign reversed.</li></ul><p>Siphons work only if the absolute pressure at the summit stays above the vapour pressure of water. Here the summit pressure is −80 kPa gauge, about 21 kPa absolute (with 101 kPa atmospheric), which is above the vapour pressure at 20 °C (2.3 kPa), so the siphon runs; the summit cannot be higher than about 8 m above the water level.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Siphon summit: p/γ = −(z + hv + hL) in gauge.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-55", year: 2018, no: 55, subject: "fluid", topic: "Stream function of a doublet",
q: `The stream function of a doublet with horizontal axis and of strength μ is`,
opts: ["(μ/2π) r", "(μ/2πr) cos θ", "(μ/2π) r sin θ", "(μ/2π)(sin θ/r)"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) (μ/2π)(sin θ/r) (up to sign)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A doublet is the limit of a source and a sink of equal strength brought together. Its stream function is ψ = −(μ/2π)(sin θ/r) and its potential φ = (μ/2π)(cos θ/r), the sign depending on whether the doublet points along +x or −x; the form of the stream function has the sin θ/r dependence.</p><div class="formula">Doublet: ψ = −(μ/2π) sin θ/r</div><ul><li><b>(a)</b> (μ/2π)r is not a function of angle, so cannot describe a doublet.</li><li><b>(b)</b> (μ/2πr)cos θ is the velocity potential, not the stream function.</li><li><b>(c)</b> (μ/2π) r sin θ is the stream function of uniform flow (U r sin θ), not a doublet.</li></ul><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>Source + sink of equal strength brought together.</li><li>ψ = <b>−(μ/2π)(sin θ/r)</b>.</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Doublet: ψ ∝ sinθ/r, φ ∝ cosθ/r.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-56", year: 2018, no: 56, subject: "fluid", topic: "Steady level in a tank with an orifice", doubt: true,
q: `A vertical cylindrical tank, 2 m diameter has, at the bottom, a 5 cm diameter, sharp-edged orifice, for which C<sub>d</sub> = 0·6. Water enters the tank at a constant rate of 9 l/sec. At what depth above the orifice will the level in the tank become steady?`,
opts: ["2·95 m", "2·75 m", "2·60 m", "2·50 m"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2·75 m (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The level becomes steady when the outflow equals the inflow: Q = C<sub>d</sub>a√(2gh).</p><ol class="work"><li>a = π(0.05)²/4 = 1.9635 × 10⁻³ m².</li><li>0.009 = 0.6 × 1.9635 × 10⁻³ × √(19.62 h) → √(19.62h) = 7.64.</li><li>h = 58.4/19.62 = 2.98 m (nearest to 2.95 m).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2.95 m is the option nearest to our computed 2.98 m, the answer we first chose.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2.60 m needs a discharge of 8.4 l/s.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2.50 m needs a discharge of 8.3 l/s.</li></ul><div class="note">In doubt (key followed). The calculation with the data given gives h = 2.98 m, nearest to option (a) 2.95 m; the key's (b) 2.75 m would need C<sub>d</sub> = 0.62.</div><p><b>Remember:</b> Steady: inflow = Cd a √(2gh).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-57", year: 2018, no: 57, subject: "fluid", topic: "Wind drag on an antenna",
q: `A transmitter antenna is of a vertical pipe, 20 cm diameter and 25 m height, on top of a tall structure. It is subjected to wind speed of 20 m/sec. Density of air is 1·22 kg/m³; its viscosity is 1·8 × 10⁻⁵ Ns/m². Drag coefficient of a (tall) circular cylinder is tabulated as: Re = 10², 10³, 1·3 × 10³, 10⁴, 1·5 × 10⁴, 1·06 × 10⁵, 1·2 × 10⁵, 4·5 × 10⁵ with C<sub>D</sub> = 1·6, 1·05, 0·95, 1·0, 1·08, 1·0, 0·89, 0·26. What is the drag experienced?`,
opts: ["737 N", "700 N", "670 N", "63 N"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 737 N</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Wind drag on an antenna.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> F<sub>D</sub> = C<sub>D</sub> · ½ρV²A, A = D × L (projected area)</div><ol class="work"><li>Re = ρVD/μ = 1.22 × 20 × 0.2/(1.8 × 10⁻⁵) = 2.71 × 10⁵.</li><li>Interpolating between 1.2 × 10⁵ (0.89) and 4.5 × 10⁵ (0.26): C<sub>D</sub> ≈ 0.89 − (2.71 − 1.2)/(4.5 − 1.2) × 0.63 = 0.60.</li><li>½ρV²A = 0.5 × 1.22 × 400 × (0.2 × 25) = 1220 N.</li><li>F<sub>D</sub> = 0.604 × 1220 = 737 N.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 700 N would need C<sub>D</sub> = 0.574.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 670 N would need C<sub>D</sub> = 0.55.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 63 N is about a twentieth of the correct value, as if the area were 0.2 × 25 divided by 20.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Find Re first, interpolate C_D from the table, then ½ρV²A.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-58", year: 2018, no: 58, subject: "fluid", topic: "Boundary layer on a flat plate",
q: `A smooth flat plate with a sharp leading edge is placed along a free stream of water flowing at 2·5 m/sec. At what distance from the leading edge will the boundary layer transition from laminar to turbulent flow? Take density of water as 1000 kg/m³ and its viscosity as 1 centipoise. Also, what will be the boundary layer thickness at that distance?`,
opts: ["12·8 cm and 0·113 cm", "14·2 cm and 0·113 cm", "12·8 cm and 0·125 cm", "14·2 cm and 0·125 cm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 12·8 cm and 0·113 cm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Blasius laminar thickness δ = 5x/√Re<sub>x</sub>.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formulae:</b> transition at Re<sub>x</sub> = ρVx/μ = Re<sub>cr</sub>;</div><ol class="work"><li>The key takes Re<sub>cr</sub> = 3.2 × 10⁵: x = 3.2 × 10⁵ × 0.001/(1000 × 2.5) = 0.128 m = 12.8 cm.</li><li>δ = 5 × 0.128/√(3.2 × 10⁵) = 0.64/565.7 = 1.13 × 10⁻³ m = 0.113 cm.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 14.2 cm would need Re<sub>cr</sub> = 3.55 × 10⁵, and 0.113 cm does not match that distance.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.125 cm does not fit 12.8 cm: 5x/√Re gives 0.113 cm.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Both values are inconsistent with the same Reynolds number.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> x = Re_cr μ/(ρV); δ = 5x/√Re.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-59", year: 2018, no: 59, subject: "fluid", topic: "Rotation of a cylindrical container",
q: `What is the rotational speed in rpm of a 0·8 m diameter cylindrical container, held with axis vertical, if the fluid contained in it rises to 0·6 m height at the sides and leaves a circular space 0·3 m diameter on the bottom uncovered?`,
opts: ["90·2 rpm", "88·4 rpm", "86·0 rpm", "83·7 rpm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 88·4 rpm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The free surface is a paraboloid, z = (ω²/2g)(r² − r₀²) measured from the bottom, with r₀ = 0.15 m (the dry circle).</p><div class="formula">z = ω²r²/(2g) (paraboloid)</div><ol class="work"><li>At the wall r = 0.4 m, z = 0.6 m: 0.6 = (ω²/19.62)(0.16 − 0.0225) = (ω²/19.62)(0.1375).</li><li>ω² = 0.6 × 19.62/0.1375 = 85.6; ω = 9.25 rad/s.</li><li>N = 60ω/2π = 88.4 rpm.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 90.2 rpm would give a wall height of 0.625 m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 86.0 rpm would give a wall height of 0.567 m.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 83.7 rpm would give a wall height of 0.537 m.</li></ul><p>The pressure at any point in a rotating liquid follows p = p₀ + γ(z − z_s) hydrostatically below the paraboloid surface, so the container wall carries a pressure that increases with the speed. The volume of the paraboloid of revolution of the dry part is half the circumscribed cylinder, which can also be used as a check on the fluid volume.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Paraboloid: z = ω²(r² − r₀²)/2g.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-60", year: 2018, no: 60, subject: "fluid", topic: "Boundary layer thickness ratio",
q: `If δ₁ and δ₂ are the laminar boundary layer thicknesses at a point M distant x from the leading edge when the Reynolds number of the flow are 100 and 484, respectively, then the ratio δ₁/δ₂ will be`,
opts: ["2·2", "4·84", "23·43", "45·45"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2·2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a laminar boundary layer δ = 5x/√Re<sub>x</sub>, so at the same x, δ ∝ 1/√Re (a higher stream speed gives a thinner layer).</p><ol class="work"><li>δ₁/δ₂ = √(Re₂/Re₁) = √(484/100) = 2.2.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 4.84 uses the ratio itself, not its square root.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 23.43 does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 45.45 does not satisfy the relation used above.</li></ul><p>Laminar boundary layers grow as √x: at a given point the layer is thinner when the free stream speed is higher. For Reynolds numbers 100 and 484, the thickness ratio of 2.2 means the layer is 2.2 times thicker at the lower speed. The momentum and displacement thicknesses follow the same dependence, and the layer stays laminar until Re reaches about 5 × 10⁵.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Laminar δ ∝ 1/√Re at fixed x.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-61", year: 2018, no: 61, subject: "fluid", topic: "Error in a V-notch discharge",
q: `In a 90° triangular notch, the error in the estimated discharge for a given head due to an error of 1% in cutting the vertex angle is`,
opts: ["zero", "1%", "π/2 %", "π %"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) π/2 %</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a V-notch Q = (8/15)C<sub>d</sub>√(2g) tan(θ/2) H^2.5, so for a given head dQ/Q = d[tan(θ/2)]/tan(θ/2).</p><ol class="work"><li>d[tan(θ/2)] = ½sec²(θ/2) dθ; at θ = 90°, sec²45° = 2, so d tan = dθ, and tan 45° = 1.</li><li>dQ/Q = dθ (radians). An error of 1 % in 90° is 0.9° = 0.01571 rad.</li><li>dQ/Q = 0.01571 = 1.571 % = (π/2) %.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Zero would need Q independent of the angle.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1 % is the error in the angle itself; the discharge error is larger because of the tangent.</li><li><b>(d)</b> π % would result from a vertex angle error of 2 %.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> dQ/Q = dθ for a 90° notch.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-62", year: 2018, no: 62, subject: "geo", topic: "Field identification of soils", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements:<ol><li>All soils can be identified in the field by visual examination</li><li>Fine-grained soils can be identified in the field by visual examination and touch</li><li>Fine-grained soils can be identified in the field by dilatancy test</li><li>By visual examination, only coarse-grained soils can be identified</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Visual examination (grain size, shape, gradation) identifies coarse-grained soils only. Fine-grained soils (silts and clays) cannot be told apart by the eye; they need field tests such as dilatancy, dry strength and toughness.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: fine-grained soils cannot be identified by visual examination alone.</li><li><b>2</b> False as worded: sight and touch alone are not enough; the field tests (dilatancy, toughness, dry strength) are needed.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the dilatancy (shaking) test separates silts from clays.</li><li><b>4</b> True: by visual examination only coarse-grained soils can be identified.</li></ul><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Fine soils: dilatancy, dry strength, toughness tests.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-63", year: 2018, no: 63, subject: "fluid", topic: "Critical depth in a triangular channel",
q: `An open channel is of isosceles triangle shape, with side slopes 1 vertical and n horizontal. The ratio of the critical depth to specific energy at critical depth will be`,
opts: ["2/3", "3/4", "4/5", "5/6"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 4/5</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> At critical flow Q²T/(gA³) = 1. For a triangle A = ny², T = 2ny, so Q²·2ny/(g n³y⁶) = 1 → Q² = g n² y⁵/2.</p><ol class="work"><li>v²/2g = Q²/(2gA²) = n²y⁵ g/(2 × 2g n²y⁴) = y/4.</li><li>E<sub>c</sub> = y<sub>c</sub> + y<sub>c</sub>/4 = 1.25y<sub>c</sub>.</li><li>y<sub>c</sub>/E<sub>c</sub> = 1/1.25 = 4/5.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2/3 is the ratio for a rectangular channel (E<sub>c</sub> = 1.5y<sub>c</sub>).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 3/4 is the ratio for a parabolic channel (E<sub>c</sub> = 4/3 y<sub>c</sub>).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5/6 is not the ratio of any common channel shape.</li></ul><p>The ratio y_c/E_c for the other common channels is 2/3 (rectangular), 3/4 (parabolic), 4/5 (triangular) and between them for trapezoidal sections. Thus the critical depth is 4/5 of the specific energy in a triangular channel and the minimum specific energy is 1.25 times the critical depth.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> y_c/E_c: rectangle 2/3, parabola 3/4, triangle 4/5.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-64", year: 2018, no: 64, subject: "fluid", topic: "Hardy Cross correction",
q: `A pipe network is shown with all needful input data to compute the first iteration improved magnitudes of the initially assumed flows in the branches. What will be the such improved flow magnitudes in branches AB and CD? Consider to first decimal accuracy.<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2018-64.png" alt="Figure for question 64">`,
opts: ["A to B : 5·1; C to D : 3·1", "A to B : 5·7; C to D : 2·8", "A to B : 4·9; C to D : 3·4", "A to B : 5·5; C to D : 3·8"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) A to B 5·1; C to D 3·1</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Hardy Cross:</b> ΔQ = −ΣkQ|Q|/Σ2k|Q| for the loop, taking clockwise flow as positive.</p><div class="formula">ΔQ = −ΣkQ|Q|/Σ2k|Q|</div><ul class="sym"><li>Loop A-B-C-D with k: AB 4, BC 3, CD 2, DA 5. Initial flows: AB 3 (clockwise), BC 4 (anticlockwise, C to B), CD 1 (clockwise), DA 7 (anticlockwise, A to D).</li></ul><ol class="work"><li>ΣkQ|Q| = 4(9) − 3(16) + 2(1) − 5(49) = 36 − 48 + 2 − 245 = −255.</li><li>Σ2k|Q| = 2(4 × 3 + 3 × 4 + 2 × 1 + 5 × 7) = 2(61) = 122.</li><li>ΔQ = 255/122 = +2.09 (clockwise).</li><li>AB = 3 + 2.09 = 5.1 (A to B); CD = 1 + 2.09 = 3.1 (C to D).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 5.7 and 2.8 apply a correction of about 2.7, not ΔQ = 2.09.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 4.9 and 3.4 apply the correction with the wrong sign in one pipe.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 5.5 and 3.8 do not apply one common ΔQ to the loop.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> ΔQ added to clockwise pipes, subtracted from anticlockwise ones.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-65", year: 2018, no: 65, subject: "fluid", topic: "Depth from the Froude number",
q: `A 2 m wide rectangular channel carries a discharge of 10 m³/s. What would be the depth of flow if the Froude number of the flow is 2·0?`,
opts: ["1·72 m", "1·36 m", "0·86 m", "0·68 m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 0·86 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula:</b> Fr = V/√(gy) = q/(√g y^1.5), so y = [q/(Fr √g)]^(2/3).</p><ol class="work"><li>q = 10/2 = 5 m²/s.</li><li>y^1.5 = 5/(2 × 3.132) = 0.798 → y = 0.798^(2/3) = 0.86 m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1.72 m gives Fr = 0.71, subcritical.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1.36 m gives Fr = 0.93, subcritical.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.68 m gives Fr = 2.8.</li></ul><p>For this channel the critical depth is yc = (q²/g)^(1/3) = (25/9.81)^(1/3) = 1.37 m. A depth of 0.86 m (below yc) means the flow is supercritical with a velocity of 5/0.86 = 5.8 m/s, consistent with a Froude number of 2.0. The conjugate (sequent) depth would be 1.9 × 0.86 ≈ 1.8 m.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Fr = q/(√g y^1.5).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-66", year: 2018, no: 66, subject: "fluid", topic: "M3 profile",
q: `M₃ Profile is indicated by which of the following conditions?`,
opts: ["y₀ &gt; y<sub>c</sub> &gt; y", "y &gt; y₀ &gt; y<sub>c</sub>", "y<sub>c</sub> &gt; y₀ &gt; y", "y &gt; y<sub>c</sub> &gt; y₀"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) y₀ &gt; y<sub>c</sub> &gt; y</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> M denotes a mild slope (y₀ &gt; y<sub>c</sub>). The zones are numbered from the top: zone 1 above both depths (y &gt; y₀), zone 2 between them (y₀ &gt; y &gt; y<sub>c</sub>) and zone 3 below both (y &lt; y<sub>c</sub>). The M₃ curve has a depth below the critical depth: y₀ &gt; y<sub>c</sub> &gt; y.</p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> y &gt; y₀ &gt; y<sub>c</sub> is the M₁ profile (backwater behind a dam).</li><li><b>(c)</b> y<sub>c</sub> &gt; y₀ &gt; y describes a steep slope, so it would be S₃.</li><li><b>(d)</b> y &gt; y<sub>c</sub> &gt; y₀ describes a steep slope, so it would be S₁.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Mild slope: M1 above y0, M2 between, M3 below yc.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-67", year: 2018, no: 67, subject: "fluid", topic: "Floating logs and secondary currents",
q: `Floating logs of wood tend to move to the mid-river reach on the water surface. This is due to`,
opts: ["Least obstruction from the banks", "2-cell transverse circulation in the flow", "Fastest velocity along the mid-river reach", "Near-symmetry of the isovels across the section is conducive to principle of least work"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2-cell transverse circulation in the flow</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a river the flow is not purely longitudinal: two counter-rotating transverse (secondary) circulation cells exist, with the surface flow directed from the banks toward the centreline and a return flow near the bed. Floating bodies are carried toward the middle at the surface.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Least obstruction from the banks does not push the logs toward the centre.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The fastest velocity at the centre explains the speed of the logs, not their transverse movement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Symmetry of isovels describes the shape of the velocity field but gives no transverse force.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Secondary currents converge at the surface mid-river.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-68", year: 2018, no: 68, subject: "fluid", topic: "Froude number from the sequent depth ratio",
q: `The sequent depth ratio in a rectangular channel is 14. The Froude number of the supercritical flow will be`,
opts: ["6·62", "7·55", "8·45", "10·25"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 10·25</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Formula (hydraulic jump):</b> y₂/y₁ = ½(√(1 + 8F₁²) − 1).</p><div class="formula">y₂/y₁ = ½(√(1 + 8F₁²) − 1)</div><ol class="work"><li>14 = ½(√(1 + 8F₁²) − 1) → √(1 + 8F₁²) = 29.</li><li>1 + 8F₁² = 841 → F₁² = 105 → F₁ = 10.25.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 6.62 gives a depth ratio of 8.9.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 7.55 gives a depth ratio of 10.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 8.45 gives a depth ratio of 11.9.</li></ul><p>The sequent depth ratio of 14 corresponds to a strong jump (F₁ &gt; 9) with a high energy loss: about 64 % of the energy in a jump with F₁ = 10 is dissipated. The jump length is about 6 y₂, which is why stilling basins use baffle blocks and sills to shorten the jump.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> F₁² = [(2r + 1)² − 1]/8.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-69", year: 2018, no: 69, subject: "fluid", topic: "Head loss in a hydraulic jump",
q: `In a hydraulic jump, the depths on the two sides are 0·4 m and 1·4 m. The head loss in the jump is nearly`,
opts: ["0·45 m", "0·65 m", "0·80 m", "0·90 m"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 0·45 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Head loss in a hydraulic jump.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> h<sub>L</sub> = (y₂ − y₁)³/(4y₁y₂)</div><ol class="work"><li>h<sub>L</sub> = (1.4 − 0.4)³/(4 × 0.4 × 1.4) = 1/2.24 = 0.446 m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 0.65 m would need a depth ratio of about 4.5.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.80 m is not given by the formula.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.90 m is not given by the formula.</li></ul><p>The loss of 0.446 m can be checked from the energy equation: E₁ = y₁ + q²/2gy₁² and E₂ = y₂ + q²/2gy₂², where q² = g y₁y₂(y₁ + y₂)/2 = 9.81 × 0.4 × 1.4 × 1.8/2 = 4.94. E₁ = 0.4 + 4.94/(19.62 × 0.16) = 1.97 m and E₂ = 1.4 + 4.94/(19.62 × 1.96) = 1.53 m, so E₁ − E₂ = 0.44 m.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> h_L = (y₂ − y₁)³/(4y₁y₂).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-70", year: 2018, no: 70, subject: "fluid", topic: "Non-dimensional specific speed of a pump",
q: `A 20 cm centrifugal pump runs at 1400 rpm delivering 0·09 m³/sec against a head of 45 m with an efficiency of 87%. What is its non-dimensional specific speed using rps as the relevant data component?`,
opts: ["0·482", "0·474", "0·466", "0·458"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 0·458</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Non-dimensional specific speed of a pump.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> non-dimensional specific speed N<sub>s</sub> = ω√Q/(gH)^(3/4), with ω in rad/s</div><ol class="work"><li>ω = 2π × 1400/60 = 146.6 rad/s; √Q = 0.3.</li><li>(gH)^(3/4) = (441.45)^0.75 = 96.4.</li><li>N<sub>s</sub> = 146.6 × 0.3/96.4 = 0.456 ≈ 0.458.</li></ol><p>With N in rev/s (23.3) the number would be 0.073, which is not among the options; the keyed value corresponds to the angular speed in rad/s.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.482 does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 0.474 does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.466 does not satisfy the relation used above.</li></ul><p>The non-dimensional specific speed is a design characteristic of the pump and equals about 0.46 for a radial-flow centrifugal pump. Values below 0.2 indicate a radial impeller with a high head, between 0.5 and 1.5 a mixed-flow and above 2 an axial-flow pump. The efficiency of 87 % and the impeller diameter are not needed.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> N_s = ω√Q/(gH)^0.75.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-71", year: 2018, no: 71, subject: "fluid", topic: "Pumps in parallel",
q: `Two identical centrifugal pumps are connected in parallel to a common delivery pipe of a system. The discharge performance curve of each of the pumps is represented by H = 30 − 80Q². The discharge-head equation of the parallel duplex pump set is`,
opts: ["H = 30 − 80Q²", "H = 15 − 20Q²", "H = 30 − 20Q²", "H = 15 − 80Q²"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) H = 30 − 20Q²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In parallel the heads are equal and the discharges add: each pump delivers Q/2 at head H.</p><div class="formula">Parallel: H same, Q adds</div><ol class="work"><li>H = 30 − 80(Q/2)² = 30 − 20Q².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> This is the curve of a single pump.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 15 − 20Q² halves the shut-off head, which happens only for two pumps in series on a different basis.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 15 − 80Q² halves the shut-off head without changing the Q² coefficient.</li></ul><p>For parallel operation the combined curve is obtained by adding the discharges at the same head: the quadratic coefficient falls to a quarter, 80/4 = 20. The pumps in parallel give less than twice the flow in a system with friction because the system head rises with the flow, so the operating point lies where the combined curve cuts the system curve.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Parallel: add Q at the same H.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-72", year: 2018, no: 72, subject: "fluid", topic: "Affinity laws for a pump",
q: `Consider the following data relating to the performance of a centrifugal pump: speed = 1200 rpm, flow rate = 30 l/s, head = 20 m, and power = 5 kW. If the speed of the pump is increased to 1500 rpm, assuming the efficiency is unaltered, the new flow rate and head, respectively, will be`,
opts: ["46·9 l/s and 25·0 m", "37·5 l/s and 25·0 m", "46·9 l/s and 31·3 m", "37·5 l/s and 31·3 m"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 37·5 l/s and 31·3 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Affinity laws:</b> Q ∝ N and H ∝ N².</p><div class="formula">Q ∝ N, H ∝ N²</div><ol class="work"><li>N₂/N₁ = 1500/1200 = 1.25.</li><li>Q₂ = 30 × 1.25 = 37.5 l/s.</li><li>H₂ = 20 × 1.25² = 31.25 m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 46.9 l/s is 30 × 1.5625 (N², wrongly used for Q) and 25 m is 20 × 1.25 (N, wrongly used for H).</li><li><b>(b)</b> The flow is right but 25.0 m uses H ∝ N.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The head is right but 46.9 l/s uses Q ∝ N².</li></ul><p>Power also scales: P ∝ N³, so P₂ = 5 × 1.25³ = 9.77 kW. The efficiency stays the same because the operating point moves to a dynamically similar point on the characteristic. The affinity laws apply only for the same pump and for small changes in speed, as the viscous effects change at large changes.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Q ∝ N, H ∝ N², P ∝ N³.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-73", year: 2018, no: 73, subject: "fluid", topic: "Work done by a jet on moving vanes",
q: `The work done by a kN of water jet moving with a velocity of 60 m/sec when it impinges on a series of vanes moving in the same direction with a velocity of 9 m/sec is`,
opts: ["60·2 kN m", "55·6 kN m", "46·8 kN m", "45·0 kN m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 46·8 kN m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a series of flat vanes (normal to the jet) all the water leaving the nozzle strikes the vanes, and the work done per unit weight is (V − u)u/g.</p><div class="formula">Work/unit weight = (V − u)u/g (series of vanes)</div><ol class="work"><li>Work per kN = (60 − 9) × 9/9.81 = 46.8 kN·m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 60.2 kN·m is not found from the formula.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 55.6 kN·m is not found from the formula.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 45.0 kN·m would result from taking g = 10 and V − u = 50.</li></ul><p>For a single moving vane the work is (V − u)u(1 + cos φ)/g per unit weight of water striking, but with a series of vanes the whole jet is used, which is why the available water is that discharged by the nozzle and the work is per kN of the water issuing. The jet efficiency is the work done divided by the kinetic energy 60²/2g = 183.5 kN·m, giving 25.5 %.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Series of vanes: W = (V − u)u/g per unit weight.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-74", year: 2018, no: 74, subject: "fluid", topic: "Efficiency of a draft tube", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The velocity heads of water at the inlet and outlet sections of a draft tube are 3·5 m and 0·3 m, respectively. The frictional and other losses in the draft tube can be taken as 0·5 m. What is the efficiency of the draft tube?`,
opts: ["84·4%", "80·0%", "77·1%", "74·4%"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 84·4%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The draft tube efficiency is the ratio of the actual conversion of kinetic head to pressure head to the ideal (loss-free) conversion.</p><div class="formula">η_d = (actual pressure recovery)/(ideal recovery (V₁² − V₂²)/2g)</div><ol class="work"><li>Ideal conversion = v₁²/2g − v₂²/2g = 3.5 − 0.3 = 3.2 m.</li><li>Actual conversion = 3.2 − 0.5 (losses) = 2.7 m.</li><li>η = 2.7/3.2 = 84.4 %.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 80.0 % does not match the working above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 77.1 % = 2.7/3.5 compares the actual regain with the whole inlet velocity head, which includes the 0.3 m leaving loss that is not recoverable.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 74.4 % does not match the working above.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Draft tube η = actual regain / ideal regain.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-75", year: 2018, no: 75, subject: "hydro", topic: "Effects of excess groundwater pumping",
q: `Which of the following situations can be attributed to sustained excessive groundwater pumping in a basin?<ol><li>Drying up of small lakes and streams over a period in spite of normal rainfall</li><li>Deterioration of groundwater quality in certain aquifers</li><li>Land subsidence in the basin</li><li>Increase in seismic activity</li><li>Increased cost of groundwater extraction</li></ol>`,
opts: ["2 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 5 only", "3 and 4 only", "1 and 5 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2, 3 and 5 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Over-pumping lowers the water table faster than recharge.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the water table falls below stream beds and lake bottoms, so they dry up even in normal rainfall.</li><li><b>2</b> True: saline or polluted water is drawn in from neighbouring aquifers or the sea.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the compaction of the drained aquifer causes subsidence.</li><li><b>4</b> False: pumping does not cause earthquakes.</li><li><b>5</b> True: wells must be deepened and pumping heads rise, so costs increase.</li></ul><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 3, 5, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 5.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 2, 5, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Over-pumping: falling table, poor quality, subsidence, higher cost.</p>` }
);

