window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2018-76", year: 2018, no: 76, subject: "hydro", topic: "Horton's infiltration depth",
q: `Horton's infiltration equation was fitted to data from an infiltration test. It was found that the initial infiltration capacity was 20 mm/h, final infiltration capacity was 5 mm/h and the exponential decay constant was 0·5 h⁻¹. If the infiltration was at capacity rates, the total infiltration depth for a uniform storm of 10 h duration would be`,
opts: ["80 mm", "50 mm", "30 mm", "20 mm"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 80 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The depth over the storm F = ∫f dt = f<sub>c</sub>t + [(f₀ − f<sub>c</sub>)/k](1 − e^(−kt)).</p><div class="formula"><b>Horton:</b> f = f<sub>c</sub> + (f₀ − f<sub>c</sub>)e^(−kt)</div><ol class="work"><li>f<sub>c</sub>t = 5 × 10 = 50 mm.</li><li>(f₀ − f<sub>c</sub>)/k = 15/0.5 = 30 mm; (1 − e^(−5)) = 0.9933 → 29.8 mm.</li><li>F = 50 + 29.8 = 79.8 ≈ 80 mm.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 50 mm is the final-rate contribution only.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 30 mm is the transient part only.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 20 mm is the initial capacity per hour, not a depth over 10 hours.</li></ul><p>The infiltration capacity falls from 20 to 5 mm/h during the 10-hour storm, and since the storm gives rain at least equal to the capacity, the infiltration is at capacity throughout. The average infiltration rate is 8 mm/h, much nearer the final rate; the decay constant of 0.5 per hour means that 95 % of the decay takes place in 6 hours.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> F = fc t + (f₀ − fc)(1 − e^(−kt))/k.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-77", year: 2018, no: 77, subject: "fluid", topic: "Governors of turbines",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding turbines:<ol><li>The main function of a governor is to maintain a constant speed even as the load on the turbine fluctuates</li><li>In the case of Pelton turbines, the governor closes or opens the wicket gates</li><li>In the case of Francis turbines, the governor opens or closes the needle valve</li><li>In the case of a Kaplan turbine, the governor swings the runner blades appropriately in addition to further closing or further opening of the wicket gates</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A governor controls the water flow to keep the speed constant.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: that is its purpose.</li><li><b>2</b> False: a Pelton turbine has a nozzle with a needle (spear) valve, which the governor moves; wicket gates belong to reaction turbines.</li><li><b>3</b> False: a Francis turbine has wicket (guide) vanes, not a needle valve.</li><li><b>4</b> True: in a Kaplan turbine the governor adjusts both the guide vanes and the runner blade angle.</li></ul><p>Hence 1 and 4 only.</p><p>Governors of reaction turbines (Francis and Kaplan) act on the wicket gates through a servomotor, while those of impulse turbines act by a spear valve in the nozzle and may deflect the jet to avoid water hammer. The governor works by a centrifugal pendulum that senses the speed, a control valve, an oil-pressure servomotor and an oil pump.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pelton: needle valve; Francis: wicket gates; Kaplan: both.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-78", year: 2018, no: 78, subject: "fluid", topic: "Surge front speed in an open channel",
q: `Consider the occurrence of a surge at the water surface of a wide rectangular channel flow, as in the figure, where the one-dimensionally considered velocities are v₁ and v₂ and the depths are d₁ and d₂, with the surge height h, whereby d₂ − d₁ = h, moving at a speed of V<sub>W</sub> over depth d₁. Joint application of continuity and momentum principles will indicate the surge front speed V<sub>W</sub>, to be<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2018-78.png" alt="Figure for question 78">`,
opts: ["V<sub>W</sub> = √(gd₁) (1 + 3h/2d₁)^(1/2)", "V<sub>W</sub> = √(gd₁) (1 + 3h/2d₁ + ½(h/d₁)²)^(1/2)", "V<sub>W</sub> = √(gd₁) (1 + h/d₁)^(1/2)", "V<sub>W</sub> = √(gd₁) (1 + (h/d₁)²)^(1/2)"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) form (b)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Reduce to a steady jump by moving with the surge. Continuity and momentum for a wide rectangular channel give V<sub>W</sub>² = g d₂(d₁ + d₂)/(2d₁) for a surge moving into still water.</p><ol class="work"><li>Put d₂ = d₁ + h: V<sub>W</sub>² = g(d₁ + h)(2d₁ + h)/(2d₁).</li><li>= g d₁(1 + h/d₁)(2 + h/d₁)/2 = g d₁[1 + 3h/(2d₁) + h²/(2d₁²)].</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Drops the h² term, correct only for very small surges.</li><li><b>(c)</b> (1 + h/d₁) is not the result of the momentum balance.</li><li><b>(d)</b> (1 + (h/d₁)²) drops the linear term.</li></ul><p>The surge is an unsteady, moving hydraulic jump. By choosing a frame moving with the surge, the flow becomes steady and the jump equations apply with the relative velocities v₁ + V_W and v₂ + V_W. For small surges h ≪ d₁, the speed tends to √(gd₁), the wave speed of an infinitesimal disturbance.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Surge speed: V² = g d₂(d₁ + d₂)/2d₁.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-79", year: 2018, no: 79, subject: "hydro", topic: "Limitations of the unit hydrograph",
q: `Which of the following will pose difficulties in adopting u.h.g. principles and processes in evaluating flood hydrographs of basins?<ol><li>Non-uniform areal distribution within a storm</li><li>Intensity variation within a storm</li><li>The centre of the storm varying from storm to storm in case of large catchments</li><li>Dividing into a number of sub-basins and routing the individual DRHs through their respective channels to obtain the composite DRH at the basin outlet</li><li>Large storages within the catchment</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 5 only", "1, 2, 3 and 5 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 5 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The unit hydrograph assumes a rainfall of uniform intensity, uniform areal distribution and a linear, time-invariant catchment.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Difficulty: unit hydrograph theory assumes a uniform spatial distribution of rain.</li><li><b>2</b> Difficulty: intensity is assumed uniform within the duration.</li><li><b>3</b> Difficulty: on a large catchment a shifting storm centre changes the shape of the hydrograph.</li><li><b>4</b> Not a difficulty: dividing a large basin into sub-basins and routing is the remedy that allows the unit hydrograph to be used.</li><li><b>5</b> Difficulty: large storages make the response non-linear.</li></ul><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, 5, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 5, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 5.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Unit hydrograph: uniform rain, linear system, small basin.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-80", year: 2018, no: 80, subject: "hydro", topic: "φ-index from a direct runoff hydrograph",
q: `Rainfall of magnitude 4·3 cm, followed by 3·7 cm, occurred on two consecutive 4 h durations on a catchment area of 25 km², and there resulted a DRH (after isolation of base flow in the flood flow hydrograph) with ordinates (m³/s) at 0, 4, 8, … 44 h: 0, 9, 16, 20, 20, 17·8, 13·4, 9·4, 6·2, 3·7, 1·8, 0. (Adopt trapezoidal formula) What is the φ index value?`,
opts: ["0·149 cm/h", "0·155 cm/h", "0·161 cm/h", "0·167 cm/h"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·155 cm/h</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The area under the DRH is the direct runoff volume. Total rain = 4.3 + 3.7 = 8.0 cm in 8 h.</p><div class="formula">Runoff depth = DRH volume/area</div><div class="formula">φ = (P − R)/t_e</div><ol class="work"><li>Trapezoidal rule with Δt = 4 h: sum of interior ordinates = 9 + 16 + 20 + 20 + 17.8 + 13.4 + 9.4 + 6.2 + 3.7 + 1.8 = 117.3 m³/s (end ordinates are zero).</li><li>Volume = 117.3 × 4 × 3600 = 1.689 × 10⁶ m³.</li><li>Runoff depth = 1.689 × 10⁶/(25 × 10⁶) = 0.0676 m = 6.76 cm.</li><li>Loss = 8.0 − 6.76 = 1.24 cm over 8 h → φ = 0.155 cm/h.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.149 cm/h would give a runoff of 6.81 cm.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.161 cm/h would give a runoff of 6.71 cm.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.167 cm/h would give a runoff of 6.66 cm.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> φ = (rain − runoff depth)/duration.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-81", year: 2018, no: 81, subject: "hydro", topic: "Hydraulic conductivity from a flow rate",
q: `Groundwater flows through an aquifer with a cross-sectional area of 1·0 × 10⁴ m² and a length of 1500 m. Hydraulic heads are 300 m and 250 m at the groundwater entry and exit points in the aquifer, respectively. Groundwater discharges into a stream at the rate of 750 m³/day. Then the hydraulic conductivity of the aquifer is`,
opts: ["1·50 m/day", "2·25 m/day", "3·50 m/day", "4·25 m/day"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2·25 m/day</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Darcy's law:</b> Q = K i A, with i = Δh/L.</p><ol class="work"><li>i = (300 − 250)/1500 = 0.0333.</li><li>K = Q/(iA) = 750/(0.0333 × 10⁴) = 2.25 m/day.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 1.50 m/day would give a flow of 500 m³/day.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3.50 m/day would give a flow of 1167 m³/day.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 4.25 m/day would give a flow of 1417 m³/day.</li></ul><p>The hydraulic gradient is 0.033 m/m and the Darcy velocity is Q/A = 0.075 m/day. A hydraulic conductivity of 2.25 m/day is typical of clean sand and fine sand, and the actual seepage velocity is higher: with a porosity of 0.3 it is 0.25 m/day. The direction of flow is from the point of higher head to lower.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> K = Q L/(A Δh).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-82", year: 2018, no: 82, subject: "fluid", topic: "Classification of a turbine by specific speed",
q: `A hydraulic turbine develops 5000 kW under a head of 30 m when running at 100 rpm. This turbine belongs to the category of`,
opts: ["Pelton wheel", "Francis Turbine", "Kaplan Turbine", "Propeller Turbine"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) Francis turbine</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Specific speed:</b> N<sub>s</sub> = N√P/H^(5/4) (P in kW, H in m).</p><ol class="work"><li>N<sub>s</sub> = 100 × √5000/30^1.25 = 7071/70.2 ≈ 101.</li></ol><p>Ranges: Pelton 10–60 (up to 70 multi-jet), Francis 60–300, Kaplan and propeller above about 300. A specific speed of about 100 is a Francis turbine.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Ns = N√P/H^1.25 ≈ 100√5000/30^1.25 ≈ 100. Pelton wheels work only up to Ns ≈ 35–60.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Kaplan turbines need Ns of about 300–1000 and low heads, so Ns ≈ 100 is too low.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Propeller (fixed-blade) turbines also need Ns of about 300–1000, so Ns ≈ 100 is too low.</li></ul><p>The specific speed is defined for a turbine at the unit head and unit power. For Francis turbines, the lower the specific speed the more radial the flow; high-head runners have N_s of 60–120 and low-head runners 250–300. Above 300 the flow becomes axial, and propeller or Kaplan turbines are used for heads below about 60 m.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Ns < 60 Pelton; 60–300 Francis; > 300 Kaplan.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-83", year: 2018, no: 83, subject: "hydro", topic: "φ index from rainfall and runoff",
q: `The rate of rainfall for the successive 30 min periods of a 3-hour storm are: 1·6, 3·6, 5·0, 2·8, 2·2 and 1·0 cm/hour. The corresponding surface runoff is estimated to be 3·2 cm. Then, the φ index is`,
opts: ["1·5 cm/h", "1·8 cm/h", "2·1 cm/h", "2·4 cm/h"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1·8 cm/h</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> φ is the constant rate of loss such that the rain in excess of it equals the runoff. Rain depths per 0.5 h: 0.8, 1.8, 2.5, 1.4, 1.1, 0.5 cm (total 8.1 cm). Trial φ = 1.8 cm/h: the first and last periods (1.6 and 1.0) are below φ, so loss is the rain itself there.</p><ol class="work"><li>Excess over φ = 1.8: (3.6 − 1.8) + (5.0 − 1.8) + (2.8 − 1.8) + (2.2 − 1.8) = 1.8 + 3.2 + 1.0 + 0.4 = 6.4 cm/h.</li><li>Runoff = 6.4 × 0.5 = 3.2 cm, equal to the given runoff. So φ = 1.8 cm/h.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> φ = 1.5 gives runoff = 0.5 × (0.1 + 2.1 + 3.5 + 1.3 + 0.7) = 3.85 cm, too high.</li><li><b>(c)</b> φ = 2.1 gives runoff = 0.5 × (1.5 + 2.9 + 0.7 + 0.1) = 2.6 cm, too low.</li><li><b>(d)</b> φ = 2.4 gives runoff = 0.5 × (1.2 + 2.6 + 0.4) = 2.1 cm, too low.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Trial φ until excess rain = runoff.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-84", year: 2018, no: 84, subject: "geo", topic: "Critical centre for a purely cohesive slope",
q: `For stability analysis of slopes of purely cohesive soils, the critical centre is taken to lie at the intersection of`,
opts: ["The perpendicular bisector of the slope and the locus of the centre", "The perpendicular drawn at the one-third slope from the toe and the locus of the centre", "The perpendicular drawn at the two-third slope from the toe and the locus of the centre", "Directional angles"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) directional angles</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a purely cohesive (φ = 0) soil, Fellenius located the centre of the critical slip circle by the <b>directional angles</b> α and β measured from the slope and the horizontal at the toe and the top of the slope (tabulated for the slope angle). The lines drawn at these angles intersect at the critical centre.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A perpendicular bisector of the slope gives the centre for a circle through the toe and crest, not the critical one.</li><li><b>(b)</b> The perpendicular at the one-third point is used in the graphical construction for c–φ soils with the locus of centres, not this case.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The two-third point is not used in the construction.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Fellenius: centre by directional angles (φ = 0).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-85", year: 2018, no: 85, subject: "hydro", topic: "Water logging",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding water logging:<ol><li>Water logging is the rise of groundwater table leading to possible increase in salinity resulting in a reduction in the yield of crops</li><li>Water logging cannot be eliminated in certain areas but can be controlled only if the quantity of water percolating into that soil is checked and reduced</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) both 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Water logging occurs when the water table rises into the root zone.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the saturated root zone lacks air and the capillary rise brings salts, so salinity builds up and yields fall.</li><li><b>2</b> True: in some areas with flat topography or poor drainage the problem cannot be wholly removed, but it is controlled by reducing seepage (lining canals, controlling irrigation) and by drainage.</li></ul><p>Both are correct, so (c).</p><p>Water logging is controlled by lining canals and water courses, by a proper water use (avoiding over-irrigation), by drainage (surface and sub-surface) and by pumping from tube wells. Land is said to be waterlogged when the water table is within the root zone, about 1.5 m of the ground surface; the reclamation of salt-affected soils needs flushing with good-quality water after drainage.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Water logging: rising table, salinity, lower yield.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-86", year: 2018, no: 86, subject: "hydro", topic: "Moving average of rainfall",
q: `Annual rainfall values at station A in mm for the years 2001 to 2010 are 586, 621, 618, 639, 689, 610, 591, 604, 621, 650. If simple central 3-year moving mean of this rainfall record is calculated, the maximum and minimum values in the moving mean list would be`,
opts: ["689 mm and 602 mm", "649 mm and 602 mm", "689 mm and 586 mm", "649 mm and 586 mm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 649 mm and 602 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The 3-year central moving mean has eight values (2002–2009):</p><div class="formula">3-year moving mean = (xᵢ₋₁ + xᵢ + xᵢ₊₁)/3</div><ol class="work"><li>(586 + 621 + 618)/3 = 608.3; (621 + 618 + 639)/3 = 626.0.</li><li>(618 + 639 + 689)/3 = 648.7; (639 + 689 + 610)/3 = 646.0.</li><li>(689 + 610 + 591)/3 = 630.0; (610 + 591 + 604)/3 = 601.7.</li><li>(591 + 604 + 621)/3 = 605.3; (604 + 621 + 650)/3 = 625.0.</li></ol><p>Maximum = 648.7 ≈ 649 mm; minimum = 601.7 ≈ 602 mm.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 689 mm is the single highest rainfall, not a moving mean.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 689 and 586 are the highest and lowest raw values, not moving means.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 586 mm is the lowest raw value; the smallest moving mean is 602 mm.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Moving mean smooths the extremes.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-87", year: 2018, no: 87, subject: "hydro", topic: "Khosla's theory",
q: `Khosla's formulae for assessing pressure distribution under weir floors are based on`,
opts: ["Potential flow in permeable layers just beneath the floors", "Boundary layer flow with pressure drop longitudinally", "Conformal transformation of potential flow into the w plane", "Simplification of 3-D flow"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) conformal transformation into the w-plane</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Khosla's method of independent variables solves Laplace's equation for seepage under a weir by conformal transformation (Schwarz–Christoffel) of the flow region into the complex potential w-plane, and then gives the pressures at key points of the floor and piles as formulas in terms of depth-to-floor-width ratios.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Potential flow is the physical basis, but the method is the conformal transformation, which the question asks for.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Boundary layer flow is not involved in seepage.</li><li><b>(d)</b> The method is two-dimensional; 3-D effects are handled by corrections.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Khosla: conformal transformation of seepage flow.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-88", year: 2018, no: 88, subject: "hydro", topic: "Uplift in a siphon aqueduct", selfCorrected: true,
q: `In a siphon aqueduct, the worst condition of uplift on the floor occurs when`,
opts: ["The canal is full and the drainage is empty, with water table at drainage bed level", "The canal is empty and the drainage is full, with water table at drainage bed level", "Both the canal and the drainage are full", "The canal is empty and the drainage is full, with water table below the floor"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) canal full, drainage empty, water table at drainage bed</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The uplift on the floor of the aqueduct barrel arises from the seepage head under the trough. It is greatest when the head above the floor is a minimum and the water table pressure underneath is highest. For the barrel (drainage) floor, the worst case is when the drainage channel is empty (no water load on the floor to counter it) but the water table is at the drainage bed level, while the canal is running full and maximises seepage.</p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> With the drainage full, the water load on the floor offsets the uplift.</li><li><b>(c)</b> With both full, the load on the floor from the drainage water reduces the net uplift.</li><li><b>(d)</b> If the water table is below the floor, there is no uplift.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Worst uplift: no water load on the floor, but high seepage head.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-89", year: 2018, no: 89, subject: "env", topic: "Zero hardness",
q: `Zero hardness of water is achieved by`,
opts: ["Lime-soda process", "Ion exchange treatment", "Excess lime treatment", "Excess alum dosage"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) ion exchange treatment</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Ion exchange (zeolite) softening replaces all calcium and magnesium ions by sodium, so the hardness falls to practically zero.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The lime–soda process can reduce the hardness only to about 15–50 mg/l, the solubility of CaCO₃ and Mg(OH)₂.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Excess lime removes carbonate and magnesium hardness but leaves residual hardness.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Alum is a coagulant and does not soften water.</li></ul><p>Ion-exchange (zeolite) softening replaces calcium and magnesium by sodium: Ca²⁺ + Na₂Z → CaZ + 2Na⁺. The resin is regenerated with brine. The treated water has zero hardness but the sodium content rises, so it is often blended with raw water. Water of zero hardness is needed for boilers, textile and pharmaceutical use.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Zeolite softening gives zero hardness.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-90", year: 2018, no: 90, subject: "env", topic: "Five-day BOD",
q: `Five-days BOD of a 10% diluted sample having D<sub>O</sub> = 6·7 mg/l, D<sub>S</sub> = 2 mg/l and consumption of oxygen in blank = 0·5 mg/l, will be`,
opts: ["22 mg/l", "42 mg/l", "62 mg/l", "82 mg/l"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 42 mg/l</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Five-day BOD.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> BOD₅ = [(D₀ − D₅) − blank correction]/P, with P the decimal dilution</div><ol class="work"><li>D₀ − D₅ = 6.7 − 2.0 = 4.7 mg/l.</li><li>Subtract the blank: 4.7 − 0.5 = 4.2 mg/l.</li><li>BOD₅ = 4.2/0.10 = 42 mg/l.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 22 mg/l would use a dilution of 0.19.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 62 mg/l would result from dividing by about 0.068.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 82 mg/l would come from 6.7 + 2 − 0.5 ... not the depletion.</li></ul><p>The seeding of a sample is needed when the waste has no microorganisms; the 'blank' is the oxygen consumption of the dilution water itself. A 10 % dilution is normally used for samples with a BOD of about 20 to 70 mg/l, and the oxygen depletion should lie between 2 mg/l and 5 mg/l in the 5-day test at 20 °C to be valid.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> BOD = (depletion − blank)/dilution fraction.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-91", year: 2018, no: 91, subject: "env", topic: "Alkalinity and hardness",
q: `Which one of the following statements related to testing of water for municipal use is correctly applicable?`,
opts: ["Pseudo-hardness is due to presence of fluoride in water", "When alkalinity ≥ total hardness, Carbonate hardness in mg/l = Total hardness in mg/l", "Bicarbonate alkalinity = total alkalinity − (carbonate alkalinity − hydroxide alkalinity)", "Hydroxide alkalinity = Carbonate alkalinity + Bicarbonate alkalinity"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) if alkalinity ≥ total hardness, carbonate hardness = total hardness</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Carbonate (temporary) hardness is the part of hardness balanced by alkalinity: it equals the smaller of the two. When alkalinity ≥ total hardness, all the hardness is carbonate hardness.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Pseudo-hardness is caused by high sodium (which behaves like hardness in soap action), not fluoride.</li><li><b>(c)</b> The bicarbonate alkalinity relation is not stated correctly: it is total alkalinity minus the carbonate and hydroxide alkalinities in the proper combination.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Hydroxide alkalinity is not the sum of carbonate and bicarbonate alkalinities.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Carbonate hardness = min(alkalinity, total hardness).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-92", year: 2018, no: 92, subject: "env", topic: "Capacity of a service reservoir",
q: `The capacity of a service reservoir in a campus should cater to`,
opts: ["Sum total of balancing storage, breakdown storage and fire reserve", "Sum total of balancing storage and fire reserve", "Sum total of breakdown storage and fire reserve", "Balancing storage only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) balancing + breakdown + fire storage</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A service (distribution) reservoir has three parts: balancing storage (to meet the hourly variation of demand against a constant supply), breakdown storage (about 1/4 of the day's demand, for pipe or pump failures) and fire reserve (a fixed volume for fire fighting). The capacity is the sum of all three.</p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Omits the breakdown storage.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits the balancing storage, which is the main part.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Balancing storage alone leaves no reserve for breakdowns or fires.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Service reservoir = balancing + breakdown + fire.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-93", year: 2018, no: 93, subject: "env", topic: "Groundwater pollutants",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding groundwater pollutants:<ol><li>Most of the groundwaters are generally non-alkaline</li><li>A moderate amount of fluoride, about 0·6 mg/l to 1·5 mg/l, in drinking water, would help in good development of teeth</li><li>Natural waters do not have dissolved mineral matter in them</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Natural waters carry dissolved minerals and are usually alkaline.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: most groundwaters are alkaline because of the bicarbonates dissolved from limestone and other rocks.</li><li><b>2</b> True: 0.6–1.5 mg/l fluoride helps in the development of strong teeth; more than 1.5 mg/l causes fluorosis.</li><li><b>3</b> False: natural waters always contain dissolved minerals (calcium, magnesium, sodium, bicarbonate, chloride, sulphate).</li></ul><p>So 2 only.</p><p>Groundwater is generally harder and more mineralised than surface water because it is in contact with rocks for a long time. Fluoride at 0.6–1.5 mg/l prevents dental caries; above 1.5 mg/l it causes dental fluorosis, and above 3 mg/l skeletal fluorosis. The Indian drinking-water limit is 1.0 mg/l (acceptable) and 1.5 mg/l (permissible).</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Fluoride: 0.6–1.5 mg/l helps teeth, more is harmful.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-94", year: 2018, no: 94, subject: "env", topic: "Anchorage of water pipelines",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding anchorage of pipelines conveying water:<ol><li>At bends, pipes tend to pull apart</li><li>At bends, forces exerted on the joints due to longitudinal shearing stresses are enormous and the joints may get loosened</li><li>To avoid problems by hydrodynamic effects, pipes are anchored using concrete blocks which absorb side thrusts at bends</li><li>Pipes are also anchored on steep slopes</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Water pipes must be anchored where the water exerts an unbalanced force:</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the pressure of the flowing water pushes a bend outwards, so the pipes tend to pull apart at the joints.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the forces at the bend joints are large and may loosen them.</li><li><b>3</b> True: concrete thrust blocks absorb the side thrust at bends, tees and dead ends.</li><li><b>4</b> True: on steep slopes the pipe tends to slide down, so it is anchored with blocks.</li></ul><p>All four are correct.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Anchor blocks at bends, tees, dead ends and on steep slopes.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-95", year: 2018, no: 95, subject: "env", topic: "Bioenergy as a renewable source",
q: `Consider the following statements with reference to bioenergy as a renewable energy source:<ol><li>Plants ensure continuous supply of gas due to their continuous growth</li><li>Cost of obtaining energy from biogas is less than that from fossil fuels</li><li>Digestion of sludge may produce H₂S and NO<sub>x</sub> which are injurious to human health</li><li>'Floating dome' installation is the preferred option as it supplies gas at constant pressure irrespective of quantity of gas produced</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Biogas is a renewable fuel from the anaerobic digestion of organic matter.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: plant matter grows continuously and feeds the digester, so the supply is sustained.</li><li><b>2</b> True: biogas from waste is cheaper than fossil fuels.</li><li><b>3</b> False: digestion produces H₂S in small amounts, but NO<sub>x</sub> is a product of high-temperature combustion, not of digestion.</li><li><b>4</b> True: the floating gas holder rises and falls with the gas volume, giving a constant gas pressure.</li></ul><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Floating dome biogas plant: constant pressure.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-96", year: 2018, no: 96, subject: "env", topic: "Waste stabilization ponds",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding waste stabilization ponds:<ol><li>The pond has a symbiotic process of waste stabilization through algae on one hand and bacteria on the other</li><li>The oxygen in the pond is provided by algae through photosynthesis</li><li>The detention period is of the order of two to three days</li><li>The bacteria which develop in the pond are aerobic bacteria</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In a stabilization pond bacteria break down the waste and release CO₂, nutrients; algae use them with sunlight and give oxygen.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the symbiosis of algae and bacteria is the working principle.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the oxygen comes from photosynthesis (and some surface reaeration).</li><li><b>3</b> False: detention periods are of the order of 5–30 days (facultative ponds), far longer than two to three days.</li><li><b>4</b> False: the bacteria are of several kinds: aerobic in the upper layer, anaerobic at the bottom of facultative ponds.</li></ul><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Stabilization ponds: algae–bacteria symbiosis, long detention.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-97", year: 2018, no: 97, subject: "env", topic: "Recarbonation after softening",
q: `The purpose of re-carbonation after water softening by the lime-soda process is the`,
opts: ["Removal of excess soda from the water", "Removal of non-carbonate hardness in the water", "Recovery of lime from the water", "Conversion of precipitates to soluble forms in the water"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) conversion of precipitates to soluble forms</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> After lime–soda softening the water is supersaturated with CaCO₃ and is highly alkaline (pH 10–11). Bubbling CO₂ (recarbonation) lowers the pH and converts the fine carbonate precipitates to soluble bicarbonates, so that they do not deposit on filter sand and pipes.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Soda is added to remove non-carbonate hardness; recarbonation does not remove excess soda.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Non-carbonate hardness is removed by the soda, not by CO₂.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Lime is not recovered by CO₂ in the water treatment plant.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Recarbonation: lowers pH, dissolves fine CaCO₃ scale.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-98", year: 2018, no: 98, subject: "hydro", topic: "Environmental flow",
q: `Environmental flow of a river refers to the quantity, quality and timing of the flow`,
opts: ["Required in the river to sustain the river ecosystem", "Required to maintain healthy ecological conditions in the command area of a river development project", "Generated by the ecosystem of the catchment of the river", "As the minimum requirement to support the cultural practices of the community living on the banks of the river"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) required in the river to sustain the river ecosystem</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Environmental flow (e-flow) is the quantity, quality and timing of water flow required to sustain the freshwater and estuarine ecosystems and the human livelihoods and well-being that depend on them. It is measured in the river itself.</p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> The command area of a project is a different matter (irrigated lands).</li><li><b>(c)</b> E-flow is not generated by the catchment ecosystem; it is a requirement.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Cultural needs are a part of the broader concern but not the definition.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> E-flow: what the river needs to stay healthy.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-99", year: 2018, no: 99, subject: "env", topic: "Moisture content of municipal solid waste",
q: `The moisture content of a certain Municipal Solid Waste with the following composition (wet % by weight / dry % by weight): Food waste 10/3, Paper 35/30, Yard waste 20/10, Others 35/20 will be`,
opts: ["100%", "63%", "37%", "13%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 37%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The table gives each component's mass as % of the wet sample (wet weight) and the dry mass of the same sample.</p><div class="formula">Moisture % = (wet − dry)/wet × 100</div><ol class="work"><li>Total wet mass = 10 + 35 + 20 + 35 = 100 (units).</li><li>Total dry mass = 3 + 30 + 10 + 20 = 63.</li><li>Moisture = (100 − 63)/100 = 37 %.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 100 % would mean no solids.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 63 % is the dry fraction.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 13 % does not satisfy the relation used above.</li></ul><p>The moisture content of municipal solid waste is about 15–40 % in India and so high that the heating value is low; the calorific value of waste with 37 % moisture is typically 800 to 1500 kcal/kg. Food and yard wastes hold the most moisture; paper holds the least here. The moisture content is determined by drying a sample at 105 °C for 24 hours.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Moisture % = (wet − dry)/wet × 100.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-100", year: 2018, no: 100, subject: "geo", topic: "Shrinkage limit and Atterberg limits",
q: `Consider the following statements:<ol><li>When a soil sample is dried beyond its shrinkage limit, the volume of the soil slowly decreases</li><li>Plastic limit is always lower than the liquid limit for any type of soil</li><li>At the liquid limit, the soil behaves like a liquid and possesses no shear strength at all</li><li>When subjected to drying, the volume of the soil remains unchanged once the water content of the soil goes below its shrinkage limit</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Atterberg limits mark the water contents at which a soil changes state: liquid limit &gt; plastic limit &gt; shrinkage limit.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: below the shrinkage limit the volume does not change any more.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the plastic limit is lower than the liquid limit (the plasticity index is positive); in a non-plastic soil both are reported as NP.</li><li><b>3</b> False: at the liquid limit the soil has a small but definite shear strength (about 2.5 kPa), it is not zero.</li><li><b>4</b> True: the shrinkage limit is the water content below which no further volume reduction occurs.</li></ul><p>So 2 and 4 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Below the shrinkage limit: no more volume change.</p>` }
);

