window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2018-101", year: 2018, no: 101, subject: "env", topic: "The troposphere",
q: `Consider the following statements in respect of the troposphere:<ol><li>The gaseous content constantly churns by turbulence and mixing</li><li>Its behaviour makes the weather</li><li>The ultimate energy source for producing any weather change is the sun</li><li>The height of the troposphere is nearly 11 km at the equatorial belt and is 5 km at the poles</li></ol>Which of these are true of the troposphere?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The troposphere is the lowest layer of the atmosphere, containing most of its mass and water vapour.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: heating from the ground causes convection, turbulence and mixing.</li><li><b>2</b> True: clouds, rain, wind and storms all occur in it.</li><li><b>3</b> True: all weather changes are driven by solar energy.</li><li><b>4</b> False as worded: the troposphere is thickest at the equator (about 16–18 km) and thinnest at the poles (about 8 km); the 11 km and 5 km figures do not fit.</li></ul><p>So 1, 2 and 3 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Troposphere: ~17 km at the equator, ~8 km at the poles.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-102", year: 2018, no: 102, subject: "geo", topic: "Degree of saturation",
q: `A sand sample has a porosity of 30% and specific gravity of solids as 2·6. What is its degree of saturation at moisture content of 4·94%?`,
opts: ["40%", "35%", "30%", "25%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 30%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Degree of saturation.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> S e = w G</div><ol class="work"><li>e = n/(1 − n) = 0.30/0.70 = 0.4286.</li><li>S = wG/e = 0.0494 × 2.6/0.4286 = 0.2997 ≈ 30 %.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 40 % would need w = 6.6 %.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 35 % would need w = 5.8 %.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 25 % would need w = 4.1 %.</li></ul><p>In terms of the three-phase diagram, taking V = 1 m³ the volume of voids is 0.3 m³ and of solids 0.7 m³; the mass of solids is 0.7 × 2.6 = 1.82 t, water mass = 0.0494 × 1.82 = 0.09 t = 0.09 m³ filling 30 % of the voids. A soil with S below 25 % is described as dry or damp, between 25 and 50 % as moist.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> S e = w G.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-103", year: 2018, no: 103, subject: "geo", topic: "Unit weight of saturated soil",
q: `What will be the unit weight of a fully saturated soil sample having water content of 38% and grain specific gravity of 2·65?`,
opts: ["19·88 kN/m³", "17·88 kN/m³", "16·52 kN/m³", "14·65 kN/m³"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 17·88 kN/m³</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Unit weight of saturated soil.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formulae:</b> for S = 1, e = wG; γ<sub>sat</sub> = (G + e)γ<sub>w</sub>/(1 + e)</div><ol class="work"><li>e = 0.38 × 2.65 = 1.007.</li><li>γ<sub>sat</sub> = (2.65 + 1.007) × 9.81/2.007 = 17.88 kN/m³.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 19.88 kN/m³ would need a void ratio of 0.79.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 16.52 kN/m³ would need a void ratio of 1.3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 14.65 kN/m³ would need a void ratio of 1.7.</li></ul><p>The same result follows from the three-phase diagram: for 1 g of solids the water is 0.38 g (0.38 cm³) and the solids volume is 1/2.65 = 0.377 cm³, so the total is 0.757 cm³ for a mass of 1.38 g, a density of 1.823 g/cm³ = 17.88 kN/m³. Saturated unit weights of soils lie between 16 and 22 kN/m³.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> γsat = (G + e)γw/(1 + e), e = wG.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-104", year: 2018, no: 104, subject: "geo", topic: "Volume of soil at a new void ratio",
q: `How many cubic metres of soil having void ratio of 0·7 can be made from 30 m³ of soil with void ratio of 1·2?`,
opts: ["36·6 m³", "30·0 m³", "25·9 m³", "23·2 m³"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 23·2 m³</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The volume of solids stays constant: V<sub>s</sub> = V/(1 + e).</p><div class="formula">V_s = V/(1 + e) constant</div><ol class="work"><li>V<sub>s</sub> = 30/2.2 = 13.64 m³.</li><li>New volume = 13.64 × (1 + 0.7) = 23.2 m³.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 36.6 m³ would be the volume at a higher void ratio of 1.7... (a larger volume, impossible when compacting).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 30 m³ would mean no change of void ratio.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 25.9 m³ does not satisfy the relation used above.</li></ul><p>The soil is compacted or excavated and filled with a change of volume only in the voids. In terms of the change in void ratio, ΔV/V₁ = Δe/(1 + e₁) = (1.2 − 0.7)/2.2 = 22.7 %, so the volume falls by 6.8 m³ from 30 to 23.2 m³. This type of calculation is used in estimating borrow-pit quantities and fill volumes.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Volume of solids is constant.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-105", year: 2018, no: 105, subject: "geo", topic: "Friction angle from a triaxial test",
q: `A dry sand specimen is put through a tri-axial test. The cell pressure is 50 kPa and the deviator stress at failure is 100 kPa. The angle of internal friction for the sand specimen is`,
opts: ["15°", "30°", "45°", "55°"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 30°</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a dry cohesionless sand, c = 0 and σ₁ = σ₃ + σ<sub>d</sub> = 50 + 100 = 150 kPa.</p><div class="formula">sin φ = (σ₁ − σ₃)/(σ₁ + σ₃) (c = 0)</div><ol class="work"><li>sin φ = (σ₁ − σ₃)/(σ₁ + σ₃) = 100/200 = 0.5.</li><li>φ = 30°.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 15° would give sin φ = 0.26.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 45° would give sin φ = 0.71.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 55° would give sin φ = 0.82.</li></ul><p>The same result follows from the Mohr–Coulomb envelope for a cohesionless soil: the circle with σ₃ = 50 and σ₁ = 150 kPa has a radius of 50 and a centre at 100, and the envelope τ = σ tan φ is tangent to it, giving sin φ = 50/100 = 0.5. The failure plane is at 45° + φ/2 = 60° to the major principal plane.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> sin φ = (σ₁ − σ₃)/(σ₁ + σ₃).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-106", year: 2018, no: 106, subject: "geo", topic: "Assumptions in consolidation theory",
q: `The theory of consolidation predicts settlement due to primary consolidation; it cannot include settlement due to initial compression nor due to secondary consolidation. This happens because of the following assumptions made in developing the theory:<ol><li>Soil grains and water are incompressible</li><li>Soil is fully saturated</li><li>Compression takes place in the vertical direction only</li><li>Time lag in consolidation is entirely due to low permeability of soil</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "3 and 4 only", "1, 2, 3 and 4"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Terzaghi's theory is built on these assumptions:</p><ul><li><b>1</b> The grains and the water are incompressible, so a volume change equals the volume of water squeezed out, and no initial compression occurs.</li><li><b>2</b> The soil is fully saturated, so there is no air to compress.</li><li><b>3</b> Flow and compression are one-dimensional (vertical).</li><li><b>4</b> The time lag comes only from the low permeability, so there is no secondary (creep) compression.</li></ul><p>All four are correct.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Terzaghi: saturated, incompressible phases, 1-D, hydrodynamic lag only.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-107", year: 2018, no: 107, subject: "geo", topic: "Consolidation of soils",
q: `Consider the following statements:<ol><li>Secondary consolidation results due to prolonged dissipation of excess hydrostatic pressure</li><li>Primary consolidation happens under expulsion of both air and water from voids in early stages</li><li>Initial consolidation in the case of fully saturated soils is mainly due to compression of solid particles</li><li>Primary consolidation happens more quickly in coarse-grained soils than in fine-grained soils</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Consolidation has three parts: initial (immediate), primary and secondary.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: secondary consolidation occurs after the excess pore pressure has dissipated, by plastic readjustment of the particles (creep).</li><li><b>2</b> False as a statement on primary consolidation of a saturated soil: only water is expelled; the expulsion of air belongs to partly saturated soils.</li><li><b>3</b> Accepted: in a fully saturated soil the initial compression is the small elastic compression of the solid particles and pore water (and no air).</li><li><b>4</b> True: coarse soils are much more permeable, so they consolidate quickly.</li></ul><p>So 3 and 4 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Secondary = creep after pore pressure has gone.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-108", year: 2018, no: 108, subject: "geo", topic: "Mohr's circle and soil testing",
q: `Consider the following statements with regard to Soil Testing:<ol><li>The origin and pole are at the same point in a Mohr's circle</li><li>The shear stress is maximum on the failure plane</li><li>Mohr's circle drawn with data from an unconfined compression test passes through the origin</li><li>Maximum shear stress occurs on a plane inclined at 45° to the principal plane</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Mohr's circle is the stress-circle of a point:</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: the origin of the σ–τ axes and the pole (the origin of planes) are different points; the pole is found by drawing a line parallel to a known plane.</li><li><b>2</b> False: the maximum shear stress acts on planes at 45° to the principal planes; the failure plane is inclined at 45° + φ/2 and carries a smaller shear stress than that.</li><li><b>3</b> True: in an unconfined test σ₃ = 0, so the circle passes through the origin.</li><li><b>4</b> True: the maximum shear stress is on planes at 45° to the principal planes.</li></ul><p>So 3 and 4 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pole ≠ origin; τmax at 45°; failure plane at 45° + φ/2.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-109", year: 2018, no: 109, subject: "geo", topic: "Degree of saturation at maximum dry density",
q: `A soil yielded a maximum dry unit weight of 18 kN/m³ at a moisture content of 16% during a Standard Proctor Test. What is the degree of saturation of the soil if its specific gravity is 2·65?`,
opts: ["98·42%", "95·50%", "84·32%", "75·71%"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 95·50%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Degree of saturation at maximum dry density.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formulae:</b> γ<sub>d</sub> = Gγ<sub>w</sub>/(1 + e);</div><div class="formula">S e = wG</div><ol class="work"><li>1 + e = 2.65 × 9.81/18 = 1.444 → e = 0.444.</li><li>S = wG/e = 0.16 × 2.65/0.444 = 0.955 = 95.5 %.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 98.42 % would need e = 0.431.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 84.32 % would need e = 0.503.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 75.71 % would need e = 0.56.</li></ul><p>The value S = 95.5 % is slightly below the zero-air-voids line: at maximum dry density in the Proctor test, a soil compacted at optimum moisture is 85 to 95 % saturated, never 100 %, because some air remains in the voids. The air-voids percentage here is (1 − 0.955) × e/(1 + e) = 3.1 %.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> e from γd, then S = wG/e.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-110", year: 2018, no: 110, subject: "geo", topic: "Assumptions of Coulomb's wedge theory",
q: `Consider the following assumptions regarding Coulomb's Wedge Theory:<ol><li>There is equilibrium of every element within the soil mass of the material</li><li>There is equilibrium of the whole of the material</li><li>Backfill is wet, cohesive, and ideally elastic</li><li>The wall surface is rough</li></ol>Which of the above assumptions are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Coulomb's theory considers the equilibrium of the failing wedge as a whole, not of every element, and allows friction on the wall.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: only the wedge as a whole is in equilibrium (the Rankine theory considers every element).</li><li><b>2</b> True: the equilibrium of the whole wedge gives the earth pressure.</li><li><b>3</b> False: the backfill is dry, cohesionless, homogeneous and isotropic.</li><li><b>4</b> True: the wall face is rough, so wall friction δ acts and the resultant is inclined to the normal.</li></ul><p>So 2 and 4 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Coulomb: whole wedge, rough wall, dry cohesionless fill.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-111", year: 2018, no: 111, subject: "geo", topic: "Depth of a vertical cut in clay",
q: `In a clayey soil having 50 kN/m² as unit cohesion and 18 kN/m³ as unit weight, an excavation is made with a vertical face. Taking Taylor's stability number as 0·261, what is the maximum depth of excavation so that the vertical face remains stable?`,
opts: ["5·30 m", "7·06 m", "10·6 m", "12·4 m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 10·6 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Depth of a vertical cut in clay.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> stability number s<sub>n</sub> = c/(γH<sub>c</sub>), so H<sub>c</sub> = c/(γ s<sub>n</sub>)</div><ol class="work"><li>H<sub>c</sub> = 50/(18 × 0.261) = 50/4.698 = 10.64 m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 5.30 m is half the answer (a common slip of using 2c).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 7.06 m does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 12.4 m would need s<sub>n</sub> = 0.224.</li></ul><p>For a vertical cut in cohesive soil the unsupported depth is also commonly taken as 4c/γ = 11.1 m (Rankine, with tension crack depth 2c/γ = 5.6 m). The stability number 0.261 is the Taylor value for a slope angle of 90°, the lowest of the stability numbers, which means a vertical face is the least stable slope.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> H_c = c/(γ s_n).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-112", year: 2018, no: 112, subject: "geo", topic: "Boussinesq's vertical stress",
q: `What is the Boussinesq's vertical stress at a point 6 m directly below a concentrated load of 2000 kN applied at the ground surface?`,
opts: ["53·1 kN/m²", "26·5 kN/m²", "11·8 kN/m²", "8·8 kN/m²"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 26·5 kN/m²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Boussinesq's vertical stress.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> directly below the load (r = 0), σ<sub>z</sub> = 3Q/(2πz²)</div><ol class="work"><li>σ<sub>z</sub> = 3 × 2000/(2π × 36) = 6000/226.2 = 26.5 kN/m².</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 53.1 kN/m² is twice the right value (using 3Q/πz²).</li><li><b>(c)</b> 11.8 kN/m² would result from using Q/(2πz²) × a wrong factor.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 8.8 kN/m² would result from Q/(2πz²) × 2.</li></ul><p>Boussinesq's influence factor at r = 0 is 3/2π = 0.4775, so σ = 0.4775 × 2000/36 = 26.5 kN/m². The stress falls with the square of the depth: at 12 m it would be 6.6 kN/m². The value is for a point load on an elastic half-space, and a real footing spreads the load, which lowers the stress near the surface.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Under the load: σ = 0.4775 Q/z².</p>` },

{ id: "2018-113", year: 2018, no: 113, subject: "rcc", topic: "Internal couple in RC and prestressed members",
q: `Consider the following statements:<ol><li>In a reinforced concrete member subjected to flexure, the externally applied moment is resisted by an internal couple formed by steel and concrete and their magnitudes vary with the applied moment, while the lever arm of the internal couple remains constant</li><li>In a prestressed concrete member, the external moment is resisted by an internal couple, but it is the lever arm that changes with the loading conditions and the stress in steel remains practically constant</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) both 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The two members resist the moment differently.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: in a cracked RC section the lever arm is roughly constant, and the forces in steel and concrete change with the moment.</li><li><b>2</b> True: in a prestressed member the compressive and tensile forces stay nearly constant (the prestress), and the centre of pressure moves, so the lever arm changes with the moment.</li></ul><p>Both are correct, so (c).</p><p>For a reinforced concrete beam the lever arm is about 0.9 of the effective depth in the working range, which is why the steel area is found from M/(σst × j × d). In a prestressed beam the lever arm increases with the moment from nearly zero at transfer, since the pressure line shifts with the load; this is why prestress is used for large spans.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> RC: forces vary, lever arm constant. PSC: forces constant, lever arm varies.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-114", year: 2018, no: 114, subject: "survey", topic: "GPS",
q: `Consider the following statements with regard to Global Positioning Systems (GPS):<ol><li>The position of an object can be exactly determined by a single satellite</li><li>The position of the observer (moving person or vehicle) on ground is determined by an orbiting satellite</li><li>Atomic clocks are fixed in satellites to calculate the positioning of the satellite to aid in determining travel times</li><li>Absolute positioning, where accuracy of 1 cm to 5 cm is needed, depends upon the health of the satellite</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 2, 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> GPS position comes from distances to several satellites.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: at least four satellites are needed (three coordinates and the receiver clock error).</li><li><b>2</b> True: the position of a moving observer is determined from signals of the orbiting satellites.</li><li><b>3</b> True: atomic clocks on the satellites give the signal travel times.</li><li><b>4</b> True: high-accuracy positioning depends on the health of the satellites and on their signal quality.</li></ul><p>So 2, 3 and 4 only.</p><p>GPS gives positions from signals of at least four satellites: each gives a range from the signal time, and three ranges fix the position while the fourth removes the receiver clock error. Accuracy of a few metres is obtained with a single receiver (absolute positioning), and of centimetres by differential or real-time kinematic methods with a base station.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> GPS needs four satellites for a fix.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-115", year: 2018, no: 115, subject: "survey", topic: "Levelling with an inverted staff",
q: `A temporary bench mark has been established at the soffit of a chejja on a window opening, and its known elevation is 102·405 m above mean sea level. The backsight used to establish the height of the instrument is by an inverted staff reading of 1·80 m. A foresight reading, held normally, is 1·215 m on a recently constructed plinth. The elevation of the plinth is`,
opts: ["95·42 m O.D", "99·39 m O.D", "102·42 m O.D", "105·99 m O.D"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 99·39 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> An inverted staff is held with its foot against the soffit and read downward from the point, so the line of sight lies <b>below</b> the soffit by the staff reading.</p><div class="formula">RL = HI + inverted staff reading</div><ol class="work"><li>Height of instrument = 102.405 − 1.80 = 100.605 m.</li><li>Elevation of the plinth = H.I. − foresight = 100.605 − 1.215 = 99.39 m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 95.42 m results from subtracting 1.80 + ... incorrectly.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 102.42 m ignores the staff readings or treats them as a combined zero.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 105.99 m results from adding the inverted reading 1.80 and 1.215 to the bench mark.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Inverted staff: H.I. = RL of the point − reading.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-116", year: 2018, no: 116, subject: "transport", topic: "Length of a transition curve",
q: `A transition curve is to be provided for a circular railway curve of 300 m radius, the gauge being 1·5 m with the maximum superelevation restricted to 15 cm. What is the length of the transition curve for balancing the centrifugal force?`,
opts: ["72·3 m", "78·1 m", "84·2 m", "88·3 m"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 84·2 m</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Method:</b> find the equilibrium speed for the cant, then L from the rate of change of radial acceleration α (taken as 0.2 m/s³ here).</p><div class="formula">e = GV²/(127R)</div><div class="formula">L = V³/(αR)</div><ol class="work"><li>e = GV²/(127R) → V² = 150 × 127 × 300/1500 = 3810 → V = 61.7 km/h = 17.1 m/s.</li><li>L = V³/(αR) = 17.1³/(0.2 × 300) = 5010/60 = 84 m.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 72.3 m would need α = 0.23 m/s³.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 78.1 m would need α = 0.21 m/s³.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 88.3 m would need α = 0.19 m/s³.</li></ul><p>In Indian railway practice the length of the transition is taken as the largest of the values from the cant gradient (1 in 720), the rate of cant deficiency and the rate of change of radial acceleration. The key uses the radial acceleration rate of about 0.2 m/s³ with the speed balanced by the 15 cm cant, which gives the 84 m.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> L = V³/(αR) with α ≈ 0.2 m/s³ for balance.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-117", year: 2018, no: 117, subject: "survey", topic: "Remote sensing",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding Remote Sensing Survey:<ol><li>Information transfer is accomplished by use of electromagnetic radiation</li><li>Remote Sensing from space is done by satellites</li><li>Remote Sensing has no application in Earthquake prediction</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Remote sensing gathers information about an object without touching it.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: sensors record reflected or emitted electromagnetic radiation.</li><li><b>2</b> True: satellites such as the IRS and Landsat series provide data from space.</li><li><b>3</b> False: remote sensing is used for earthquake studies (mapping of faults and lineaments, ground deformation by InSAR and thermal anomalies), although it cannot predict individual earthquakes.</li></ul><p>So 1 and 2 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Remote sensing: EM radiation; satellites; used in hazard studies.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-118", year: 2018, no: 118, subject: "transport", topic: "Equilibrium superelevation",
q: `The rate of equilibrium superelevation on a road is<ol><li>Directly proportional to the square of vehicle velocity</li><li>Inversely proportional to the radius of the horizontal curve</li><li>Directly proportional to the square of the radius of the horizontal curve</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For equilibrium (centrifugal force balanced by the superelevation alone) e = V²/(gR).</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: e ∝ V².</li><li><b>2</b> True: e ∝ 1/R.</li><li><b>3</b> False: the superelevation falls as the radius increases; it is not proportional to R².</li></ul><p>So 1 and 2 only, option (a).</p><p>The usual superelevation on roads is e = V²/(225R) for V in km/h and R in m (e = V²/127R in m/m); IRC limits the maximum to 7 % and uses 75 % of the design speed for the design superelevation. For a curve of 300 m radius at 80 km/h, V²/(127R) = 0.168 and 0.75 V gives a lower superelevation of 0.094.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> e = V²/(gR) = V²/(127R) with V in km/h.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-119", year: 2018, no: 119, subject: "transport", topic: "Fatigue life of flexible pavements (IRC 37)",
q: `As per IRC 37 : 2012, the fatigue life of a flexible pavement consisting of granular base and sub-base depends upon<ol><li>Resilient Modulus of bituminous layers</li><li>Horizontal tensile strain at the bottom of bituminous layer</li><li>Mix design of bitumen</li><li>Vertical subgrade strain</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> IRC 37:2012 fatigue criterion: N<sub>f</sub> = 2.21 × 10⁻² × C × (1/ε<sub>t</sub>)^3.89 (1/M<sub>R</sub>)^0.854, where C depends on the volume of effective bitumen and air voids (mix design).</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Yes: the resilient modulus M<sub>R</sub> of the bituminous layer enters the equation.</li><li><b>2</b> Yes: the horizontal tensile strain ε<sub>t</sub> at the bottom of the bituminous layer is the main variable.</li><li><b>3</b> Yes: the mix design (volume of bitumen and air voids) enters through the factor C.</li><li><b>4</b> No: the vertical subgrade strain controls the rutting life, not the fatigue life.</li></ul><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Fatigue: tensile strain at the bottom of the bituminous layer; rutting: subgrade strain.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-120", year: 2018, no: 120, subject: "transport", topic: "Steel for metro and mono rails",
q: `Which one of the following types of steel is used in the manufacturing of metro and mono rails?`,
opts: ["Mild steel", "Cast steel", "Manganese steel", "Bessemer steel"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) manganese steel</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Rails for metro and mono-rail systems need high strength, hardness and wear resistance. Manganese steel (and head-hardened high-carbon steel) is used, as it work-hardens under the wheel load.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Mild steel is too soft and wears out quickly under wheel loads.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Cast steel is brittle and not suitable for rails.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Bessemer steel was used for early rails but is inferior in quality and not used for modern rails.</li></ul><p>The head of the rail is subjected to high contact stress, so the rail steel is rolled from high-carbon manganese steel with 0.6–0.8 % carbon and 0.8–1.3 % manganese, and the head is hardened for wear resistance. Rails for metro and monorail use special rolled sections or girder rails; 'manganese steel' is also used for points and crossings.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Rails need wear resistance: manganese steel.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-121", year: 2018, no: 121, subject: "geo", topic: "Void ratio by the paraffin method",
q: `A sample of dry soil is coated with a thin layer of paraffin and has a mass of 460 g. It displaced 300 cc of water when immersed in it. The paraffin is peeled off and its mass was found to be 9 g. If the specific gravity of soil solids and paraffin are 2·65 and 0·9 respectively, the voids ratio of soil is nearly`,
opts: ["0·92", "0·71", "0·59", "0·48"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 0·71</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Method:</b> remove the volume of paraffin from the displaced volume to get the volume of the soil; the dry mass gives the solid volume.</p><div class="formula">V_soil = V_displaced − V_paraffin</div><div class="formula">e = V_v/V_s</div><ol class="work"><li>Mass of soil = 460 − 9 = 451 g; volume of paraffin = 9/0.9 = 10 cc.</li><li>Total volume of soil V = 300 − 10 = 290 cc.</li><li>Volume of solids V<sub>s</sub> = 451/2.65 = 170.2 cc; V<sub>v</sub> = 290 − 170.2 = 119.8 cc.</li><li>e = 119.8/170.2 = 0.70 ≈ 0.71.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 0.92 would result from not subtracting the paraffin mass from the soil mass.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 0.59 would result from a total volume of 270 cc.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 0.48 would result from a total volume of 252 cc.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Paraffin method: subtract the paraffin volume from the displacement.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-122", year: 2018, no: 122, subject: "transport", topic: "Marshalling yard",
q: `Marshalling yard in railway system provides facilities for`,
opts: ["Maintenance of rolling stock", "Safe movements of passengers and coaches", "Receiving, breaking up, re-forming and dispatching onwards – of trains", "Receiving, loading, unloading and delivery of goods and vehicles, and scheduling their further functioning"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) receiving, breaking up, re-forming and dispatching trains</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A marshalling yard is where goods trains are received, broken up, sorted according to destination, re-formed into new trains and dispatched onwards.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Maintenance of rolling stock is done in workshops and sheds.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Passenger yards deal with the movement of passengers and coaches.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Goods yards (goods sheds) deal with loading, unloading and delivery of goods.</li></ul><p>The yard has reception lines, sorting sidings, departure lines, hump or flat shunting arrangements and a control tower. In a hump yard the wagons are pushed over a rise and roll by gravity to their sorting siding. Marshalling yards are located at junctions and at the terminals of large railway systems, and are different from the goods yards where the goods are loaded and delivered.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Marshalling yard: sorting and re-forming of goods trains.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-123", year: 2018, no: 123, subject: "transport", topic: "Composite Sleeper Index",
q: `'Composite Sleeper Index' is relevant in determining:<ol><li>Required and adoptable sleeper density</li><li>Durability of sleeper units</li><li>Mechanical strength of the stock of wooden sleepers</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 only", "3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The Composite Sleeper Index (CSI) is a number obtained from the mechanical properties of a timber (such as its strength and hardness) that is used to judge the suitability of a wood species for sleepers; it relates to the mechanical strength of the wooden sleepers.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Not determined by the CSI: sleeper density depends on the traffic and track design.</li><li><b>2</b> Not determined by the CSI: durability depends on the treatment and species.</li><li><b>3</b> Yes: the CSI classifies the mechanical strength of wooden sleepers.</li></ul><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> CSI: strength classification of timber for sleepers.</p>` },

{ id: "2018-124", year: 2018, no: 124, subject: "transport", topic: "Webster's optimum cycle time",
q: `The normal flows on two approach roads at an intersection are respectively 500 pcu/h and 300 pcu/h. The corresponding saturation flow is 1600 pcu/h on each road. The total lost time per single cycle is 16 s. The optimum cycle time by Webster's method is`,
opts: ["72·5 s", "58·0 s", "48·0 s", "19·3 s"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 58·0 s</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Webster:</b> C₀ = (1.5L + 5)/(1 − Y), with Y the sum of the critical flow ratios of the phases.</p><div class="formula">C₀ = (1.5L + 5)/(1 − Y)</div><ol class="work"><li>y₁ = 500/1600 = 0.3125; y₂ = 300/1600 = 0.1875; Y = 0.5.</li><li>C₀ = (1.5 × 16 + 5)/(1 − 0.5) = 29/0.5 = 58 s.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 72.5 s would need Y = 0.6.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 48 s would need Y = 0.40.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 19.3 s is not a cycle time (it is shorter than the lost time plus minimum greens).</li></ul><p>The cycle time is then divided among the phases in proportion to the critical flow ratios: green times g₁ = (58 − 16) × 0.3125/0.5 = 26.25 s and g₂ = 42 × 0.1875/0.5 = 15.75 s. The delay is smallest near Webster's cycle time, and the formula holds for undersaturated conditions Y &lt; 1; the cycle is limited to 120 s.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> C₀ = (1.5L + 5)/(1 − Y).</p>` },

{ id: "2018-125", year: 2018, no: 125, subject: "transport", topic: "Velocity of waves",
q: `In the offshore region at a particular harbour facility, an oscillatory wave train approaches with wavelength of 80 m where the mean sea depth is 30 m. What would be the velocity of the individual waves?`,
opts: ["17·15 m/s", "16·05 m/s", "15·15 m/s", "14·05 m/s"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 17·15 m/s (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The key gives 17.15 m/s, which equals √(gd) = √(9.81 × 30) = 17.15 m/s, the shallow-water wave speed.</p><div class="formula">Shallow water: c = √(gd)</div><p>The general (Airy) formula is c = √[(gλ/2π) tanh(2πd/λ)]. With λ = 80 m, d = 30 m: tanh(2.356) = 0.982 and c = √(124.9 × 0.982) = 11.1 m/s, which is not among the options.</p><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 16.05 m/s does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15.15 m/s does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 14.05 m/s does not satisfy the relation used above.</li></ul><p><b>Solution:</b></p><ol class="work"><li>c = √(9.81 × 30) = <b>17.15 m/s</b> (key's value).</li></ol><p><b>Remember:</b> Wave speed: shallow √(gd); general Airy formula with tanh.</p>` }
);

