// ESE 2017 Prelims Paper II, Civil Engineering, Series A (MKA-S-CEN), Q51–75
// Answers follow the official UPSC key (Series A; Q22 dropped), checked against our own solution made before reading it.
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS = window.PYQ_QUESTIONS || [];
window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-51", year: 2017, no: 51, subject: "rcc", topic: "IS 456 provisions",
q: `Consider the following statements in the light of IS : 456 - 2000:<ol><li>There is an upper limit on the nominal shear stress in beams (even with shear reinforcement) due to the possibility of crushing of concrete in diagonal compression.</li><li>A rectangular concrete slab whose length is equal to its width may not be a two-way slab for certain definable support conditions.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) both 1 and 2</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Both statements follow IS 456.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: τ<sub>v</sub> must not exceed τ<sub>c,max</sub> (e.g. 2.8 N/mm² for M 20 to 4 N/mm² for M 40), otherwise the section has to be enlarged since the diagonal compression struts would crush.</li><li><b>2</b> True: a square slab supported on only two opposite edges spans in one direction only (one-way action).</li></ul><p>Both are correct: (c).</p><p>The maximum shear stress limits for beams in IS 456 (Table 20) depend on the concrete grade: 1.6 for M 15 up to 4.0 N/mm² for M 40 and above. The nominal shear stress τ<sub>v</sub> = V<sub>u</sub>/bd is compared with these, and the beam section is increased if it is exceeded.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> τ_v ≤ τ_c,max; two-way action needs support on four edges.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-52", year: 2017, no: 52, subject: "rcc", topic: "Anchorage slip loss in prestressing",
q: `A simply supported prestressed concrete beam is of 25 m span. The initial stress is 1000 MPa. The slip in the jack during tensioning has been 2 mm. If E<sub>s</sub> = 200 GPa, the loss of prestress due to anchorage slip is`,
opts: ["16%", "12%", "10%", "1·6%"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1·6%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Anchorage slip loss in prestressing.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> loss of stress = (Δ/L)E<sub>s</sub></div><ol class="work"><li>Δ/L = 2/25 000 = 8 × 10⁻⁵.</li><li>Loss = 8 × 10⁻⁵ × 2 × 10⁵ = 16 MPa.</li><li>Percentage = 16/1000 = 1.6 %.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 16 % confuses the 16 MPa with a percentage.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 12 % does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 10 % does not satisfy the relation used above.</li></ul><p>In practice the slip loss is greater for short members, because the same slip is a larger fraction of a shorter length. For a 25 m beam it is small, 1.6 %, which is why the loss from anchorage slip is called out only for short beams and post-tensioned cables.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Slip loss = Δ E_s/L.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-53", year: 2017, no: 53, subject: "rcc", topic: "Tall buildings under earthquake",
q: `Which of the following measures are relevantly considered for earthquake loading and lateral stability of tall buildings?<ol><li>Minimizing gravity loads</li><li>Adding masses at floor levels</li><li>Ensuring ductility at the locations of maximum moments</li><li>Providing shear walls</li><li>Providing basement</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 5 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "3, 4 and 5 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Earthquake forces are proportional to the mass, so the building should be light, ductile and stiff against lateral loads.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Yes: less weight means smaller seismic forces.</li><li><b>2</b> No: adding mass raises the earthquake force.</li><li><b>3</b> Yes: ductile detailing lets the building dissipate energy.</li><li><b>4</b> Yes: shear walls give lateral stiffness.</li><li><b>5</b> Not counted by the key: a basement is not a measure specific to lateral stability, although it can raise stability against overturning.</li></ul><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, 5, which are incorrect; Omits 3, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 5, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Seismic design: light, ductile, stiff.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-54", year: 2017, no: 54, subject: "rcc", topic: "Torsion in a building from eccentric lateral force",
q: `The figure shows the plan view of a single-storey masonry shear wall building with rigid roof diaphragm. The traces of locations of the centre of mass (CM) and the centre of stiffness (CS) are as shown. Neglecting accidental eccentricity, the direction of the twisting moment on the diaphragm, for the lateral force direction shown, is<img class="fig" src="img/ce/2017-54.png" alt="Figure for question 54">`,
opts: ["Clockwise", "Anticlockwise", "Opposite to the direction of lateral force", "None of the above"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) clockwise</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The lateral force acts through the centre of mass (CM, above the CS in the plan) and the reaction acts through the centre of stiffness (CS). The force V acts to the right at a level above the CS, so its moment about the CS is the force times the offset.</p><ol class="work"><li>Take the CS as origin: CM is above it. A rightward force at a point above the origin gives a moment M = x·F<sub>y</sub> − y·F<sub>x</sub> = −y V (negative).</li><li>Negative moment: clockwise.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> Anticlockwise would result if the CM lay below the CS.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A twisting moment is a rotation sense; it cannot be "opposite to the lateral force".</li><li><b>(d)</b> There is a definite sense for the twist.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Moment of V about the CS: r × F.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-55", year: 2017, no: 55, subject: "cpm", topic: "Non-critical activity",
q: `Which of the following statements is/are correct for a non-critical activity?<ol><li>It demands very special attention and action.</li><li>One can do with normal attention to this activity with some leeway for action.</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A non-critical activity has float, so a delay up to its float does not delay the project.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: special attention is needed for critical activities, which have zero float.</li><li><b>2</b> True: with float available, normal attention is enough and there is some leeway in time.</li></ul><p>Floats are what give the manager leeway: the total float of a non-critical activity is the delay it can suffer without delaying the project, so effort is concentrated first on the critical activities, which have zero float, and the others are scheduled with the resources left.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Critical: zero float. Non-critical: has float.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-56", year: 2017, no: 56, subject: "cpm", topic: "Tendering",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding tendering for a civil work:<ol><li>Earnest money deposit (EMD) is a prerequisite to tender for a work.</li><li>It is not essential to call for the contractor's credentials when a tender is invited.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Tenders are invited from eligible contractors who deposit earnest money.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: EMD (usually 1–2 % of the estimated cost) must accompany the tender; it shows seriousness and is forfeited if the bidder withdraws.</li><li><b>2</b> False: the contractor’s credentials (experience, turnover, equipment, past performance) are called for in order to prequalify the bidders.</li></ul><p>The EMD is returned to unsuccessful bidders and adjusted against the security deposit of the successful one. The tender documents also call for the credentials of the bidder (experience, financial standing, equipment), by which an unsuitable bidder is screened out before the bids are opened.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> EMD with the tender; credentials for pre-qualification.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-57", year: 2017, no: 57, subject: "cpm", topic: "Quality control", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding quality control:<ol><li>It refers to absolute conformity to specifications.</li><li>It may not vouch against overspending.</li><li>It may unknowingly resort to overdesign in the hope of risk minimization.</li><li>It is intended to reduce maintenance costs.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Quality control checks that the work meets the specification and reduces later repair costs.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: its aim is conformity of the work to the specifications.</li><li><b>2</b> Not accepted by the key; overspending is a matter of cost control, not of quality control, but the key does not count it here.</li><li><b>3</b> Not accepted by the key: overdesign is a design matter.</li><li><b>4</b> True: a good-quality job needs less maintenance.</li></ul><p>So 1 and 4 only, (d).</p><p>Quality control in construction is the set of measures taken to see that materials and workmanship conform to the specifications. Its cost is more than repaid by the lower cost of repairs and maintenance, whereas cost control and design economy are separate management functions.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> QC = conformity to specification, lower maintenance.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-58", year: 2017, no: 58, subject: "fluid", topic: "Dimensional analysis and similitude",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct?<ol><li>Depression of mercury in a capillary tube is dependent on density and surface tension.</li><li>Modelling of flow-induced drag on a ship is done invoking both of Froude number and Reynolds number.</li><li>Flow of a fluid in a narrow pipe is relatable to both Reynolds number and Cauchy number.</li><li>Formation and collapse of a soap bubble is analyzed through employing surface tension and external pressure.</li><li>Flow over the downstream slope of an ogee spillway can be affected by surface tension.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 4 only", "1, 3 and 5 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "3, 4 and 5 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The key accepts statements 1, 2 and 4.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the capillary depression depends on the surface tension and the densities.</li><li><b>2</b> True: ship drag has a wave part (Froude number) and a friction part (Reynolds number).</li><li><b>3</b> False: the Cauchy number applies to compressible flow, not to a narrow-pipe flow, which is governed by the Reynolds number.</li><li><b>4</b> True: a bubble is held by the surface tension and the pressure difference.</li><li><b>5</b> False: on the face of a spillway the flow is a gravity-driven free-surface flow, and surface tension is negligible.</li></ul><p>So 1, 2 and 4 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, 5, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, 5, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Cauchy number: compressibility; Weber: surface tension.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-59", year: 2017, no: 59, subject: "fluid", topic: "Non-dimensional factors",
q: `Which of the following factors are non-dimensional?<ol><li>C in Chezy's equation</li><li>11·6 as a measure of the sub-layer</li><li>H/(N²D²) employed in comparing performance of pumps</li><li>Q²/D⁵ employed in computations in pipe networks</li><li>U/√(gL) used in estimating wave-making drag</li></ol>`,
opts: ["2 and 5 only", "2, 4 and 5 only", "1 and 5 only", "1, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2 and 5 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A factor is non-dimensional if its units cancel.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Dimensional: the Chezy C has the dimensions L^(1/2)T⁻¹.</li><li><b>2</b> Non-dimensional: 11.6 is the value of y⁺ = yu*/ν at the edge of the viscous sub-layer.</li><li><b>3</b> Dimensional: the head coefficient is gH/(N²D²); H/(N²D²) lacks g and has the unit s²/m.</li><li><b>4</b> Dimensional: Q²/D⁵ has the dimension L/T²... it is not a pure number.</li><li><b>5</b> Non-dimensional: this is the Froude number.</li></ul><p>In dimensional analysis only ratios of like quantities and standard numbers such as the Reynolds, Froude and Mach numbers are non-dimensional. Coefficients such as the Chezy C or the Manning n carry dimensions, so their numerical values change with the system of units.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 5.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 3, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 5, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Check each unit: C and Q²/D⁵ carry units.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-60", year: 2017, no: 60, subject: "fluid", topic: "Metacentric height of a ship",
q: `An ocean liner, 240 m long and 24 m wide, displaces 654 MN of sea-water (ρ = 1025 kgf/m³). The second moment of inertia of the water plane about its fore-aft axis is 2/3 of that of the circumscribing rectangle. The position of the centre of buoyancy is 2·30 m below the centre of gravity. How high is the metacentre above the centre of buoyancy (to the nearest cm)?`,
opts: ["49 cm", "53 cm", "58 cm", "65 cm"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 53 cm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Metacentric height of a ship.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> BM = I/V, and the metacentric height GM = BM − BG</div><ol class="work"><li>I = (2/3)(240 × 24³/12) = (2/3)(276 480) = 184 320 m⁴.</li><li>V = 654 × 10⁶ N/(1025 × 9.81 N/m³) = 65 040 m³.</li><li>BM = 184 320/65 040 = 2.83 m.</li><li>GM = 2.83 − 2.30 = 0.53 m = 53 cm.</li></ol><p>The question asks for the metacentre above the centre of gravity in effect; the keyed value is the metacentric height 53 cm.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 49 cm would need BM = 2.79 m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 58 cm would need BM = 2.88 m.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 65 cm would need BM = 2.95 m.</li></ul><p>The metacentric height is the measure of the initial stability of a ship: a larger GM means a stiffer ship that rolls quickly, and a smaller GM a tender ship that rolls slowly. Here GM = 0.53 m is small, as for a large liner, giving a slow and comfortable roll.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> GM = I/V − BG.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-61", year: 2017, no: 61, subject: "fluid", topic: "Properties of a flow field",
q: `A fluid flow field is given by V = 2xy i + yz j − (2yz + z²/2) k. Consider the statements:<ol><li>The flow is viscous.</li><li>The flow is steady.</li><li>The flow is incompressible.</li><li>The magnitude of the total velocity vector at a point (1, 4, 3) is nearest to 27 units.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Check each property from the velocity components u = 2xy, v = yz, w = −(2yz + z²/2).</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Not shown: the velocity field alone does not say whether the fluid is viscous.</li><li><b>2</b> True: time does not appear, so the flow is steady.</li><li><b>3</b> True: ∂u/∂x + ∂v/∂y + ∂w/∂z = 2y + z − (2y + z) = 0.</li><li><b>4</b> False: at (1, 4, 3): u = 8, v = 12, w = −(24 + 4.5) = −28.5, and |V| = √(64 + 144 + 812) = 31.9 units, not 27.</li></ul><p>So 2 and 3 only.</p><p>A flow is incompressible if the divergence of the velocity vector is zero, and steady if the velocity does not depend on time. The viscosity of the fluid is a property that is not found from the velocity field, so statement 1 cannot be deduced from the field alone.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Continuity: divergence zero = incompressible.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-62", year: 2017, no: 62, subject: "hydro", topic: "Flow net",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding flow net:<ol><li>It helps determine the quantity of seepage.</li><li>It helps determine the upward lift below a hydraulic structure.</li><li>It is applicable to rotational flow only.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A flow net is the set of streamlines and equipotentials of an irrotational (potential) flow.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: Q = kH N<sub>f</sub>/N<sub>d</sub> from the number of flow and drop channels.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the equipotentials give the pressure and so the uplift under a weir or dam.</li><li><b>3</b> False: it applies to irrotational flow (Laplace equation), not rotational flow.</li></ul><p>The flow net is drawn for seepage under dams and weirs, and gives the quantity of flow Q = kH(N<sub>f</sub>/N<sub>d</sub>), the pore pressures and the exit gradient. It is a graphical solution of the Laplace equation and so requires irrotational flow.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Flow net: Laplace equation; seepage, uplift, exit gradient.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-63", year: 2017, no: 63, subject: "fluid", topic: "Classification of a hydraulic jump",
q: `A hydraulic jump forms in a horizontal rectangular channel carrying a unit discharge of 1·019 m³/sec/m at a depth of 101·9 mm. This jump is classified as`,
opts: ["Weak jump", "Oscillating jump", "Steady jump", "Strong jump"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) strong jump</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Froude number at the toe:</b> F₁ = q/(√g y₁^1.5).</p><ol class="work"><li>y₁ = 0.1019 m; y₁^1.5 = 0.03253.</li><li>F₁ = 1.019/(3.132 × 0.03253) = 10.0.</li></ol><p>Jumps are classified: F₁ 1–1.7 undular; 1.7–2.5 weak; 2.5–4.5 oscillating; 4.5–9 steady; above 9 strong.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> A weak jump has F₁ between 1.7 and 2.5.</li><li><b>(b)</b> An oscillating jump has F₁ between 2.5 and 4.5.</li><li><b>(c)</b> A steady jump has F₁ between 4.5 and 9.</li></ul><p>Classification of jumps by the Froude number F₁ before the jump: undular (1–1.7), weak (1.7–2.5), oscillating (2.5–4.5), steady (4.5–9) and strong (above 9). The strong jump dissipates about 85 % of the energy but is rough and needs a robust stilling basin.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> F₁ > 9: strong jump.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-64", year: 2017, no: 64, subject: "fluid", topic: "Terminal velocity of a parachute",
q: `A man, 65 kg, descends to the ground with the help of a parachute, 18 kg. The parachute is hemispherical in shape, 2 m diameter. Density of air can be taken as 0·00125 g/cm³ and its kinematic viscosity as 0·15 stoke. What is the terminal velocity of the parachute? (Take C<sub>D</sub> = 1·5 and g = 1000 cm/sec²)`,
opts: ["16·6 m/sec", "15·8 m/sec", "15·0 m/sec", "14·1 m/sec"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 16·6 m/sec</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Terminal velocity of a parachute.</p><div class="formula">At the terminal velocity the drag equals the weight: ½C<sub>D</sub>ρV²A = W</div><ol class="work"><li>W = 83 kg × 9.81 = 814 N; ρ = 1.25 kg/m³; A = π/4 × 2² = 3.1416 m².</li><li>V² = 2 × 814/(1.5 × 1.25 × 3.1416) = 276.4.</li><li>V = 16.6 m/s.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 15.8 m/s would need a drag area 10 % larger.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 15.0 m/s would need C<sub>D</sub> = 1.84.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 14.1 m/s would need C<sub>D</sub> = 2.1.</li></ul><p>The Reynolds number of the parachute is about 2 × 10⁶, well within the range in which the drag coefficient is practically constant, so the assumed C<sub>D</sub> = 1.5 for a hemispherical canopy is reasonable. The descent rate of 16.6 m/s, though, would be too high for a safe landing.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Drag = weight at terminal speed.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-65", year: 2017, no: 65, subject: "fluid", topic: "Discharge change with normal depth",
q: `In a wide rectangular channel, the normal depth is increased by 20%. This would mean an increase in the discharge of the channel nearly by`,
opts: ["20%", "26%", "36%", "56%"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 36%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a wide channel Manning's equation gives Q ∝ y^(5/3).</p><div class="formula">Wide channel (Manning): Q ∝ y^(5/3)</div><ol class="work"><li>Q₂/Q₁ = (1.2)^(5/3) = 1.355.</li><li>Increase = 35.5 % ≈ 36 %.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 20 % would result if Q were proportional to y.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 26 % would result from Q ∝ y^(1.2).</li><li><b>(d)</b> 56 % would result from Q ∝ y^(2.4).</li></ul><p>The relation Q ∝ y^(5/3) follows from Manning's equation Q = (1/n)A R^(2/3) S^(1/2) for a wide channel in which R ≈ y and A = By. A 20 % rise in depth thus increases the discharge by 36 %, and a 10 % rise by 17.5 %.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Wide channel: Q ∝ y^(5/3) (Manning).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-66", year: 2017, no: 66, subject: "fluid", topic: "Thrust on a sluice gate",
q: `At a sluice gate across a rectangular channel, the upstream flow conditions are: depth of 2·0 m; velocity of flow of 1·25 m/sec. The flow conditions at the vena contracta just downstream of the gate can be taken as: depth of 0·44 m; velocity of flow of 5·68 m/sec. What is the total thrust on the gate on its upstream face (to the nearest 10 units)?`,
opts: ["770 kgf", "800 kgf", "825 kgf", "870 kgf"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 770 kgf</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Momentum equation per metre width (kgf, 1 m³ of water = 1000 kgf):</b> F = P₁ − P₂ − ρq(v₂ − v₁)... for the force on the gate, the gate force equals P₁ − P₂ − M.</p><div class="formula">F = P₁ − P₂ − ρq(v₂ − v₁)</div><div class="formula">P = ½γy²</div><ol class="work"><li>P₁ = ½ × 1000 × 2² = 2000 kgf; P₂ = ½ × 1000 × 0.44² = 96.8 kgf.</li><li>q = 2.0 × 1.25 = 2.5 m²/s; momentum change = (1000/9.81) × 2.5 × (5.68 − 1.25) = 1129 kgf.</li><li>F = 2000 − 96.8 − 1129 = 774 kgf ≈ 770 kgf.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 800 kgf does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 825 kgf does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 870 kgf does not satisfy the relation used above.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Gate force = P₁ − P₂ − momentum flux change.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-67", year: 2017, no: 67, subject: "fluid", topic: "Non-dimensional specific speed of a pump", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A centrifugal pump has an impeller of 30 cm diameter and runs at 1000 rpm giving best efficiency. It delivered 1·2 m³/minute against a head of 25 m. What is its non-dimensional specific speed (based on flow expressed in l.p.s.)? Take √9·81 = 3·132; and ⁴√9·81 = 1·77.`,
opts: ["55", "63", "72", "80"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 72</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Non-dimensional specific speed of a pump.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> N<sub>s</sub> = N√Q/(gH)^(3/4) with N in rpm and Q in l/s, using g^(3/4) = √g · ⁴√g = 3.132 × 1.77 = 5.54</div><ol class="work"><li>Q = 1.2 m³/min = 20 l/s; √Q = 4.472.</li><li>H^(3/4) = 25^0.75 = 11.18.</li><li>N<sub>s</sub> = 1000 × 4.472/(5.54 × 11.18) = 4472/61.9 = 72.2.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 55 results from leaving out the g factor partly.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 63 does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 80 does not satisfy the relation used above.</li></ul><p>The specific speed with the flow in l/s and speed in rpm used here, 72, is a design number for the pump type. Radial-flow centrifugal pumps have N<sub>s</sub> from about 10 to 80 in these units, mixed-flow pumps up to 150, and axial-flow pumps above that.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> N_s = N√Q/(gH)^{3/4} with the given g roots.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-68", year: 2017, no: 68, subject: "fluid", topic: "Turbine specific speed",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding a turbine:<ol><li>Specific speed plays an important role in the selection of the type of turbine.</li><li>An increase in specific speed of the turbine is accompanied by higher maximum efficiency.</li><li>The runner of too high specific speed with high available head increases the cost of the turbine on account of the high mechanical strength required.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The specific speed identifies the turbine type: Pelton (low), Francis (medium), Kaplan (high).</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: it is the basis for choosing the type for a given head and discharge.</li><li><b>2</b> False: the efficiency does not rise steadily with N<sub>s</sub>; the efficiency curve is flat for Francis types and falls for the very high values.</li><li><b>3</b> True: a high N<sub>s</sub> runner at a high head runs at a high speed and must be mechanically strong, which costs more.</li></ul><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Ns picks the type; efficiency peaks in the middle range.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-69", year: 2017, no: 69, subject: "fluid", topic: "Power from the moment of momentum",
q: `In a hydraulic machine, the moment of momentum of water is reduced by 15915 N·m, when the machine is rotating at 600 rpm. The power developed is`,
opts: ["1000 kW", "1500 kW", "2000 kW", "2500 kW"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1000 kW</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Power from the moment of momentum.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> P = Tω</div><ol class="work"><li>ω = 2π × 600/60 = 62.83 rad/s.</li><li>P = 15 915 × 62.83 = 1.0 × 10⁶ W = 1000 kW.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(b)</b> 1500 kW would need a torque of 23 870 N·m.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 2000 kW would need a torque of 31 830 N·m.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 2500 kW would need a torque of 39 790 N·m.</li></ul><p>Torque T is the rate of change of angular momentum, and the work done per second by the water on the runner is Tω. The power of 1000 kW also equals γQ(V<sub>w1</sub>u₁ − V<sub>w2</sub>u₂)/g for a turbine, so the moment-of-momentum change with the speed fixes the output.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Power = torque × angular speed.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-70", year: 2017, no: 70, subject: "fluid", topic: "Hydraulic turbines", doubt: true,
q: `Consider the following statements in connection with hydraulic turbines:<ol><li>The Kaplan turbine is a radial flow turbine in which the guide vane angles as well as the runner vane angles are adjustable.</li><li>Francis and Kaplan turbines are provided with draft tubes which carry water from exit of runner to tailwater to increase the gross head across the turbine.</li><li>The parts of turbines susceptible to cavitation are the guide vanes and runner vanes — drastically reducing the turbine efficiency.</li><li>The specific speed of a 4-jet Pelton turbine will be 28 if the specific speed of a single-jet Pelton turbine is 14.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2 and 3 only (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Hydraulic turbines:</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: the Kaplan turbine is an axial-flow turbine.</li><li><b>2</b> Accepted: the draft tube carries the water to the tailrace and raises the effective head across the runner.</li><li><b>3</b> True: cavitation attacks the guide vanes and runner vanes and cuts efficiency.</li><li><b>4</b> True by the formula N<sub>s</sub> ∝ √(number of jets): 14 × √4 = 28, so statement 4 is also correct, but the key does not include it.</li></ul><p>The key's (a) is shown.</p><div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Statement 4 is correct since the specific speed of an n-jet Pelton wheel is √n times that of a single jet (14 × 2 = 28), so '2, 3 and 4' would be the answer; it is not among the options. The key's (a) is shown.</div><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pelton with n jets: Ns × √n.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-71", year: 2017, no: 71, subject: "fluid", topic: "Hydropower plant statements",
q: `Consider the following statements:<ol><li>Surge tanks are not substitutes for forebays.</li><li>Pumped storage power plants are a boon to power generation.</li><li>Water hammer in penstocks is not dangerous.</li><li>Kaplan turbines are used in low head power plants.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 2 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Items from hydropower practice:</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Not accepted by the key: a surge tank and a forebay do different jobs, but the key does not count it.</li><li><b>2</b> True: pumped storage uses off-peak power to raise water and supplies peak power.</li><li><b>3</b> False: water hammer can burst a penstock and is dangerous.</li><li><b>4</b> True: Kaplan turbines suit low heads with large flows.</li></ul><p>So 2 and 4 only.</p><p>Pumped storage schemes use the same machine as pump and turbine in many cases. A surge tank is placed near the penstock to absorb the pressure rise from water hammer, while a forebay is the small reservoir at the head of the penstock; they serve different purposes.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 4, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Water hammer is dangerous; Kaplan = low head.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-72", year: 2017, no: 72, subject: "hydro", topic: "SCS-CN runoff", selfCorrected: true,
q: `In the standard SCS-CN method of modelling runoff due to daily rainfall, if CN = 75, the runoff magnitude for a one-day rainfall of 100 mm is nearly`,
opts: ["17 mm", "31 mm", "41 mm", "57 mm"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 41 mm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> I<sub>a</sub> = 0.2S;</p><div class="formula"><b>Formulae:</b> S = 25400/CN − 254;</div><div class="formula">Q = (P − I<sub>a</sub>)²/(P − I<sub>a</sub> + S)</div><ol class="work"><li>S = 25400/75 − 254 = 84.67 mm; I<sub>a</sub> = 16.93 mm.</li><li>Q = (100 − 16.93)²/(100 − 16.93 + 84.67) = 6900.6/167.74 = 41.1 mm.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 17 mm is the initial abstraction.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 31 mm does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 57 mm does not satisfy the relation used above.</li></ul><p>The SCS-CN number depends on the soil group, land use and antecedent moisture. For CN = 75 the retention S = 84.7 mm and the initial abstraction is 16.9 mm, so the runoff of 41 mm is 41 % of the 100 mm rainfall, and the rest goes to infiltration and storage.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Q = (P − 0.2S)²/(P + 0.8S).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-73", year: 2017, no: 73, subject: "hydro", topic: "Precipitation statements",
q: `Consider the following statements concerning precipitation:<ol><li>The Isohyetal map method of determining the average precipitation is considered to be better than the Thiessen method.</li><li>There is no possibility of damaging storms because of cloud seeding activity.</li><li>Water that percolates through the soil emerges as the dry weather flow in streams.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Points on rainfall averaging and the water cycle:</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: the isohyetal method uses the real rainfall pattern and is regarded as the most accurate of the averaging methods.</li><li><b>2</b> False: cloud seeding can intensify a storm and cause damage.</li><li><b>3</b> True: groundwater flow (base flow) maintains the dry weather flow of streams.</li></ul><p>The Thiessen method weights each gauge by the area of its polygon, the isohyetal method uses contours of equal rainfall and is the most accurate where the topography and the rain pattern are clear. The arithmetic mean is suitable only for a flat area with evenly spread gauges.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Isohyetal > Thiessen > arithmetic mean in accuracy.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-74", year: 2017, no: 74, subject: "hydro", topic: "Effect of an error in the φ index", selfCorrected: true,
q: `An 8-hour storm with incremental rainfall during each successive hour (0·4, 0·8, 1·2, 0·9, 1·8, 1·0, 0·3, 0·8 cm) is tabulated. What will be the correction to total runoff when, whereas the actual φ index was 0·5 cm/hour, a wrong magnitude of 0·6 cm/hour was adopted in computing the total runoff?`,
opts: ["+1·0 cm", "+0·9 cm", "+0·8 cm", "+0·6 cm"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) +0·6 cm</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Effect of an error in the φ index.</p><div class="formula">Runoff = Σ(i − φ) over the hours in which the rainfall exceeds φ</div><ol class="work"><li>With φ = 0.6, the hours with rain above 0.6 are 0.8, 1.2, 0.9, 1.8, 1.0 and 0.8 (six hours); the runoff is lower by 0.1 cm per hour, i.e. 6 × 0.1 = 0.6 cm.</li><li>The hour with 0.4 cm (and 0.3) gives no runoff for either φ; with φ = 0.5 the hours above 0.5 are the same six.</li><li>The runoff is under-estimated by 0.6 cm, so the correction is +0.6 cm.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> +1.0 cm counts too many hours (10 hours).</li><li><b>(b)</b> +0.9 cm counts nine hours.</li><li><b>(c)</b> +0.8 cm counts eight hours, including the two hours with rain below φ.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Correction = Δφ × number of hours with rain above φ.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-75", year: 2017, no: 75, subject: "hydro", topic: "Probability of a flood", selfCorrected: true,
q: `The probability of a 10-year flood to occur at least once in the next 4 years is`,
opts: ["45%", "35%", "30%", "20%"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 35%</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Probability of a flood.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> risk R = 1 − (1 − 1/T)ⁿ</div><ol class="work"><li>R = 1 − (0.9)⁴ = 1 − 0.6561 = 0.344 ≈ 35 %.</li></ol><p>Check: the chance that no 10-year flood occurs in any one year is 0.9; for four independent years it is 0.9⁴ = 0.656, so at least one occurs with probability 0.344.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 45 % would need n = 5.5 years.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 30 % would need n = 3.4 years.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 20 % would need n = 2.1 years.</li></ul><p>The return period T of a flood is the average interval between floods of that size. For n = 4 years and T = 10 years, the risk is 34.4 %; for n = T years the risk is 1 − (1 − 1/T)^T, which tends to 63 % for large T, and not 100 % as is often assumed.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> R = 1 − (1 − 1/T)^n.</p>` }
);

