window.PYQ_QUESTIONS.push(
{ id: "2017-76", year: 2017, no: 76, subject: "hydro", topic: "Tortuosity of a river",
q: `Tortuosity of a meandering river is the ratio of`,
opts: ["Meander length to width of the meander", "Meander length to width of the river", "Curved length along the river to the direct axial length of the river", "Direct axial length of the river to the curved length along the river"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) curved length to direct axial length</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The tortuosity (sinuosity) of a river is the measure of how much it winds: the length of the river along its channel divided by the straight-line (valley or axial) length between the two ends. It is always at least 1.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> The ratio of meander length to meander width describes the shape of a single loop, not the tortuosity.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Meander length to river width is a ratio used for the geometry of meanders (about 10–14 channel widths).</li><li><b>(d)</b> This is the inverse ratio, which is less than 1.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Tortuosity = channel length / valley length ≥ 1.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-77", year: 2017, no: 77, subject: "hydro", topic: "Groundwater aquifer terms",
q: `Consider the following statements in respect of ground water aquifers:<ol><li>Specific storage is specific capacity per unit depth of the aquifer.</li><li>Specific capacity is storage coefficient per unit aquifer depth.</li><li>Specific capacity is a constant for a given well.</li><li>For one-dimensional flow in a confined aquifer between two water bodies, the piezometric head line in the aquifer is a straight line.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["2 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only", "1 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 and 4 only (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> The key accepts statements 2 and 4.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Not accepted: specific storage is the volume of water released per unit volume of aquifer per unit fall of head.</li><li><b>2</b> Accepted by the key as worded.</li><li><b>3</b> False: the specific capacity of a well (yield per unit drawdown) varies with the pumping rate and duration.</li><li><b>4</b> True: for steady one-dimensional flow in a confined aquifer between two reservoirs, Darcy’s law gives a linear head variation.</li></ul><p>So 2 and 4 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, 3, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Confined 1-D steady flow: linear piezometric head.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-78", year: 2017, no: 78, subject: "hydro", topic: "Permeability by the Hazen relation", selfCorrected: true,
q: `An extended layer of soil with homogeneous rounded grains has 10% of the material finer than 0·07 mm. The constant to be adopted to determine its permeability has been recommended as 750. What is its permeability?`,
opts: ["2·583 m/day", "2·857 m/day", "3·244 m/day", "3·675 m/day"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3·675 m/day</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> <b>Hazen's relation:</b> k = C d₁₀², taking d₁₀ in mm and k in m/day.</p><ol class="work"><li>d₁₀² = 0.07² = 0.0049 mm².</li><li>k = 750 × 0.0049 = 3.675 m/day.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 2.583 m/day would need d₁₀ = 0.0587 mm.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 2.857 m/day would need d₁₀ = 0.0617 mm.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 3.244 m/day would need d₁₀ = 0.0658 mm.</li></ul><p>Hazen's formula is meant for clean, uniform sands with d₁₀ between 0.1 and 3 mm and a uniformity coefficient below 5. The permeability computed from it is only approximate, and is therefore checked by a constant-head test in the laboratory for important work.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> k = C d10² (units as the constant is stated).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-79", year: 2017, no: 79, subject: "hydro", topic: "Soil–water–crop relationship", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements in connection with soil-water-crop relationship:<ol><li>Water utilization by plants is mainly from capillary water.</li><li>The amount of irrigation water required to meet the evapotranspiration needs of the crop during its full growth duration is its consumptive irrigation requirement.</li><li>The depth of water required to bring the soil-moisture level of a given soil up to its field capacity is called hygroscopic water.</li><li>With continuous increase in quantity of water applied, the yield of most crops increases up to a certain limit and then is expected to be constant.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "3 and 4 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Soil water exists as gravity, capillary and hygroscopic water.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: capillary water is the part held by the soil that plants can draw on.</li><li><b>2</b> True: this is the definition of consumptive irrigation requirement (CIR).</li><li><b>3</b> False: the depth needed to bring the soil to field capacity is the water-deficiency (irrigation) depth; hygroscopic water is a film held too strongly for plants.</li><li><b>4</b> Not accepted: beyond an optimum, extra water reduces the yield (water logging) rather than keeping it constant.</li></ul><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 2, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Capillary water is the plants’ main supply; hygroscopic water is unavailable.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-80", year: 2017, no: 80, subject: "hydro", topic: "Lacey and Kennedy theories",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding design of channel by Lacey and Kennedy:<ol><li>The theoretical concept of silt transportation is the same in both theories.</li><li>Lacey improved upon Kennedy's formula.</li><li>There are no defects in either the theories of Lacey or of Kennedy.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Kennedy (1895) gave the critical velocity theory; Lacey (1930s) proposed the regime theory.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> Accepted by the key: both rest on the idea that silt is held in suspension by the vertical components of eddies.</li><li><b>2</b> True: Lacey improved on Kennedy by introducing a silt factor and a definite shape for the regime channel.</li><li><b>3</b> False: both have defects (Kennedy needs trial and error and neglects the bed slope; Lacey’s equations are empirical).</li></ul><p>The key's (a) is shown.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Kennedy: critical velocity; Lacey: regime.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-81", year: 2017, no: 81, subject: "hydro", topic: "Dams", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements in respect of dams:<ol><li>In a gravity dam, a grout curtain is provided near the toe to reduce the exit gradient.</li><li>A drainage gallery with its drainage pipe system provided in a gravity dam reduces the uplift pressure at all levels below the upstream water level.</li><li>An earthquake acceleration of 0·1 g acting vertically downward causes a decrease of 10% in the unit weight of concrete and of water in a gravity dam.</li><li>The Tehri dam is a gravity dam.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "2 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Statements on gravity dams:</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: the grout curtain is provided near the heel (upstream) to cut off seepage and reduce uplift; it is not at the toe.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the gallery and drains reduce the uplift below the upstream water level.</li><li><b>3</b> True: a downward vertical acceleration 0.1 g reduces the effective unit weights by 10 %.</li><li><b>4</b> False: Tehri is an earth and rockfill dam.</li></ul><p>So 2 and 3 only.</p><p>The grout curtain of a gravity dam is placed near the heel to cut off the seepage and reduce uplift, and the toe is protected by a cut-off wall or apron. A drainage gallery with drain holes lowers the uplift pressure in the dam body, as the key accepts.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Grout curtain at the heel; Tehri is earth and rockfill.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-82", year: 2017, no: 82, subject: "hydro", topic: "River training",
q: `Objectives for river training are:<ol><li>High flood discharge may pass safely through the reach.</li><li>Sediment load (including bed and suspended load) may be transported efficiently.</li><li>By making the river course unstable whereby to increase bank erosion.</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> River training means controlling the river so that it behaves in a desired manner.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: floods should pass safely without overtopping.</li><li><b>2</b> True: sediment should be carried efficiently without silting or scouring.</li><li><b>3</b> False: the object is to make the course stable and to prevent bank erosion, not to increase it.</li></ul><p>Training works include guide banks, groynes (spurs), embankments, cut-offs and pitching. They are meant to give a stable alignment, to carry floods and sediment safely, to protect the banks and the structures, and to keep a good depth for navigation.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> River training: safe floods, sediment passage, stable course.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-83", year: 2017, no: 83, subject: "env", topic: "Coagulant aids",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding coagulant aids:<ol><li>They are employed when temperature is low as they then accelerate the process of coagulation.</li><li>They are employed when flocs are small and water is coloured.</li><li>With their use, water purification capacity of the plant is increased.</li><li>Most widely used materials are oxidants, absorbents, weighing agents, activated carbon and polyelectrolytes.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 2 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Coagulant aids are added with the main coagulant to improve floc formation.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: in cold water the coagulation is slow, and an aid such as activated silica speeds it up.</li><li><b>2</b> True: small, weak flocs and coloured water are helped by aids.</li><li><b>3</b> True: better flocs allow higher loading and increase the plant’s capacity.</li><li><b>4</b> True: oxidants, adsorbents, weighting agents, activated carbon and polyelectrolytes are in common use.</li></ul><p>All four are correct.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Aids: activated silica, polyelectrolytes, clay, lime.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-84", year: 2017, no: 84, subject: "sa", topic: "Reading a shear force diagram", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements with regard to the shear force diagram for the beam ABCD (SFD: +50 kN at A falling linearly through E to a value below zero at C, a 20 kN step at C, constant to D, then a rise to +10 kN at B):<ol><li>The beam ABCD is an overhanging beam having supports at A and D only.</li><li>The beam carries a point load of 20 kN at C.</li><li>The beam carries a concentrated load of 10 kN at the end B.</li><li>The beam is an overhanging beam having supports at C and D only.</li><li>The beam carries a uniformly distributed load of 70 kN over the left hand portion AC only.</li></ol><img class="fig" src="img/ce/2017-84.png" alt="Figure for question 84">`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 5 only", "2, 3 and 4 only", "2, 4 and 5 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 3 and 5 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Read the diagram: the sloping line from A to C is a uniformly distributed load on AC; a vertical step is a reaction or point load; the end B has a downward 10 kN load.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: with the first support at A (the 50 kN jump at A is its reaction) and the second at D, the beam overhangs at B.</li><li><b>2</b> False: the 20 kN step at C is not a point load of that kind in the keyed reading.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the shear returns to zero at B through a 10 kN step: the concentrated load at the free end.</li><li><b>4</b> False: the supports are at A and D, not at C and D.</li><li><b>5</b> True: the slope of the shear line on AC means a uniform load whose total is 70 kN.</li></ul><p>So 1, 3 and 5 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 5, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3, 5.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 5, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, 4, which are incorrect; Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Slope of SFD = load intensity; steps = point loads and reactions.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-85", year: 2017, no: 85, subject: "env", topic: "Disinfectant", selfCorrected: true,
q: `A good disinfectant:<ol><li>Should be persistent enough to prevent regrowth of organisms in the distribution system.</li><li>Must be toxic to micro-organisms at concentrations well above the toxic thresholds of humans and higher animals.</li><li>Should have a fast rate of kill of micro-organisms.</li></ol>Select the correct answer using the codes given below.`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3", "1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A good disinfectant is toxic to microbes but harmless to people at the dose used.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: it should leave a residual that protects the water in the pipes.</li><li><b>2</b> False: it must be toxic to micro-organisms at concentrations well <b>below</b> the toxic level for humans and animals.</li><li><b>3</b> True: a fast kill keeps the contact time and the tank volume small.</li></ul><p>Disinfection kills pathogens but not all organisms (sterilisation does). Chlorine is persistent and cheap, ozone and ultraviolet are faster but leave no residual, so chlorine (or chloramine) is added after them to protect the water in the distribution system.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Disinfectant: persistent, fast, safe to humans.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-86", year: 2017, no: 86, subject: "env", topic: "Bleaching powder requirement", doubt: true,
q: `How much bleaching powder (having 20% available chlorine) is needed to disinfect 10,000 litres of water whose chlorine demand is 1·0 mg/l? It should be ensured that, after about 1 hour contact time, 0·2 mg/l chlorine is available.`,
opts: ["30 g", "40 g", "48 g", "68 g"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 48 g (as keyed)</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Chlorine dose = demand + residual = 1.0 + 0.2 = 1.2 mg/l.</p><div class="formula">Cl₂ dose = demand + residual</div><div class="formula">Bleaching powder = Cl₂ needed/available fraction</div><ol class="work"><li>Chlorine needed = 1.2 mg/l × 10 000 l = 12 000 mg = 12 g.</li><li>Bleaching powder = 12/0.20 = 60 g.</li></ol><p>The keyed 48 g corresponds to 9.6 g of chlorine, i.e. 0.96 mg/l, which does not match the working above.</p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 30 g would supply only 6 g of chlorine (0.6 mg/l).</li><li><b>(b)</b> 40 g would supply 8 g (0.8 mg/l), below the dose needed.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 68 g would supply 13.6 g (1.36 mg/l), more than needed.</li></ul><div class="note">In doubt (key followed). Our calculation gives 60 g, which is not among the options; the key's (c) 48 g could not be reproduced.</div><p><b>Remember:</b> Dose = demand + residual; powder = Cl₂/available fraction.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-87", year: 2017, no: 87, subject: "fluid", topic: "Brake horse power of a pump",
q: `The approximate value of BHP of a pump to supply 1 m³/s water to a town at RL 400 m from a source, whose water level is at RL 320 m, would be`,
opts: ["940", "1000", "1070", "1120"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1070</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Brake horse power of a pump.</p><div class="formula"><b>Formula:</b> BHP = wQH/(75 η) with w in kgf/m³, Q in m³/s, H in m, power in metric horse power</div><ol class="work"><li>H = 400 − 320 = 80 m (losses neglected, η = 1 as no efficiency is given).</li><li>BHP = 1000 × 1 × 80/75 = 1067 ≈ 1070.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 940 would need an efficiency above 100 %.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 1000 corresponds to H = 75 m.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 1120 would correspond to H = 84 m.</li></ul><p>The brake horse power is the power at the pump shaft; with an efficiency of 70 % it would be about 1500 hp. The suction and delivery losses add to the static head of 80 m, so the actual pumping head is greater, and the motor is selected with a margin on the power.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> hp = wQH/75 (metric).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-88", year: 2017, no: 88, subject: "env", topic: "Removal of impurities",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding removal of impurities from water:<ol><li>Settleable solids are removed by filtration.</li><li>Volatile solids are removed through sedimentation.</li><li>Dissolved solids are removed through reverse osmosis.</li><li>Colloidal solids are removed by coagulation.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "3 and 4 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Each class of impurity needs its own process.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: settleable solids are removed by sedimentation; filtration removes the fine suspended matter that is left.</li><li><b>2</b> False: volatile (organic) solids are not removed by sedimentation alone.</li><li><b>3</b> True: reverse osmosis removes dissolved salts.</li><li><b>4</b> True: colloids are destabilised and flocculated by coagulation.</li></ul><p>Water treatment processes in sequence: screening, aeration, coagulation and flocculation (colloids), sedimentation (settleable solids), filtration (fine suspended solids), disinfection (microbes), and special processes such as reverse osmosis or ion exchange for dissolved salts.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Settleable: sedimentation; colloidal: coagulation; dissolved: RO/ion exchange.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-89", year: 2017, no: 89, subject: "env", topic: "Contact stabilization process", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements regarding contact stabilization process:<ol><li>Primary settling tank is not required in some cases.</li><li>BOD removal occurs in two stages.</li><li>Aeration volume requirements are approximately 50% of those of a conventional — or tapered — aeration plant.</li><li>Returned sludge is aerated for 30 min to 90 min in sludge aeration tank.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 2,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (c) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> In the contact stabilization process the waste is mixed briefly (15–30 min) with the stabilised returned sludge, which adsorbs the organic matter; the sludge is then settled and re-aerated.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: with the adsorbing sludge, the primary settling tank can be omitted in small plants.</li><li><b>2</b> True: BOD is removed in two stages, adsorption (contact) and stabilisation (oxidation).</li><li><b>3</b> True: the total aeration volume is about 50 % of that of a conventional plant.</li><li><b>4</b> False: the returned sludge is aerated for about 3–6 hours, not 30–90 minutes (the 30–90 min is the contact time).</li></ul><p>So 1, 2 and 3 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Contact: 0.5–1.5 h; stabilisation of RAS: 3–6 h.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-90", year: 2017, no: 90, subject: "env", topic: "Pyrolysis",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding pyrolysis:<ol><li>It is an irreversible chemical change brought about by the action of heat in oxygen-free atmosphere.</li><li>Range of temperature is 500°C to 1000°C.</li><li>Internal heating causes organic matter to decompose physically and chemically rather than burn.</li><li>It is a highly exothermic process.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 1, 2 and 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Pyrolysis is the thermal decomposition of organic matter in the absence of oxygen.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: it is an irreversible chemical change by heat without oxygen.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the usual range is 500–1000 °C.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the organic matter decomposes into gas, oil and char instead of burning.</li><li><b>4</b> False: pyrolysis is <b>endothermic</b>; heat has to be supplied.</li></ul><p>Pyrolysis of solid waste produces three products: a gas (CO, H₂, CH₄), a liquid (oil and tar) and a solid char. The heat for the process is supplied from outside or from burning a part of the products. The gas is used as a fuel, which makes it an energy recovery process.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 4, which is incorrect; Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Pyrolysis: endothermic, no oxygen; combustion: exothermic.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-91", year: 2017, no: 91, subject: "env", topic: "Air pollution",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding air pollution:<ol><li>The pollutant caused by incomplete combustion of organic matter is carbon monoxide.</li><li>Depletion of ozone in outer atmosphere may trigger skin cancer.</li><li>Acid rains are caused by SO₂ and NO<sub>x</sub>.</li><li>The permissible standard for SO₂ in air for residential areas in India is 80 μg/m³.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1, 2, 3 and 4", "1, 3 and 4 only", "2 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 3 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1, 2, 3 and 4</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> All the statements are accepted by the key.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: incomplete combustion gives CO.</li><li><b>2</b> True: the ozone layer shields the earth from ultraviolet radiation that can cause skin cancer.</li><li><b>3</b> True: SO₂ and NO<sub>x</sub> form sulphuric and nitric acids in the air.</li><li><b>4</b> True: the NAAQS annual standard for SO₂ in residential areas is 80 μg/m³ (the 24 h value is 120 μg/m³ in the older standard).</li></ul><p>Other air pollutants of concern are particulate matter (PM10, PM2.5), nitrogen oxides, ozone, lead and hydrocarbons. CO binds with haemoglobin about 200 times more strongly than oxygen. SO₂ and NO<sub>x</sub> combine with water in the air to give acid rain with a pH below 5.6.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, 3, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 4, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> NAAQS (old): SO₂ 80 μg/m³ annual, residential.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-92", year: 2017, no: 92, subject: "geo", topic: "Expansive soils", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements:<ol><li>Illite is the mineral largely responsible for the swelling and shrinkage behaviour of clayey soils.</li><li>A differential free swell value of 55% indicates a soil with low degree of expansiveness.</li><li>Higher the plasticity index of a soil, greater its swelling potential.</li><li>A low shrinkage limit of a soil indicates possibility of swelling at low water content.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 4 only", "3 and 4 only"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Swelling of clay is controlled by its mineral type and plasticity.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: montmorillonite, not illite, is the mineral mainly responsible for swelling.</li><li><b>2</b> False: a free swell of 50–100 % means a <b>high</b> degree of expansiveness for the index in the classification (low is below 20 %).</li><li><b>3</b> True: a higher plasticity index goes with higher swelling.</li><li><b>4</b> True: a low shrinkage limit means the soil changes volume over a larger water-content range and swells even at low moisture.</li></ul><p>So 3 and 4 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, 4, which are also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3, 4.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Montmorillonite swells most; illite moderately; kaolinite least.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-93", year: 2017, no: 93, subject: "geo", topic: "Indicators of full saturation",
q: `Consider the following effects as indicative of complete saturation of a soil sample:<ol><li>Pore water pressure is positive.</li><li>Volume of water to volume of voids is equal to 1.</li><li>Relative density is equal to 1.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> A fully saturated soil has no air in the voids.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: pore water pressure is positive (the water fills the voids and is under pressure).</li><li><b>2</b> True: degree of saturation S = V<sub>w</sub>/V<sub>v</sub> = 1.</li><li><b>3</b> False: relative density describes the compactness of a sand (between 0 and 1) and has nothing to do with saturation.</li></ul><p>The degree of saturation S = V<sub>w</sub>/V<sub>v</sub> is 100 % when the soil is fully saturated; the water is then under a positive pressure below the water table. The relative density (density index) is a different quantity which tells how compact a cohesionless soil is.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> S = Vw/Vv = 1; relative density ≠ saturation.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-94", year: 2017, no: 94, subject: "geo", topic: "Consolidation",
q: `Consider the following statements:<ol><li>Secondary consolidation of soil follows Terzaghi's one-dimensional theory of consolidation.</li><li>Consolidation is a function of total stress.</li><li>Even after complete dissipation of excess pore pressure, the soil undergoes a little more consolidation.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["3 only", "1 and 2 only", "2 and 3 only", "1 only"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 3 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Consolidation involves primary (hydrodynamic) and secondary (creep) parts.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: Terzaghi’s theory covers primary consolidation only; secondary compression is not part of it.</li><li><b>2</b> False: consolidation is a function of the effective stress.</li><li><b>3</b> True: secondary compression continues after the excess pore pressure has dissipated.</li></ul><p>Terzaghi's one-dimensional theory covers the dissipation of excess pore pressure (primary consolidation) and assumes that the effective stress increase equals the excess pore pressure that is lost. Secondary compression is treated empirically by the coefficient C<sub>α</sub>.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 3.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 1, 2, which are incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Terzaghi: primary only; effective stress governs.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-95", year: 2017, no: 95, subject: "geo", topic: "Cohesion from an undrained triaxial test",
q: `An undrained triaxial compression test is carried out on a saturated clay sample under a cell pressure of 50 kN/m². The sample failed at a deviator stress of 100 kN/m². The cohesion of this clay sample would be`,
opts: ["25 kN/m²", "50 kN/m²", "75 kN/m²", "100 kN/m²"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 50 kN/m²</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> For a saturated clay under undrained loading, φ<sub>u</sub> = 0 and the cohesion c<sub>u</sub> is half the deviator stress at failure.</p><div class="formula">c_u = (σ₁ − σ₃)/2 (φ_u = 0)</div><ol class="work"><li>c<sub>u</sub> = (σ₁ − σ₃)/2 = 100/2 = 50 kN/m² (the cell pressure has no effect).</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 25 kN/m² would result from dividing by 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 75 kN/m² adds the cell pressure to half the deviator stress.</li><li><b>(d)</b> 100 kN/m² is the deviator stress itself.</li></ul><p>In an undrained test on a saturated clay the Mohr circles for different cell pressures have the same diameter, so the failure envelope is horizontal with φ<sub>u</sub> = 0, and c<sub>u</sub> = (σ₁ − σ₃)/2 = 50 kN/m² is independent of the cell pressure, as it should be.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> cu = σ_d/2 when φu = 0.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-96", year: 2017, no: 96, subject: "geo", topic: "Permeability of soils",
q: `Consider the following statements regarding permeability of soils:<ol><li>Permeability of coarse grained soil is inversely proportional to the specific surface at a given porosity.</li><li>Direct measurement of permeability of a soil specimen at any stage of loading in oedometer test can be made only in fixed-ring type oedometer.</li><li>The permeability of an aquifer increases with decrease in temperature of water moving through it.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1 and 3 only", "2 and 3 only", "1, 2 and 3"], ans: 0,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (a) 1 and 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Permeability depends on the soil structure and on the fluid.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: in the Kozeny–Carman relation k ∝ 1/S², so a larger specific surface gives a lower permeability.</li><li><b>2</b> True: with a fixed ring, water can be passed through the specimen at any stage of loading; in a floating ring the specimen moves and the permeability cannot be measured directly.</li><li><b>3</b> False: cold water is more viscous, so the permeability falls as the temperature falls.</li></ul><p>The permeability of a soil is k = (γ<sub>w</sub>/μ)K, where K depends on the pore structure; since the viscosity of water falls as the temperature rises, k is greater at higher temperatures. Test results are therefore reported for a standard temperature of 27 °C.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2.</li><li><b>(b)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 3, which is incorrect.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> k ∝ γ/μ: warmer water flows more easily.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-97", year: 2017, no: 97, subject: "geo", topic: "Stress distribution theories",
q: `Which of the following statements are correct?<ol><li>Stress Isobar can be prepared using Boussinesq's stress distribution theory.</li><li>Equivalent point load method yields accurate results.</li><li>Newmark's method relates the vertical stress with the help of influence chart.</li><li>Westergaard's method helps in determination of stress distribution for layered soils.</li></ol>`,
opts: ["1, 2 and 3 only", "1, 3 and 4 only", "1, 2 and 4 only", "2, 3 and 4 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 3 and 4 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Methods of stress distribution under loads on soil:</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: pressure bulbs (isobars) are drawn from the Boussinesq equation.</li><li><b>2</b> False: the equivalent point load method is approximate, and is accurate only at a distance of more than about twice the width of the loaded area.</li><li><b>3</b> True: Newmark’s influence chart gives the vertical stress under an irregular area.</li><li><b>4</b> True: Westergaard’s theory is for a soil reinforced by thin rigid layers (stratified soil).</li></ul><p>So 1, 3 and 4 only.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 4, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 3, 4.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Includes 2, which is incorrect; Omits 1, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Boussinesq: homogeneous; Westergaard: stratified.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-98", year: 2017, no: 98, subject: "geo", topic: "Reinforced soil and geotextiles", selfCorrected: true,
q: `Consider the following statements:<ol><li>Functions of reinforcement in reinforced soil and in reinforced concrete are comparable.</li><li>The design of a geotextile reinforced wall is similar in principle to that of a reinforced earth wall.</li></ol>Which of the above statements is/are correct?`,
opts: ["1 only", "2 only", "Both 1 and 2", "Neither 1 nor 2"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 2 only</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Both reinforced earth and geotextile walls use tensile inclusions in soil.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> False: in reinforced concrete the steel takes the tension that concrete cannot, by bond, while in reinforced soil the strips hold the soil by friction along their length and act as tie-backs; the functions differ.</li><li><b>2</b> True: both are designed on the same principle of the tensile resistance and the anchorage of the reinforcement in the soil.</li></ul><p>In reinforced earth (Vidal), strips of steel or geosynthetics are laid in layers in the fill, and the friction between the fill and the strips resists the pull-out. The same principle of tensile reinforcement applies to a geotextile-wrapped wall, which is designed for tensile strength and anchorage length.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect; Omits 2, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 2.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Includes 1, which is incorrect.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Reinforced earth: friction-based tension inclusions.</p>` },

{ id: "2017-99", year: 2017, no: 99, subject: "geo", topic: "Settlement of soils",
q: `Consider the following statements:<ol><li>Immediate settlement takes place as soon as the load is placed.</li><li>Secondary settlement is significant in the case of organic soil.</li><li>Secondary settlement is estimated based on the 'void ratio versus time curve' for a particular load under consolidation test.</li></ol>Which of the above statements are correct?`,
opts: ["1 and 2 only", "1, 2 and 3", "2 and 3 only", "1 and 3 only"], ans: 1,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (b) 1, 2 and 3</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> All three statements are correct.</p><ul><li><b>1</b> True: immediate (elastic) settlement occurs as the load is applied.</li><li><b>2</b> True: organic soils and peats show large secondary compression.</li><li><b>3</b> True: the slope of the void ratio–log time curve after primary consolidation gives C<sub>α</sub>, with which the secondary settlement is estimated.</li></ul><p>Settlement of a foundation has three parts: immediate settlement (elastic, at once), primary consolidation (dissipation of pore pressure) and secondary compression (creep). The total of the three is used for design, with the secondary part significant only for organic soils.</p><p><b>Options:</b></p><ul><li><b>(a)</b> Omits 3, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(b)</b> <b>Correct.</b> Exactly the correct statements: 1, 2, 3.</li><li><b>(c)</b> Omits 1, which is also correct.</li><li><b>(d)</b> Omits 2, which is also correct.</li></ul><p><b>Remember:</b> Secondary settlement: C_α log(t₂/t₁).</p>` },

{ id: "2017-100", year: 2017, no: 100, subject: "geo", topic: "Footing load from a plate load test",
q: `In a plate load test on a soil, at a particular magnitude of the settlement, it was observed that the bearing pressure beneath the footing is 100 kN/m² and the perimeter shear is 25 kN/m². Correspondingly, the load capacity of a 2 m square footing at the same settlement will be`,
opts: ["200 kN", "300 kN", "400 kN", "600 kN"], ans: 3,
analysis: `<h3>Answer: (d) 600 kN</h3><p><b>Concept:</b> Footing load from a plate load test.</p><div class="formula"><b>Perimeter shear method:</b> Q = q A + p P (bearing pressure times area plus perimeter shear times perimeter)</div><ol class="work"><li>q A = 100 × 2 × 2 = 400 kN.</li><li>p P = 25 × (4 × 2) = 200 kN.</li><li>Q = 600 kN.</li></ol><ul><li><b>(a)</b> 200 kN is the perimeter term alone.</li><li><b>(b)</b> 300 kN does not satisfy the relation used above.</li><li><b>(c)</b> 400 kN is the base term alone, leaving out the perimeter shear.</li></ul><p>The perimeter shear method of Terzaghi and Peck is used when the plate-test results from a small plate are to be transferred to a larger footing in clay, with the bearing pressure and the perimeter shear separated, because the perimeter effect does not scale with the area.</p><p><b>Remember:</b> Q = qA + pP.</p>` }
);

